window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"bert-jansch-jack-orion":["Jansch's third album and his first given almost entirely to traditional songs: the long title ballad, Blackwaterside, Nottamun Town, The Waggoner's Lad, Henry Martin, Pretty Polly, and MacColl's The First Time Ever I Saw Your Face. John Renbourn plays second guitar on several tracks, and the two guitars weave independent lines in a style that would become Pentangle's. Jansch plays banjo on a couple of songs.","Recorded in 1966 for Transatlantic, as the London folk scene around Les Cousins club was absorbing the ballad repertoire and the collectors' archives. Jansch and Renbourn were sharing a flat and had just made a duet album. The ballads came from Child, from Cecil Sharp's English and Appalachian collections, and from other singers in the clubs. Bill Leader again made the recordings, simply and closely miked.","Jimmy Page listened closely to Blackwaterside and adapted its guitar arrangement as the instrumental Black Mountain Side on Led Zeppelin's first album in 1969, without credit; the matter was never settled in court. Listen to the guitar in Blackwaterside, which runs a drone in a modal tuning under the melody, the same method Davy Graham had used behind Shirley Collins.","It was well reviewed in the folk press, and later critics have placed it among his best. Its influence came through its musicians: Page, Sandy Denny, and Pentangle all drew on it. It is sometimes judged less personal than his first album, but it is where Jansch showed what a guitarist could do with the old ballads.","Start with Blackwaterside, then play Led Zeppelin's Black Mountain Side. Then the title ballad, all ten minutes of it, and hear Pentangle stretch it further on Cruel Sister. Compare his First Time Ever with Peggy Seeger's. Notice how the guitar keeps its drone even when the melody moves far away."],"planxty-planxty":["The band's debut, known from its dark sleeve as 'the black album'. Songs such as Raggle Taggle Gypsy, The Blacksmith and MacColl's Sweet Thames Flow Softly alternate with dance tunes and the harper Carolan's Sí Bheag, Sí Mhór on the pipes. Liam O'Flynn's uilleann pipes carry the tunes; Dónal Lunny's bouzouki and Andy Irvine's mandolin weave counter-melodies around them instead of strumming chords, and Christy Moore and Irvine share the singing.","The four came together in 1972 to back Christy Moore's solo album Prosperous, recorded in a country house in Co. Kildare, and liked the sound enough to stay. Their live shows in Dublin caused a sensation, and Polydor released the debut in 1973, when Irish traditional music was split between the céilí band and the ballad group. Planxty offered a third way.","The Greek bouzouki had come to Irish music only recently, brought back by Johnny Moynihan and adapted by Lunny and Irvine with a flat back and different stringing; this is its founding record. The name Planxty is a term from Carolan's harp music for a tune written in tribute to a patron. Listen for the counterpoint between bouzouki and mandolin under the pipes.","It was an immediate success in Ireland and is now considered one of the most influential Irish albums ever made, the source of a string-band texture copied by countless groups. Some older traditional players disliked the arrangements; within a few years they had become the norm. Christy Moore and Dónal Lunny went on to shape Irish popular music for decades, in Moving Hearts and beyond.","Start with Raggle Taggle Gypsy running into the tune that follows it and listen to the bouzouki. Then Sí Bheag, Sí Mhór for the pipes alone. Compare the Chieftains' first album, a decade earlier, and The Bothy Band two years later."],"planxty-the-well-below-the-valley":["Planxty's second album, released only months after the first, with the same line-up and the same interlocking strings under the pipes. Its heart is the title track, a strange, unresolving ballad sung by Christy Moore, in which a stranger at a well questions a woman about her children until her hidden crimes come out. Dance sets, Andy Irvine's songs and pipe airs complete the record.","Recorded in 1973 while the band was touring constantly across Ireland and Britain, it consolidated their success. Moore learned the title ballad from John Reilly, a singer from the Irish Travelling community recorded by collectors in the 1960s, whose repertoire held some of the rarest ballads in Ireland. The album also shows the band widening its repertoire, with Andy Irvine bringing songs learned on his travels and from older Irish singers into the arrangements.","The title song is a version of Child 21, The Maid and the Palmer, a ballad about a woman confronted with her sins, sometimes linked to the story of the Samaritan woman at the well in John's Gospel. Child had found only fragments; Reilly's version was one of very few complete ones from oral tradition, and Planxty's recording made it known.","Received as a worthy follow-up, it has sometimes been rated slightly behind the debut, though the title track is now among the band's best-known recordings. Its importance for ballad scholarship is its use of a Traveller source, part of a new respect for Travellers as tradition-bearers. The band split two years later, so the album also marks the end of its first phase.","Hear the title song first and notice that it never quite resolves, musically or morally. Then go back to Lord Randal for the same question-and-answer method. Finish with a dance set to hear the band at full speed. Keep the words in front of you the first time."],"the-chieftains-the-chieftains-1":["Instrumental Irish dance music and slow airs, arranged for a small ensemble: jigs, reels, slides and a hornpipe, with Paddy Moloney's uilleann pipes at the centre, tin whistle, wooden flute, fiddle and bodhrán around them. Instead of everyone playing the tune together in unison, as in a céilí band, the arrangements pass it from one instrument to another, add harmonies and drop players out, so each set has a shape like a small chamber piece.","Claddagh Records was founded in Dublin by Garech Browne, the Guinness heir, to record Irish traditional music and poetry, and this was one of its first releases. The players came from Seán Ó Riada's Ceoltóirí Chualann, which had shown in the early 1960s that traditional tunes could be arranged for concert performance. The band stayed part-time amateurs for over a decade.","The approach of passing a tune around the ensemble came directly from Ó Riada, a composer trained in European classical music, who treated a traditional dance set as a small concerto grosso. Listen for the moments where the pipes drop out and the whistle and flute carry the tune alone, then return with a drone underneath.","It made little noise on release but set a template later groups have followed ever since. The Chieftains' later career of celebrity collaborations has sometimes been criticised for diluting the music; their early Claddagh records remain the ones traditional players respect most. Collectors and players alike prize it for its unhurried tempos and for tunes that were then little known outside the regions they came from.","Pick one reel set and follow the tune as it moves between instruments. Then hear a slow air on the pipes and listen for the ornaments. Compare with Stanford's Irish Symphony, which uses tunes of the same kind in a Victorian orchestra."],"the-chieftains-women-of-ireland":["A slow air, played without words. The melody rises in long, arching phrases and is passed between uilleann pipes, fiddle and flute over a soft, sustained accompaniment, building gradually to a restrained climax with a steady drum pulse before falling away. It has the gravity of a lament, though the poem it was written for is a love song. Harp and fiddle trade the lead at points, and the pipes return for the last phrase.","The words are an eighteenth-century Irish poem by Peadar Ó Doirnín; the tune was written in the 1960s by Seán Ó Riada, the composer whose ensemble spawned the Chieftains. The Chieftains' instrumental version was recorded for their fourth album. Stanley Kubrick used it in Barry Lyndon (1975), whose soundtrack also features Handel and Schubert, and the film made it widely known.","Many listeners assume the melody is centuries old; it is a modern composition by a classically trained composer, written deliberately in the style of a traditional air. Its placing in Barry Lyndon, beside Handel's Sarabande, makes the point: Kubrick heard it as belonging with baroque music. The poem itself lists famous women of Irish legend and history.","It has become one of the best-known Irish melodies and has been recorded by singers such as Kate Bush and Sinéad O'Connor with the original Irish words. Traditional players sometimes object that its fame has overshadowed older airs; others hold it up as proof that the tradition can still grow new tunes.","Listen for the drum entering and the gradual build. Then hear a sung version with the Irish words. Finally watch the relevant scenes of Barry Lyndon, and compare with Bax's Tintagel, another composer's Celtic seascape. Notice how long the melody takes to breathe out."],"the-bothy-band-the-bothy-band":["The debut of a six-piece that played Irish reels and jigs faster and harder than anyone before. Matt Molloy's flute, Paddy Keenan's uilleann pipes and Tommy Peoples's fiddle play the tunes in tight unison over Dónal Lunny's bouzouki, Mícheál Ó Domhnaill's guitar and Tríona Ní Dhomhnaill's clavinet or harpsichord. Songs in English and Irish, sung by the Ó Domhnaill siblings in Donegal style, break up the dance sets.","Lunny formed the band in 1974 after leaving Planxty, drawing on the young musicians gathering at Dublin sessions. The debut appeared in 1975, when Irish traditional music was newly popular with a young audience that also listened to rock. They toured widely in Britain and Europe; the high tempos and volume made their concerts feel like rock shows. Their studio sound tried to catch that live energy.","Ní Dhomhnaill's clavinet, an electric keyboard associated with funk, gives the rhythm section a percussive snap. Listen for the way the three melody instruments play not quite identical versions of each tune, each ornamenting in its own style: the tiny differences are what makes the unison sound so thick. The songs, by contrast, are slow and unhurried.","Traditional musicians argued about the speed, which some felt robbed the tunes of their lift. The band's example nonetheless won out; their line-up and approach became the model for later groups such as Altan and Lúnasa. The album is regarded as one of the essential Irish traditional records. Their live album remains the favourite of many fans.","Start with any reel set and try to follow just one instrument. Then compare the same kind of tune on The Chieftains 1 to hear how much faster and fuller it has become. Play it loud. Then listen once more to a song by Tríona Ní Dhomhnaill, where the band pulls right back."],"the-bothy-band-old-hag-you-have-killed-me":["The band's second album, with Kevin Burke now on fiddle. The title comes from a jig; the record mixes long dance sets with songs, including the Scottish Gaelic mouth-music of Fionnghuala, sung by the Ó Domhnaill siblings as a rhythmic, wordplay-driven dance song without instruments. The ensemble sound is the same dense unison of pipes, flute and fiddle over bouzouki and keyboards, somewhat better recorded and more varied than the debut.","Released in 1976, the year the band was at its busiest on tour in Ireland, Britain and Europe. Burke, a London-born fiddler of Sligo parents, brought the smoother Sligo style in place of Tommy Peoples's Donegal attack. The band broke up in 1979, and Matt Molloy went on to join the Chieftains. Their sound became a pattern for touring Irish bands.","Fionnghuala is mouth music, puirt à beul, the Gaelic practice of singing dance tunes with syllables so people can dance without instruments. Hearing it between the instrumental sets shows the root of the whole style: the tunes are meant for bodies, and the voices here are doing the job of a fiddle.","Critics received it as confirming the band's standing; it is often named with the debut as their best. Some traditional commentators continued to find the tempos excessive. Fionnghuala became a favourite of later Celtic groups. The band recorded only two more albums before breaking up, making this the centre of its short career and, for many listeners, the easiest place to begin.","Start with Fionnghuala, then any reel set. Compare the fiddle with the first album to hear the different regional styles. Then hear Grainger's or Holst's folk-dance arrangements for the orchestral take on the same impulse. Keep the volume up: the density is the point."],"jimmie-rodgers-blue-yodel-no-1":["Under three minutes of one man and one guitar. Rodgers sings four loose twelve-bar blues stanzas about a woman who has left him and the pistol he intends to buy, strumming a plain bass-and-brush accompaniment with short runs between the lines. At the end of each verse he snaps up into falsetto for a three-note yodel that answers the sung phrase like a second singer. Nothing else is on the record: no band, no harmony, no reverb to hide in.","Rodgers had worked as a brakeman on Mississippi railroads until tuberculosis ended that life. He recorded for Victor during the company's rush into Southern vernacular music, made practical by the electrical microphone, which could finally catch a soft voice and a guitar without a recording horn. Victor was selling discs to rural households that had just bought their first phonograph. The record sold in the hundreds of thousands and turned a sick former railroad man into the first full-time star of the music.","Listen closer: the yodel is not Alpine. Rodgers grew up around Black railroad and levee workers, and this falsetto break belongs to the same world as the field holler, a leap into head voice used as punctuation rather than display. Notice too that he does not keep strict twelve-bar lengths. He adds and drops beats as the words need, and the guitar simply follows him. Every later country singer inherited that licence.","Victor's ledgers called it a blue yodel because nobody yet had a word for what it was. The disc effectively created the commercial category first sold as hillbilly and later as country. Scholars still argue about how much Rodgers took from the Black blues singers he heard on the railroads, and whether that is best described as theft, as exchange, or as one Southern vernacular that segregated marketing afterwards cut in two.","Play it twice: once for the story, once listening only to where the yodel lands against the guitar's beat. Then put on a Delta blues from the same decade and hear how nearly identical the twelve-bar frame is. Look for a transfer made from a clean original pressing; aggressive noise reduction thins the voice and files the edge off the yodel."],"jimmie-rodgers-blue-yodel-no-9":["The ninth in Rodgers's numbered blues series, and the one with company. Louis Armstrong plays trumpet behind him and Lil Hardin Armstrong plays piano, the three of them running through a slow twelve-bar about a man on a street corner, a woman, and a policeman who takes an interest. Armstrong does not solo so much as converse, filling every gap Rodgers leaves at the end of a line, and answering the yodels with bent, smeared phrases of his own.","The session took place in Los Angeles in 1930, arranged on short notice while all three were passing through. By then Rodgers was the best-selling country artist alive and Armstrong was remaking American singing and trumpet playing at once; the record companies filed them under different catalogues and sold them to different shops. The Depression was already closing in on the record business, and within three years Rodgers would be dead of tuberculosis at thirty-five.","Listen closer: Rodgers is the amateur here and it shows in the timing. He comes in early on at least one verse, and Armstrong simply moves, stretching a note to cover the gap. The trumpet is mixed close enough that you can hear breath in it. Armstrong went uncredited on the label, which is why the record spent decades being described as a curiosity rather than what it is, a meeting between the two most influential singers of the era.","For years the disc was filed as a novelty, an odd afternoon. It reads differently now, as evidence that the walls between hillbilly and race records were a marketing decision rather than a musical one. Some writers hear the session as unequal, Armstrong carrying a limited singer; others hear two men from the same Southern soundworld recognising each other. Both readings survive because the record supports both.","Follow the trumpet first and ignore the words entirely: Armstrong is writing a second song in the spaces. Then play it again for the voice and notice how Rodgers's yodel and Armstrong's bent notes do the same expressive job. Pair it with a Hot Five side from a few years earlier to hear how little Armstrong had to change to fit."],"carter-family-single-girl-married-girl":["Sara Carter sings alone, unhurried, over Maybelle Carter's guitar, alternating two verses that set the same woman's two possible lives against each other: the single girl going where she pleases in a fine new dress, the married girl rocking a cradle and crying. The tune barely moves, four phrases that fall and settle, and the accompaniment does nothing to comment. The flatness is the point. Sara delivers the bleakest lines without a flicker of emphasis.","Recorded at Ralph Peer's field sessions in Bristol, on the Tennessee-Virginia line, in the summer of 1927, the same fortnight that produced Jimmie Rodgers's first sides. Peer had brought portable electrical equipment south to find material he could copyright and sell. The Carters were a farming family from Poor Valley, Virginia, who had driven over a mountain road to get there. By some accounts A. P. Carter was out of the room when this one was cut, which is why only Sara sings.","Listen closer: Maybelle is barely playing. There is no picking pattern to admire, just chords and a few bass notes, and the restraint throws every word forward. Note also that the song offers no resolution and takes no position; it simply lays the two lives side by side and stops. Commercial country would spend the next fifty years learning to be that unsentimental about marriage, and mostly failing.","It was the Carters' first real seller and it financed everything that followed. Later folklorists treated their repertoire as a rescue operation on a dying oral tradition; the Carters themselves were making records for money, and A. P. copyrighted material he had collected from neighbours, including Black musicians such as Lesley Riddle who travelled with him. That double character, preservation and appropriation at once, is still the central argument about them.","Listen for what is absent: no chorus, no harmony, no moral. Then hear the same voice in the fuller family trio recordings and notice how much Sara's plainness survives the harmony. A good transfer matters here more than usual, because the whole effect lives in the grain of an unadorned voice close to a microphone."],"carter-family-wildwood-flower":["The most copied guitar part in American music. Maybelle Carter plays the melody with her thumb on the low strings while brushing chords across the high ones with her fingers, so a single guitar sounds like two instruments, lead and rhythm. Sara sings a garland of flower names and a woman abandoned by a faithless man. Between verses the guitar takes the tune alone, which is why generations of beginners learned this before anything else.","Cut in the year after Bristol, when Victor was recording the Carters steadily and their discs were reaching farms across the South. The song was not theirs: it descends from an 1860 American parlour ballad, printed with piano accompaniment for middle-class drawing rooms, which had drifted into oral circulation and come back up to them unrecognisable. The Carters recorded it as their own, in the ordinary practice of the day, and their arrangement replaced the original everywhere.","Listen closer: the words make almost no sense, and that is the evidence. The parlour original's botanical vocabulary, pale amaranthus and aronatus and the rest, was passed mouth to mouth through people who had never seen the printed page, and each singer reshaped what they misheard into something that sounded plausible. Sara sings the garbled version without hesitation. The song's own history is audible inside its lyric.","Guitarists argue about whether Maybelle invented the thumb-lead style or adapted what she had heard, and Lesley Riddle, the Black guitarist who travelled with A. P. collecting songs, is usually part of that conversation now. Nobody disputes the consequence. The Carter scratch became the default way to accompany a country song, and the tune became a standard test piece, played at every level from a first lesson to the concert stage.","Ignore the singing for one pass and follow only the thumb, then follow only the brushed chords, and hear two players emerge from one pair of hands. Compare a bluegrass flatpicked version afterwards and notice what speed costs the tune. Any decent reissue of the Victor sides will do; the guitar sits forward in the original balance."],"carter-family-can-the-circle-be-unbroken":["A hymn chorus wrapped around a funeral. The verses describe a hearse arriving, a mother carried out, a son walking behind and asking the undertaker to drive slow; the chorus lifts into three-part harmony and asks whether the family circle will be unbroken in a better home above. Maybelle's guitar keeps its steady thumb-and-brush pattern throughout, unmoved by any of it, and the harmony enters exactly on cue each time, like something rehearsed at a graveside.","A. P. Carter took a 1907 gospel hymn, kept its chorus and its question, and wrote new verses that put a specific corpse in a specific hearse. By 1935 the Carters were recording prolifically and would soon be broadcasting from a border radio station in Texas whose transmitter reached most of North America at night. The Depression had emptied the record shops but not the airwaves, and this song travelled further on radio than on shellac.","Listen closer: the verses are in the past tense and the chorus is in the future, and the song never lets the two meet. The narrator has already watched his mother go; the reunion stays hypothetical, phrased as a question in every single repetition. Country gospel usually answers its own questions. This one declines to, which is why it works equally well as consolation and as an expression of doubt.","It became the American folk revival's communal closing number, sung at festivals and funerals by people with no interest in the Carters, and its title ended up naming the 1972 album that introduced the old-time repertoire to a rock audience. Purists sometimes object that the mass-singing version smooths away the harshness of the verses. The complaint is fair and has changed nothing.","Listen to the verses alone first, with the chorus treated as an interruption, and the song turns bleak. Then listen for the moment the three voices lock; the shift from one voice to three is the whole emotional mechanism. Follow it with a Sacred Harp recording to hear the harmony tradition these singers were raised inside."],"hank-williams-lovesick-blues":["Williams enters alone on the word I, holds it, and breaks it in half with a yodel before the band has played a note. What follows is a fast, grinning complaint about a woman who has gone, driven by a shuffling bass and a steel guitar that answers each line. The vocal keeps cracking upward on purpose, sometimes twice in a phrase, so the song sounds simultaneously wretched and delighted with itself. It is over in well under three minutes.","Williams did not write it. It began as a 1922 Tin Pan Alley show song and had already passed through vaudeville and the recordings of the blackface yodeller Emmett Miller and the singer Rex Griffin, from whom Williams took his arrangement. His publisher Fred Rose thought the song was a mistake and old hat. It became the biggest hit of Williams's life and won him his Grand Ole Opry debut, where the audience reportedly refused to let him leave the stage.","Listen closer: the yodel break is a controlled crack between chest and head voice, and Williams places it on the stressed syllable of the most self-pitying words, turning misery into a joke at his own expense. Note also how little the band does. There is no solo of any length; everything is deference to the voice. The arrangement is essentially Griffin's, which makes Williams's originality a matter of delivery alone.","Rose's judgement was wrong and country's commercial history turned on it. Critics since have worried at the fact that the record that made the music's defining writer was a borrowed vaudeville number, filtered through the minstrel stage; that lineage is now discussed openly rather than tidied away. Nobody argues about the performance. It is still the standard demonstration of what a voice can do to a song it did not write.","Count the yodel breaks on a first hearing, then stop counting and just feel where they fall against the beat, always slightly ahead. Compare Rex Griffin's earlier version to see exactly what Williams added. Any well-mastered reissue of the MGM singles works; avoid the later overdubbed editions that bury the band under strings."],"hank-williams-i-saw-the-light":["Two minutes of pure uplift, taken at a brisk walking pace. Four short verses trace a sinner wandering in the night until the light finds him, each ending in a chorus so plainly built that a room full of strangers can join it on first hearing. The band plays it like a dance tune, with fiddle and steel trading brief fills, and the singer sounds untroubled throughout. No minor chord appears anywhere in the song.","Williams wrote it early, before the hits, and it became the number he closed shows with for the rest of his short life. Gospel was not a side venture in this music but its other half: the same bands played honky-tonks on Saturday and revival songs on Sunday radio, and audiences expected both from the same man. Williams took the melody from a Carter Family gospel side, which was ordinary practice, and put his own words on it.","Listen closer: the lyric is entirely in the past tense. Every claim of salvation is a report of something that happened earlier, and the song never says the light is still there. Williams, who spent his adult life in alcoholism and chronic pain and died at twenty-nine, reportedly told his mother more than once that there was no light for him. Knowing that changes the brightness of the record without altering a note of it.","It is sung in churches by people who would not otherwise own a Hank Williams record, and it has become the standard closing hymn at country funerals, including those of musicians whose lives it did not describe. Critics divide over whether to hear it straight or ironically. The most persuasive readings refuse to choose, treating the gap between the singer's life and the song's certainty as the thing the record is actually about.","Hear it immediately after one of his desolate songs; the pairing is the point, and Williams himself put the two sides of his work on consecutive nights of the same week. Listen for the fiddle, which never once darkens. Bluegrass and gospel quartet versions are everywhere, but start with the original, where the tempo is faster than most people remember."],"hank-williams-im-so-lonesome-i-could-cry":["Three verses, no chorus, almost no motion. Williams sings a series of images from a country night, a whippoorwill too blue to fly, a midnight train whining, a falling star, a silent moon behind clouds, and after each one states the plain fact of his loneliness. Don Helms's steel guitar sits very high above the voice and answers every line with two or three notes. The band plays as quietly as a band can while still being audible.","Recorded in Nashville in 1949, at the moment Williams turned from a regional draw into a national one. It was issued as the other side of a cheerful novelty number, which tells you what his label expected to sell. Country songs of the period were usually built on situations, a cheating wife, a lost job; this one has no situation at all, only weather and an interior state, which was not what the jukebox trade was ordering.","Listen closer: Williams never says why he is lonesome. There is no woman in the lyric, no departure, no cause of any kind. The images are borrowed from folk commonplaces and arranged so that each natural fact is also a person, the whippoorwill too blue to fly, the leaves that die. Helms tuned his steel unusually high for these sessions, and that thin, tight upper register is the sound most people mean when they say the word lonesome.","It is the Williams record that non-country listeners tend to know, and the one poets quote. It has been recorded by jazz singers, soul singers and rock bands without the slightest resistance from the material, which suggests how little of it is generic. Arguments today are mostly about the later MGM reissues that added orchestral overdubs to Williams's voice after his death, a practice now generally regarded as vandalism.","Take one image at a time and notice that the music does nothing to illustrate any of them. Then listen only to the steel guitar, which is effectively a second singer and often the more expressive of the two. Seek the undubbed original mix. Afterwards, try a song cycle about a solitary winter walk and hear how close the two traditions come."],"hank-williams-your-cheatin-heart":["A slow shuffle in which the singer promises an unfaithful lover a future of sleepless nights and useless tears. The melody climbs on the accusation and falls on the verdict, and Williams bends the last note of nearly every line downward, a small vocal catch that became one of the most imitated mannerisms in American singing. Steel guitar and fiddle alternate short answering phrases. The whole thing is structurally simple enough to teach in five minutes.","Recorded in Nashville in the autumn of 1952 at what proved to be one of his final sessions. By then Williams had been dismissed from the Grand Ole Opry for unreliability, was in constant pain from a spinal condition, and was drinking and taking morphine. He died in the back of a car on New Year's Day 1953, aged twenty-nine, and the single was released weeks afterwards into a country music that had just lost its centre.","Listen closer: the song is addressed entirely to someone who is not present, and the singer takes no action in it. He does not confront, leave or forgive; he simply predicts. Every verb about the other person is in the future tense, which turns the record from a complaint into something closer to a curse. Williams reportedly dictated the words to his second wife on a car journey, talking about his first.","Its posthumous release fixed it as the last word of a man who had just died, which no amount of later analysis can quite undo. It gave its title to a film, entered the standard repertoire of singers with no country connection at all, and is routinely named the single most important country song. The main scholarly argument now concerns the overdubbed reissues, and how much of Williams's recorded legacy reaches listeners in a form he would recognise.","Listen for the downward catch at the end of the lines; once you hear it you will hear it in every country singer since. Then compare a smooth pop version from the 1950s and notice how much the polish removes. The mono single mix, with the band dry and close, is the one to find."],"patsy-cline-i-fall-to-pieces":["A mid-tempo song about meeting an ex-lover in public and pretending to be fine. Cline sings almost conversationally in the verses and then opens her chest voice on the title phrase, so the collapse the words describe happens audibly in the tone. Behind her a male vocal quartet hums sustained chords where a steel guitar would once have been, a soft electric guitar plays a rocking two-note figure, and brushed drums keep everything horizontal.","Owen Bradley produced it in his Nashville studio as part of a deliberate attempt to keep country selling after rock and roll took its teenage audience. Fiddles and steel were dropped, backing choirs and light strings brought in, and the resulting style was marketed as the Nashville Sound. Cline reportedly disliked the song at first. It was climbing the charts in the summer of 1961 while she was in hospital after a severe car crash, and it made her a national name.","Listen closer: there is no drama in the arrangement at all, no crescendo, no key change, nothing to signal an emotional event. Everything is carried by the difference between how Cline sings the verse lines and how she sings the four words of the title. Notice also the vocal quartet: they never sing words, only vowels, which is what lets them function as an orchestra rather than as a gospel group.","Traditionalists heard the Nashville Sound as a surrender, country music trading its fiddles for a string section to chase pop money, and that argument has never entirely stopped. The counter-case is this record: the smoother the setting, the more exposed the voice. Cline's reputation grew steadily after her death in a plane crash in 1963, and she is now credited with proving that a woman could carry a country career without a male star attached.","Listen once for the two vocal registers and where exactly she crosses between them. Then play a honky-tonk record from ten years earlier and hear what was traded away and what was gained. Her studio recordings are best in mono; the later stereo remixes push the backing voices forward and make the arrangement sound more crowded than Bradley intended."],"patsy-cline-crazy":["A torch ballad taken slowly, with a melody that keeps climbing where you expect it to fall. Floyd Cramer's piano opens with a few unhurried chords; the vocal quartet breathes underneath; Cline enters low and stays conversational until the bridge, where she pushes into a full, wide tone and then pulls straight back. There is no instrumental solo. The record's whole architecture is one voice moving between restraint and release across two and a half minutes.","The song was written by Willie Nelson, then an unknown Nashville songwriter scraping by on demo fees, in a style closer to a jazz standard than to country. Cline initially disliked its wandering phrasing. She recorded it in Bradley's studio in the late summer of 1961, weeks after a car crash that had left her with broken ribs, which restricted how long she could hold a note; the vocal was completed once she had healed enough to sustain the lines.","Listen closer: the melody almost never lands where a country tune would. Nelson wrote long phrases that cross the bar lines and resolve late, which is why the song sits so comfortably in the repertoire of jazz singers. Notice too that Cline sings the title word differently every time it appears, four readings of one syllable. The Jordanaires' hum in the second verse quietly outlines a chord the piano has left out.","It became, by most reckonings, the most played song on American jukeboxes, and it is the record usually offered as the definitive answer to anyone who calls the Nashville Sound a dilution. Cline died eighteen months later at thirty. Debate now runs less about the arrangement than about the industry it typified, and about how many Nashville women of the 1960s were given the same production budget and the same freedom.","Start with the bridge, the one passage where she opens fully, then go back to the top and hear how carefully she has been holding back. Follow it with Willie Nelson's own version, which is almost spoken, to hear two completely different songs inside one set of words. The mono single is the intended balance."],"johnny-cash-i-walk-the-line":["Three instruments and a voice, locked into a dry chugging pulse that sounds like a freight train at moderate speed. Luther Perkins picks a bass-string figure with no vibrato and almost no decay; Marshall Grant slaps an upright bass; Cash strums and sings a vow of fidelity in a register so low it seems to be resisting the melody. Between each verse he hums a single pitch, unaccompanied, for a couple of seconds, and the whole song shifts into a new key.","Recorded at Sun Studio in Memphis for Sam Phillips, in the same building and the same year that Elvis Presley left for a major label. Cash was a former air force radio operator selling appliances door to door; he had written the song for his first wife while stationed overseas. Phillips wanted it faster than Cash intended, and the released version is the compromise. Sun sold it to country and pop markets at once, which almost nothing did in 1956.","Listen closer: the buzzing edge on the rhythm guitar is a piece of waxed paper woven through the strings, a trick Cash used to get a snare-like click from a band with no drummer. The humming between verses is not a stylistic flourish but a practical necessity; the song modulates five times, and he needed the pitch. That makes a record celebrated for its plainness one of the more harmonically restless singles of its decade.","It was Sun's biggest country seller and it fixed the boom-chicka-boom rhythm as Cash's signature for forty years. Critics have argued ever since about whether the song is sincere or anxious: a vow repeated that insistently, by a man who would soon wreck the marriage it was written for, can be heard either way. Cash encouraged both readings and never settled the question in interviews.","Try to hear the key changes on a first pass; most listeners never notice them, which is the trick. Then listen to the guitar alone for its complete lack of sustain, the sound of a cheap amplifier used deliberately. Play it against a train-rhythm piece from another tradition and hear how universal the pulse is. The Sun mono master is the one."],"johnny-cash-ring-of-fire":["The song opens not with a guitar but with two trumpets playing a bright mariachi fanfare in thirds, and they return between every verse. Underneath, the familiar chugging rhythm continues, so the record is Cash's Memphis pulse with a Mexican border band bolted on top. He sings about desire as a descending ring of fire, low and steady, and the trumpets answer each time with something almost festive, entirely at odds with the words.","June Carter, of the Carter Family and later Cash's wife, wrote it with Merle Kilgore about her feelings for him while both were married to other people. Anita Carter recorded it first, quietly. Cash said the trumpet idea came to him in a dream, and he cut his version for Columbia in Nashville in 1963, when he was deep in amphetamine use and his career was unsteady. It restored him commercially at once.","Listen closer: nothing in the arrangement is sad, and the lyric is about a destructive compulsion. That mismatch is the whole design. Notice also that the trumpets never play behind the voice, only between the lines, so the record alternates between two moods rather than blending them. The falling shape of the trumpet figure mirrors the falling shape of the phrase about going down.","Mariachi horns on a country single were startling in 1963, and the record widened what a Nashville arrangement was allowed to contain. Authorship has been the recurring argument: Cash's first wife claimed late in life that he had written it himself and credited June Carter, a claim the documented publishing does not support and most historians reject. The song's afterlife in adverts and films has worn its strangeness down.","Listen to the trumpets in isolation and notice they are playing a party. Then read the words as if the trumpets were not there. The gap between the two is the record. Follow it with a Copland score that uses brass the same way, as a bright frontier colour, and hear the shared American mythology."],"johnny-cash-at-folsom-prison":["A live album recorded in front of prisoners, opening with Cash announcing his own name and going straight into a song about shooting a man to watch him die. The set runs through murder ballads, jokes, a duet with June Carter and a long stretch of songs about confinement, with the band playing at one dynamic throughout and the audience audible all the way. It closes with a song written by an inmate who was sitting in the room while Cash sang it.","Cash had wanted to record inside a prison for a decade and Columbia had refused; a new producer, Bob Johnston, agreed. Two shows were played at the California state prison at Folsom in January 1968, and the album assembles the stronger one. Cash had just come through the worst of his amphetamine years. He had never served a prison sentence himself, only nights in county cells, a distinction the record quietly declines to make clear.","Listen closer: the whoop that follows the line about shooting a man in Reno is not what it appears. Prison officials had restricted how the men could react, and the crowd noise on the album was shaped in the mix, with applause moved and added. Listen also for the prison announcements cutting across the music; those were left in deliberately, to keep reminding you where you are standing.","It sold enormously, revived a stalled career and made Cash a figure for the counterculture as well as for country radio. It also set the terms of a long argument about authenticity, since the outlaw persona it cemented was largely constructed. Later scholarship has drawn attention to Glen Sherley, the inmate whose song closes the record, who was released partly through Cash's advocacy and whose life afterwards went badly.","Listen to the first four minutes without skipping: the announcement, the silence, the first chord and the first reaction together do more than any single song. Then attend to the crowd rather than the band. Seek the original single-disc album rather than the expanded edition, which restores both shows and dilutes the pacing that made the record work."],"johnny-cash-american-recordings":["One voice and one acoustic guitar for forty minutes, with nothing added. Cash, then in his sixties, sings thirteen songs: his own murder ballad about Delia, a nineteenth-century hymn, and pieces borrowed from a Canadian poet-songwriter, a Los Angeles barroom writer and a heavy metal frontman. The guitar playing is basic and occasionally uneven. The recording is so close that you hear the chair, the breath and the dry click of a plectrum against wound strings.","Cash had been dropped by his label and was playing dinner theatres when Rick Rubin, a producer known for hip-hop and metal, offered him a contract and a single condition: no band, no Nashville, just songs. Most of the album was cut in Rubin's living room and in Cash's Tennessee cabin, with two tracks taken from a small Los Angeles club. The idea was to record him the way a folklorist would record an old singer in a kitchen.","Listen closer: the song called Thirteen was written for him, at his request, by a metal singer whose band Cash had never heard, and it is built entirely from the imagery Cash had been singing since the 1950s. Note also what the silence does. With no drums to mark time, Cash's phrases stretch and shorten as he pleases, and several tracks are audibly slower at the end than the beginning.","The record won a Grammy in a folk category, sold to an audience that had never bought country, and began a sequence of albums that ran to his death in 2003. Some longtime admirers found the framing calculated, an old man styled as a prophet by a young producer with an ear for gravity. Others point out that the repertoire choices were Cash's and that the voice, worn and unhidden, does the persuading.","Play it at low volume in a quiet room; the recording was made for that scale and collapses through a car stereo. Start with the opening track and its slow-building dread. Then go back to a 1950s Sun single and hear the same man with forty years and no band between him and you. The debt to a solitary winter song cycle is not accidental."],"loretta-lynn-coal-miners-daughter":["A banjo figure opens it and keeps rolling underneath for three minutes while Lynn recites, in strict chronological order, the facts of her childhood in a Kentucky hollow: the shack, the father on the night shift, the mother washing on a scrubboard and reading the Bible by coal-oil light, eight children, no money, no self-pity. The melody is almost a chant, four repeated phrases. Nothing in the arrangement dramatises anything; the details do the work alone.","Lynn had married at fifteen and had four children before she was twenty, and she wrote her own material at a time when very few women in Nashville did. Owen Bradley produced it. The song appeared as country radio was reaching an audience of migrants from exactly these places, people who had left the coalfields for factory towns in Ohio and Michigan, and who heard their own family history read back to them in the third person plural.","Listen closer: there is no chorus. The song never returns to a hook, which is why it sounds like testimony rather than a single, and Lynn reportedly wrote many more verses than were recorded, with the rest cut purely for length. Notice that the poverty is described without a single abstract word. Everything is an object: the scrubboard, the coal oil, the hand-me-down shoes. The one general statement comes in the last line.","It gave its name to her memoir and to the 1980 film made from it, and it turned a singer into a representative figure for Appalachian working people, a role she accepted and occasionally resented. Critics debate whether its plainness is artless or extremely controlled; the manuscript evidence of heavy cutting supports the second reading. It is now routinely cited as the model for autobiographical songwriting in country music.","Count the concrete nouns on one hearing. Then listen for the banjo, which is doing the job a string section would do in a Nashville Sound production, and note how different the social claim sounds as a result. Pair it with an orchestral evocation of a Southern childhood and hear two classes remembering the same region."],"loretta-lynn-the-pill":["A fast, cheerful honky-tonk shuffle in which a farm wife informs her husband that the years of pregnancy are over and she is going out. The imagery is agricultural and unsparing: she has been the hen in the incubator while he was the rooster running loose, and now the maternity dresses are going in the bin. Lynn sings it grinning, at the top of her range, and the band plays it like a dance number. It takes under three minutes.","The oral contraceptive had been available in the United States for well over a decade, but access for married rural women had come slowly and the subject was unmentionable on country radio. Lynn recorded the song some years before her label would release it, and when it appeared in 1975 many stations refused to play it. It reached a large audience anyway, and rural doctors reportedly said it did more practical good than their leaflets.","Listen closer: the song never once uses a clinical word or makes a political claim. It is entirely domestic, addressed to one husband in one kitchen, and its argument is about fairness within a marriage rather than rights in the abstract. That is why it got past listeners who would have rejected a feminist statement, and it is also why the humour is so pointed. Note the bright major key throughout; nothing in the music admits to controversy.","The bans made it news and the news made it sell, a pattern Lynn had already seen with earlier songs about drinking husbands and other women. Critics then and now split over whether it is a feminist landmark or a comedy record that happened to touch a nerve; Lynn herself framed it as simple description of what women she knew were doing. It remains among the most banned singles in the format's history.","Listen for how much of the meaning sits in the delivery rather than the words, particularly the laugh she keeps almost suppressing. Then hear one of her earlier songs about a rival woman to see how consistently she wrote from inside a marriage rather than about one. The original single mix keeps the band bright and the voice forward."],"george-jones-the-grand-tour":["A man invites you into his house and shows you around. Room by room he points out the furniture his wife chose, the bed they shared, and finally a nursery with a child's things still in it; she has gone and taken the child. Jones sings in the polite, level voice of a guide, and only breaks it on the last word of each verse. Strings, steel guitar and a soft rhythm section keep the tour moving without ever raising their voices.","Billy Sherrill produced it for Epic in Nashville, working in the plush, string-heavy style the trade called countrypolitan, in which a session orchestra did the work that fiddles had once done. Jones's own marriage to Tammy Wynette was disintegrating in public at the time, and audiences heard the song against that; the writers had drafted it independently. Jones was drinking heavily and had begun missing sessions and concerts, a pattern that would define the next decade.","Listen closer: the conceit is a real estate viewing, and it never slips. Every emotional detail is presented as a feature of the property, which is why the record can describe a ruined family without a single direct statement of feeling. Notice where the strings enter, always on the move between rooms, never under the revelation itself. The nursery arrives in the final verse with no musical preparation at all.","It is the connoisseur's George Jones record rather than the famous one, and singers cite it as the hardest to bring off, because the restraint has to hold for three minutes before it pays. Countrypolitan production has been out of fashion and back again; Sherrill's strings, once dismissed as syrup, are now read as the deliberate cushion that lets a voice like this one whisper instead of cry.","Follow the floor plan. Hear it as a walk through a house and notice how the camera, so to speak, keeps moving. Then listen only to the last word of each verse, where the guide's composure fails for a syllable. Follow it with a Romantic song cycle about a man revisiting the places a lover has left; the method is the same."],"george-jones-he-stopped-loving-her-today":["A friend describes a man who kept a woman's letters and photograph for decades after she left, swearing he would love her until he died. The narrator gives the joke a turn in the title phrase, and then the bridge is spoken rather than sung: the man is dead, and she has come to see him one last time. A string section swells at the final chorus and Jones takes the song up a key, his voice thinning at the top.","Billy Sherrill spent a long time getting this record finished. Jones's addiction to alcohol and cocaine had reached the point where sessions collapsed and concerts were missed so often that promoters advertised him as No Show Jones; the vocal was assembled over many visits across more than a year. Jones reportedly disliked the melody and told Sherrill nobody would buy a song that morbid. It restored his career and is the record he sang every night afterwards.","Listen closer: the title works as a double meaning that the song withholds until the bridge, and the spoken passage exists because a sung line would have given it away too early. Notice the key change at the end, unusual in a ballad this restrained; it arrives exactly where the funeral does. The strings play a falling figure between verses that never resolves until the last bar.","It is routinely voted the greatest country song ever recorded, which has made it hard to hear freshly. Its defenders point to the construction, a punchline withheld for two and a half minutes; its sceptics call it a gimmick dressed in a string arrangement. The story of how it was extracted from a man in freefall has become part of the record's meaning, and some writers argue that biography is doing work the song cannot do alone.","Listen once knowing nothing and let the bridge land. Then listen again for the strings, and for how carefully Sherrill keeps them off the vocal until the last chorus. Afterwards try a song cycle that ends at a graveside; country and lieder solve the same problem, how to make a small voice carry a death, in almost the same way."],"tammy-wynette-stand-by-your-man":["Two short verses and a chorus that arrives twice, the second time a whole step higher. Wynette begins almost gently, advising that it is hard to be a woman giving all your love to one man, then pushes into a wide, cracking upper register on the title line, with a catch on the first word that sounds like a sob and is entirely deliberate. Strings and steel guitar rise underneath. The record is under three minutes and spends most of them climbing.","Wynette wrote it with her producer Billy Sherrill in his Nashville office, reportedly very quickly, near the end of a session that needed one more song. She had worked as a hairdresser in Alabama and had three children before her recording career began. The single appeared in 1968, into an America already arguing loudly about what women owed their marriages, and it became the best-selling record ever made by a woman in country music.","Listen closer: the verse is a warning and only the chorus is advice. Wynette spends the first half of the song saying that this arrangement is hard, that he will do things you cannot understand, and only then delivers the title. The modulation into the final chorus is what most people remember as emotion; it is in fact a mechanical device, and it works because her voice has to strain to reach the new key.","It was attacked by feminists on release and defended by Wynette as a song about love rather than submission. Hillary Clinton's dismissive reference to it in a 1992 television interview restarted the argument on a national scale and prompted an apology. Recent criticism has tended to read the record against Wynette's own life of five marriages and financial exploitation, hearing the crack in the voice as evidence against the words.","Listen to the verse and the chorus as two different songs, because they say different things. Then track the modulation and notice how audibly she is working at the top. Pair it with an opera in which a woman's self-sacrifice is presented as the noble choice, and the question the record raises turns out to be a very old one."],"tammy-wynette-d-i-v-o-r-c-e":["A mother and father are spelling out words so their four-year-old will not understand them. The song's hook is the spelling itself, letter by letter, and Wynette sings those letters in the same warm, careful tone she uses for everything else, which is what makes it unbearable rather than cute. The band plays a moderate shuffle with steel guitar fills. At the end the child's own future is spelled out as well, and the song stops without comment.","Written by the same pair who would later write He Stopped Loving Her Today, and produced by Billy Sherrill in the year of Stand by Your Man, so Wynette had two of the decade's defining country singles inside twelve months. She was building the public role that earned her the title First Lady of Country Music, singing about marriages under strain while her own were collapsing, and country radio in 1968 was still uneasy about the word divorce in a title.","Listen closer: the spelled words are not all sad ones. The device is introduced with ordinary domestic secrets before it is used for the real thing, so the listener learns the code in the first verse and is then made to decipher the second. Notice that the child never speaks and is never described, only referred to. The song's whole emotional weight rests on a person who is not in it.","Novelty spelling songs were a familiar country device and the writers used the convention against itself, which is why the record has outlasted the genre it came from. It has been parodied often enough that some listeners meet the joke before the song. Critics who dislike it hear a manipulative trick; those who defend it point out that the trick is doing something no direct statement could, which is to make the listener perform the concealment.","Notice that you decode the letters faster than the child would, and that the discomfort is exactly that gap. Then listen for how little Wynette changes her tone across the song. Pair it with a ballad in which a child is the unwitting centre of a catastrophe narrated by adults; the technique of withholding is identical."],"dolly-parton-coat-of-many-colors":["Parton tells a story from her childhood: her mother sewed her a coat out of a box of rags, stitching it while telling the Bible story of Joseph, and the children at school laughed at her for wearing rags. The arrangement is light, an acoustic guitar and a discreet steel, and Parton sings high and clear with almost no ornament. There is no chorus; the song moves straight through the incident from the sewing to the mockery to the last line.","Parton was recording for RCA under the wing of Porter Wagoner, whose television show had taken her from a Tennessee mountain family to a national audience. She reportedly wrote this on the back of a dry-cleaning receipt from one of Wagoner's flamboyant stage suits, a detail she has told often and which the receipt itself, now in a museum, supports. Country radio in 1971 rewarded songs about rural poverty, and she had lived the material.","Listen closer: the song makes a theological argument and never announces it. The mother's Bible story gives the coat its meaning, the schoolchildren refuse that meaning, and the child accepts her mother's reading over theirs, which is the only conflict in the piece. Notice also that Parton does not describe the coat's colours; she describes the box of rags and the sewing. The object is never actually shown to us.","Parton has repeatedly named it her favourite of her own songs, and it has become the anchor of a public persona built on the distance between where she started and where she arrived. Critics sometimes note how effectively that persona works as a commercial instrument, and how carefully the poverty is presented without bitterness. The song's afterlife in books and television films has made it more sentimental than the recording is.","Listen for what the arrangement declines to do: no strings, no swell at the humiliation, no key change at the end. Then hear her sing it forty years later and notice how little the reading has changed. Pair it with an orchestral setting of a remembered Southern childhood to hear the same landscape from a different class."],"dolly-parton-jolene":["An acoustic guitar figure circles a minor chord four times before the voice enters, and it keeps circling, unchanged, for the whole song. Parton addresses a rival by name and begs her not to take a man she could take without trying, cataloguing the woman's beauty in detail that sounds close to infatuation. The melody is built from a descending three-note phrase repeated obsessively. Parton doubles her own voice in places, so the plea seems to come from two people at once.","Recorded for RCA in Nashville while she was still appearing on Porter Wagoner's television show and preparing to leave it. She has said the name came from a young fan at an autograph table and the situation from a red-haired bank teller who flirted with her husband. The early 1970s were the point at which Nashville's women stopped being novelty acts on male-led shows, and this single, along with what came next, funded her independence.","Listen closer: the song has no bridge, no key change and effectively no chord progression, just the same short loop under everything. All the variation is in the voice. Notice that the narrator never speaks to her husband and never blames him; the entire drama is conducted between two women, one of whom is silent. The guitar's circling figure is also the reason the song survives transplanting into rock, metal and electronic settings without losing its shape.","It has become one of the most covered songs written in the last fifty years, taken up by artists with no connection to country at all, and it survives being slowed to a dirge or sped into a punk song. Critics have written a great deal about the desire audible in the narrator's description of her rival. Parton has always declined the reading, which has not slowed it down.","Listen to the guitar loop alone and notice it never develops; then listen to how many different emotions Parton puts over the identical four bars. Try a radically slowed cover afterwards to hear what the tempo was hiding. The original RCA single mix, with the voice close and the band dry, is still the best."],"dolly-parton-i-will-always-love-you":["A farewell sung at walking pace, with a spoken passage in the middle where Parton simply talks to the person she is leaving and wishes them well. The melody rises through the verse and settles on the title phrase without any display; she never belts, and the highest notes are the quietest. An acoustic guitar carries it, with light strings entering late. Parton's vibrato, fast and narrow, does most of the expressive work.","She wrote it as a goodbye to Porter Wagoner, whose television show and business partnership had made her career and had become a cage; she has said she played it to him in his office and that he wept and then agreed to let her go. Elvis Presley wanted to record it, but his manager required half the publishing, and she refused. That refusal became one of the most consequential business decisions in American popular music.","Listen closer: the song is a resignation letter, not a love song, and the words work as precisely for a professional parting as for a romantic one, which is why it reads differently once you know who it was written to. Notice the spoken bridge. Parton drops out of singing entirely at the emotional centre, so the most direct statement in the song is not sung at all.","Parton took it to the top of the country chart twice, in 1974 and again in 1982. Whitney Houston's 1992 recording, sung as a soul ballad with an unaccompanied opening and an enormous final chorus, became one of the best-selling singles ever made and for most listeners replaced the original. Critics comparing the two usually frame it as restraint against release; Parton has praised the cover while noting that she wrote it quiet.","Hear Parton's version first if you only know Houston's; the shock is how small it is. Listen for the spoken section and for the fact that nothing swells behind it. Then compare the two recordings back to back as two answers to the same question, whether a farewell should be whispered or sung at full voice."],"merle-haggard-mama-tried":["Two and a half minutes, fast, with a bright electric guitar figure that snaps rather than sustains. Haggard sings about a boy who would not be told, a widowed mother who did everything right, and a life sentence he is now serving. Roy Nichols plays a clipped Telecaster lead with a hard treble edge, and the rhythm section pushes slightly ahead of the beat throughout. The famous line about turning twenty-one in prison arrives in the first verse and never lets go.","Haggard had in fact served time in San Quentin as a young man for an attempted burglary, and had been in the audience when Johnny Cash played there. His records were made in Los Angeles rather than Nashville, with a band of West Coast players whose hard, trebly electric sound had been shaped in the dance halls of Bakersfield, a town full of families who had come west from Oklahoma in the Depression. That sound existed partly as a refusal of Nashville's strings.","Listen closer: the song is not strictly true and does not pretend to be. Haggard was never sentenced to life, and the exaggeration turns memoir into ballad, which is the form's old privilege. Notice that the mother is the only person described with any warmth, and that the narrator accepts full blame in every verse. Listen for the banjo buried in the introduction, a nod to the Appalachian music his Oklahoma parents brought west.","It gave its name to an album and became the centre of Haggard's prison songs, a body of work whose credibility rested on his having actually been inside. Later writers have examined how carefully that biography was managed, and the pardon he received in the 1970s. The Bakersfield sound is now heard as the first significant argument against Nashville's centralised production, and as the ancestor of every subsequent country rebellion.","Listen to the guitar tone rather than the words on a first pass; the bright, undecorated Telecaster is the whole regional argument in one sound. Then compare a countrypolitan ballad of the same year. Follow it with an orchestral outlaw ballad from a generation earlier and hear how old the American appetite for a sympathetic criminal is."],"merle-haggard-okie-from-muskogee":["A brisk, almost jaunty shuffle in which a man from a small Oklahoma town lists what they do not do there: smoke marijuana, burn draft cards, wear beads or let their hair grow long. Fiddle and steel guitar trade cheerful fills, the band swings, and Haggard sings it deadpan, with no audible irony and no audible anger either. It runs under three minutes and the chorus is designed to be shouted back by a crowd.","Written on a tour bus with his drummer while passing a road sign for Muskogee, in the autumn of 1969, with American cities and campuses in open conflict over the Vietnam War. Haggard's audience included working people whose sons were being drafted while students protested. The record sold enormously, was played at political rallies, and turned a Bakersfield bandleader into a national symbol in a fight he had not obviously enlisted in.","Listen closer: the song is written in the first person plural, and Haggard was not from Muskogee and did not live that life. He described it at different times as a joke on his own band, as a character study of his father's generation, and as something he meant. The deadpan delivery is what makes all three readings possible, and the band's cheerfulness refuses to settle the question either way.","It was received as a conservative anthem and has never quite escaped that. Haggard spent decades complicating it, later writing anti-war material and speaking with sympathy about the people the song mocked, which convinced some listeners and not others. Critics now tend to read it as the moment country music's political identity hardened in public, whatever its author intended, and as a case study in how a record's meaning stops belonging to the person who made it.","Listen for the absence of scorn in the voice, which is the strangest thing about the record and the reason the argument survives. Then hear his later songs about hard times for working people and decide how consistent a writer he was. Pair it with an American orchestral piece built on marching bands and hymn tunes to hear the same patriotism handled with more irony."],"willie-nelson-red-headed-stranger":["A narrative album about a preacher who kills his wife and her lover and rides west, told across short songs, fragments and instrumental passages that often last under two minutes. The sound is startlingly bare: Nelson's nylon-string guitar, his sister Bobbie's upright piano, a quiet bass, brushed drums, and long stretches of near-silence between phrases. Themes recur between tracks so the record plays as one continuous thing rather than a collection of songs.","Nelson had spent the 1960s in Nashville writing hits for other singers and failing as a performer, then moved back to Texas and grew his hair. A new contract with Columbia gave him complete creative control, which no Nashville artist of his standing had held. He recorded the album quickly and cheaply in a studio near Dallas. Executives who heard the tapes reportedly assumed they were demos and asked when the real sessions would begin.","Listen closer: almost nothing on the record is new. The title song is a cowboy number written for a children's radio show in the 1950s, and the album's biggest hit was a country ballad from the 1940s; Nelson wrote the connecting material and let the borrowed songs carry the plot. Notice the silences. He leaves gaps where a band would normally play, and those gaps are what make the thirty-three minutes feel like a landscape.","Columbia released it under protest and it became one of the best-selling country albums of the decade, which effectively ended the label system's argument that Nashville producers knew best. It is the record usually credited with proving that outlaw country was commercial rather than merely defiant. Critics still divide over whether the sparseness is an aesthetic choice or a budget, and Nelson has cheerfully agreed with both.","Play it whole and in order; individual tracks are fragments and make little sense alone. Listen for the guitar tone, a nylon-string classical instrument with a worn hole in its face, which sounds like nothing else in country music. Then hear a nineteenth-century song cycle about a man walking away from a ruined love and notice how similar the strategy is."],"willie-nelson-stardust":["Ten standards from the American songbook, sung by a country singer with his own road band and produced by a soul musician. Nelson phrases far behind the beat, arriving late on almost every line and catching up by the end of the phrase, which turns familiar melodies slightly unfamiliar. Mickey Raphael's harmonica takes the solos a saxophone would normally take. The arrangements are unhurried and small; nothing is orchestrated, and the whole record moves at one gentle tempo.","Booker T. Jones, the organist behind the Memphis soul records of the 1960s, was Nelson's neighbour in California and agreed to produce. Nelson chose songs he had known since childhood, including pieces by Gershwin and by Kurt Weill, and made the record at a point when his outlaw success had bought him the freedom to do something his label considered commercial suicide. Columbia expected it to fail. It stayed on the country chart for roughly a decade.","Listen closer: his phrasing is the whole record. Nelson sings almost nothing on the beat, and the band holds a steady pulse precisely so the delay is audible; it is the technique of a jazz singer applied to a country voice with no vibrato to hide behind. Notice too that several of these songs began in Broadway shows and film scores, so the album quietly reunites two branches of American song that the record industry had spent fifty years separating.","It was heard at the time as a gamble and is now read as the record that made the standards repertoire available again to singers outside pop, decades before that became a common late-career move. Some jazz listeners find the intonation approximate and the band plain. The usual defence is that a songbook this over-recorded needed a voice with no polish at all to sound like a person again.","Pick one song you know well in another version and follow only where Nelson places the words against the beat. Then listen to the harmonica solos as substitute saxophone. Pair it with a Gershwin recording to hear how far these melodies travel. The original album sequence, which opens slow and stays slow, is part of the effect."],"emmylou-harris-pieces-of-the-sky":["Harris's first album as a country singer, built around a clear, unforced soprano with a distinctive tremor at the top. The band is loose and warm, with pedal steel and electric guitar taking turns. The repertoire runs from a close-harmony duet in the Louvin Brothers manner to a Beatles song to a honky-tonk drinking number, and at its centre is her own song about a friend's death, which is the only piece here she wrote and the reason the record exists.","Harris had been a folk singer in Washington when Gram Parsons recruited her to sing harmony on his solo albums and taught her the country repertoire. Parsons died in 1973, and she assembled a group of Los Angeles session players, later known as the Hot Band, to make this. It arrived while country rock was already a commercial category, but Harris's interest ran backwards, towards the brother duets and honky-tonk records the Californian bands had only glanced at.","Listen closer: she sings the Louvin Brothers song as a duet with herself, overdubbing the harmony line, which is how a singer without a brother joins a brother-duet tradition. Her elegy for Parsons places its grief in one specific geographical image, a road between two cities, and never names him. The Hot Band's steel player and lead guitarist are audibly playing 1950s licks on 1970s equipment, which is the record's whole argument.","It established Harris as the most reliable interpreter in country music, a singer who could make any song sound like part of one tradition, and it began a run of albums that carried older repertoire to a rock audience. The recurring criticism is that she wrote very little, which she has answered by pointing at the results. Her curatorial instinct is now generally treated as a form of authorship.","Start with the self-harmonised duet to hear the technique, then the elegy, then work outwards. Listen for how the steel guitar is recorded, dry and close, unlike the reverb-washed country rock of the period. Follow it with a pastoral elegy from another tradition; the business of mourning a friend through borrowed material is very old."],"emmylou-harris-wrecking-ball":["Harris's voice, by then twenty years lower and rougher, set inside vast reverberant space. Daniel Lanois built the record out of tremolo guitars, deep drums recorded in a room rather than close, and washes of sustained sound, so that the songs seem to be arriving from the far end of a hall. The material is borrowed from rock and folk songwriters rather than country ones. There is almost no steel guitar and no fiddle anywhere on it.","Country radio had stopped playing Harris, and rather than chase the format she went to a producer known for atmospheric rock records. The album was made in New Orleans, and the songs were chosen from writers she admired, including a Canadian rock songwriter whose title track gives the record its name, a young Appalachian revivalist, and a Texas songwriter then at her peak. It was released into a Nashville market that had no idea what to do with it.","Listen closer: the reverb is not decoration but structure. Lanois records the drums far from the microphone so that the decay of each hit becomes part of the harmony, and Harris's voice sits inside that decay rather than in front of it, which is why the album sounds like weather. Notice that several songs end without a final chord, simply fading into the room tone that has been present all along.","It won a Grammy, was ignored by country radio, and is now generally credited with giving the Americana category its sound and much of its self-image. The complaint from traditionalists was that the production swamps the songs; the counter-argument is that Harris had spent two decades serving material faithfully and was entitled to a record that served her voice instead. It remains the model every atmospheric roots album is measured against.","Listen on headphones in the dark; the record is built on depth and collapses on small speakers. Start with the title track and attend to the drums rather than the voice. Then play her 1975 debut immediately afterwards to hear twenty years of wear as an instrument. An ambient piano piece from a century earlier makes a surprisingly good companion."],"bill-monroe-mule-skinner-blues":["A Jimmie Rodgers blue yodel taken at a gallop. Bill Monroe and His Blue Grass Boys drive the old tune far faster than Rodgers ever did, with a tight string band pushing hard on every beat and Monroe's voice sitting high and hard above it, leaping into falsetto yodels at the end of each verse. It lasts under three minutes, and the first thing you notice is speed: this is country music played with the urgency of a fiddle contest.","Monroe auditioned for the Grand Ole Opry in Nashville in October 1939 with this song, and he was hired on the spot; the Opry's George D. Hay reportedly told him that if he ever left, he would have to fire himself. He cut it for RCA's Bluebird label in 1940. Country music on the radio was then dominated by smooth western swing and cowboy singers; Monroe's Kentucky band sounded older and wilder at once.","Listen to how the band treats the song's blues shape. Rodgers sang it loose and conversational over one guitar; Monroe keeps the blues structure but hardens the rhythm into a relentless chop. The banjo, later the signature of bluegrass, is absent here: Monroe's band sound was still forming, and the recording shows the style half-built, with the high lonesome voice already in place and the instrumental engine still arriving.","It was an immediate Opry favourite and became Monroe's calling card for decades. Historians treat it as the moment the Blue Grass Boys' identity took shape, though most date full-fledged bluegrass to the 1945–46 band with Flatt and Scruggs. The song itself went on to a long life in other hands, from Dolly Parton's hit version in 1970 to rockabilly and punk covers.","Play Jimmie Rodgers's Blue Yodel No. 1 first to hear the relaxed model, then this. Notice how the tempo alone changes the meaning: the loneliness becomes restlessness. Then jump ahead to Monroe's 1946 recordings with Flatt and Scruggs to hear what the missing banjo would add."],"bill-monroe-blue-moon-of-kentucky":["A slow waltz of heartbreak, sung by Bill Monroe in his high, keening tenor over the classic Blue Grass Boys: Lester Flatt's guitar, Earl Scruggs's banjo, Chubby Wise's fiddle and a string bass. The fiddle sighs through the introduction, Monroe's voice soars over a steady three-beat sway, and the mandolin fills the gaps with bright tremolo. It is gentle and plaintive, a song about a moon that shines on a lover who has gone.","Monroe wrote it in the mid-1940s and recorded it for Columbia in 1946, with the band many consider the first true bluegrass line-up; it was issued in 1947. Postwar country audiences were moving to cities and factories, and Monroe's music carried the sound of rural Kentucky with it. He named his whole style after the Bluegrass State, and this song made the naming explicit.","The song's second life is the thing to know. In July 1954, at Sun Studio in Memphis, a young Elvis Presley and his trio sped it up into four-beat rockabilly for the B-side of his first single. Monroe, rather than objecting, re-recorded the song himself soon afterwards with a slow waltz opening that breaks into a fast section, answering Elvis in his own style.","It became the Kentucky state bluegrass song and one of the most recorded numbers in the genre. Critics point to it as the clearest example of how close early rock and roll stood to hillbilly music: the same song sits at the root of both. Arguments continue over whether Monroe's original or the Elvis version matters more historically; the fair answer is that each needs the other.","Hear Monroe's 1946 waltz, then Elvis's 1954 reshaping, then Monroe's later hybrid version, one after another. Listen for the fiddle in the original: Chubby Wise's long, sliding phrases are as much the voice of the song as Monroe's singing. Then notice what Elvis keeps, the melody, and what he throws away, the waltz."],"bill-monroe-uncle-pen":["A brisk, affectionate portrait of a fiddler. Monroe sings of his uncle and the old tunes he played, and the band answers each verse with fiddle breakdowns that are themselves the tribute. The rhythm is a fast two-beat, the fiddle leads rather than decorates, and the chorus lifts into Monroe's trademark high harmony. It is a song about music, and it proves its point by playing the kind of music it describes.","Pendleton Vandiver, Monroe's mother's brother, was a fiddler in Rosine, Kentucky, who took the orphaned teenage Monroe in and played him old-time tunes at country dances. Monroe recorded this memorial in 1950, by then signed to Decca and leading a band that trained generations of players. Uncle Pen had died years earlier; the song fixed him as the fountainhead of the whole bluegrass style.","Notice what the song claims about origins. Monroe consistently credited Uncle Pen's fiddle bowing and Black blues guitarist Arnold Shultz as his two great teachers. The fiddle part here imitates an old-time Kentucky style, with its rocking rhythm and drone strings, grafted onto the modern bluegrass band. You are hearing Monroe place himself on a family tree, deliberately.","It became a bluegrass standard and a staple of fiddle-contest repertoire. In 1984 Ricky Skaggs took it to number one on the country chart, confirming its reach decades later. Historians value it as Monroe's own account of where bluegrass came from, while noting that his story tends to underplay the African American threads he elsewhere acknowledged.","Listen to the fiddle breaks between verses as the real subject. Then compare with a field recording of an old-time Appalachian fiddler to hear the tradition Monroe is honouring. Ricky Skaggs's 1984 version shows how the song sat in a slicker Nashville setting."],"flatt-and-scruggs-foggy-mountain-breakdown":["An instrumental showcase built around Earl Scruggs's banjo. Three fingers pick in rolling patterns so fast that single notes blur into a cascade, while guitar, mandolin, fiddle and bass pass solos around in turn. The tune is simple, a few bars repeated and varied, but the sound is unmistakable: a bright, metallic, forward-leaning rush. Most people know it without knowing its name, as the soundtrack to a car chase.","Lester Flatt and Earl Scruggs left Bill Monroe's band in 1948 and formed the Foggy Mountain Boys. They recorded this for Mercury in late 1949. Scruggs had perfected a three-finger picking style in North Carolina that turned the banjo, a rhythm instrument in string bands, into a lead voice capable of long, flowing runs. Postwar audiences for country instrumentals were small; the record sold modestly at first.","Listen for the moment the chord sequence darkens partway through each pass, a turn to a minor-sounding chord that gives the tune its urgency. Also notice that nobody plays drums: the forward drive comes entirely from the mandolin's off-beat chop and the bass. In 1967 Arthur Penn used the old recording in Bonnie and Clyde, and a new 1968 recording won a Grammy, nearly twenty years after the original.","It became the most famous banjo tune in the world and is now a rite of passage for every bluegrass picker. The film revived Flatt and Scruggs's career and pulled bluegrass into the counterculture. Purists sometimes resent its ubiquity as a comic chase cue, but musicians still use it as the benchmark of Scruggs-style technique.","Put on the 1949 Mercury original and follow the banjo alone for one chorus, then switch attention to the mandolin's chop underneath. Then watch the chase scene in Bonnie and Clyde. For contrast, hear Doc Watson flatpicking a fiddle tune on guitar."],"stanley-brothers-man-of-constant-sorrow":["An old Appalachian lament recast by Carter and Ralph Stanley in their early bluegrass style. Ralph takes the lead in a piercing, grainy high voice, telling of a wanderer who has lost home and love and will die a stranger; the band answers with spare mandolin and fiddle fills. The song is slow and grave, closer to a ballad than a breakdown, and the sound is stark rather than showy.","The song was probably first published around 1913 by Dick Burnett, a blind Kentucky fiddler, and circulated in variants through the mountains. The Stanley Brothers, from Virginia's Clinch Mountains, recorded it for Columbia around 1950, when they were forging their own mountain-flavoured variant of Bill Monroe's style. Their version made the song central to the bluegrass repertoire.","The Stanleys' vocal sound is the lesson. Where Monroe's tenor was bright, Ralph's is raw and nasal, rooted in the unaccompanied Primitive Baptist singing of his childhood. Listen to how he bends and holds certain words, a mountain church habit. Bob Dylan put a very different version on his first album in 1962, and the 2000 film O Brother, Where Art Thou? made it a hit again for a new generation.","It is now among the best-known songs in American folk music. For decades, however, it lived mainly in the bluegrass and folk-revival circuits; the O Brother soundtrack's success brought it to mass audiences and a Grammy. Scholars still debate its origins, which may predate Burnett, and folklorists treat its many variants as a case study in oral tradition.","Start with the Stanleys' recording and listen only to Ralph's voice. Then hear the Soggy Bottom Boys' version on the O Brother soundtrack, sung by Dan Tyminski, to see how the song was polished for film. Dylan's 1962 reading shows what the folk revival heard in it."],"stanley-brothers-rank-stranger":["A gospel song of homecoming and estrangement. Carter Stanley sings the verses in his warm, plain baritone: a man returns to the home of his youth and finds every face unknown. At the chorus Ralph Stanley's high tenor enters above him, ghostly and piercing, and the song turns from sorrow to a hope of reunion beyond death. The accompaniment is light and slow, leaving the two voices exposed.","The song was written by the Missouri gospel composer Albert E. Brumley, author of I'll Fly Away, and published in the 1940s. The Stanley Brothers recorded it in 1960, in the middle of a period when rock and roll had cut deeply into bluegrass's audience and they were making a living on small labels and on the road. Gospel material was always a large part of their repertoire.","The song works by vocal handover. Carter's lead is intimate and earthly; Ralph's answering voice seems to come from somewhere else entirely, which is exactly what the words are about. Listen to the chorus as a dialogue between the living and the dead. After Carter's death in 1966, when Ralph sang it with other partners, audiences heard the song as a memorial to his brother.","It is widely considered the Stanleys' finest recording and one of the great bluegrass gospel performances. It rarely gets the pop attention of Man of Constant Sorrow, but musicians cite it constantly. Its eeriness has made it a touchstone for later Americana singers who draw on the gothic, sacred strain in mountain music.","Wait for the chorus: the entry of Ralph's voice is the whole point. Listen for how little the instruments do. Then hear the Carter Family's Can the Circle Be Unbroken for another song about home, death and reunion, and Gillian Welch's Revival for its modern descendants."],"doc-watson-doc-watson":["Doc Watson's first solo album: a North Carolina mountain man, mostly alone with a flat-top guitar, playing ballads, blues, hymns and fiddle tunes. His baritone is relaxed and warm, and his guitar is a revelation, picking fast fiddle melodies note by note with a flat pick, clean and unhurried at speeds that sound impossible. Pieces like Black Mountain Rag and Deep River Blues show the two sides: dazzling instrumental runs and easy, rolling fingerpicked blues.","Watson, blind since infancy, grew up in Deep Gap, North Carolina, and had played electric guitar in a local rockabilly and swing band. In 1960 the folklorist Ralph Rinzler met him while recording Clarence Ashley, and brought him to the urban folk revival, where he quickly became a star at Newport and in coffeehouses. Vanguard released this album in 1964 at the height of that revival.","Watson learned many fiddle tunes on guitar because his band had no fiddler and dance crowds wanted them: the style that amazed folk audiences was a practical workaround from playing square dances on an electric guitar. Listen for how his flatpicked lines copy fiddle bowing, with little slides and double notes. Much of the repertoire came from his family and from the Carter Family and Jimmie Rodgers records he heard as a boy.","It established Watson as the defining flatpicking guitarist, and the style it popularised became standard in bluegrass guitar, notably through Clarence White and Tony Rice. Folk revival audiences treated him as an authentic tradition-bearer; later writers stress how eclectic his tastes actually were, including jazz and rockabilly. The album remains a starting point for acoustic guitarists.","Start with Black Mountain Rag for the flatpicking, then Deep River Blues for the fingerpicked side. Listen to how calm his voice stays while the guitar races. Then hear Old Time Music at Clarence Ashley's to meet him in his home setting."],"nitty-gritty-dirt-band-will-the-circle-be-unbroken":["A sprawling triple album in which a California folk-rock band sits down in Nashville with the elders of country and bluegrass. Maybelle Carter, Roy Acuff, Earl Scruggs, Doc Watson, Merle Travis, Jimmy Martin and fiddler Vassar Clements lead songs and tunes, with the young band backing them. Between numbers the tape keeps rolling, catching studio chat and count-ins. It ends with the whole company singing the Carter Family hymn of the title.","In 1971 the Nitty Gritty Dirt Band, long-haired Californians, persuaded musicians from a generation and a political world apart to record with them. The Vietnam War divided country's audience from rock's, and many Nashville figures distrusted hippies. Recorded quickly in a Nashville studio, largely live, it was released by United Artists in 1972 and sold far beyond expectations.","Listen to the talk between tracks. The candid moments, such as Acuff sizing up the young players before a take, and old musicians recalling where they learned a tune, are part of the record's argument that tradition passes hand to hand. Maybelle Carter's autoharp and guitar on Wildwood Flower connect straight back to her 1928 recording.","It became a landmark of cross-generational collaboration and brought many listeners to bluegrass and old-time country. Critics praise its warmth and documentary value more than any single performance. Two sequels followed, in 1989 and 2002; the 1972 original is still regarded as the one that mattered.","Do not listen straight through the first time. Pick the Maybelle Carter tracks, the Doc Watson tracks and the closing title song, then fill in the rest over several sittings. Notice how the young band plays under the elders rather than over them. Compare the finale with the Carter Family's original Can the Circle Be Unbroken from the 1930s."],"emmylou-harris-roses-in-the-snow":["Emmylou Harris, then one of country's biggest stars, makes an all-acoustic bluegrass and old-time record. Her clear, high voice rides over dobro, fiddle, banjo and flatpicked guitar, singing mountain gospel, Carter Family and Stanley Brothers songs, the old hymn Wayfaring Stranger, and a bluegrass version of Simon and Garfunkel's The Boxer. The arrangements are spare and bright, and the harmony singing is close and old-fashioned.","Harris had built her career on country rock since Gram Parsons's death. In 1980, with bluegrass unfashionable in Nashville, she and her producer and husband Brian Ahern chose an acoustic, traditional sound, drawing on her band's young players, above all Ricky Skaggs, with guests including Tony Rice and Jerry Douglas and harmony from stars such as Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Johnny Cash. Warner Bros. released it, and it sold well.","Listen to Ricky Skaggs, who sings harmony and plays fiddle and mandolin throughout and did much to shape the arrangements; the record foreshadows his own run of hit albums that brought traditional sounds back to country radio in the 1980s. The Boxer, turned into a bluegrass song, argues quietly that the folk revival and mountain music were never far apart.","It was a surprise success and is widely credited with helping the new traditionalist movement that followed in 1980s country. Some bluegrass purists found Harris's voice too polished; most welcomed a major star lending her platform to the tradition. It is often ranked among her finest albums.","Start with Wayfaring Stranger, then The Boxer, then a Carter Family song. Listen for Skaggs's high harmony against Harris's lead, and for the dobro between verses. Then go back to Gram Parsons's Grievous Angel to hear where her country voice was formed, and forward to Gillian Welch's Revival for the tradition's next turn."],"flying-burrito-brothers-the-gilded-palace-of-sin":["Honky-tonk, Southern soul and Los Angeles rock rolled into one. Gram Parsons and Chris Hillman sing close brother-style harmonies over Sneaky Pete Kleinow's pedal steel, which is often run through a fuzz box until it snarls like an electric guitar. Songs like Sin City and Hot Burrito No. 1 sit beside two Memphis soul covers, Do Right Woman and The Dark End of the Street. It is sad, sly and strange.","After leaving the Byrds, Parsons and Hillman formed the Burritos in Los Angeles in 1968 and released this on A&M early in 1969. They wore custom Nudie suits embroidered with pills, marijuana leaves and flames, turning the rhinestone costume of Nashville into a counterculture joke that was also sincere. Parsons called his ideal Cosmic American Music: country, soul and rock as one Southern inheritance.","The two soul covers are the key. Do Right Woman had been recorded by Aretha Franklin two years earlier; Parsons sings the Southern soul songs straight, as country laments, arguing that the Black and white music of the South were one family. Listen also for Kleinow's steel imitating horns and electric guitars.","It sold poorly but became a cult touchstone, cherished by the Rolling Stones, Elvis Costello, and later the alt-country movement. Critics now rank it with Sweetheart of the Rodeo as a founding country-rock document, sometimes above it for its originality. Hillman's contribution has been reappraised as central to the songs.","Begin with Hot Burrito No. 1 for Parsons's aching vocal, then Sin City for the harmony. Then compare Do Right Woman with Aretha Franklin's version. The Rolling Stones' Let It Bleed shows the Burritos' influence on British rock."],"gram-parsons-grievous-angel":["Gram Parsons's second and last solo album, built on his duets with Emmylou Harris. Their voices twine through Love Hurts, the title song and In My Hour of Darkness, his thin, fragile tenor leaning on her clear high harmony. The band, including members of Elvis Presley's touring group, plays spare, elegant country; the mood is wistful and road-weary, shifting between honky-tonk ballads and brighter country rock.","Parsons recorded it in Los Angeles in summer 1973 and died that September, aged 26, at Joshua Tree in the California desert, after a drug overdose. Reprise released it in January 1974. Harris, a folk singer he had discovered in Washington, D.C., had been learning country harmony from him; the album made her name and launched her career.","The medley of Cash on the Barrelhead and Hickory Wind is presented as a live bar recording, but it was cut in the studio with crowd noise and chatter added, a mock-live frame for Parsons's favourite song. Listen for the laughing and clinking glasses. In My Hour of Darkness, written with Harris, mourns friends including Clarence White and sounds now like his own elegy.","Received respectfully, it grew into a cult classic and set the template for the male-female country duet in Americana. Critics praise the harmonies above almost anything else in his work. The myth of his death, including the theft and burning of his body by his road manager, has sometimes overshadowed the music.","Start with Love Hurts and listen only to how Harris shapes the harmony. Then hear the Everly Brothers' close-harmony style, which Parsons loved. After this, Emmylou Harris's Pieces of the Sky shows her carrying the torch forward."],"the-band-the-band":["Often called the brown album, after its sepia cover. Four Canadians and an Arkansan play songs that sound like old America: the Civil War, farms, travelling medicine shows, floods. Three lead singers, Levon Helm, Rick Danko and Richard Manuel, trade verses in rough, overlapping voices; Garth Hudson's organ and horns thicken the sound. The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down and Up on Cripple Creek are its best-known songs.","After backing Bob Dylan in his electric years and working with him in upstate New York, the Band recorded this in 1969 in a pool house in the Hollywood hills converted into a studio. Rock in 1969 was loud, psychedelic and political; this record went the other way, toward period dress and the past. Robbie Robertson wrote most of the songs; Capitol released it that autumn.","Listen to the instruments swapping roles. Danko sometimes plays fiddle, Manuel drums on some tracks, Hudson overdubs horns himself, and Helm's drumming sits behind the beat in a relaxed Southern shuffle. The recording was deliberately dry and close, as if the band were in a room with you, at a time when big reverb was the norm.","It was received as a landmark and has stayed one, a foundation stone of what later came to be called Americana. The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down, told from a defeated Confederate's point of view, has drawn sharper criticism in recent decades. Arguments over songwriting credits between Robertson and the others became bitter in later life.","Start with Up on Cripple Creek for Hudson's clavinet and wah pedal, then The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down. Notice how often voices overlap rather than harmonise neatly. Then compare with Sweetheart of the Rodeo, released a year earlier."],"john-prine-john-prine":["A debut of plain, sure-footed songs. Prine's voice is dry and a little rough, and his band stays out of the way. The songs are tender and funny in the same breath: Hello in There on loneliness in old age, Angel from Montgomery sung as a middle-aged woman, Sam Stone on a wounded veteran's addiction, Paradise on strip mining in Kentucky. The detail is small and domestic; the effect is enormous.","Prine was a mail carrier and army veteran in Chicago, singing at open-mic nights, when Kris Kristofferson heard him and helped him to a contract with Atlantic. The album was recorded in Memphis with producer Arif Mardin and released in 1971, as the Vietnam War dragged on. Prine was widely billed as a new Dylan, a label he shrugged off.","Paradise names the Peabody Coal Company and Muhlenberg County, Kentucky, where Prine's parents came from and where surface mining had destroyed the landscape. Listen for how gently he sings it. Angel from Montgomery was written from the viewpoint of a woman, something his male peers rarely did; Bonnie Raitt's 1974 version made it a standard.","It won strong reviews but modest sales at the time; its songs were spread through covers. It is now regarded as one of the great singer-songwriter debuts. Prine's reputation rose steadily over five decades, and his death in 2020 during the pandemic prompted a wide reassessment of his craft.","Start with Hello in There and Angel from Montgomery, then Paradise. Listen for the jokes placed right next to grief, and for how little the band does behind him. Then hear Bonnie Raitt's version of Angel from Montgomery to see how the song travelled, and Townes Van Zandt's Pancho and Lefty for a darker sibling in the same trade."],"townes-van-zandt-pancho-and-lefty":["A border ballad about a Mexican bandit and the friend who may have betrayed him. Van Zandt sings in a quiet, grave baritone over fingerpicked guitar and discreet accompaniment. The story is told in fragments, with dust, desert and a lonely hotel in Cleveland, and never quite says what happened. The melody rises and falls like an old cowboy song, but the storytelling is modern: elliptical, cinematic, morally unresolved.","Van Zandt, from a wealthy Texas family, lived much of his life in poverty, drifting and struggling with alcohol and depression. The song appeared on The Late Great Townes Van Zandt, a 1972 album on the small Poppy label. It had little impact until others sang it: Emmylou Harris in 1977, and above all Willie Nelson and Merle Haggard, whose 1983 duet was a country number one.","Van Zandt often said he did not know where the song came from and that it arrived nearly complete. Listen for what the lyric withholds: we never learn whether Lefty betrayed Pancho or simply survived him. Pancho may nod to Pancho Villa, but the song is not a history. The mystery is the design, and it lets every singer take a side.","It is now among the most admired songs in country and folk writing, frequently cited by songwriters as a model. Van Zandt himself sold few records in his lifetime; admiration came mainly from fellow writers such as Steve Earle, who called him one of the best ever. His original recording is still less known than the Nelson–Haggard version.","Listen to Van Zandt's version first, in a quiet room. Then play the Nelson–Haggard duet and notice how a hit arrangement brightens it. Steve Earle's album of Van Zandt songs, Townes, is a good next step."],"steve-earle-guitar-town":["Country songs played with rock-and-roll swagger. Earle sings in a raspy Texas drawl over twangy electric guitars and a lean band, the Dukes. The songs are about working men, small towns, cars and the urge to leave: the title track, Someday and Hillbilly Highway. It sounds like Nashville crossed with Bruce Springsteen, crisp and tuneful but with dirt under its fingernails.","Earle had spent a decade as a Nashville songwriter and scuffling rockabilly act. MCA released this debut in 1986, produced by Tony Brown and Emory Gordy Jr., in the same wave that brought Dwight Yoakam and Randy Travis: a turn back to traditional sounds after the pop-heavy country of the early 1980s. It found an audience on country radio and among rock critics at once.","Listen to the guitar sound. Earle's twangy electric parts were deliberately bright and hard, halfway between Buck Owens's Bakersfield honky-tonk and heartland rock. Someday, about a young man pumping gas and dreaming of escape, uses the same working-class vocabulary as Springsteen, but its setting is the country side of the tracks.","It was a critical and commercial success, and it has since been credited as a precursor of alt-country. Earle's later albums moved further toward rock and politics, and his troubles with addiction and prison in the early 1990s interrupted his career. After his return he became one of the elder statesmen of Americana.","Start with Someday, then the title track. Notice the tight, economical arrangements, no song much over four minutes. Compare with Merle Haggard for the older working-man tradition, and then Copperhead Road for Earle's harder turn."],"steve-earle-copperhead-road":["Earle's hardest-rocking album. The title track opens with a droning figure that sounds like bagpipes before drums crash in, telling a three-generation family story of moonshine, war and illegal crops in the Tennessee hills. The rest of the record mixes storytelling ballads, loud country rock and the Irish-tinged Johnny Come Lately, recorded with the Pogues. It is heavier and angrier than Guitar Town.","Released in 1988 by MCA's rock-oriented imprint Uni, the album was marketed to rock fans because country radio found Earle too rough. The Vietnam War and its veterans were a live subject in late-1980s America, after films like Platoon; several songs carry the war's shadow. Earle was also in the grip of addiction during this period.","The title track's narrator is a Vietnam veteran, the grandson and son of bootleggers, who brings home new techniques from the war. Listen for how the drone at the start imitates Scottish pipes, a hint at the Scots-Irish ancestry of Appalachia that the whole song trades on. Johnny Come Lately sets a World War II homecoming beside a Vietnam one.","The title song became Earle's best-known track, a staple of rock radio and line-dance floors. The album sits awkwardly between country and rock, which divided critics at the time. It is now recognised as a key border-crossing record for Americana.","Play the title track loud, listening for the moment the drums crash in after the droning opening, and follow the story across its three generations. Then hear Johnny Come Lately with the Pogues for the Irish side. Going back to Guitar Town afterwards shows how far and how fast Earle travelled in two years."],"lucinda-williams-lucinda-williams":["A lean, guitar-based record by a Louisiana-born singer with a drawling, cracked voice. The songs are about desire, restlessness and places: Passionate Kisses, Changed the Locks, Crescent City. The band plays unfussy rock and roll, country shuffle and blues, with none of Nashville's gloss. Williams sings as if thinking aloud, slurring and stretching vowels, and the directness of the writing is the point.","Williams, daughter of the poet Miller Williams, had made two folk-blues albums on Folkways in the late 1970s and then spent years unsigned; country labels found her too rock, rock labels too country. The British independent Rough Trade released this in 1988. It sold modestly but circulated among musicians and critics.","Two songs here became hits for other artists: Mary Chapin Carpenter's Passionate Kisses won a Grammy in 1994, and Tom Petty recorded Changed the Locks. Listen for how Williams's own version of Passionate Kisses sounds defiant, even impatient, while Carpenter's is sunnier: the same words, a different self.","It is now regarded as a founding document of Americana, a genre name that did not yet exist. Critics singled out her writing immediately; the recognition from a wider public came a decade later with Car Wheels. The album was reissued with bonus material in 2014.","Start with Passionate Kisses, then Changed the Locks. Pay attention to her vowels: she makes a single word carry an argument. Then jump to Car Wheels on a Gravel Road to hear what ten years did."],"lucinda-williams-car-wheels-on-a-gravel-road":["A set of songs soaked in the American South: Louisiana back roads, lost loves, dead friends. Williams's voice is weary and tough, the band loose and rootsy, with slide guitar and accordion colouring the rock rhythm. The title song remembers a child's view from the back seat of a car; Drunken Angel mourns a songwriter; Lake Charles is a road elegy. Every track has a place name in it or nearby.","Recorded over roughly six years, through several studios and producers, including a period with Steve Earle and Ray Kennedy and a final polish with Roy Bittan of Springsteen's E Street Band. Williams's perfectionism became notorious. Mercury released it in 1998, and it won the Grammy for Best Contemporary Folk Album.","Listen to 2 Kool 2 Be 4-Gotten, which echoes the Delta blues world of Robert Johnson, a nod to the region's Black musical history. Drunken Angel is about the Texas songwriter Blaze Foley, killed in 1989. The album's scrupulous naturalism, from gravel under tyres to crickets, was built through dozens of retakes, not captured casually.","It was a critical landmark and her commercial breakthrough, topping many year-end polls. Some critics found the long gestation over-polished; most heard the opposite, a record that sounds lived-in. It remains the album most often used to define what Americana means.","Begin with the title track, then Lake Charles and Drunken Angel. Listen for the concrete nouns in every line. Afterwards hear Robert Johnson's Cross Road Blues to feel the ground under 2 Kool 2 Be 4-Gotten."],"uncle-tupelo-no-depression":["A punk trio plays country songs, and the collision sounds natural. Jay Farrar and Jeff Tweedy trade lead vocals over loud, distorted guitars and thrashing drums, then drop suddenly to acoustic guitars and hushed singing. The songs deal with small-town decline, drinking and dead-end jobs in the American Midwest. The title track is an old Carter Family song about the Depression, played straight.","Farrar, Tweedy and drummer Mike Heidorn grew up in Belleville, Illinois, a working-class town across the river from St. Louis. They played punk in their teens and discovered their parents' country records. This debut was recorded in Boston and released by the small Rockville label in 1990, as grunge was forming; it sold little at first.","The album's title and its cover of the Carter Family's No Depression in Heaven, from 1936, give the game away: Uncle Tupelo heard the hard-times songs of the 1930s as the true ancestors of punk. Listen for how the band keeps the old hymn's plain melody while raising the volume, and for how Farrar's and Tweedy's voices divide the album between gravity and warmth.","It is regarded as the founding record of alternative country; a magazine founded in 1995 took the name No Depression, and alt-country fans became known by it. The band split in 1994 into Son Volt and Wilco. Critics now debate whether the label alt-country helped or trapped the music that followed.","Play Graveyard Shift for the punk side, then the title track for the hushed country side, and notice how sudden the switch is. Listen to the Carter Family's 1936 original afterwards. Then hear Son Volt's Trace and Wilco's Yankee Hotel Foxtrot to follow the two singers as their paths split after 1994."],"son-volt-trace":["Jay Farrar's first album after Uncle Tupelo. His deep, grave voice sings of rivers, highways and the Midwest, alternating slow acoustic ballads with fiddle and lap steel against loud, distorted rock songs. Windfall, the opening track, is a song about driving at night with the radio on; Tear Stained Eye and Drown follow. The mood is melancholic, the sound open and spacious.","Uncle Tupelo split in 1994. Farrar formed Son Volt with Tupelo drummer Mike Heidorn and the brothers Jim and Dave Boquist, and Warner Bros. released Trace in 1995, the same year Tweedy's Wilco debuted. Alt-country was becoming a scene with its own magazine and radio format, and the two ex-bandmates were compared at every turn.","Listen to the fiddle and lap steel on Windfall: the arrangement almost imitates an old radio signal fading in and out, matching the song's subject, a late-night AM station on the road. Farrar's lyrics are strewn with Mississippi River imagery; the river runs through the whole record like a map.","It was the better-received of the two post-Tupelo debuts and remains Son Volt's high point. Critics then assumed Farrar would be the more important songwriter; Wilco's later experiments reversed that judgment. Trace is still considered one of the purest alt-country albums.","Start with Windfall, ideally in a car at night, and listen to the fiddle and steel drifting like a fading radio. Then play Drown for the loud side of the band. Compare with Wilco's Yankee Hotel Foxtrot to hear where the other half of Uncle Tupelo went, and why fans argued about them for years."],"wilco-yankee-hotel-foxtrot":["Folk-rock songs dissolving into noise, static and electronic texture. Jeff Tweedy sings in a frail, conversational voice; beneath him the band builds melodies that keep being interrupted by radio hiss, clanging percussion and drones. I Am Trying to Break Your Heart opens in a clatter; Jesus, Etc. is a lovely string-laced ballad; Poor Places ends in a wall of noise. Country's plain storytelling is still there, but half buried.","Wilco recorded it in Chicago in 2001 with Jim O'Rourke mixing. Reprise, their label, rejected it as uncommercial and released the band. Wilco streamed the album free on their website in September 2001, just after the attacks on New York, and Nonesuch, another Warner label, released it in April 2002. Songs about towers and war suddenly seemed prophetic.","The title comes from a shortwave numbers-station recording, a woman's voice reading the phonetic alphabet, taken from The Conet Project compilation. Listen for it at the end of Poor Places. The documentary I Am Trying to Break Your Heart filmed the sessions, including the departure of multi-instrumentalist Jay Bennett, and the album's story became part of its reception.","It was hailed as a landmark and became a symbol of artists defeating a major label through the internet. Some alt-country fans felt the band had abandoned its roots. Critics now see it as proof that the Uncle Tupelo tradition could absorb experimental rock and electronics without losing its heart.","Start with Jesus, Etc., the most straightforward song, then I Am Trying to Break Your Heart. Listen for the static, radio fragments and clatter as part of the music, not interference. Afterwards hear No Depression to measure the distance travelled from Uncle Tupelo, and watch the documentary of the sessions if you want the backstory."],"gillian-welch-revival":["Gillian Welch and David Rawlings sing new songs that sound a century old. Their two voices lock in close, dry harmony; their two guitars interlock, Welch's rhythm steady, Rawlings's lead bright and angular. The songs are about mountain poverty, orphanhood, moonshine and faith: Orphan Girl, Annabelle, Tear My Stillhouse Down. The sound is sparse, slow and dusty, with occasional muted drums and bass added by producer T Bone Burnett.","Welch, raised in Los Angeles, fell in love with the Stanley Brothers at college and moved to Nashville, where she met Rawlings at Berklee in Boston. Almo Sounds released this debut in 1996. Emmylou Harris had already recorded Orphan Girl on Wrecking Ball in 1995. Welch's authenticity as a Californian writing Appalachian songs was questioned from the start.","Listen to Rawlings's guitar. He plays an old small-bodied archtop guitar with a sharp, pinched tone, and his lead lines often cut across the melody rather than supporting it. The duo's harmonies deliberately avoid sweetness, imitating the brother-duet sound of the Stanleys and the Louvins.","It won strong reviews and a Grammy nomination, and it helped create the audience for O Brother four years later. The authenticity debate has faded; Welch is now recognised as among the best songwriters of her generation. Some prefer later albums for their variety, but Revival remains the purest statement.","Start with Orphan Girl, then compare Emmylou Harris's version on Wrecking Ball from the year before. Listen closely to the interplay of the two guitars, his darting and hers steady, and to the dry, unsweetened harmony. Then go back to the Stanley Brothers' Rank Stranger to hear the sound the duo learned from."],"gillian-welch-time-the-revelator":["Just two voices and two guitars, with a banjo here and there, recorded in Nashville's old RCA Studio B. The songs are slow and long, bending mountain music toward meditation: Revelator opens; Elvis Presley Blues remembers the King's shaking hips; Everything Is Free comments on the music industry; I Dream a Highway, nearly fifteen minutes, closes the album in a drifting reverie. The mood is dreamlike and patient.","Welch and Rawlings, having left their label, founded their own, Acony Records, and released this in 2001. Napster and file-sharing were upending the music business; Everything Is Free takes up the subject calmly, as a question of how a musician lives. The O Brother soundtrack, on which Welch appeared, was then making old-time music newly popular.","Studio B was where Elvis and the Everly Brothers recorded; Welch and Rawlings chose it for its room sound. Listen to the room ambience on Elvis Presley Blues, and for how the song makes Elvis a figure of folk myth, like John Henry. The album's title plays on the old gospel song John the Revelator.","It was received as a major record and is often ranked the finest of the 2000s in its field. Some reviewers found the long songs slow; others praised the courage of that pace. Its release on the duo's own label became a model for independent roots musicians.","Hear Elvis Presley Blues first, then Everything Is Free. Leave time for I Dream a Highway and let it drift. Afterwards hear Blind Willie Johnson's John the Revelator for the gospel source of the title."],"alison-krauss-so-long-so-wrong":["Contemporary bluegrass of great delicacy. Alison Krauss sings in a clear, pure, almost weightless soprano; her band, Union Station, plays with precision and restraint. Jerry Douglas's dobro glides between verses, the banjo rolls softly, and ballads alternate with fast instrumentals. Dan Tyminski takes lead on some songs with a harder, more traditional voice. The textures are silky, the tempo mostly unhurried.","Krauss, a fiddle prodigy from Illinois, signed with the independent Rounder label as a teenager. By the mid-1990s a greatest-hits collection had made her a platinum-selling star, remarkable for bluegrass. So Long So Wrong, released by Rounder in 1997, was a full band album that won the Grammy for Best Bluegrass Album.","Listen to how the voice is placed in the mix, close and soft rather than projected, much as in pop balladry. Traditional bluegrass singers shout to carry over the band; Krauss's intimacy was a studio-age innovation that brought the genre to listeners who had never heard of Bill Monroe. The instrumentals show that the band's chops are as hard as any traditional outfit.","It was praised as a high-water mark of modern bluegrass and won the Grammy for Best Bluegrass Album. Traditionalists occasionally found Krauss's sound too smooth, closer to pop balladry than to the hard, projected singing of Monroe or Ralph Stanley, and the old argument over what counts as real bluegrass followed her for years. Her popularity nonetheless opened the genre to new audiences, and Union Station's players went on to form the core of the O Brother soundtrack.","Start with the title track, then a Dan Tyminski lead vocal for contrast between his hard traditional voice and her soft one. Listen for Jerry Douglas's dobro sliding under the singing, and for the tight instrumentals. Afterwards go back to Bill Monroe's Blue Moon of Kentucky to measure how far bluegrass singing had moved."],"alison-krauss-raising-sand":["Robert Plant of Led Zeppelin and Alison Krauss sing old and obscure songs together, softly. Their voices, his husky, hers crystalline, blend in close harmony over dark, reverberant arrangements: tremolo guitars, brushed drums, spare fiddle. Songs by the Everly Brothers, Townes Van Zandt, Tom Waits and Allen Toussaint are slowed into a hazy, mysterious mood. It sounds like a late-night radio show from 1958 heard through a desert haze.","Producer T Bone Burnett chose the songs and the musicians, including guitarist Marc Ribot. Rounder released it in 2007. Plant, the most famous hard-rock singer of the 1970s, and Krauss, bluegrass's biggest star, seemed an unlikely pair; the gamble paid off. In 2009 it won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","Listen to how the singers rarely belt. Plant, once famous for howling, sings almost at a whisper, and the arrangements leave space where one expects a solo. Gone Gone Gone comes from the Everly Brothers, and the vocal blend imitates their close fifths and thirds. Townes Van Zandt's Nothin' becomes a brooding drone.","It was a critical and commercial success and swept the Grammys. Some bluegrass fans missed Krauss's band; some rock fans missed Plant's roar. It is now seen as one of the defining records of the 2000s Americana boom, alongside O Brother.","Start with Please Read the Letter, then Gone Gone Gone, and listen to how the two voices blend rather than compete. Notice how the reverb and tremolo guitars create distance, as if the songs were drifting in from another decade. Then play the Everly Brothers to hear the harmony tradition Plant and Krauss are borrowing from."],"t-bone-burnett-o-brother-where-art-thou":["A soundtrack for a Coen brothers film set in 1930s Mississippi, compiled and produced by T Bone Burnett. It mixes old recordings with new performances in old styles: Ralph Stanley's unaccompanied O Death, the Soggy Bottom Boys' Man of Constant Sorrow, Alison Krauss, Gillian Welch and Emmylou Harris singing a lullaby, the Fairfield Four, and a 1959 prison work-song field recording. Gospel, bluegrass and blues sit side by side.","The Coens built their film around the music, recording much of it before shooting. Released in 2000 on Mercury's roots label Lost Highway, the album received almost no radio play but sold several million copies, won the Grammy for Album of the Year in 2002, and spawned the Down from the Mountain concert tour. It turned Americana into a mass-market category.","George Clooney mimes in the film, but the voice of Man of Constant Sorrow belongs to Dan Tyminski of Union Station. Listen for the field recording of Po' Lazarus, made by Alan Lomax at a Mississippi prison; its lead singer, James Carter, was traced decades later and presented with royalties. Ralph Stanley's O Death is sung in the old unaccompanied Baptist style.","Its success astonished the music industry and prompted a revival of interest in bluegrass and old-time music. Some traditionalists grumbled that the album polished the old sounds; most musicians in the field credit it with reviving their careers. It remains one of the best-selling soundtracks ever.","Start with Ralph Stanley's unaccompanied O Death, then the Soggy Bottom Boys' Man of Constant Sorrow, then the prison work song Po' Lazarus. Notice how old recordings and new ones sit side by side without jarring. Then go to Harry Smith's Anthology of American Folk Music for the older originals the album echoes."],"carolina-chocolate-drops-genuine-negro-jig":["A string band of young Black musicians playing old-time Piedmont fiddle and banjo music. Rhiannon Giddens's strong voice leads ballads and blues; fiddle and banjo drive dance tunes; bones, jug and beatboxing supply rhythm. The repertoire mixes traditional tunes with modern covers, including Blu Cantrell's R&B hit Hit 'Em Up Style played as a string-band song. The sound is lively, rough-edged, and plainly joyful.","Giddens, Dom Flemons and Justin Robinson met at the Black Banjo Gathering in North Carolina in 2005 and apprenticed themselves to Joe Thompson, an elderly African American fiddler. Their mission was to reclaim the Black roots of the string band, erased by the racial marketing of records in the 1920s. Nonesuch released this in 2010, produced by Joe Henry; it won the Grammy for Best Traditional Folk Album.","The title probably comes from an old 19th-century tune title; the band reclaims the offensive label deliberately. Listen for the banjo, which descends from West African gourd lutes brought by the enslaved. The album quietly argues that bluegrass and country began partly as Black music, a history that the Stanleys and Monroe knew and the record business obscured.","It was widely praised and became a touchstone for the growing movement to recognise Black contributions to country and folk. Giddens went on to a major solo career and a MacArthur fellowship. Some critics found the album's range uneven; its historical argument is now widely accepted.","Start with Hit 'Em Up Style, then a traditional fiddle tune, and notice that the two do not sound as far apart as you expect. Listen for the bones and beatbox driving the rhythm. Then hear Elizabeth Cotten and Mississippi John Hurt for earlier Black Piedmont traditions that this band set out to honour."],"j-d-crowe-and-the-new-south":["An all-acoustic bluegrass album with a modern feel. J.D. Crowe's banjo leads a quintet with Tony Rice on guitar, Ricky Skaggs on mandolin and fiddle, and Jerry Douglas on dobro. They play bluegrass standards alongside songs by Gordon Lightfoot and folk writers, with smooth harmonies and crisp, clean solos. The tempo is brisk, the sound polished and exact, and the dobro and guitar play melody lines as fluid as jazz.","Crowe, a Kentuckian who had played with Jimmy Martin, had led an electric band in the early 1970s. For this Rounder release in 1975 the group went acoustic, but kept its pop and folk repertoire. The small independent Rounder label, founded by students in Massachusetts, was becoming the home of new bluegrass, and this album became one of its best sellers.","Listen to Tony Rice's guitar solos: he built on Doc Watson's and Clarence White's flatpicking and made the guitar a full lead voice in bluegrass. The cat number, Rounder 0044, is how bluegrass players still refer to the record. Almost every member went on to lead a major band: Rice, Skaggs and Douglas shaped the next thirty years of the music.","It is often called the most influential bluegrass album of the 1970s, bridging Monroe's tradition and the progressive bluegrass that followed. Critics praise its balance of polish and drive: the repertoire drew on Gordon Lightfoot and contemporary folk writers, yet the playing stayed acoustic and disciplined. Players still refer to it simply by its catalogue number, as jazz fans do with Blue Note records, and its line-up is regarded as one of the great bluegrass ensembles.","Listen to one track three times: once for the banjo, once for Tony Rice's guitar, once for Jerry Douglas's dobro. Notice how clean and exact the ensemble is. Then compare with Foggy Mountain Breakdown to hear how the bluegrass band sound had evolved in twenty-five years, and follow any player whose sound you like into his own records."],"jason-isbell-southeastern":["A quiet, mostly acoustic album of songs about sobriety, love and death. Isbell's voice is clear and steady; the arrangements are spare, with fingerpicked guitar, soft fiddle from Amanda Shires, and occasional full-band rock songs. Cover Me Up is a love song about recovery; Elephant describes a friend dying of cancer; Live Oak is a dark ballad about a man hiding his past. The mood is intimate and direct.","Isbell had played in the Alabama band Drive-By Truckers until 2007 and struggled with alcohol. In 2012 he went to rehab and married Shires. Producer Dave Cobb recorded this in Nashville, and it came out on Isbell's own Southeastern Records in 2013. It became his breakthrough, embraced by Americana radio and critics, largely outside the Nashville country establishment.","Listen to Elephant: the song names the illness only once and circles it, refusing melodrama, much as John Prine's Hello in There describes old age through a few household details. Isbell's craft lies in plain, exact particulars rather than big images. Cover Me Up, the opening song, is about fear of relapse as much as romance, and Amanda Shires's fiddle and harmony, heard across the record, tie the album to the marriage it describes.","It won Album of the Year at the Americana Music Honors in 2014 and ranks among the most admired records of the 2010s, largely without help from mainstream country radio. It re-established the confessional singer-songwriter as central to Americana after a decade dominated by bands. Critics often compare Isbell's craft with Prine's and his Southern working-class settings with Springsteen's; some found the record too quiet after his Drive-By Truckers work, a view that has faded.","Start with Cover Me Up, then Elephant, and listen for the small details that carry the weight, a name, a place, a joke. Notice how rarely the band gets loud. Then compare with John Prine's Hello in There, another song that finds grief in ordinary rooms, and with Lucinda Williams's Car Wheels on a Gravel Road."],"sturgill-simpson-metamodern-sounds-in-country-music":["Honky-tonk country with a cosmic twist. Simpson's deep baritone recalls Waylon Jennings; the band plays straight classic country, with pedal steel and twangy guitar, until songs veer into psychedelic sound: backwards guitars, swirling reverb. Turtles All the Way Down muses on religion, mind-altering experiences and love; It Ain't All Flowers dissolves into a tape-loop haze. A cover of the 1980s synth-pop song The Promise turns into a country ballad.","Simpson, a Kentuckian and former Navy sailor and railroad worker, made this on a small budget in Nashville with Dave Cobb and released it on his own label in 2014. It appeared when mainstream country was dominated by polished pop-rock, and it was hailed as a return to outlaw roots. The title nods to Ray Charles's 1962 album Modern Sounds in Country and Western Music.","Listen to It Ain't All Flowers: the backwards guitars and tape effects come from Beatles-era psychedelia, not from country at all. Simpson reportedly took the idea of metamodernism from writing on contemporary philosophy, and the album's mix of sincerity and irony follows it. The Promise, originally by When in Rome, was chosen to show that any song can be a country song.","It won widespread acclaim and made Simpson a leading voice of independent country; his next album was nominated for Album of the Year. Some critics found the outlaw-revival label reductive; he himself resisted it. It remains a touchstone for country musicians working outside Nashville.","Start with Turtles All the Way Down, then The Promise, and listen for the moment classic honky-tonk gives way to swirling psychedelic sound. Notice how short and tight the whole record is. Then hear Willie Nelson's Red Headed Stranger for the outlaw tradition, and the Beatles' Tomorrow Never Knows for the tape tricks."],"ike-turner-rocket-88":["A jump-blues boogie about a new Oldsmobile, sung by saxophonist Jackie Brenston over Ike Turner's band, the Kings of Rhythm. Turner's piano opens with a hammered boogie figure, the saxes riff in unison, and underneath everything sits a fuzzy, buzzing guitar doubling the bass line. It lasts under three minutes and never leaves its groove. The sound you remember is that guitar: dirty, blurred and loud, a noise no respectable record of 1951 was supposed to make.","The band drove up from Clarksdale, Mississippi, to Sam Phillips's Memphis Recording Service in March 1951. Phillips, not yet running his own label, leased the master to Chess in Chicago, which credited it to Jackie Brenston and His Delta Cats, leaving Turner's name off. It became a number-one rhythm-and-blues hit. Car songs were already a jump-blues staple; the Rocket 88 was Oldsmobile's fast new V8, a symbol of postwar Black mobility and money.","The fuzz was an accident. On the drive up, guitarist Willie Kizart's amplifier fell from the car and its speaker cone was torn; Phillips reportedly stuffed it with paper and recorded it anyway. That broken speaker is one of the first distorted guitar sounds on a hit record, the ancestor of every fuzz pedal. Listen, too, for how closely the tune follows earlier boogie car songs such as Jimmy Liggins's Cadillac Boogie; it was a genre piece, made new by its sound.","Its claim to be the first rock and roll record was made by Sam Phillips himself and repeated by historians ever since, though it has rivals going back to the 1940s and many musicians hear it simply as good jump blues. The argument matters less than what followed: the royalties built Phillips's Sun label, Bill Haley covered it within months, and Turner nursed the grievance of the missing credit for the rest of his life.","Play it loud and listen past the vocal to the guitar and bass under the saxophones; that grinding low buzz is the point. Then play Louis Jordan's jump records from the late forties to hear how little the song form changed and how much the sound did. Follow with Bill Haley's cover and Little Richard's Good Golly Miss Molly, which borrowed Turner's piano introduction."],"big-joe-turner-shake-rattle-and-roll":["A twelve-bar blues at an easy, rolling tempo: Big Joe Turner's enormous voice, a riffing saxophone section and a backbeat you could set a clock by. Verse after verse he orders his partner out of bed and into the kitchen, with the title phrase shouted back by the band as a chorus. It is relaxed rather than frantic, which is exactly why it swings; the power comes from the size of the voice, not the speed.","Turner was a Kansas City veteran in his forties when Atlantic recorded this in New York in early 1954. The song was written by the label's arranger Jesse Stone under the name Charles Calhoun. Atlantic's rhythm-and-blues records were beginning to be bought by white teenagers, and within months Bill Haley's Comets had covered it for Decca, with the lyrics cleaned up, and taken it into the pop charts.","Compare the two versions side by side and the story of early rock and roll is in the differences. Turner's lyric is frankly adult and full of blues double meanings; Haley kept the tune and the chorus but swapped out the suggestive verses for harmless ones. Elvis Presley's 1956 version mixes the two. Listen also for the saxophone riff that answers each vocal line: it is the Kansas City big-band habit of riffing, shrunk to a small combo.","It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart and has been in the canon of rock's origins ever since, a standard example of how Black records were covered and sanitised for white audiences. Turner's own career revived on the back of it, and later critics, rediscovering his 1930s boogie-woogie sides, came to hear him as a bridge between swing-era Kansas City and the rock and roll of the fifties.","Start with Turner's original and notice how little he strains; the voice is huge without shouting. Then hear Haley's cover and ask what was lost and what was gained. Turner's earlier Roll 'Em Pete with pianist Pete Johnson shows where the style came from; the Atlantic compilations of his fifties singles are the best way in."],"bill-haley-rock-around-the-clock":["Two minutes and ten seconds of countdown and release: Haley counts the hours from one o'clock onward over a stop-time rhythm, then the band crashes into a twelve-bar shuffle with slapped double bass, drums hitting the backbeat and a honking sax. A guitar solo of rapid-fire repeated notes rips through the middle. The shape is simple, the tempo brisk, the mood cheerful; it is a dance record by a band that had come up playing country and western swing.","Recorded in New York in April 1954 for Decca, with Milt Gabler producing; Gabler had made Louis Jordan's jump-blues hits and aimed to give Haley the same drive. On release it did only modestly. In 1955 the film Blackboard Jungle used it over the opening credits, and teenagers danced in the cinema aisles; it went to number one and began rock and roll's spread across the world, including riots at screenings abroad.","Listen to Danny Cedrone's guitar solo: its breakneck repeated-note run had already appeared, almost note for note, on Haley's earlier record Rock the Joint. Cedrone died within months of the session, before the record became famous. The song itself, by Max Freedman and James Myers, had been recorded first by another band; Haley's slapped bass and drum backbeat are what made it different.","For decades it was spoken of as the record that started rock and roll, a claim later historians complicated by pointing to the Black rhythm-and-blues records that came before it. Haley himself, older, plump and friendly, was quickly displaced by Elvis. But its sales and its film moment made it a genuine turning point in the music business, and it remains one of the best-selling singles ever made.","Listen first to the stop-time opening and the moment the full band hits; that release is the whole trick. Then listen for the slapped bass clicking under the beat. Put it beside Louis Jordan's records, which Gabler also produced, to hear the lineage, and beside Elvis's Sun sides to hear how much wilder the next year sounded."],"elvis-presley-thats-all-right":["Three men and no drummer: Elvis strumming an acoustic guitar and singing, Scotty Moore's electric guitar picking bright country licks, and Bill Black slapping a double bass for rhythm. The song is a blues about a lover who has gone, taken fast and light, with Elvis's voice leaping up into a high, playful register. The room echo, Sam Phillips's slap-back, makes three players sound like a band. It is under two minutes of pure buoyancy.","On 5 July 1954, at Sun Records in Memphis, a session for ballads was going nowhere when, during a break, Presley began clowning on Arthur Crudup's 1946 blues. Phillips asked them to do it again and recorded it. Disc jockey Dewey Phillips played it repeatedly on WHBQ within days. The B-side was Bill Monroe's bluegrass waltz Blue Moon of Kentucky, sped up: a Black blues and a white country song back to back.","The pairing on the two sides of the single is the whole programme of rockabilly: Crudup's Mississippi blues on one side, Monroe's bluegrass on the other, and both played the same way. Listen to how Moore's guitar never plays the blues licks you might expect but country fingerpicking. And listen for the absence of drums; the rhythm is carried entirely by Black's bass and Presley's strumming, which is why it floats.","Local radio listeners reportedly assumed the singer was Black, and some country stations would not play it. It sold modestly outside the South. Its reputation grew backwards: once Presley was famous, this first single was recognised as the moment the synthesis happened, and critics from Greil Marcus onward have treated the Sun recordings as his finest work. Crudup, for his part, received little money from it for years.","Hear Arthur Crudup's original first, then this. Notice how Presley keeps the melody but changes the feeling from resignation to delight. Listen to the whole run of Sun singles in order, ideally in a mono transfer, and pay attention to the way the echo makes the small room sound enormous."],"elvis-presley-mystery-train":["A train song driven by a chugging, locomotive rhythm: Scotty Moore's guitar plays a looping figure, Bill Black slaps the bass, and Elvis sings of a long train that has taken his woman and might bring her back. The song is a blues but the feel is ominous and propulsive, with the echo thickening everything. It builds to a lighter, triumphant final verse, as if the train is now bringing her home.","Junior Parker had recorded the song for Sun in 1953 as a slow, brooding blues. Presley's version was his fifth and last Sun single, released in 1955; by the end of that year Sam Phillips had sold his contract to RCA for about $35,000 to keep the label solvent. The record reached the national country charts, and Elvis was already touring the South as a sensation.","Moore's guitar figure was borrowed from another Parker record, Love My Baby, grafted onto this song. At the very end, listen closely and you can hear Elvis laugh, a moment of release left in the take. Critic Greil Marcus named his 1975 book on American music after this record, reading the train as a symbol of everything that carries American music forward and away.","Many critics consider it the best of the Sun sides, the one where the country and blues sources are most completely melted together. It stands at the end of Elvis's Sun period, and so has often been heard as a farewell to the small, strange world of Memphis before the fame machine. The debt to Junior Parker is now credited and discussed as fully as the performance itself.","Play Junior Parker's 1953 original, then this, and listen to how the tempo and the guitar transform a lament into a charge. Listen for the laugh at the end. Then hear Johnny Cash's train songs from the same studio for a sense of the Sun sound across artists."],"elvis-presley-heartbreak-hotel":["A slow, stark blues about loneliness, sung almost in a whisper and then a moan, with a walking bass and a guitar that stabs a chord at the end of each vocal line and then leaves silence. Floyd Cramer's piano tinkles in the background, and a bluesy guitar solo arrives halfway. The whole thing is drenched in echo, so the voice seems to come from the end of a long empty corridor. It is less than two and a half minutes of gloom made thrilling.","Elvis's first RCA recording, made in Nashville in January 1956 with his Sun trio plus Cramer and Chet Atkins. The song was by Mae Boren Axton and Tommy Durden, reportedly prompted by a newspaper story about a lonely man's suicide. RCA executives worried it sounded nothing like the Sun singles. Television appearances that spring pushed it to number one, and it became his first million-seller.","The echo was not added electronically in the usual way; the engineers reportedly used a hallway in the building as an echo chamber, which is why the voice sounds cavernous. Listen to how much of the record is silence: the band stops at the end of each line, and the tension comes from the gaps. It is, in effect, a torch song played as a blues.","Its success settled RCA's gamble and made Presley a national figure within weeks. In Britain it was a revelation: John Lennon, Keith Richards and others later remembered hearing it on the radio as a turning point in their lives. Some purists preferred the lighter Sun sound, but the record's darkness made rock and roll seem capable of real emotional weight.","Listen to the gaps: count how long the band stays silent after each line. Then compare it to That's All Right to hear how the same trio became something heavier in a bigger studio. Seek a mono release; stereo remixes blur the echo that makes it."],"elvis-presley-hound-dog":["A furious, fast twelve-bar blues that barely lasts two minutes and a quarter: drums like gunfire, Scotty Moore's guitar spraying two wild solos, the Jordanaires chanting behind, and Elvis snapping out insults at a worthless dog of a man. It hits hard from the first beat and never lets up. The drum fills between choruses, D.J. Fontana rattling around the kit like a machine gun, are the sound most people remember.","Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller wrote the song for Big Mama Thornton, whose growling 1953 version was a rhythm-and-blues number one. Elvis learned a comic rewrite from a Las Vegas lounge act, Freddie Bell and the Bellboys, and performed it on television in 1956 to outrage. He recorded it in New York in July 1956, working through dozens of takes; it was coupled with Don't Be Cruel and sold in millions.","The lyric Elvis sings is not quite Thornton's: the Bellboys had changed it into a song about an actual dog, with a line about rabbits that makes little sense next to her version, which is an adult woman telling off a freeloading man. Leiber reportedly disliked Elvis's take for exactly that. Listen, too, to the way the Jordanaires' clapping and chanting sits almost buried; it is a gospel quartet habit turned secular.","It was a huge double-sided hit and became a battleground: his hip-swinging television performances of it drew condemnation from critics and clergy, and Steve Allen had him sing it in a tuxedo to a basset hound. Since then the debate has shifted to Big Mama Thornton, whose original is now often preferred and whose small reward from the song has become a standard example of how the business treated Black women artists.","Hear Big Mama Thornton's 1953 original first, a slow, menacing blues with Johnny Otis's band, and then this. Notice the tempo, the drums and the change of meaning. Listen to the guitar solos for how raw and clumsy they are allowed to be; that roughness is part of the excitement."],"chuck-berry-maybellene":["A car chase in two and a half minutes: the singer in a V8 Ford chasing an unfaithful girlfriend in a Cadillac over the hills, reported like a sports commentary. The beat is a fast country two-step turned to rhythm and blues, Johnnie Johnson's piano rolls underneath, and Berry's guitar solo in the middle is jagged and bright. His diction is crisp and precise; every word lands, which was new in a genre used to shouted blues.","Berry came to Chicago in May 1955 carrying a tape; Muddy Waters sent him to Leonard Chess, who was more interested in his country-flavoured song than his blues. Maybellene was adapted from Ida Red, a western-swing tune made famous by Bob Wills. Disc jockey Alan Freed was given a co-writing credit, a common payola arrangement, and plugged it heavily. It went to number one on the rhythm-and-blues chart and into the pop top ten.","Under the rock and roll surface is a hillbilly fiddle tune: listen to the song's structure and the chorus melody and you can hear Ida Red. That country root is why the record sounded so strange on Black radio and so familiar on white. Freed's name in the credits, and that of another associate, took a share of Berry's royalties for decades.","It was an instant national hit and established Chess as a rock and roll label as well as a blues one. Historians now often put it forward as the true beginning of rock and roll, because it fused country and rhythm and blues so naturally in the hands of a Black writer. Berry's fight to recover his credit became an early chapter in rock's long argument over authorship and exploitation.","Listen to it as a story: the chase, the rain, the engine, the catch. Then find Bob Wills's Ida Red to hear the tune's country origin. Follow with Johnny B. Goode to hear how much Berry's guitar sound developed in three years."],"chuck-berry-roll-over-beethoven":["A manifesto disguised as a party song. It opens with Berry's ringing guitar introduction, then his voice asks a disc jockey for rhythm and blues and tells the great classical composers to make room. The band is a tight quartet with Johnnie Johnson's piano filling every gap. The tone is joking rather than angry, but the message is clear: a new music has arrived, and it has a beat.","Released by Chess in 1956, when rock and roll was being attacked by parents, preachers and the press. Berry reportedly wrote it with his sister Lucy in mind, who monopolised the family piano with classical pieces when he wanted to play rhythm and blues. Its claim that the old music should step aside matched a moment when radio was fragmenting and teenagers were buying records for themselves.","Berry names Tchaikovsky as well as Beethoven, telling the older composer to pass the news along, which gives the song its comic twist: it is written as a message to be delivered. Listen for the way the introduction's double-string guitar figure recurs as the solo; the whole song is built on that riff. The Beatles' 1963 cover, sung by George Harrison, turned it into a British invasion standard.","Critics have always read it as a statement of rock and roll's cultural challenge to high art. Its tone is now heard as playful rather than hostile; Berry admired classical music, and the song mocks snobbery more than Beethoven. It entered the rock canon through its covers, especially the Beatles', and remained a concert staple for Berry for sixty years.","Hear it next to the opening of Beethoven's Fifth, the music Berry is joking about. Then listen to the Beatles' version for how a British band hears it seven years later. Pay attention to the drive of Johnson's piano, which does as much as the guitar to push the song forward."],"chuck-berry-school-days":["A day at an American high school told in sharp, crowded verses: the classroom, the teachers, the lunchroom, then the release of the final bell and a run down to the jukebox. Each sung line is answered by a guitar phrase, voice and instrument trading like two people in conversation. The final verse turns from school to a hymn of praise for rock and roll itself. It is loose, fast and witty, a miniature film of teenage life.","Released in 1957, the year Berry toured the country on package shows and appeared in rock and roll films. It became one of his biggest hits, topping the rhythm-and-blues chart. Berry was then thirty, far older than the teenagers he wrote about; he observed them from outside, with a novelist's eye for detail. American high schools were newly desegregating, and the song's schoolroom is pointedly undescribed by race.","The call-and-response between voice and guitar comes from the blues and from gospel, where a preacher and congregation answer each other. Berry turns it into comedy: the guitar seems to comment on each line. Listen for the closing tribute to rock and roll, which became a slogan in its own right and gave its title to a 1987 documentary about Berry.","It has always been praised as one of the most observant of Berry's teenage songs, a model for the narrative songwriting of the Beach Boys and the Beatles. Some critics find the formula wearing thin by this point in his run, but the lyric's detail and the guitar dialogue keep it fresh, and its closing lines are among the most quoted in rock.","Listen to it as a conversation: hear each vocal line, then wait for the guitar's answer. Follow the day from morning to the jukebox. Then hear Sweet Little Sixteen and Johnny B. Goode, recorded soon after, to follow Berry's portrait gallery of American youth."],"chuck-berry-johnny-b-goode":["The most famous guitar introduction in rock: a burst of ringing double-string phrases over a stop, then the band falls in. The song tells of a poor country boy near New Orleans who can play the guitar like ringing a bell and dreams of seeing his name in lights. The chorus is a crowd urging him on. It is the rock and roll success story in two minutes and forty seconds, and the guitar tells it as much as the words.","Recorded in Chicago for Chess and released in 1958, at the height of Berry's run. It is largely autobiographical, and named partly after Goode Avenue in St. Louis, where Berry grew up, and partly after his pianist Johnnie Johnson. Berry later said he changed the hero's race in the lyric so that radio would play it, a small act of self-censorship that speaks to its moment.","The opening guitar figure is not wholly original: it closely follows the introduction Carl Hogan played on Louis Jordan's 1946 jump-blues record Ain't That Just Like a Woman, which Berry acknowledged. The debt runs straight from jump blues to rock. In 1977 NASA placed the song on the Voyager Golden Record, making it the only rock and roll recording to have left the solar system.","It was a pop and rhythm-and-blues hit and has since been named repeatedly by critics and musicians as the defining rock and roll record. Virtually every guitarist of the sixties learned the introduction. Debate now focuses less on its greatness than on Berry's own paradox: a Black artist writing the archetypal American rock success story, then watching others become far richer playing it.","Listen to the introduction a few times on its own, then hear Louis Jordan's Ain't That Just Like a Woman to catch the source. Notice how the stop-time verses build tension. Then hear it played by others, from the Beatles' circle to Jimi Hendrix, to see how it became a common language."],"little-richard-tutti-frutti":["It begins with a shouted string of nonsense syllables, a drummer's rhythm put into the mouth, and then the band slams in. Little Richard pounds the piano in hard, even eighth notes, the saxophones honk, Earl Palmer's drums drive the beat, and every verse is punctuated by whoops and a high falsetto shriek. The words are about two girls, but the words hardly matter. It lasts two and a half minutes and feels like being shoved.","Recorded in September 1955 at Cosimo Matassa's J&M studio in New Orleans, with producer Bumps Blackwell and a band of the city's best session players. The session was going badly until, in a break, Richard played a bawdy song from his club act; Blackwell had songwriter Dorothy LaBostrie clean up the words on the spot. Released by Specialty, it became a rhythm-and-blues hit, and Pat Boone's tame cover sold even more.","The famous opening shout was, by Richard's own account, a vocal imitation of a drum pattern he had been singing to himself while washing dishes in Macon. Listen to the piano: those relentless, even eighth notes replaced the lilting shuffle of older rhythm and blues and pointed toward the straight rock beat of the sixties. The original club lyric was sexually explicit; LaBostrie's version kept only the tune and the excitement.","It was received as a novelty by many adults and as a shock by others; its gender-blurring, gospel-shouting performer frightened the respectable. Since then its standing has only risen: it was among the first records added to the Library of Congress's National Recording Registry in its category, and critics often call it the moment rock and roll found its voice. The Boone cover is now remembered mostly as a cautionary tale.","Play it loud and follow the piano's even hammer rather than the voice. Then play Pat Boone's version for the contrast. After that, hear a gospel shouter like Marion Williams, whom Richard admired, to understand where the whoops come from."],"little-richard-long-tall-sally":["A breakneck twelve-bar about a gossipy uncle, a woman who knows how to have a good time, and a lot of ducking back in the alley to avoid being seen. It is even faster and harder than Tutti Frutti: the piano hammers, the saxophones blast short riffs, Lee Allen tears off a tenor solo, and Richard's voice leaps into falsetto at the end of lines. It lasts barely two minutes.","Recorded for Specialty in New Orleans and released in 1956. Bumps Blackwell later said they deliberately made the words tumble out too fast for a white crooner to copy, after Pat Boone's cover of Tutti Frutti had outsold the original. Boone covered it anyway. The song was credited to Richard, Blackwell and Enotris Johnson, a woman who reportedly brought in the idea for a song about a relative.","Listen to the pace of the lyric: whole phrases are compressed into a breath, a deliberate barrier to imitation. The song's hero is a man hiding from his wife, a comic scenario borrowed from vaudeville and blues. Richard's falsetto whoop at the end of lines was studied by Paul McCartney, whose version with the Beatles in 1964 copies it almost exactly.","It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart and made the pop top ten, outselling Boone's version this time. It has since become one of the most covered rock and roll songs; the Beatles made it the closer of many shows. Critics regard it as Richard at his most concentrated, the pure distilled essence of his Specialty sound.","Try to follow the words at speed; then give up and ride the piano. Compare McCartney's version with the Beatles to hear how closely a young Liverpool singer copied a Georgia shouter. Then hear the rest of the Specialty sides collected on Here's Little Richard."],"little-richard-heres-little-richard":["Twelve tracks, most under two and a half minutes, collecting the singles that made Little Richard's name: Tutti Frutti, Long Tall Sally, Rip It Up, Ready Teddy, Slippin' and Slidin', Jenny, Jenny and others. Heard together they have astonishing consistency: the same pounding piano, honking saxes and New Orleans rhythm section, and the voice switching between shouts, groans and a high gospel whoop. A few slower blues give breathing space, but the pace rarely drops.","Released by Specialty in 1957, Richard's debut album was assembled from recordings made in New Orleans and Los Angeles over the previous two years. In the fifties the album was an afterthought to singles in rock and roll; this one was a collection of hits for fans who wanted them in one place. Within months of its release, Richard announced on an Australian tour that he was quitting rock and roll for the ministry.","Listen to the backing band, much of it the same New Orleans players who backed Fats Domino and others at Cosimo Matassa's studio. Drummer Earl Palmer's playing across these tracks is a document of the shift from shuffle rhythm to the straight rock beat. Richard's gospel upbringing is everywhere: the moans and whoops come from Pentecostal worship, turned to profane use he later regretted.","It became one of the most celebrated albums of fifties rock, regularly placed in critics' lists of the greatest ever made. Its reputation rests on the songs rather than any album concept. Richard himself, torn between church and stage, spent much of his life alternately disowning and reclaiming these records. Today he is honoured as a founder who received too little credit and pay in his prime.","Play it straight through and let the sameness become a force. Listen for the moments where the saxophone solo takes over and then Richard's voice crashes back. Pair it with Fats Domino's hits, cut in the same studio, to hear how different a singer can make the same band sound."],"fats-domino-the-fat-man":["A rolling New Orleans boogie: Domino's left hand walks the bass while his right hammers repeated chords, the band riffs behind him, and he sings a cheerful self-portrait about his size and his nickname. In the middle he hums a wordless solo that imitates a muted trumpet. It is relaxed and bouncy rather than wild. The piano is the whole engine; everything else fills in around it.","Recorded at Cosimo Matassa's J&M studio in New Orleans in December 1949 and released by Imperial early in 1950. It was the first product of Domino's partnership with trumpeter and bandleader Dave Bartholomew, and it reportedly sold a million copies over the following years. It was adapted from Junker Blues, a song about addiction associated with Champion Jack Dupree, rewritten as a harmless piece of self-mockery.","Listen to the wordless vocal break: Domino imitates a trumpet with his voice, a trick from the New Orleans street tradition. The drumming, possibly by Earl Palmer, uses a heavy backbeat that would become standard in rock and roll. The song's model, Junker Blues, had a darker subject, mentioned only in passing here, which Bartholomew replaced with a comic portrait.","Its commercial success made Imperial and Domino and has been cited as one of several claimants to the first rock and roll record, years before the term was current. Critics praise it now as the purest example of New Orleans rhythm and blues and the gentle, piano-led strain of early rock that ran parallel to the harder Memphis and Chicago sounds.","Follow the left hand of the piano: it is a boogie-woogie bass line that the whole band follows. Then listen for the humming solo. Hear Jelly Roll Morton or James P. Johnson for the long New Orleans and stride piano lineage behind it, and then Domino's later hits for the smoother sound."],"fats-domino-aint-that-a-shame":["A lament about a lover who has left, sung with such warmth and good humour that it hardly sounds sad. It opens with Domino's voice alone on the title line, then the band falls in with a steady backbeat. Domino's piano triplets roll under the whole song, and a tenor saxophone takes a brief solo. At under two and a half minutes, it is a model of economy: two verses, a chorus and a lilt.","Written by Domino and Dave Bartholomew, it was released by Imperial in 1955, just as rock and roll was being noticed by white audiences. It became Domino's first big crossover pop hit. Pat Boone's cover went to number one on the pop chart, a notorious example of white artists covering Black records; Domino's original also sold strongly, and his audience kept widening.","John Lennon said this was the first song he learned to play, on a banjo his mother taught him. Listen for the piano triplets under a straight backbeat; that combination is a New Orleans hallmark. Boone reportedly wanted to correct the grammar of the title, which tells you what the cover versions were designed to do.","It was received as an irresistible pop record and has remained a standard, covered by Cheap Trick and many others. The story of the Boone cover has come to stand for a whole period of the business. Domino's own version has long since outlasted Boone's in critical esteem, and his easygoing style is increasingly recognised as a crucial ingredient of early rock.","Listen for the triplets in the piano: count three in each beat. Hear Pat Boone's cover once, then return to Domino to feel the difference. Follow with Blueberry Hill to hear his style applied to an older pop standard."],"fats-domino-blueberry-hill":["A 1940 Tin Pan Alley song about a lost romance, remade as a slow, rolling New Orleans ballad. The piano opens with rippling triplets, Domino sings in his warm, relaxed voice with its Creole vowels, and the band sways behind him. There is no hurry and no display, just a steady rock and a melody sung with affection. It is the sound of rock and roll at its most tender.","The song had been recorded by Glenn Miller's band and sung by Gene Autry in a 1941 film. Domino recorded it in Los Angeles in 1956, and Imperial released it that autumn; it became his biggest pop hit. He was touring constantly and appearing in rock and roll films, a star of the new music who was also, with his standards, reaching older listeners.","Accounts say Domino could not get through a complete take without fumbling the words, and the released record was edited together from several; listen for the join if you can find it. His Creole accent colours the vowels in a way that makes a New York song sound like Louisiana. The piano triplets under a slow beat became a New Orleans signature later heard in soul ballads.","It reached number two on the pop chart and number one in rhythm and blues and is his most enduring record. Critics have praised the way it bridges generations, a prewar pop song made new. It showed that rock and roll could absorb the older repertoire rather than simply opposing it, a lesson Elvis and the Beatles also took.","Listen to the piano introduction and the slow, swaying beat, then hear Glenn Miller's version from 1940 to see what Domino changed. Follow with Otis Redding's ballads to hear the New Orleans triplet feel travel into soul."],"bo-diddley-bo-diddley":["One chord and one rhythm for two and a half minutes. Bo Diddley's guitar, thick with tremolo, pounds out a syncopated pattern that everyone knows even if they do not know its name, while Jerome Green's maracas and a tom-tom drum lock into it. The lyric is a string of playground boasts about a mockingbird and a diamond ring. There is no chord change, no melody to speak of; the rhythm is the song.","Recorded in Chicago in 1955 and released on Checker, a Chess subsidiary, with I'm a Man on the flip side. It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart. Later that year Diddley appeared on Ed Sullivan's television show; asked to sing a popular country hit, he played his own song instead, and was reportedly banned from the programme. The Chess label was simultaneously launching Chuck Berry.","The beat, often written out as a shave-and-a-haircut pattern, has roots in the African-American hambone body-slapping tradition and in older Caribbean and African rhythms. Listen to the guitar: Diddley built his own tremolo effect, and the rhythm guitar is used as a drum. The lyric is adapted from the children's lullaby Hush Little Baby, recast as a swaggering boast.","It was a hit and a puzzle for critics who expected songs to change chords. Over time its influence became obvious: the Bo Diddley beat turned up in Buddy Holly, the Rolling Stones, the Who, the Strangeloves and countless others. Diddley felt, with justice, that his contribution was undervalued and underpaid, a complaint now widely accepted.","Clap along and find the pattern. Then listen to how the maracas and the guitar interlock. Follow with Buddy Holly's Not Fade Away and the Rolling Stones' version of it to hear the beat travel; then hear I'm a Man, the B-side, which Muddy Waters answered with his own record."],"bo-diddley-who-do-you-love":["A menacing boast in a voodoo register: Bo Diddley lists the uncanny credentials of a suitor who wears a snake for a necktie and lives in a house made of rattlesnake hide, and asks the woman whom she will choose. The band, with a sharp lead guitar from Jody Williams, drives hard over a single chord. It is faster and harder than his first record, with a raw edge that feels close to punk.","Released on Checker in 1956. Diddley's Chicago recordings drew on the same Southern migrant culture as Muddy Waters's; the hoodoo imagery of charms and tricks was familiar from blues lyrics going back decades. Rock and roll was at its commercial peak, and Chess used Diddley's distinctive style to compete with the flood of rockabilly.","Listen for Jody Williams's lead guitar, whose stinging lines influenced later Chicago players. The imagery is not random: snake skins, graveyards and skulls are hoodoo conjurer images from Southern folk magic, the same world as Muddy Waters's mojo songs. The song's second life came through covers: Ronnie Hawkins's 1963 version with a young band later known as The Band, and a sprawling psychedelic version by Quicksilver Messenger Service.","It was not a large hit on release but became one of Diddley's most covered songs, and critics praise it as one of the rawest records of the fifties. Its lyric's supernatural boasting links it to both blues tradition and to the swagger of later rock and hip-hop. Its reputation grew steadily through the sixties revival.","Listen for the lead guitar's stabs between vocal lines, then hear Ronnie Hawkins's version for how a Canadian bar band pushed it harder still. Pair with Muddy Waters's mojo songs to hear the shared folk-magic world."],"carl-perkins-blue-suede-shoes":["A stop-time count-off, a twelve-bar blues, and a cheerful warning: do whatever you like to me, but leave my shoes alone. Perkins's guitar solos are clean, bright country licks with blues bends, the bass slaps, and the whole band sits in Sam Phillips's slap-back echo. It is loose and friendly, the sound of a Tennessee dance band discovering rhythm and blues.","Perkins wrote it in late 1955, reportedly after watching a dancer at a gig fuss over his shoes; Johnny Cash is also credited with suggesting the idea. Sun released it on New Year's Day 1956, and it became the label's first million-seller, crossing to the pop, country and rhythm-and-blues charts. In March, on the way to a television appearance, Perkins was badly injured in a car crash, and Elvis's cover took over.","Listen to the stop-time opening: the band hits a chord, falls silent, and Perkins counts in the verse. That arrangement is borrowed from blues and jump records. His guitar style, country fingerpicking with bluesy string bends, became the model for George Harrison, who learned many of Perkins's songs. The Beatles recorded three of them.","It was a landmark hit for Sun and made Perkins briefly a star. Critics have always regarded it as a quintessential rockabilly record and Perkins as one of the most gifted of the Sun artists; his bad luck with the car crash became part of the story. Elvis's version is better known, but many prefer Perkins's, lighter and more swinging.","Hear Perkins's original first, then Elvis's 1956 RCA version, and notice how Elvis pushes the tempo and the drama. Listen to the guitar solos for the mix of country and blues. Follow with Johnny Cash's Sun records and Jerry Lee Lewis's to hear the whole label."],"jerry-lee-lewis-whole-lotta-shakin-goin-on":["A pounding boogie where Jerry Lee Lewis sings, talks, growls and hammers the piano with a left hand like a pile-driver. The band is spare: guitar and drums. Halfway through, he drops to a near-whisper, coaxing the listener to shake, before crashing back. The piano runs up and down the whole keyboard in glissandos. It sounds improvised because largely it was.","The song had been recorded by Big Maybelle in 1955. Lewis played it in clubs and reportedly recorded it at Sun in a single take in 1957. Radio stations hesitated over its suggestiveness, but a television appearance on Steve Allen's show that summer, where Lewis kicked away his piano stool, sent it to the top of the country and rhythm-and-blues charts and near the top of pop.","Listen to the spoken section: Lewis drops the tempo of his delivery while the band keeps going, a preacher's technique from his Pentecostal upbringing, the same one his cousin Jimmy Swaggart used in the pulpit. His left hand plays a boogie bass line with an almost mechanical steadiness while the right hand plays anything it likes.","It was denounced by some as indecent and loved by teenagers; its commercial success made Lewis a star. Critics now rank it among the most exciting of all rock and roll records and treat Lewis's piano style as a founding element of rock keyboard playing. Its reputation survived the scandal that wrecked his career a year later.","Follow the left hand all the way through: it never stops. Then listen for the spoken breakdown and the moment the full sound returns. Hear Big Maybelle's original, then Little Richard's piano records to compare two different kinds of wildness."],"jerry-lee-lewis-great-balls-of-fire":["Under two minutes of piano fury: Lewis hammers chords with both hands, sings about love that shakes his nerves and rattles his brain, and sweeps his hand down the keyboard in glissandos. The drums keep a strict, hard backbeat. It is tight and fast, with none of the loose talking of his previous hit. The sound of the record is almost all piano and voice.","Written by Otis Blackwell and Jack Hammer and recorded at Sun in late 1957. Lewis sang it in the film Jamboree, and it became his biggest international hit. Months later, on a British tour, the press discovered he had married his thirteen-year-old cousin, Myra Gale Brown; the tour collapsed and his American pop career with it.","At the session, a tape survives of Lewis arguing with Sam Phillips about whether rock and roll was the devil's music, Lewis insisting it was sinful and he could not save souls singing it. Knowing this, listen to the title phrase, which comes from a biblical image of fire, and hear a Pentecostal preacher's son singing against his own conscience.","It was a huge hit and is his signature song; Lewis's fall made it a symbol of rock and roll's first collapse, when its stars died, were jailed, drafted or disgraced. Critics treat it as the peak of his style. The 1989 biopic of the same title revived interest; historians now dwell on the recorded argument as a key document of rock's religious tensions.","Listen for the glissandos and the hard, double-handed chords. Then find the recorded argument between Lewis and Phillips to hear the struggle behind the song. Follow with Little Richard's records, and his decision to quit for the church, for the same tension in another life."],"buddy-holly-thatll-be-the-day":["A mid-tempo rock song about a lover's threat to leave, answered with defiant disbelief. It opens with a sliding guitar phrase, then Holly's voice, full of hiccups and swoops, carries a catchy verse and chorus, with backing voices answering. Jerry Allison's drums are busy and inventive. At two and a quarter minutes, it is compact and perfectly balanced, a small-group rock record where every part is a hook.","The title came from a line John Wayne repeats in the 1956 film The Searchers. Holly had recorded the song in Nashville for Decca in 1956, but the label shelved it. In February 1957 he recut it at Norman Petty's studio in Clovis, New Mexico, with the Crickets; Brunswick released it under the band's name, and it went to number one in the United States and Britain.","The first recording the teenage Quarrymen, John Lennon's band before the Beatles, ever made in 1958 was a version of this song. Listen to the guitar solo, which is less a display than a restatement of the melody, and to Allison's drums, far more varied than most rock drumming of the period. Clovis gave them unlimited studio time, which let them polish.","It made Holly and the Crickets stars overnight and established a model of the self-contained band that wrote and played its own material. Critics now see it as the first of a small number of records that shaped the sixties British bands more than any others. Holly's early death, less than two years later, sealed its place in rock's memory.","Listen for the hiccup in Holly's voice and the busy drums. Then hear the Quarrymen's 1958 home recording to understand what young British musicians took from it. Follow with Peggy Sue and Not Fade Away to hear how Holly's small band kept reinventing itself."],"buddy-holly-peggy-sue":["A love song built on a drumbeat: Jerry Allison plays continuous rolling paradiddles on the snare, a hypnotic rattle under the whole song. Holly sings the girl's name over and over, stretching and hiccuping it, and his guitar strums bright chords, then slams out a solo of loud, ringing strums rather than single notes. It is simple, obsessive and joyful.","Recorded at Norman Petty's Clovis studio in 1957 and released under Holly's own name on Coral, while the Crickets' records appeared on Brunswick, a label arrangement that let the same band have two careers. The song was originally called Cindy Lou; Allison asked Holly to rename it after his girlfriend, Peggy Sue Gerron. It became a top-three pop hit.","Allison reportedly practised the drum pattern in a separate room from the band so the echo and balance would work. Listen to the guitar solo: at the moment it begins, the tone suddenly becomes louder and brighter, reportedly because a bandmate reached over and flipped the pickup switch on Holly's guitar mid-performance. Petty's echo on the drums gives them their hypnotic rattle.","It was a major hit and has remained one of Holly's best-loved records. Critics praise its restraint and ingenuity: few notes, big effect. Its drum part is one of the most celebrated in rock and roll, and Holly's name-song style influenced generations of songwriters. A sequel, Peggy Sue Got Married, followed after his death.","Listen to the drums alone for the first time through. Then listen for the switch in guitar tone at the solo. Follow with the Beatles' early records to hear how much they learned about strummed-chord solos and close drum work."],"buddy-holly-not-fade-away":["A love song built on the Bo Diddley beat, with percussion reportedly played on a cardboard box. Holly's voice promises that his love will not fade, the Crickets answer in chorus, and the guitar strums the syncopated pattern with almost nothing else. The spareness is the point: it is a rhythm, a vow and some handclaps, and it grips.","Recorded at Clovis in 1957 and released as the B-side of the Crickets' Oh, Boy!. The song was credited to Charles Hardin, Holly's given names, and producer Norman Petty, who took a share of many Holly compositions. Holly borrowed the rhythm from Bo Diddley, whose records were being played across the country, and gave it a lighter, Texan feel.","Listen to the percussion: drummer Jerry Allison is said to have played on a cardboard box rather than a drum kit, which gives the dry, papery thump. The song's afterlife is large: the Rolling Stones made it their first big British hit in 1964, turning it into a harmonica stomp, and the Grateful Dead played it hundreds of times in concert.","As a B-side it was not a hit in 1957; its importance came through its covers, which made it a rock standard. Critics now hear it as a bridge between Bo Diddley's Chicago rhythm and the British bands of the next decade, and as one of Holly's most direct and unadorned records.","Clap the Bo Diddley rhythm along with it, then hear Bo Diddley's own record. Then listen to the Rolling Stones' 1964 version and hear how the same song became something rougher in London."],"everly-brothers-bye-bye-love":["Two voices in close harmony, singing a breakup song so bright and brisk that the sadness barely registers. It opens with a strummed acoustic guitar figure, then Don and Phil Everly sing together, one voice a third above the other, bending together on each word. The rhythm is a country shuffle with a rock and roll backbeat. The sound you remember is the harmony: two brothers who sound like one voice split in two.","Written by Felice and Boudleaux Bryant, a Nashville husband-and-wife team, the song had reportedly been turned down by dozens of acts before the Everlys recorded it for Cadence in 1957. It went to number two on the pop chart and topped the country chart. The brothers had grown up singing on their family's radio show in Iowa and Tennessee.","Listen to the opening guitar: Don Everly used an open tuning and a strum pattern he learned from Bo Diddley's records, a rhythm-and-blues trick hidden under a country sound. The close harmony descends from the Appalachian brother duets, the Louvin Brothers and the Delmore Brothers, who themselves came from gospel and country traditions.","It was a huge crossover hit and established the Everlys as stars. Critics recognise their harmony as the source of the Beatles', Simon and Garfunkel's and the Byrds' vocal sound; Paul McCartney and Art Garfunkel both named them as models. The song remains a standard example of how country tradition entered rock and roll.","Listen to the harmony alone: try to follow one voice and then the other. Then hear the Carter Family's close harmonies to catch the country source, and the Beatles' Please Please Me for how the Everly sound was reused."],"everly-brothers-all-i-have-to-do-is-dream":["A slow, dreamy ballad in which the two voices sing about a love that exists only in sleep. Chet Atkins's electric guitar shimmers with tremolo, a single ringing chord pulsing in the background, and the Everlys' harmony is at its most intimate. The song moves slowly and gently, with a rising bridge. It is the sound of late-fifties teenage longing, delicate rather than wild.","Written by Boudleaux Bryant and recorded in Nashville in 1958 for Cadence, with some of the city's best session musicians. It became one of the few records to top the pop, country and rhythm-and-blues charts at once, and was a number one in Britain. It was part of the softening of rock and roll in 1958, as its wildest stars faded.","Listen to the guitar: Chet Atkins's tremolo figure is the opening sound and the recurring glue, a single chord shimmering like water. The brothers' harmony is so close that on some syllables the two voices seem to merge into one tone, a quality that close-harmony singers call blend. The subject, dreaming of love rather than having it, links to a long line of pop night songs.","It was an enormous hit and one of the most successful records of the Everlys' career. Critics praise it as the finest example of their harmony and of the Nashville studio sound of the late fifties. It has been covered many times, and its dreamy mood influenced the Beach Boys and later pop balladry.","Listen for the tremolo guitar in the background, then focus on how the two voices merge. Compare with Bye Bye Love for the contrast of mood. Then hear Simon and Garfunkel's close harmonies to trace the influence."],"wanda-jackson-lets-have-a-party":["A frantic party song, barely two minutes long, in which Wanda Jackson's raspy, growling voice cuts through a hard-driving rockabilly band with pounding piano and slashing guitar. The lyric is a simple invitation to dance and celebrate. What you hear is the voice: rough, snarling and joyful, a female counterpart to Little Richard and Elvis, with none of the politeness expected of women singers in 1958.","The song was written by Jessie Mae Robinson and first sung by Elvis Presley in his 1957 film Loving You. Jackson recorded it for Capitol in 1958, and it appeared on her album; in 1960 a disc jockey began playing the album track, and Capitol released it as a single, making it her first pop hit. She had toured with Elvis, who encouraged her to sing rock and roll.","Listen to the way Jackson growls on certain words, a vocal rasp she developed to match the male rockabilly singers; it came from her country training, where she had been a yodelling honky-tonk singer. The record is also a reminder that the rockabilly label Capitol found in Gene Vincent and in Jackson was run by the same producer, Ken Nelson.","It was a modest pop hit in 1960 but gave Jackson her enduring reputation as the queen of rockabilly. Critics and punk musicians rediscovered her in later decades; she was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 2009, and Jack White produced an album for her in 2011. Her importance as a pioneer woman in rock is now secure.","Listen to Elvis's version in Loving You first, then Jackson's, and notice how much harder she sings. Follow with her Fujiyama Mama and then with Little Richard, whose wildness she matched."],"gene-vincent-be-bop-a-lula":["A breathy, echo-soaked rockabilly song in which Gene Vincent almost whispers the nonsense title and a loving description of his girl, then yelps. The Blue Caps back him with a slapping double bass, crisp drums and Cliff Gallup's lead guitar, which bursts out in two hot solos. The echo makes Vincent's voice seem to float, and the contrast between hush and scream is the song's excitement.","Recorded in Nashville in May 1956 for Capitol, whose producer Ken Nelson hoped Vincent would rival Elvis; the song was co-credited to his manager. It became a top-ten hit. Vincent had a badly injured leg from a motorcycle accident and wore a brace. His career faded in the United States, but in Britain and France he remained a star into the sixties.","Listen to Cliff Gallup's guitar solos: fast, clean and full of unexpected jazz-influenced runs, they inspired later guitarists including Jeff Beck, who recorded an album of Gallup's parts. Also listen for drummer Dickie Harrell's scream behind the vocal, a wild moment in a controlled record. The echo on Vincent's voice was part of the record's appeal.","It was a hit and remains Vincent's signature. Critics praise Gallup's playing and the record's moody sound; Vincent is often seen as rock and roll's first outsider, a model for the leather-clad rebel image that British rockers and punks took up. John Lennon was a fan.","Listen for the contrast between the whisper and the yelp, then focus on Gallup's guitar. Then hear Eddie Cochran, Vincent's friend and touring partner, and Jeff Beck's tribute to Gallup's playing."],"eddie-cochran-summertime-blues":["A teenage complaint in two minutes: a boy wants to go out, but his boss, his parents and even his congressman stand in the way. Cochran strums heavy, chunky chords on the guitar, handclaps punctuate the rhythm, and between vocal lines a deep voice plays the authority figure who says no. It is funny, angry and catchy, with a stop-and-start structure that throws the complaints into relief.","Written by Cochran and his manager Jerry Capehart and recorded in Hollywood in 1958 for Liberty. Cochran was a studio experimenter who overdubbed his own guitar and bass parts. The song became a top-ten hit. Less than two years later, on a British tour with Gene Vincent in April 1960, Cochran was killed in a taxi crash in Wiltshire, aged 21.","Listen to the deep voice answering each complaint: it is Cochran himself, lowering his voice to play the boss, parent and politician. The guitar sound, heavy strummed chords rather than a lead line, was unusual and anticipated the power chords of sixties rock. The Who's thunderous version on Live at Leeds and Blue Cheer's distorted one turned it into a hard-rock anthem.","It was received as a novelty hit and has become one of the most enduring songs of teenage protest in rock and roll. Critics see Cochran's early death as a great loss to rock; his studio methods and chord-based guitar playing link the fifties to the sixties. It has been covered by the Who, Blue Cheer, the Clash and many others.","Listen for the deep answering voice and the handclaps. Then hear the Who's version to feel how the song's anger became volume. Pair it with Gershwin's Summertime for a completely different idea of the season."],"the-beatles-rubber-soul":["Fourteen songs in thirty-six minutes, and the first Beatles album with no filler covers. The sound is acoustic guitars, close three-part harmony and a new density of keyboards: a harmonium under 'We Can Work It Out', a fuzz bass on 'Think for Yourself', Harrison's sitar threading through 'Norwegian Wood'. Ringo Starr's drums sit back in the mix. You will know it by the elastic vocal blend of 'Nowhere Man', three voices in bare octaves before the band enters.","Recorded at EMI's Abbey Road studios in about four weeks in the autumn of 1965, squeezed between tours and a Christmas deadline. Bob Dylan had shown the group that pop lyrics could carry doubt and dislike, and the Byrds had shown that folk chords could ring through electric twelve-strings. Lennon and McCartney were twenty-five and twenty-three, newly wealthy, newly bored of screaming. The title is a joke about plastic soul, a phrase they picked up in America.","The guitar solo in 'In My Life' is George Martin playing a piano part at half speed and doubling the tape speed on playback, which is why it sounds like a harpsichord no harpsichord could manage. Listen also to 'Norwegian Wood': the sitar doubles the vocal line exactly rather than improvising around it, a Western tune wearing an Indian instrument rather than an Indian raga.","Reviewers at the time heard a good pop record; the reappraisal came quickly. Brian Wilson has said repeatedly that an album with no weak track sent him to make Pet Sounds, and that record sent the Beatles back to the studio for Revolver. The American Capitol version, which drops songs and adds two from Help!, is a different and folkier record; the British running order is the one to hear.","Start with 'Norwegian Wood' and 'In My Life', then play side two straight through for the shift from the sly to the tender. The 2009 stereo remaster is clean, but the 1965 mono mix is the one the band approved and the vocals sit harder in it. Follow with Pet Sounds and hear the argument continue."],"the-beatles-revolver":["Fourteen songs in thirty-five minutes, each a different sound world. 'Eleanor Rigby' is scored for eight string players with no Beatles instruments at all; 'Love You To' opens on solo sitar and tabla; 'Got to Get You into My Life' brings in brass; 'Tomorrow Never Knows' rides tape loops over one chord. Harrison gets three songs, his largest share. The gesture you will recognise is the hard, dry, compressed guitar of 'Taxman'.","Recorded at Abbey Road across the spring of 1966 with George Martin producing and the twenty-year-old Geoff Emerick newly promoted to engineer. The four-track machines had not changed but the working method had: bouncing tracks, close-miking drums against EMI rules, running tape backwards, and the artificial double-tracking device built in-house by Ken Townsend. None of it could be reproduced on stage, and the band stopped touring that August.","The count-in at the start of 'Taxman' is a decoy, dubbed over the real one, and the stabbing guitar solo is McCartney's rather than Harrison's. 'I'm Only Sleeping' contains a guitar part written out backwards, played, and then reversed on tape. On 'Yellow Submarine' the party noises were made in the studio with the band and friends blowing through straws into buckets of water.","It arrived without much noise, since the Beatles were still a screaming phenomenon and few critics listened this closely, and it has risen steadily since, often above Sgt. Pepper in polls, on the grounds that the invention serves the songs rather than the concept. The American release again dropped three Lennon songs. Debate now centres on the 2022 remix, which unpicks the four-track masters with software and gives the drums a weight vinyl never had.","Play it in sequence: the album is built as a shuffle of extremes, and the jolt from 'Here, There and Everywhere' to 'Yellow Submarine' is deliberate. Notice how little reverb there is; everything sits close and dry. Then hear the two records it argues with, Pet Sounds a few months earlier and Sgt. Pepper a year later."],"the-beatles-tomorrow-never-knows":["Three minutes over a single unchanging chord, with Ringo Starr's drum figure heavy and looped and a tambura drone underneath. Five tape loops, the best known a speeded-up guitar that sounds like a seagull, were run from machines around the building and faded in live at the mixing desk. Lennon's voice arrives ordinary, then switches halfway to a thin, wavering tone fed through a rotating Leslie speaker cabinet.","Recorded at Abbey Road in April 1966, the first song taped for Revolver. Lennon's words are adapted from The Psychedelic Experience, Timothy Leary's rewriting of the Tibetan Book of the Dead. McCartney had been going to Stockhausen and Berio events in London and making loops at home on a domestic Brenell recorder with the erase head disabled, so the tape saturated on itself into something metallic.","The loops are not synchronised to the beat and never repeat in the same alignment, so the texture keeps shifting while not a single note changes. Lennon asked to sound like a chanting lama on a mountaintop; the Leslie cabinet, normally used for organs, was the studio's answer. The backwards guitar cutting in at the halfway point is reportedly the 'Taxman' solo reversed.","It closed Revolver where most listeners expected a rocker, and reviewers in 1966 largely passed over it. Its standing grew with everything it made possible: the loop as a compositional unit in pop, the drone as a harmonic bed, the voice treated as raw material. Producers still chase Starr's drum sound. Some hear a tourist's version of Indian music in the drone, and that criticism has weight.","Listen once for the drums alone, since the pattern never varies and that is what lets everything else float, then again on headphones for the loops entering from the sides. Play Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge afterwards for where the technique came from, and the Velvet Underground for where the drone went."],"the-beatles-strawberry-fields-forever":["Four minutes that begin with a Mellotron flute setting, a keyboard playing tapes of real flutes, and end with a false fade, a return, and a swirl of backwards cymbal, cellos and trumpets. Lennon sings slightly behind the beat throughout. There is no conventional chorus: the title line opens the song and then recedes, and the harmony keeps sliding a step lower than the ear expects.","Written in Almería in the autumn of 1966 while Lennon was filming How I Won the War, his first long stretch away from the band. Strawberry Field was a Salvation Army children's home near his aunt's house in Liverpool. Recorded at Abbey Road over five weeks, an unheard-of span then, and issued in February 1967 as a double A-side with 'Penny Lane'. The single stopped at number two in Britain.","Two completely different recordings are spliced about a minute in: a lighter band take and a later scored version, in different keys and at different tempos. Lennon wanted the first half of one and the second of the other, so Martin and Emerick slowed the faster tape and sped the slower until the pitches met. The join is audible once you know where to listen.","Some reviewers found it wilfully obscure; it has since become the record people reach for when dating the moment pop left its own conventions. The mono and stereo mixes differ in the ending. The 1996 Anthology release of the early takes, voice and guitar alone, began a long argument about whether the orchestral version improved the song or buried it.","Play the Anthology demo first and then the finished single: the distance between them is the story. Listen for the splice about sixty seconds in, and stay past the fade, where the coda returns with a tape-manipulated voice muttering under the cellos. Pair it with 'Good Vibrations', built the same modular way a few months earlier."],"the-beatles-sgt-pepper":["Thirteen songs presented as a show by a fictional band, several running together without gaps. The palette is wide: a forty-piece orchestra on two tracks, an Indian ensemble on 'Within You Without You', harmonium and harpsichord, a fairground collage on 'Being for the Benefit of Mr Kite!'. It ends with 'A Day in the Life', where an orchestral crescendo rises twice out of nothing and a piano chord is left to decay for forty seconds.","Recorded at Abbey Road between December 1966 and April 1967 over some seven hundred studio hours, with the band no longer touring and EMI's four-track machines linked in pairs to stretch capacity. Peter Blake and Jann Haworth's cover, a crowd of cardboard heroes, cost more than most albums did to make. It appeared in Britain in late May 1967 and became the sound of that summer.","The orchestral crescendo was written as an instruction rather than as notes: each player was to move from the lowest note on their instrument to the highest over twenty-four bars, in their own time. The British pressing ends with a fifteen-kilohertz tone audible mainly to dogs, then a loop of babble cut into the run-out groove that repeats until the needle is lifted. Stockhausen stands in the back row of the cover.","It was received as proof that pop could be art, won four Grammys, and then spent decades being blamed for exactly that. Critics now often prefer Revolver, and the charge that Pepper is an album of production rather than songs is old and not baseless. The 2017 remix, made from the session tapes, restores punch that four-track bouncing had flattened and has moved opinion again.","Hear it in one sitting, in mono if you can: the band sat through the mono mix and left the stereo to the engineers. Listen for the crowd noise stitching the first two tracks together, and for the two crescendos in 'A Day in the Life', the second of which stops dead. Then play The Velvet Underground & Nico, released ten weeks earlier, for the opposite view of 1967."],"the-beatles-abbey-road":["Seventeen tracks, the last eight run together as a medley of unfinished songs on side two. A Moog synthesizer appears on a Beatles record for the first time, in the white-noise wind of 'Here Comes the Sun' and the wordless triple-tracked harmonies of 'Because'. 'I Want You (She's So Heavy)' grinds through a repeated figure for eight minutes and stops mid-bar. The recognisable sound is the clean, warm bass under close vocal thirds.","Recorded in the summer of 1969 after the fraught Get Back sessions, on EMI's new eight-track desk, with George Martin back at the band's request: they asked to make one more record the old way. Lennon and McCartney were pulling apart, Harrison brought 'Something' and 'Here Comes the Sun', Starr sang 'Octopus's Garden'. It was the last album the four of them recorded together.","The chords of 'Because' came from Lennon hearing Ono play the opening of Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata and asking her to play it backwards. 'Her Majesty', twenty-three seconds long, was cut from the medley and ordered destroyed; the engineer spooled it onto the end of the tape out of habit, and the accident became the first hidden track. The closing guitar solos alternate McCartney, Harrison and Lennon in rotation.","Reviews were mixed, and some heard the medley as an admission that nobody could finish a song. Sales said otherwise, and the side-two sequence became a model for how to end an album. Argument continues over whether the medley is a suite or a scrapbook, and the sleeve produced an absurd rumour that McCartney had died. Harrison's two songs are now usually named the best on it.","Start at 'You Never Give Me Your Money' and let side two run to the end without stopping. Listen for the Moog in 'Because' and for the three guitar voices trading in 'The End'. If you want the sound as it was, the 1969 stereo mix is the only one the band made; the 2019 remix is brighter and pulls the bass forward."],"the-rolling-stones-satisfaction":["Three and three-quarter minutes built on a three-note guitar figure played through a fuzzbox, doubled by Bill Wyman's bass and pushed along by Charlie Watts's flat, unhurried backbeat. Jagger sings in a drawl that has become a shout by the last verse, complaining about advertising, hotel rooms and being told what to want. There is no solo. The riff simply returns, louder, with tambourine.","Richards woke in a Florida motel in May 1965, taped the riff on a cassette machine and fell asleep again; the rest of the tape is snoring. The band recorded it at Chess in Chicago and then at RCA in Hollywood during their third American tour, at the height of the marketing of British groups as an invasion. Andrew Loog Oldham, their manager and producer, released it in America first.","The fuzz tone was meant to be temporary. Richards heard the line as a horn riff in the Stax manner and thought the Gibson Maestro pedal a placeholder for brass; he was outvoted and the guide sound went out to the world. Otis Redding's cover, cut months later, restores the horns and shows what Richards had in mind. Watts plays almost nothing, and the space is what makes it move.","It was the Stones' first American number one and the record that turned them from a cover band into writers. Critics then heard insolence; critics now hear the first pop hit about consumer dissatisfaction rather than romantic disappointment. The fuzz tone was copied within months and was a cliché by 1967, which makes hearing its original strangeness an act of effort.","Play the mono single rather than an album version; the riff is more brutal when everything sits in one place. Count how few drum fills there are. Then play Otis Redding's version with horns for the record Richards wanted, and 'You Really Got Me' for the British riff that came first."],"the-rolling-stones-beggars-banquet":["Ten songs, mostly acoustic guitars recorded hot and close. 'Sympathy for the Devil' builds from congas and piano to a chorus of whoops, with Keith Richards's solo cutting across it; 'Street Fighting Man' has no electric guitar on it at all; 'Salt of the Earth' and 'Prodigal Son' go back to gospel and country blues. Brian Jones, fading out of the band, plays sitar and slide. The sound is dry, rhythmic and unglamorous.","Recorded at Olympic Studios in London in 1968 with Jimmy Miller producing, after the psychedelic misfire of Their Satanic Majesties Request. Paris and Chicago were in the streets, and 'Street Fighting Man' was written around the Grosvenor Square demonstration. Decca refused the original lavatory-wall sleeve and held the record up for months, so it finally appeared in a plain white invitation card instead.","'Street Fighting Man' was recorded onto a mono cassette machine, deliberately overloaded, and then transferred to tape: the distortion is a cheap microphone clipping. Charlie Watts plays a tiny 1930s practice kit that folded into a case. Under the chorus sit Dave Mason's shehnai and Brian Jones's sitar, which is why the track sounds both thin and enormous at once.","It restored their reputation at a stroke and began the run of four albums usually counted as their best. 'Sympathy for the Devil' drew accusations of devil worship that the band enjoyed and then regretted after Altamont. Godard filmed the sessions for One Plus One, which preserves the song assembling itself. Critics still argue whether the turn to roots music was conviction or a shrewd reading of the room.","Start with 'Street Fighting Man' and listen for the absence of electric guitar. Then 'Sympathy for the Devil', counting the instruments as they enter across the first two minutes. Watch Godard's footage afterwards. The mono mix, restored on recent reissues, has more punch than the stereo, where the acoustic guitars spread thin."],"the-rolling-stones-let-it-bleed":["Nine songs that open and close on apocalypse. 'Gimme Shelter' starts with a shivering tremolo guitar and builds to Merry Clayton's duet with Jagger; 'Love in Vain' is a Robert Johnson song rearranged with country steel guitar; 'Midnight Rambler' changes tempo three times; 'You Can't Always Get What You Want' opens with a choir and ends with French horn, gospel piano and a long fade.","Recorded in London and Los Angeles through 1968 and 1969 with Jimmy Miller, across the death of Brian Jones and the arrival of Mick Taylor, who plays on two tracks. It was released on the eve of the Altamont festival, where the band played while a man was killed in front of the stage. The decade's violence had arrived in the audience rather than on the news.","The choir on 'You Can't Always Get What You Want' is the London Bach Choir, singing an arrangement by Jack Nitzsche, and its members later objected to the company the album's other lyrics kept. Al Kooper plays both the French horn introduction and the piano. On 'Gimme Shelter', Clayton's voice cracks on a high line about murder, and Jagger can be heard reacting to it.","Reviews were strong and have stayed strong, and the record is usually paired with Beggars Banquet as the band's peak. Altamont bound it to a narrative about the sixties ending, which some critics find lazy and others unavoidable. Merry Clayton went uncredited on the sleeve for years; the 2013 documentary Twenty Feet from Stardom finally made her name known to general listeners.","Begin with 'Gimme Shelter' and play it loud enough to hear the guitar shiver before the drums come in. Then 'You Can't Always Get What You Want' in full, for the distance travelled from choir to gospel piano. Compare it with Abbey Road, released two months earlier: two British bands closing the same decade in opposite moods."],"the-kinks-you-really-got-me":["Two and a quarter minutes, almost no chords beyond two shifted up and down the neck, and a guitar tone that sounds torn rather than distorted. Ray Davies sings in a strangled shout; the band stops dead before the solo, which Dave Davies plays in a scramble of wrong-sounding notes. The song rises a whole step twice to keep the pressure climbing. There is no bridge and no let-up anywhere.","Recorded in London in the summer of 1964 after the band's first two singles failed and Pye was threatening to drop them. Davies has said the song began slower and bluesier before they raised the tempo. Shel Talmy produced, and the Kinks were teenagers from Muswell Hill playing ballrooms. A persistent story that the session guitarist Jimmy Page played the solo has been denied by everyone involved, Page included.","The sound came from vandalism. Dave Davies slit the speaker cone of a small green Elpico amplifier with a razor blade, then fed that amplifier into a larger Vox, producing a tearing distortion that no pedal then on sale could make. It is not overdrive; it is a broken loudspeaker recorded honestly, three years before fuzz became something you could buy.","It went to number one in Britain and gave the band a career. Its influence is claimed by hard rock and punk alike: two-chord riffing, and distortion as a structural element rather than a fault. Musicologists point out that the song is modal rather than blues-based, which is part of why it does not sound American. Later Davies songs are subtler, but nothing he wrote travelled further.","Play it beside 'Satisfaction' and 'My Generation' for three different answers to the same question about how loud a pop single can be. Listen for the two key changes, each a lurch upward. Then go straight to 'Waterloo Sunset' to see how far the same writer moved in three years."],"the-kinks-waterloo-sunset":["Three minutes in a slow, rocking sway, with a guitar figure that keeps falling and a chorus of wordless harmonies washed in tape echo. Ray Davies half-speaks verses about a narrator who watches the river from his window while Terry and Julie cross the bridge below. Two guitars, bass, drums and voices, almost no ornament, and a bass line that walks underneath the whole thing like traffic.","Recorded at Pye's studio in London in the spring of 1967, when the Kinks were banned from touring America and stuck at home writing about it. Davies was twenty-two and had recently been ill; the song began, he has said, with a memory of the Thames seen from a hospital window. The names Terry and Julie belong to nobody in particular, though the public decided they were Terence Stamp and Julie Christie.","The chorus harmonies were sung and then drenched in tape echo rather than stacked thickly, which is why they sound like a crowd heard at a distance. Pete Quaife's bass line hardly repeats a full bar the same way. And the narrator never goes outside: the entire lyric is set at a window, an English pop song about declining to join in.","It reached number two in Britain, and its standing has only grown; British writers regularly name it the finest single of the decade. It has been read as elegy, as agoraphobia, and as a farewell to a London about to be demolished. Davies has resisted every reading. Its failure in America at the time is part of why the Kinks became a cult rather than a stadium band.","Listen with headphones for the echo on the harmonies and for the way the guitars fall away in the last chorus. Then play Vaughan Williams's The Lark Ascending and notice the same English habit of watching something from a distance and saying nothing about yourself. Any decent mono reissue will do; the stereo mix scatters the voices."],"the-kinks-village-green-preservation-society":["Fifteen short songs in thirty-nine minutes, no hit single and no attempt at one. Harpsichord, Mellotron and acoustic guitar carry most of it and the drums stay polite. Songs list what is being lost, from draught beer to steam trains to the village green itself, while others look at photograph albums, old friends turned strangers, and a cat. The signature is the harpsichord under Ray Davies's flat, affectionate delivery.","Made through 1968 while the band was still barred from America, and released in November of that year into total indifference: it sold in the low thousands. Davies had assembled much of it as a solo project before it became a Kinks album. Britain was demolishing its Victorian centres and raising tower blocks, so the record's nostalgia is specific and recent rather than picturesque.","The Mellotron stands in for an orchestra the band could not afford, and on 'Wicked Annabella' it is turned to something closer to horror. The album exists in twelve-track and fifteen-track versions because Davies revised it after a European pressing had already gone out. 'Big Sky' is the one song where the nostalgia breaks: a god who looks down on all this and does nothing.","It sold almost nothing and is now the Kinks album most often called their best, a reversal that took twenty years and a generation of British songwriters saying so in public. Some hear conservatism; others hear a very early warning about heritage turned into a commodity. The 1998 and 2018 reissues, with their alternative running orders, turned the argument into a hobby.","Play it in the afternoon, whole, and let the songs stay small. Listen for the harpsichord and the Mellotron doing the work of strings. Start with 'Do You Remember Walter?' if you want the heart of it. Then play the Velvet Underground's White Light/White Heat from the same year and marvel at what else 1968 sounded like."],"the-who-my-generation":["Three minutes at a hectic clip. Roger Daltrey stutters the words over a riff Pete Townshend plays in blunt block chords, and John Entwistle takes a bass solo, twice, high and clattering, one of the first on a pop single. The song shifts up a key three times, each lift tightening the screw, and ends in feedback with Keith Moon's drums collapsing over themselves.","Recorded in London in October 1965 with Shel Talmy, who had produced the Kinks. Townshend was twenty and writing for an audience of mods in parkas. Entwistle kept breaking strings and the solo took several attempts on a Danelectro bass, whose strings could not then be bought separately. The stutter has been explained as amphetamine speech, as blues phrasing and as a joke; Townshend has offered all three.","The key changes are the trick: each arrives without preparation and each pushes Daltrey's voice nearer to breaking. Listen to Moon, who plays almost no backbeat at all and treats the kit as a lead instrument, filling every gap the riff leaves. The ending is not a fade but the band dismantling the arrangement in real time, which is exactly what they did on stage.","It reached number two in Britain and became a generational slogan, endlessly quoted and endlessly misread as a wish to die young. Townshend has spent decades pointing out the irony of the line while continuing to sing it into his seventies. Historians put it at the head of the line that runs to punk; the Sex Pistols' contempt for hippies is its direct heir.","Play the single mix loud and follow Moon rather than the words. Then listen again to the bass solo on its own terms, as a sound nobody had put on a hit record. For the same year's rage in different accents, compare it with 'Satisfaction' and 'You Really Got Me'."],"the-who-sell-out":["A pop album disguised as a pirate radio broadcast, its songs interrupted by jingles, deodorant advertisements and station idents the band recorded themselves. 'I Can See for Miles' is the hard centre, one chord held under a rising vocal for most of its length. 'Rael' at the end is a six-minute mini-opera. The production is bright and treble-heavy, all guitars and cymbals, with harmonies that owe more to California than to Shepherd's Bush.","Made in 1967 as the British government closed the offshore pirate stations that had helped make the Who famous; Radio London went silent that August. Townshend was writing longer forms and losing interest in singles. The sleeve, with Daltrey sitting in a bath of baked beans, was a pop-art joke about selling out, staged some years before the phrase turned sour.","The tune inside 'Rael' comes back two years later as 'Sparks' and the 'Underture' in Tommy: the mini-opera was a rehearsal for the real one. The jingles are new recordings by the band rather than licensed broadcast tape, which is why they sit in the same sound world as the songs and the seams are so hard to find.","It sold modestly and was treated as a novelty. Its standing now rests on the songs and on the idea, borrowed by everyone from Frank Zappa to the skit-laden hip-hop album, that a record can imitate a medium. The stereo and mono mixes differ substantially. 'I Can See for Miles' underperformed in Britain, which Townshend took badly enough to mention for the rest of his life.","Let it run without skipping, since the jingles are the point and they time the record. Listen to 'I Can See for Miles' for the single chord held under the verse and for Moon's rolling entrance. Then play Tommy and catch the 'Rael' theme coming back."],"the-who-tommy":["A double album of seventy-five minutes telling of a boy struck deaf, dumb and blind who becomes a pinball champion and then a messiah. The band plays it almost alone, with guitars, bass, drums, a little piano and French horn, so the opera is carried by recurring themes rather than by orchestration. 'Overture', 'Sparks' and 'Underture' state and restate the material; 'Pinball Wizard' is the song everyone knows.","Written by Townshend across 1968 and recorded at IBC in London over six months between tours. He had become a follower of Meher Baba, and the story's silence, sight and release come from that. The Who were broke and the record had to work. They played much of it live from 1969, including at Woodstock and in opera houses, which the press found either brave or ridiculous.","The 'Overture' is not decoration: nearly every theme in the piece appears there first, and 'Underture' spends ten minutes developing the 'Sparks' figure without a word sung. Entwistle plays the French horn parts himself. The narrative has holes the band patched later, since the 1975 film and the 1993 stage version both rewrite the ending, but the musical argument holds together.","Reviewers split between those who took the form seriously and those who found the story tasteless; the songs about abuse, written by Entwistle, still draw criticism. It sold hugely and made the Who a stadium band. Whether it is an opera is a question that has never been settled and probably cannot be, since it has no recitative and no singers other than the band.","Play the 'Overture' and then 'Underture' back to back to hear the material worked rather than merely sung. Otherwise start at side one and accept the plot as a frame. Compare it with a real opera about an outsider destroyed by a crowd, Britten's Peter Grimes, and the differences will teach you more than the similarities."],"the-beach-boys-pet-sounds":["Thirteen songs in thirty-six minutes, hardly any of it played by the Beach Boys: Brian Wilson wrote the arrangements, booked Los Angeles session players, then brought the group in to sing. You hear bass harmonica, harpsichord, accordion, bicycle bells, sleigh bells and an electro-theremin. The vocal stacks are the signature, four and five parts moving in contrary motion under Carl Wilson's lead on 'God Only Knows'.","Recorded in Hollywood in the first months of 1966 while the rest of the band toured Japan. Wilson had stopped touring in 1964 after a breakdown on a plane; Tony Asher, an advertising copywriter, wrote most of the lyrics with him. Capitol wanted surf songs and put out a hits compilation months later to compensate. Wilson has said the record began as an answer to Rubber Soul.","Wilson mixed in mono because he is deaf in one ear, which is why no stereo mix existed until 1997. Listen to the basses: there are often two, one of them holding a note that clashes with the chord above it, and that friction is what gives songs like 'You Still Believe in Me' their ache. The last sounds on the record are Wilson's dogs and a passing train.","It sold less than the group's surf records and Capitol treated it as a failure, while in Britain it was received as a landmark at once. McCartney has named 'God Only Knows' his favourite song and credited the album with pushing the Beatles towards Sgt. Pepper. The argument now is about Smile, the record Wilson abandoned next, and whether Pet Sounds is a peak or a first step never taken.","Start with 'God Only Knows' and 'Caroline, No', then hear the album whole for the two instrumentals that act as breathing space. Use the mono mix, since that is the record Wilson made. Afterwards play Rubber Soul, which set it off, and Sgt. Pepper, which answered it, and 1966 will make more sense."],"the-beach-boys-god-only-knows":["Two minutes fifty of shifting ground. A French horn and sleigh bells open it; the first chord is not the key it ends in, and the song never quite settles anywhere. Carl Wilson sings the lead in a plain, high voice. The bridge drops to a bare figure on harpsichord and plucked bass, and the last minute is a round, two vocal lines chasing each other while a third sings underneath, fading out unresolved.","Recorded in Hollywood in March 1966 for Pet Sounds with a studio full of session players. Asher and Wilson argued about the title, since using the word God in a pop lyric was thought likely to keep a record off American radio, and in the United States it was issued as the B-side of 'Wouldn't It Be Nice'. In Britain it was the A-side and a hit.","The song barely states its own key. The opening chord belongs elsewhere, the verses keep sliding, and the resolution you have been waiting for arrives only as the fade begins. Brian gave the lead to his brother Carl because he thought that voice had the innocence the words needed, and those words open by admitting the singer may be wrong about everything.","Musicians have led the praise: McCartney has called it the best song ever written and said it reduced him to tears, and 'Here, There and Everywhere' on Revolver is his answer to it. Critics point to the harmonic ambiguity, to the way the round refuses closure, and to the fact that a love song begins with the word 'may'. The 1997 stereo mix reveals the round more clearly; the mono keeps its mystery.","Listen to the last forty-five seconds and try to follow one voice in the round, then play it again and follow another. Notice that you cannot say what key it is in and that this does not feel like a problem. Then play 'Here, There and Everywhere' for the reply from Liverpool."],"the-beach-boys-good-vibrations":["Three minutes forty that behave like four songs joined end to end. Cellos play triplets under the verses, the chorus rides a wavering high tone from an electro-theremin, and a section in the middle drops to organ and a whispered vocal before the harmonies flood back. Nothing repeats the way pop songs repeat, and the ending is a new idea rather than a reprise of an old one.","Built over roughly seven months in 1966 across four Los Angeles studios, at a cost often reported near fifty thousand dollars, which would have been extraordinary for a single. Wilson recorded sections separately, chose the best of each and spliced them, using the studio the way a film editor uses a cutting room. Mike Love wrote the released lyric, replacing earlier words.","The instrument on the chorus is not a theremin. Paul Tanner, a trombonist, had built a slide-controlled oscillator that produced a similar tone but could be played accurately, and it is his machine you hear. The sections were recorded in different rooms with different players, so the joins are audible if you listen for the change in the air around the instruments.","It went to number one in America and Britain and is generally treated as the high point of the pop single as a constructed object. It also broke its maker, since the method it proved led straight into Smile, which collapsed. Critics disagree over whether the modular approach produced a whole or a brilliant assembly, and Wilson's later live performances, which run it straight through, sharpen the question.","Listen for the moment around two minutes when the record empties out to organ and a single voice, then rebuilds. Try to count the sections. Then play 'Strawberry Fields Forever', spliced from two takes a few months later, and hear two studios arriving at the same conclusion."],"the-byrds-mr-tambourine-man":["Two and a half minutes: one verse of Dylan's song and the chorus twice, with the rest cut away. What you remember is the introduction, Jim McGuinn's twelve-string Rickenbacker compressed until each note rings into the next, and then the three-part harmony, cool and level where Dylan was ragged. The bass runs a melodic line of its own and the drums keep a soft, insistent shuffle underneath.","Recorded in Los Angeles in January 1965. Columbia's producer did not trust the band, so apart from McGuinn's guitar the backing is played by session musicians, the Wrecking Crew; only the voices and the twelve-string are the Byrds. Dylan's own recording had not yet been issued when they cut it. McGuinn sang deliberately between Dylan's phrasing and Lennon's, aiming at both markets at once.","The compression is the record. The engineer rode the twelve-string hard so that decaying notes swell instead of dying, producing the shimmer that every jangling guitar record since has chased. McGuinn came to the instrument from banjo and cites Bach's counterpoint for the picking patterns he plays: listen to the introduction as three lines rather than as one melody.","It went to number one on both sides of the Atlantic and invented folk rock as a commercial category overnight, which Dylan noticed before he went electric at Newport that July. Purists complained about the cut verses and the session players. The trimming is now usually praised as ruthless editing rather than vandalism, and Dylan himself is reported to have approved.","Listen to the first eight seconds alone, then hear the harmony arrive. Compare Dylan's own recording, released on Bringing It All Back Home a few weeks before the single, for what was added and what was taken away. Then play 'Eight Miles High' to hear the same guitar turned loose."],"the-byrds-eight-miles-high":["Three and a half minutes that open with a climbing bass figure and then a guitar solo with nothing rock and roll about it: McGuinn plays fast modal runs on the twelve-string, doubling back on himself, closer to a saxophone than to a guitar. The harmonies are icy and the drums push hard. A second solo tears the song apart before a last verse that describes London as cold and unfriendly.","Recorded in Los Angeles in January 1966, after an earlier version cut at RCA was rejected by Columbia, and released that March. Gene Clark wrote the words about the flight and the city following the band's disastrous first British tour. McGuinn and David Crosby had been playing Coltrane and Ravi Shankar records on the tour bus. American radio banned it as a drug song, which the band denied and which killed its chart run.","The guitar solo is modelled on Coltrane's saxophone, as the band has always said, and specifically on his habit of hammering at one phrase until it breaks open. The Indian element is in the drone and the sliding vocal line rather than in any Indian instrument: there is no sitar on the record. The rejected RCA version, issued decades later, is faster and wilder.","It reached the American top twenty despite the ban and is usually called the first psychedelic hit, or the first raga rock record, depending on who is arguing. Gene Clark left the band within weeks of its release. Later critics have often preferred the RCA version. The question of whether rock could absorb jazz improvisation without sounding like a party trick starts here and is not settled.","Listen to the first solo twice, once for the notes and once for how little it resolves. Then play a Coltrane record from the early sixties and a Ravi Shankar raga, and the family resemblance will be obvious. The 1996 remaster brings the bass forward, which helps the climb at the start."],"the-byrds-sweetheart-of-the-rodeo":["Eleven songs, thirty-two minutes, of country music played straight: pedal steel, fiddle, banjo and close harmony, with Dylan songs, a Merle Haggard prison song, a Louvin Brothers hymn and a Woody Guthrie ballad among them. The twelve-string jangle is almost entirely absent. Gram Parsons sings lead on several tracks in a plain, cracked tenor and McGuinn sings the rest. Nothing on the record winks at the audience.","Recorded in Nashville and Los Angeles in 1968, months after Parsons joined the band, with Nashville session players filling out the sound. The Byrds played the Grand Ole Opry that March and were heckled for their hair. Parsons's previous label claimed his recordings, so several of his lead vocals were replaced by McGuinn before release; some were restored in 1997. He left the band before the album appeared.","The replaced vocals leave a strange doubleness: on 'The Christian Life' and 'You Don't Miss Your Water' you can hear either singer depending on the edition, and the songs change meaning with the voice. Parsons sings the Louvin Brothers' hymn without a trace of irony, which is the whole argument of the record. Listen for Lloyd Green's pedal steel answering every vocal line like a second singer.","It sold worse than any Byrds album before it and confused both audiences: rock listeners took it for a joke and country radio would not play long-haired Californians. It is now the origin point for country rock, the Eagles and everything filed as Americana. Critics still argue whether Parsons or McGuinn owns the record, and the 1997 reissue with Parsons's vocals restored is often treated as the real one.","Start with 'Hickory Wind' and 'The Christian Life', then set the 1997 reissue's Parsons vocals against the released ones. Listen for the pedal steel as a second voice rather than as decoration. Then hear Copland's Appalachian Spring for another city artist's idea of rural America, made twenty-four years earlier."],"the-velvet-underground-and-nico":["Eleven songs that swing between lullaby and assault. John Cale's electric viola drones under 'Venus in Furs' and 'Heroin'; Lou Reed plays guitars tuned so that every string sounds the same note; Maureen Tucker plays standing, mostly mallets on a bass drum laid on its side, with no cymbals at all. Nico sings three songs in a low German-accented monotone. 'European Son' ends in a crash of breaking glass.","Recorded mostly in New York in the spring of 1966 and then held back for a year while Verve worked out what to do with it. Andy Warhol was the producer in the sense of a patron: he paid, insisted on Nico, and drew the banana for the sleeve. The band played behind films and dancers as part of Warhol's Exploding Plastic Inevitable, which is where most of New York first heard them.","Cale had spent 1963 to 1965 in La Monte Young's Theatre of Eternal Music, holding single pitches for hours at a time, and the viola drones here are that discipline transplanted into a rock band. Reed's 'ostrich' tuning puts every string on one note, so a strummed chord is really a single pitch with many timbres. 'The Black Angels' Death Song' is that tuning at its harshest.","It sold poorly and was largely ignored. Brian Eno's remark that everyone who bought a copy started a band has become the standard shorthand and is roughly true of punk, post-punk and indie rock alike. The banana sleeve was withdrawn over a lawsuit concerning a photograph on the back. Warhol's role is still argued: some say he did nothing, others that doing nothing was the production.","Play 'Venus in Furs' first for the viola, then 'Sunday Morning' for the celesta and the sweetness, so you hear both halves of the band. The mono mix, restored in 2012, is closer to what they intended. Then play La Monte Young's Trio for Strings, and Sgt. Pepper for the same year's other idea of what a record could be."],"the-velvet-underground-white-light-white-heat":["Six tracks, forty minutes, recorded loud and distorted on purpose. 'The Gift' is a short story read deadpan by Cale in one channel while the band plays an instrumental in the other. 'Sister Ray' takes seventeen minutes over one chord, organ and guitar fighting for the same space, recorded in a single take with the engineer reportedly walking out. Nothing is polished and the meters stay in the red throughout.","Recorded in New York in September 1967 after Nico and Warhol had gone, with Tom Wilson producing and the band playing at stage volume in the studio. They had been touring relentlessly and had stopped caring whether anyone liked them. Reed and Cale were already in conflict about how far the noise should go, and Cale left the following year.","'Sister Ray' has no overdubs and no edits: what you hear is one performance in which Cale's overdriven organ gradually swamps Reed's guitar, and Reed plays louder rather than give way. The distortion is not an effect but amplifiers and a mixing desk pushed past their limits. In 'The Gift' you can listen to either channel alone and get a complete, quite different piece.","It sold worse than the first album and was dismissed as unlistenable. Its standing grew with every noise band that followed, from the Stooges to Sonic Youth and the whole 1980s American underground. Critics disagree about whether the harshness is a position or a tantrum. Reed said late in life that it was the truest record he ever made.","Try 'Sister Ray' with the volume higher than is comfortable and follow the organ rather than the vocals; the piece is a duel and you should pick a side. Hear 'The Gift' once in each channel. Then play the Kinks' Village Green from the same year for the widest gap two records of 1968 can manage."],"jimi-hendrix-are-you-experienced":["Eleven tracks in about forty minutes played by a trio: Hendrix's guitar, Noel Redding's bass and Mitch Mitchell's jazz-inflected drums. 'Purple Haze' opens on two notes at the widest interval the ear finds unstable; 'Foxy Lady' begins with a single shivering note of feedback; 'Third Stone from the Sun' floats over slowed-down speech; the title track runs on backwards drums and backwards guitar. Hendrix sings almost apologetically over playing that does not apologise.","Recorded in London between October 1966 and April 1967, weeks after Chas Chandler of the Animals brought the unknown Hendrix over from New York and built a band around him. Sessions moved between three studios as the budget allowed, with Eddie Kramer engineering some of them. British guitarists went to watch him and came away shaken. The American and British editions have different tracks and different covers.","The feedback is composed rather than stumbled into. Hendrix worked out where to stand in relation to his amplifier so that the note which howled was the note he wanted, which is why 'Foxy Lady' opens on a pitch and not a squeal. He played a right-handed Stratocaster upside down and restrung, so the controls fall under his hand and the springs behind the bridge respond differently.","It was a hit in Britain at once and made him famous in America after Monterey that June. The early framing as showman, spectacle and guitar-burner took years to shake and Hendrix resented it. Modern criticism concentrates on the writing and the studio work rather than the flames. The 1997 remasters supervised by Kramer and the Hendrix family are the standard editions; earlier reissues shuffled the running order.","Start with 'Third Stone from the Sun' rather than the hits, for the range of sound in a single track. Then 'Purple Haze' for the riff and 'Manic Depression' for Mitchell's drumming, which is nearer to Elvin Jones than to rock. Use the British track listing. Follow with Electric Ladyland to hear him take over the studio."],"jimi-hendrix-electric-ladyland":["A double album of seventy-five minutes that ranges from the fifteen-minute 'Voodoo Chile', a blues jam with organ and a second bassist, to the two-minute pop of 'Crosstown Traffic' and the underwater drift of '1983... (A Merman I Should Turn to Be)'. The cover of Dylan's 'All Along the Watchtower' carries four solos in four different tones. Hendrix produced it himself, and the guitars are layered rather than caught live.","Recorded in 1968 in London and mostly at the Record Plant in New York, with Hendrix booking endless hours and filling the room with guests: Steve Winwood, Jack Casady, Al Kooper and Buddy Miles all play on it. Chandler quit as producer over the waste of time and money. Redding, sidelined by Hendrix's habit of playing the bass himself, began drifting away, and the band broke up within a year.","The swirling sound on '1983' and 'House Burning Down' is phasing done by hand: two tape machines running the same material while an engineer's thumb slows one reel fractionally against the other. No pedal existed for it yet. 'All Along the Watchtower' was recorded not long after Dylan released it, and Dylan liked the arrangement enough to adopt it, playing the song Hendrix's way ever since.","It topped the American chart and drew mixed reviews, with complaints about length and self-indulgence that read oddly now. Hendrix disliked the British sleeve, which put naked women on the front without his knowledge. Assessments have risen steadily: the record is generally taken as the fullest picture of what he could do, and the argument has moved to what the unfinished fourth album might have been.","Play 'Voodoo Chile' and '1983' as a pair, one caught live in the room and one built in layers. Listen for the phasing as a physical motion rather than an effect. Compare 'All Along the Watchtower' with Dylan's own version, then go back to Are You Experienced and hear how far eighteen months took him."],"jimi-hendrix-star-spangled-banner-woodstock":["Four minutes of solo electric guitar, the band silent behind him, played on a white Stratocaster at the close of Woodstock on a Monday morning to a field that had mostly gone home. The melody is stated plainly and then attacked: at the line about the rockets' red glare the notes break into dive-bombs, sirens and something like falling ordnance, before the tune reassembles and runs straight into 'Purple Haze'.","Woodstock, 18 August 1969, delayed by rain and by the sheer scale of the crowd, so the headliner played after eight in the morning to perhaps thirty thousand people out of the hundreds of thousands who had come. American troops were in Vietnam in their greatest numbers, and the anthem was not a neutral thing to play. Hendrix, who had served briefly in the 101st Airborne, never called it protest.","He had been playing the anthem in concert for over a year, so this was not a spontaneous gesture and other recordings exist. Listen for the quotation of 'Taps', the American military funeral call, dropped in near the end. The feedback that stands for bombs is pitched rather than random: the howling notes belong to the melody he is taking apart.","Reactions split immediately between desecration and patriotism, and Hendrix told an interviewer he thought it was beautiful. It has since been used in documentaries as shorthand for an entire decade, which flattens it. Musicians point out what critics miss, the control required to make a guitar imitate artillery while keeping the tune intact. The Woodstock film made it famous; the full morning set is better than its reputation.","Watch it rather than only hearing it: the stillness of the playing against the violence of the sound is the point. Listen for 'Taps'. Then play Ives's Variations on America, written by a teenager in the 1890s, for an earlier American pulling a patriotic tune out of shape, and Penderecki's Threnody for what an orchestra can do with the same subject."],"cream-disraeli-gears":["Eleven tracks in thirty-three minutes from a trio who improvised at length on stage and were held to pop lengths in the studio. 'Sunshine of Your Love' rides a descending riff Jack Bruce wrote on bass, which Ginger Baker turns around by putting the accents in the wrong place; 'Strange Brew' and 'Outside Woman Blues' are blues repainted in psychedelic colours. Clapton's tone is thick with wah pedal and sustain.","Recorded at Atlantic Studios in New York in 1967 with Tom Dowd engineering and Felix Pappalardi producing, squeezed into a few days between American dates, with Martin Sharp's dayglo sleeve painted afterwards. Cream had formed the year before out of the British scene, Bruce and Baker from the Graham Bond Organisation and Clapton from John Mayall's Bluesbreakers, and were already being billed as a supergroup.","'Sunshine of Your Love' came out of a night at a Jimi Hendrix show: Bruce went home and wrote the riff, and Baker suggested the tom-heavy pattern that makes the beat feel displaced. Pete Brown, a poet rather than a musician, wrote most of the words, which is why the imagery is stranger than blues lyrics usually are. Clapton's solo on 'Strange Brew' copies Albert King's phrasing closely, in places note for note.","It sold heavily in America and made Cream stars there before Britain caught up. Their reputation has swung: worshipped in 1968, dismissed in the punk years as the beginning of the guitar solo as an affliction, and re-examined since as a rhythm-section story, since Bruce and Baker were jazz players who happened to be very loud. The long live jams have aged less well than these compressed songs.","Start with 'Sunshine of Your Love' and follow Baker's drums rather than the riff. Then hear 'Strange Brew' beside Albert King's playing, and Robert Johnson's recordings behind all of it. For the loud trio done differently in the same year, play Are You Experienced."],"the-doors-the-doors":["Eleven tracks with no bass player: Ray Manzarek covers the bass line with his left hand on a keyboard while his right plays the organ. 'Light My Fire' runs to seven minutes of modal soloing over two alternating chords; 'Alabama Song' is a Brecht and Weill cabaret number played on a marxophone; 'The End' closes the record with eleven minutes of drone, Spanish-tinged guitar and a spoken section on the Oedipus story.","Recorded in Los Angeles in August 1966 at Sunset Sound with Paul Rothchild producing and Bruce Botnick engineering, in about a week, because the band had played the songs nightly at the Whisky a Go Go and knew them cold. Elektra signed them partly on the recommendation of Arthur Lee of Love. The single edit of 'Light My Fire', with the solos removed, went to number one in America.","Manzarek had a classical piano training, and the circling introduction to 'Light My Fire' owes more to keyboard exercises than to rhythm and blues. The solos on the same song are modal, built on two chords that swap rather than resolve, which is a jazz procedure and not a pop one. 'Alabama Song' comes from Brecht and Weill's Mahagonny, and the band keeps its lurching cabaret meter intact.","It was a slow-burning success that exploded when the edited 'Light My Fire' reached number one, and the album stayed on the American chart for two years. Critics then and now divide over Morrison, poet or poseur, and 'The End' draws the sharpest opinions. Musicians tend to point past him to the trio, whose keyboard bass, jazz drumming and unshowy guitar make a texture nobody else had.","Hear 'Light My Fire' in full rather than the single edit, and follow the organ solo into the guitar solo as one argument. Then 'The End', preferably late and loud. Compare Weill's original 'Alabama Song', and play Love's Forever Changes for the other side of the same Los Angeles year."],"love-forever-changes":["Eleven songs in forty-three minutes for a rock band, a small string section and mariachi-style trumpet. The guitars are mostly acoustic and finger-picked, and the strings and brass answer the voices rather than swell behind them. Arthur Lee sings in a light, conversational tenor about a city he does not trust. Bryan MacLean's 'Alone Again Or' opens the album with a Spanish-flavoured figure and the trumpet solo that became the record's signature.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1967 for Elektra. The band was in poor shape, through heroin, exhaustion and a refusal to tour, and the label brought in session players for the first sessions, which shamed the group into playing the rest themselves. David Angel wrote the string and horn arrangements. Lee, a Black bandleader in a white rock scene, believed he would die young and made the record as a farewell.","The arrangements are not decoration: the trumpet in 'Alone Again Or' answers a line the singer does not finish, and the strings in 'You Set the Scene' change the harmony under a repeated vocal note. Lee could not read music and sang the parts to Angel, which is why the horn lines phrase like a voice. Listen for how much gunfire and paranoia there is in the words of a supposedly gentle record.","It sold poorly in America, did better in Britain, and the band fell apart within a year. Its standing has risen continuously since the 1980s, with British musicians among its loudest advocates. Critics read it as the dark twin of the Summer of Love, made a few miles from where the sunshine records were being cut. Lee performed it with orchestras in the years before his death in 2006.","Play 'Alone Again Or' and then 'You Set the Scene', the album's bookends, and then the whole thing in order on a quiet evening. Listen to the horns as characters rather than as backing. Then hear Pet Sounds for Californian orchestration of a sunnier kind, and Rodrigo's Concierto de Aranjuez for where that Spanish guitar colour comes from."],"the-zombies-odessey-and-oracle":["Twelve songs, thirty-five minutes, built on Rod Argent's keyboards and the band's close harmony rather than on guitars. A Mellotron supplies the choirs and strings. 'Care of Cell 44' is a cheerful pop song about a woman coming out of prison; 'A Rose for Emily' is voice and piano alone; 'Butcher's Tale' is a harmonium and a shaking voice about the Somme; 'Time of the Season' runs on a breath, a handclap and a bass hook.","Recorded at Abbey Road and Olympic in 1967 on the studio time the band had left, in rooms the Beatles had just vacated. The Zombies had already decided to split and made the record as a closing statement, paying for the stereo mix themselves when the label would not. It appeared in 1968 to no sales at all, and 'Time of the Season' became an American hit a year later, when there was no band left to tour it.","The Mellotron is there because an orchestra was unaffordable, and its slightly seasick pitch is what gives the record its light-headedness. Argent had sung in a cathedral choir as a boy, and the massed vocal writing follows choral habits rather than pop ones: parts cross, and the harmony is balanced rather than arranged as lead plus backing. The title is misspelled because the sleeve artist got it wrong and nobody corrected him.","It sold almost nothing and the band had gone by the time anyone noticed. Reissues, and a slow campaign by musicians who found it second-hand, turned it into one of the most admired British albums of its period, and the surviving members began performing it whole in 2008. Critics argue about whether its sweetness is naive or a deliberate mask over songs about prison, war and abandonment.","Start with 'Care of Cell 44' and read the words while you listen: the gap between tune and subject is the record's method. Then 'Butcher's Tale' for the opposite. Use the stereo mix the band paid for out of their own pockets. Pair it with the Kinks' Village Green, made the same year from the same English materials."],"pink-floyd-piper-at-the-gates-of-dawn":["Eleven tracks, most of them Syd Barrett's, that swing between two poles: short, childlike songs about gnomes, cats, scarecrows and a bicycle, and long instrumental voyages built from feedback, echo and Rick Wright's reedy Farfisa organ. Astronomy Domine opens it with a descending guitar riff and a voice reading out names of stars; Interstellar Overdrive, nearly ten minutes long, starts on a stabbing chromatic riff and then dissolves into drift and noise before the riff snaps back.","Recorded at Abbey Road in the spring of 1967, produced by Norman Smith, while the Beatles were making Sgt. Pepper down the corridor. Pink Floyd were the house band of London's underground, playing the UFO club to light shows. The title comes from the mystical chapter of Kenneth Grahame's The Wind in the Willows. Within a year Barrett, unravelling under LSD and pressure, had been eased out of the group he had named and led.","Listen closer: Chapter 24 sets words taken almost directly from hexagram 24 of the I Ching, 'Return' — the same Chinese oracle book John Cage had used to compose by chance sixteen years earlier. And the whimsical Bike does not end as a song at all: it tips into a collage of clockwork, bells and looped, sped-up voices that points straight at the tape experiments Floyd would build their later career on.","British reviewers took it as the best record of the London underground; the American release dropped three songs and reordered the rest, and sold modestly. Its reputation rose as Barrett's did, posthumously and mythically. The standing argument is whether it is a Barrett album with a band attached or the first Pink Floyd record; the long pieces suggest the band, the songs suggest a singular, fragile writer.","Start with Astronomy Domine for the space-rock sound, then play Bike to hear Barrett's other face, the Edwardian nursery rhyme gone slightly wrong. Give Interstellar Overdrive your full attention and notice how the band hold a single idea together with almost nothing. Seek the original British mono mix, which the band attended; the stereo was made more hastily."],"pink-floyd-echoes":["A twenty-three-minute piece that filled the whole second side of Meddle. It begins with a single high piano note sounding like sonar, pulsing through a rotating speaker. Two verses, sung in close harmony by David Gilmour and Rick Wright, describe an albatross, an underwater world and two strangers meeting. Then a long funk groove, a stretch of wind and eerie cries with almost no rhythm at all, and a slow rebuild to the final verse and a rising, spiralling choir of tape.","After Barrett's departure the band spent two years searching for a way to write together. Echoes was assembled from fragments the four had worked on separately, stitched into one shape over months in several London studios. It showed the group, now led jointly by Roger Waters's words and Gilmour's guitar, how to make long form feel inevitable. The next year they played it to the empty Roman amphitheatre in the film Live at Pompeii.","Listen closer: the screaming seabird cries in the middle section are Gilmour's guitar played through a wah-wah pedal plugged in the wrong way round. The ping at the start came from Wright's piano, amplified through a Leslie speaker. And the chromatic rising figure in the verses later reminded Waters so strongly of a song in Andrew Lloyd Webber's Phantom of the Opera that he said so publicly.","Fans treat it as the group's great long piece, the hinge between the psychedelic Floyd and the Floyd of Dark Side. Critics at the time were more cautious about Meddle as a whole. A persistent fan theory claims it syncs with the final 'Jupiter and Beyond' sequence of Kubrick's 2001, a film otherwise scored with Ligeti; the band have shrugged it off.","Listen in one sitting, loudly, with the lights down. Notice the single piano note at the start and wait for it to return near the end: that return is the whole architecture. In the long middle, stop waiting for a tune and listen to the wind. Then hear Ligeti's Atmosphères, the other kind of space music, for comparison."],"pink-floyd-dark-side-of-the-moon":["Ten tracks run together as one continuous suite about the pressures that drive people mad: time, money, travel, conflict, death. A heartbeat opens and closes it. In between come Time with its explosion of clocks, Money in a limping seven-beat metre over a loop of cash registers, a synthesizer sequence racing in stereo on On the Run, and The Great Gig in the Sky, where Clare Torry improvises a wordless vocal over Rick Wright's piano. Spoken fragments drift through the whole.","Recorded at Abbey Road in 1972 and early 1973 with engineer Alan Parsons, after the band had road-tested the suite in concert for a year. The studio now had sixteen tracks and the band had an EMS synthesizer, which made the machine textures possible. Roger Waters wrote all the lyrics, the first time one member took the words entirely. Hipgnosis's cover, a prism splitting light, carried no band name.","Listen closer: the voices were collected by Waters, who wrote questions on cards — 'When was the last time you were violent?' — and recorded answers from roadies, their wives, and Abbey Road's doorman Gerry O'Driscoll, whose line 'There is no dark side of the moon really' ends the record. Paul McCartney was asked too, but his answers were too guarded to use.","It sold on a scale almost nothing had, staying on the American album chart for years, and became the default test record for home stereos. That ubiquity made it a target for punk, which heard it as the smug sound of rock's aristocracy. Torry later sued for co-writing credit on her vocal and settled, receiving credit on later pressings. Its craft is now rarely disputed; its depth still is.","Hear it complete, in order, on good headphones, for the stereo movement of the synthesizers and footsteps. Start by following the heartbeat and noticing when it comes back. Then listen to The Great Gig in the Sky twice: once for Torry, once for the piano beneath her. Compare the tape collage with Varèse's Poème électronique to hear where the idea came from."],"pink-floyd-wish-you-were-here":["Five tracks, framed by the two halves of Shine On You Crazy Diamond, a slow, huge elegy that opens with glassy synthesizer chords and a four-note guitar call before the vocal ever arrives. Between them sit Welcome to the Machine, all throbbing synthesizer and mechanical doors; Have a Cigar, a sneer at record-industry executives sung by the folk singer Roy Harper; and the title song, a plain acoustic ballad that begins as if heard through a car radio.","Made at Abbey Road in 1975 by a band exhausted and alienated by the success of Dark Side, uncertain whether they wanted to keep going. Roger Waters shaped the record around absence: Syd Barrett's absence, and the band's own absence from their work. The theme turned eerily literal during the sessions, when an overweight man with a shaved head and eyebrows wandered into the studio; it took the band some time to recognise Barrett.","Listen closer: the radio being tuned at the start of the title song passes a fragment of Tchaikovsky's Fourth Symphony before settling on Gilmour's twelve-string. At the very end of that song, deep in the mix, is a violin: Stéphane Grappelli, the great jazz violinist, was in the building and recorded a part that was all but buried. The four-note guitar figure of Shine On became known as 'Syd's theme'.","Reviews at the time were mixed, some finding it slow and bitter after Dark Side. The band members themselves, especially Gilmour and Wright, later named it their favourite. It has become the Floyd album people are most likely to call their best, largely because of its grief, which feels more personal than the general anxieties of its predecessor.","Let Shine On take its time: the vocal arrives only after about eight minutes, and the wait is the point. Listen to the title song as a conversation between two guitars, one on the radio and one in the room. Then go back to Barrett's own songs on The Piper at the Gates of Dawn and hear who is being mourned."],"king-crimson-in-the-court-of-the-crimson-king":["Five long tracks. 21st Century Schizoid Man bursts in with a distorted vocal and a riff doubled by guitar and saxophone, then an instrumental middle section of breakneck unison runs. After it come the pastoral flute of I Talk to the Wind, the doom-laden Epitaph with its banks of Mellotron strings, the drifting Moonchild, and the title track, a medieval-sounding pageant for Mellotron choir. The contrast of brutality and ceremony is the record's signature.","Released on Island in October 1969, months after the band's first concerts, and the same summer they had played to a vast audience supporting the Rolling Stones in Hyde Park. Robert Fripp, Ian McDonald, Greg Lake and Michael Giles made the music; Peter Sinfield wrote the lyrics and ran the lights. They produced it themselves after abandoning sessions with an outside producer. The cover, a screaming face, was the only album cover its painter, Barry Godber, ever made.","Listen closer: the Mellotron, a tape-replay keyboard designed to imitate strings and choirs, wobbles and sags slightly in pitch, and that instability is part of the eerie sound. The band's concerts in 1969 closed with their own arrangement of Mars from Holst's The Planets; the grinding, ceremonial weight of Epitaph and the title track owes a clear debt to it.","It was received as a shock: a debut that seemed to arrive fully formed and outflank the psychedelic bands. It is regularly named the first true progressive rock album. The debate is over Moonchild, twelve minutes of which are quiet free improvisation that many listeners skip; defenders hear it as the band's link to the avant-garde, detractors as padding.","Play 21st Century Schizoid Man loud and listen for the moment the saxophone and guitar lock into the unison passage. Then play Epitaph and Holst's Mars side by side and hear how an English orchestral tradition passed into rock through the Mellotron. Seek the 2009 remix by Steven Wilson for clarity, or an early Island pressing for weight."],"king-crimson-larks-tongues-in-aspic":["Six tracks bookended by the two parts of the title piece. Part One opens with thumb piano and chimes so quiet you may turn the volume up, then crashes into violent, jagged riffs, before ending with solo violin. Between the parts: a delicate ballad, a slow violin-led lament, a sleazy mid-tempo song, and The Talking Drum, a hypnotic bass and percussion groove that swells for minutes before a shrieking violin tears it straight into Part Two's hammering, odd-metred riff.","Robert Fripp dissolved the first Crimson lineups and built a new one in 1972: John Wetton on bass and voice, Bill Bruford, who had just left Yes, on drums, David Cross on violin, and Jamie Muir, a free-improvising percussionist. Fripp talked of the influence of Bartók's string quartets and of Hendrix in equal measure. The group improvised heavily in concert and the album distilled that.","Listen closer: Muir's percussion includes chains, sheets of metal, bells, a thumb piano and toys; the bag of laughter at the very end of Easy Money is a novelty laughing bag. Muir left soon after the recording to live in a Buddhist monastery in Scotland, so this is the only studio record with him on it. Listen for the moment in Part One where the violin alone is left standing.","Reviews were puzzled and then admiring; it has come to be heard as the start of the band's second, heavier and more improvisational identity, a line that runs into later metal and math rock. Arguments centre on the production, which many feel undersells the band's live power, and on the scarcity of Muir's contributions in the final mix.","Turn it up for the quiet start of Part One, then brace. Listen to The Talking Drum as one long crescendo and try to notice exactly when it becomes unbearable. Afterwards hear the finale of Bartók's Fourth Quartet and the Sacrificial Dance from The Rite of Spring: the repeated, stamping, off-balance accents are the same family."],"king-crimson-red":["Five tracks from a band reduced to a trio of Robert Fripp, John Wetton and Bill Bruford, with guests. The title track is an instrumental of grinding, dissonant guitar chords over a heavy, lurching beat. Fallen Angel and One More Red Nightmare are songs; Providence is an edited free improvisation; and Starless, twelve minutes long, moves from a mournful ballad through a long, tense build on a repeating bass figure to a furious return of its opening melody on saxophone.","Recorded in London in the summer of 1974 after violinist David Cross left. By the time Red was released that autumn, Fripp had already disbanded King Crimson, declaring the rock band format exhausted. Old members returned as guests: Ian McDonald and Mel Collins on saxophones. Providence came from a concert in Providence, Rhode Island, on the band's last American tour.","Listen closer: the middle of Starless is built on the simplest material imaginable, a two-note guitar figure repeated while bass and drums grow heavier beneath it, and it is the refusal to change that creates the tension. The cover photographs the three musicians, and the back shows a needle pushed into the red of a meter: the album's title is also a warning about volume.","It sold modestly and appeared as the band vanished, so its reputation grew later. Kurt Cobain named it among his favourite records, and heavy and alternative musicians since have claimed its dense, dissonant weight as an ancestor. It is now often ranked with the debut as the group's peak, and argued over only for Providence, which some find an interruption.","Start with the title track to hear the three-man attack, then play Starless in full and let the long middle build do its work. Listen for the moment McDonald's saxophone brings back the opening theme. Compare the grinding chords with Bartók's Fifth Quartet to hear where Fripp's harmonic language came from."],"yes-fragile":["Nine tracks: four full-band pieces, including Roundabout and the long Heart of the Sunrise, alternating with five short showcases, one for each member. Roundabout opens with a harmonic chime on acoustic guitar played backwards, then Chris Squire's bass, loud, trebly and melodic, drives the whole thing. Heart of the Sunrise swings between a frantic riff in unison and near-silent vocal passages. Steve Howe's solo, Mood for a Day, is Spanish-flavoured classical guitar.","Made in London in 1971, the first Yes album with Rick Wakeman, who arrived from the Strawbs with an arsenal of keyboards including Mellotron, Minimoog and piano. Money was short and the band needed a record fast after The Yes Album; the solo spots were partly a way to fill it. It was also Roger Dean's first Yes cover, the fragile planet that began a long visual identity.","Listen closer: Wakeman's solo track, Cans and Brahms, is his arrangement for keyboards of the third movement of Brahms's Fourth Symphony, the jaunty Allegro giocoso, with synthesizer lines taking the woodwind parts. He was under contract to another label, which stopped him recording his own composition. He later called it one of the weakest things he did, which is why many listeners skip a small window into the classical debt of the whole band.","Roundabout, edited as a single, gave Yes an American hit and opened the US market for progressive rock. Critics admired the playing and complained about the lyrics, which Jon Anderson built for sound more than sense. The solo tracks remain the album's contested part; Heart of the Sunrise is usually cited as the real preview of what the band would do next.","Follow Squire's bass all the way through Roundabout: it is a lead instrument. Then play Cans and Brahms directly after the Allegro giocoso of Brahms's Fourth and hear the translation. End with Heart of the Sunrise and notice how much of its drama comes from the silences."],"yes-close-to-the-edge":["Three tracks. The title piece, almost nineteen minutes in four parts, opens with birdsong and running water, erupts into frantic, apparently chaotic group playing, then settles into a song. Its third part, I Get Up I Get Down, falls almost still for choral voices before a pipe organ crashes in and leads to a triumphant return. And You and I is a folk-like hymn in several sections; Siberian Khatru is an energetic, riff-driven closer.","Recorded in London in 1972 with engineer Eddy Offord, who assembled the long piece from many short takes spliced on tape. Jon Anderson drew on Hermann Hesse's novel Siddhartha, about a young man's spiritual search by a river. It was the last record with Bill Bruford, who left for King Crimson just before the tour. On that tour, Yes walked on to the finale of Stravinsky's Firebird played over the speakers.","Listen closer: the organ in I Get Up I Get Down is a real church organ, recorded by Rick Wakeman at St Giles-without-Cripplegate in the City of London and fitted into the multitrack afterwards. The opening chaos is also structured: the guitar keeps returning to fixed phrases that come back later as the song's melodies, so the storm contains the calm in seed form.","Many fans and critics consider it the band's peak and a definitive example of the long progressive suite. Detractors, including the early punk press, made it the emblem of rock's pretensions: side-long songs, mystical lyrics, keyboards in capes. The splice-by-splice construction also raised the question, still asked, of whether this is composition or studio editing.","Take the title track whole and in order. Notice how it returns at the end to birdsong and water: the record is a circle. When the organ enters, think of the Firebird finale that opened their concerts and hear how Yes borrowed its sense of a slow, luminous ending that becomes a blaze."],"genesis-selling-england-by-the-pound":["Eight tracks about an England being sold off: Arthurian knights among supermarket offers, gang warfare in Epping Forest, a lawnmower-loving teenager. Dancing with the Moonlit Knight opens with Peter Gabriel's voice alone, then gathers into a fast, intricate band workout. Firth of Fifth begins with a long solo piano introduction by Tony Banks and climaxes on Steve Hackett's sustained, singing guitar solo. The Cinema Show ends in a long keyboard passage in an uneven metre.","Recorded in London in the summer of 1973 by Gabriel, Banks, Mike Rutherford, Hackett and Phil Collins, several of whom had met as pupils at Charterhouse school. Britain was sliding into inflation, strikes and the loss of manufacturing; the title was borrowed from a Labour Party slogan. Gabriel's stage costumes and stories were making the band a strong concert draw, and I Know What I Like gave them a British hit single.","Listen closer: the last track, Aisle of Plenty, brings back the melody of the unaccompanied opening and turns its question — where has my country gone — into a list of supermarket names and bargain offers, puns on the chains of the day. The record is a circle from lost Albion to the checkout. Banks's piano opening of Firth of Fifth was dropped from their concerts because he could not play it reliably live.","It was well reviewed and sold strongly in Britain. It is now often ranked as the finest Genesis album of the Gabriel era. Debates circle Gabriel's lyrics, clever and densely allusive to some ears, arch to others, and the long Battle of Epping Forest, which some find overcrowded with words.","Start with Firth of Fifth for the piano opening and Hackett's solo. Then listen to the whole album from the unaccompanied first line to the reprise at the end. Compare the English pastoral colour of the quiet passages with Vaughan Williams's Tallis Fantasia: both look for an old England inside a modern one."],"genesis-the-lamb-lies-down-on-broadway":["A double album of twenty-three tracks telling one surreal story. Rael, a young Puerto Rican graffiti artist in New York, is swallowed by a cloud over Times Square and wakes in an underground world of cages, grotesque creatures and seductive lamia, searching for his brother. The songs are shorter and harder than on earlier Genesis records, with more synthesizer, ambient interludes and a streetwise, sardonic vocal. The title song opens it on a strutting piano figure.","Written and recorded in 1974 in a rented country house in Wales and a mobile studio. Peter Gabriel insisted on writing nearly all the lyrics, and relations frayed; he also left for a period when William Friedkin, director of The Exorcist, discussed a screenplay with him, and his first child was born with complications. The long tour that followed staged the story with slides and costumes, and Gabriel left the band when it ended.","Listen closer: the sleeve credits Brian Eno with 'Enossification' of Gabriel's voice. Eno was working in the same studio complex and treated some vocals through his electronics. The brief instrumentals — The Waiting Room especially, an improvised passage of noise — are the band's nearest brush with the experimental rock of the time.","Reception was divided: the story was baffling and the double length forbidding, and some fans missed the pastoral Genesis. Its reputation has risen steadily, and it now stands as one of the few progressive concept albums with the ambition of a rock opera and a genuinely strange imagination. Arguments over the plot's meaning go on, and Gabriel has admitted he does not fully know either.","Treat the first side as a set of songs rather than a plot: the title track, Fly on a Windshield and Carpet Crawlers. Then follow Rael through the whole thing with a lyric sheet. Hear it next to Bowie's Diamond Dogs era and Eno's early solo work to catch the mid-seventies turn from pastoral to urban."],"jethro-tull-aqualung":["Eleven tracks split into two sides, the first a gallery of London street characters, the second titled My God and aimed at organised religion. The title track opens on a lurching, heavy guitar riff and a portrait of a destitute old man, then shifts to acoustic guitar and back. Ian Anderson's breathy, overblown flute and his acoustic guitar alternate with Martin Barre's electric lead. Locomotive Breath begins with a long piano introduction before a relentless riff kicks in.","Recorded in London at the end of 1970 and the start of 1971, in Island Records' new Basing Street studio, with a line-up that now included the keyboard player John Evan and bassist Jeffrey Hammond. The British folk revival and the blues boom were both part of Tull's background. Anderson's anger at the Church of England, and at the gap between its teaching and its establishment, shaped the second side.","Listen closer: the title character grew out of photographs of homeless men on the Thames Embankment taken by Anderson's then wife, Jennie, who also co-wrote the song. Anderson has always denied that Aqualung is a concept album, and that critics insisted it was provoked him to make Thick as a Brick as a parody of one. Listen for the short acoustic interludes that link the sides.","It became the band's best seller and gave rock radio a set of standards, Locomotive Breath and the title song among them. Some critics found the religious polemic heavy-handed; others hear the whole as the peak of flute-led hard rock. The question of whether it is a concept album, which Anderson resents, keeps being asked.","Start with the title track and notice the sudden shifts between electric riff and quiet acoustic verse. Then play My God and hear the church as antagonist. As a contrast, listen afterwards to a Bach chorale cantata such as Wachet auf, which is what the English church once meant musically."],"jethro-tull-thick-as-a-brick":["One continuous piece across both sides of an LP, broken only because the record had to be turned over. It begins with Ian Anderson alone on acoustic guitar singing the melody that will come back at the end, then passes through dozens of sections: folk ballads, hard-rock riffs, organ interludes, rhythmic breaks, a march, a stretch of glockenspiel and flute. The band keeps returning to a handful of themes, which gives the long span its shape.","Made in London in December 1971 with the new drummer Barriemore Barlow. After critics called Aqualung a concept album, Anderson set out to make, as he put it, the mother of all concept albums as a joke. The lyrics are attributed to Gerald Bostock, a fictional eight-year-old poet disqualified from a literary contest. The record reached number one in the United States.","Listen closer: the original sleeve was a twelve-page mock provincial newspaper, the St Cleve Chronicle, with the story of Bostock's disqualification, a review of the album itself, puzzles, and village news, written mostly by the band. The satire is aimed at small-town England as well as at progressive rock's pomp, and the parody works only because the music is so earnest in its craft.","It was a hit and a puzzle: some reviewers missed the joke, others missed the music behind it. Now it is often taken as both a sendup and one of the finest examples of the thing it mocks. Anderson returned to Bostock forty years later with a sequel, which revived the question of how serious the original ever was.","Hear it complete in one sitting, and listen for the opening acoustic melody's return at the very end. Keep a list of the recurring themes as they reappear. Then compare it with Genesis's Selling England by the Pound, another portrait of English life written with long form in mind."],"can-tago-mago":["A double album that moves from songs to chaos. The first record holds Paperhouse, Mushroom and Oh Yeah, tight grooves under Damo Suzuki's murmured, half-sung vocals, and the eighteen-minute Halleluhwah, a relentless funk beat from Jaki Liebezeit that never lets up. The second record goes into the dark: Aumgn and Peking O are collages of shrieks, organ, drum machine and radio, before Bring Me Coffee or Tea eases it back to earth.","Can were formed in Cologne in 1968 by musicians from outside rock: Holger Czukay and Irmin Schmidt had studied with Stockhausen, Liebezeit came from free jazz, Michael Karoli was a young rock guitarist. Suzuki was a Japanese traveller they had found busking in Munich. They recorded in a castle near Cologne, Schloss Nörvenich, on a two-track machine, improvising for hours and editing afterwards.","Listen closer: many of the album's pieces were cut and assembled by Czukay from long improvisations on tape — the way Stockhausen's studio assembled electronic works — so the grooves are partly edited, not simply played. On Oh Yeah the vocal runs partly backwards. Halleluhwah's drum pattern is played by hand, with metronomic precision, for over eighteen minutes.","Little noticed in Britain or America on release, it became one of the most cited records by later musicians: post-punk bands, Radiohead, electronic producers. Critics now treat it as the band's central statement and the core of what the English press called krautrock, a term the Germans disliked. The second record still divides listeners between those who hear adventure and those who hear noise.","Start with Halleluhwah and resist waiting for a change: let the drums put you in a trance. Then try Aumgn at night. Hear Terry Riley's In C next to it for another way of building music from repetition, and Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge for the idea of a studio as a composer's instrument."],"can-ege-bamyasi":["Seven tracks, tighter and more song-shaped than Tago Mago. Pinch opens on a tense, busy drum pattern; Vitamin C is a lean, catchy groove with Damo Suzuki's cryptic lyrics; Soup lurches from a riff into several minutes of noise. I'm So Green is almost pop. Spoon, which closes it, rides on an early drum machine and a hypnotic organ line. Everything is built on Jaki Liebezeit's economical drumming and Holger Czukay's sparse, low bass.","Recorded in 1972 at Inner Space, the studio Can had built in a former cinema in Weilerswist near Cologne. Spoon had been written as the theme for a German television thriller, Das Messer, and became a hit single in West Germany; the money paid for the equipment. The title and cover come from a tin of okra from a Turkish grocery, a sly nod to Cologne's growing immigrant communities.","Listen closer: the album was finished under deadline pressure, and some of the tracks were pieced together from earlier jams by Czukay's tape editing. Listen to Spoon's drum machine: the band laid it under a live drummer, putting the human and the mechanical side by side a few years before Kraftwerk made the machine the whole point.","It sold better than Tago Mago in Germany and was admired but little bought abroad. Its reputation grew with each generation of admirers; hip-hop producers and indie rock bands have sampled and covered it. Critics sometimes call it the most accessible Can record, which undersells its strangeness.","Begin with Vitamin C and Spoon, then listen to Soup to hear how easily the band drops into chaos and climbs out. Follow the bass on its own for a whole track: it says very little and matters enormously. Pair with Neu!'s first album for the other face of the same Rhineland scene."],"neu-neu":["Six tracks by a duo, Klaus Dinger on drums and Michael Rother on guitar and bass. Hallogallo, ten minutes long, is the manifesto: a steady, straight, forward-driving beat, bright chiming guitars layered in loops, and almost no change of harmony, like driving on an empty motorway. The rest of the record moves between that pulse and its opposite: slow, washed-out pieces of drones and effects, water sounds, and scraps of tape.","Dinger and Rother had both passed briefly through an early Kraftwerk line-up in Düsseldorf in 1971. They recorded Neu! in a few days in Hamburg late that year with Conny Plank, the engineer who would shape much of German experimental rock. Their stated aim was music free of American and British models: no blues, no swing, no rock and roll heritage, just forward motion.","Listen closer: Dinger's beat, later called motorik, is not machine-made. It is a human drummer playing a very simple pattern with tiny, constant variations, which is why it breathes. Im Glück, the slow track, has the sounds of oars and water from a rowing boat. Listen for how Plank's mixing makes the guitars appear and fade as if passing by.","It sold only modestly at first. Its influence was delayed and immense: David Bowie and Brian Eno admired it, the motorik beat passed into post-punk, and much later into indie rock and electronic music. Critics now often place Hallogallo among the most consequential rock tracks of its decade. Its slower tracks are still less discussed than they deserve.","Listen to Hallogallo on headphones while walking or travelling, and let the beat carry you. Then compare it with Terry Riley's In C and Reich's Drumming: all three find variety inside strict repetition. Then hear Bowie's Low to see where the pulse went next."],"faust-faust-iv":["Eight tracks that swing between noise and song. The opener, Krautrock, is twelve minutes of a swelling drone of distorted guitars that takes several minutes before the drums finally enter and drive it forward. After that come songs with an odd, deadpan gentleness — The Sad Skinhead with its lopsided reggae-like bounce, the soft, dreamy Jennifer — alongside collages and fragments. The record keeps changing its mind about what it is.","Faust were assembled in 1971 in the Hamburg area by the journalist Uwe Nettelbeck and lived together in a converted schoolhouse in Wümme. By 1973 they had come to Britain and signed with the new Virgin label, which had sold their collage album The Faust Tapes at the price of a single. Faust IV was recorded at Virgin's Manor studio in Oxfordshire; Virgin dropped them soon afterwards.","Listen closer: the opening track mocks the British press label for German rock by taking it as its title. In 1972 the band had also backed Tony Conrad, a founding member of La Monte Young's drone ensemble, on his record Outside the Dream Syndicate, and the long unmoving drones of Krautrock carry that lineage from the New York minimalist underground into rock.","Sales were weak and the band broke up soon after, to reform decades later. It is now taken as one of the key German experimental rock albums and, oddly, one of the most listenable Faust records. Critics argue whether its gentleness betrays the band's anarchy or proves how wide it was.","Hear Krautrock with patience: the arrival of the drums is the event. Then go to Jennifer for the contrast. Pair it with La Monte Young's Trio for Strings to hear the drone in its original form, and with Can's Tago Mago for a sister band's approach to the same problem."],"soft-machine-third":["A double album with four pieces, one per side. Facelift, by bassist Hugh Hopper, is heavy fuzz organ and noise. Slightly All the Time is gentle, jazz-inflected and long-breathed. Moon in June is Robert Wyatt's, a drifting vocal suite with wandering, whimsical lyrics. Out-Bloody-Rageous by Mike Ratledge begins and ends with shimmering tape loops of electric piano, with a propulsive jazz-rock piece in between led by Elton Dean's saxophone.","Soft Machine came out of Canterbury, a psychedelic group of the UFO club era that had become a largely instrumental band moving toward jazz. Recorded in London in 1970 and released by CBS. That August, the band became the first rock group to play the BBC Proms at the Royal Albert Hall, a sign of how serious their music had come to seem. Moon in June was the last major piece Wyatt sang with them.","Listen closer: the tape loops at the start of Out-Bloody-Rageous work like Terry Riley's keyboard pieces, with interlocking phrases slowly drifting against each other. The band's original guitarist Daevid Allen had worked with Riley's tape-delay techniques in the mid-sixties. Moon in June also refers wryly to the band's own situation, with Wyatt singing about playing to audiences and missing home.","It did well for an unwieldy double album and remains the band's most admired record. The jazz press liked it more than the rock press did. Arguments are about balance: Moon in June is Wyatt's personal voice against a band moving away from songs, and many fans hear the album as the moment the group split in two.","Start with Out-Bloody-Rageous and listen for the loops dissolving into the band. Then hear A Rainbow in Curved Air by Terry Riley to find the source. Finish with Moon in June for Wyatt's voice, which led him on to his solo records."],"frank-zappa-freak-out":["A double album by the Mothers of Invention with Frank Zappa writing, arranging and leading. The first sides are satirical songs — Hungry Freaks, Daddy; Who Are the Brain Police?; parodies of doo-wop and teen ballads that are also loving recreations of them. The last side is The Return of the Son of Monster Magnet, a long, improvised collage of percussion, grunts and voices recorded late at night in the studio.","Released on Verve in 1966, produced by Tom Wilson, who had also produced Bob Dylan and the Velvet Underground. It was among the first rock double albums and one of the first debut albums conceived as a whole. Los Angeles's counterculture was taking shape, and Zappa set out to satirise both the straight world and the hippies about to arrive, while practising an older, stranger love of avant-garde music.","Listen closer: the sleeve prints a long list of people who shaped the band's music, and among the rock and rhythm-and-blues names sits Edgard Varèse, whose Ionisation Zappa had discovered as a teenager on a record he tracked down by mail. The percussion collage of the last side is Zappa's early answer to Varèse's all-percussion piece, filtered through a Hollywood studio.","It sold poorly and confused critics; later it was recognised as a pioneering concept album that inspired, among others, the Beatles as they began Sgt. Pepper. Opinions divide between those who hear it as sharp satire and those who find its mockery sour. Its avant-garde final side is now its most admired part.","Start with Hungry Freaks, Daddy and Who Are the Brain Police?, then skip to the final side. Hear Varèse's Ionisation next: then you understand what Zappa meant when he called himself a composer who used a rock band."],"frank-zappa-were-only-in-it-for-the-money":["Nineteen short tracks run together with spliced-in dialogue, tape tricks and sudden cuts, so the record plays as a single collage. The songs mock hippie conformity as much as the police and suburban parents: Who Needs the Peace Corps?, Flower Punk, Absolutely Free. Between them are fragments of whispered conversation and odd sound effects. The last piece, The Chrome Plated Megaphone of Destiny, is several minutes of avant-garde tape and percussion music with no rock at all.","Recorded in New York and Los Angeles in 1967, the year of the Summer of Love, by the Mothers of Invention. Zappa despised what he saw as hippie commercialism. The cover and gatefold, by Cal Schenkel, parodied the Beatles' Sgt. Pepper photograph, and the release was delayed and redesigned after legal worries. MGM/Verve also censored lines they found risky without Zappa's permission.","Listen closer: The Chrome Plated Megaphone of Destiny comes with a sleeve instruction to read Kafka's In the Penal Colony while listening. It is Zappa's homage to the musique concrète and electronic music of Varèse, whose Poème électronique had been played to crowds at the Brussels World's Fair a decade before. Zappa's 1984 remix, with newly recorded bass and drums, remains controversial.","Critics were impressed by its savagery and wit, and it has come to be heard as one of the great satirical records. Some find its attacks on hippies self-righteous; others hear it as an early warning about the commercial capture of rebellion. Fans argue about which mix to hear, and many prefer the original 1968 version.","Hear the whole record without stopping and treat the collage as music, not interruption. Then read Kafka and hear the last piece again. Compare it with Varèse's Poème électronique and The Beatles' Sgt. Pepper to catch the argument between art and pop."],"frank-zappa-hot-rats":["Six tracks, five instrumental. Peaches en Regalia opens it with a bright, intricate tune for layered woodwinds, keyboards and guitar, a miniature under four minutes. Willie the Pimp features Captain Beefheart's growled vocal over a long, snarling guitar solo from Zappa. The rest mixes long, jazz-leaning grooves with electric violin from Sugarcane Harris and Jean-Luc Ponty with composed, chamber-like interludes. Ian Underwood plays most of the wind and keyboard parts.","Zappa had disbanded the Mothers of Invention and recorded this in Los Angeles in 1969 as his first solo album. It was one of the first records made on sixteen-track tape, which let Underwood build whole woodwind sections himself. Released on Zappa's Bizarre label via Reprise, it arrived as rock and jazz were beginning to mingle, weeks before Miles Davis recorded Bitches Brew.","Listen closer: the layered woodwind lines in Peaches en Regalia, played by one man, recall the chamber writing of Stravinsky's Octet for winds, a composer Zappa revered alongside Varèse. The album is dedicated to his newborn son, Dweezil. It Must Be a Camel is full of angular, hard-to-sing intervals typical of Zappa's composed style.","It was well received and sold especially strongly in Britain. It is now often cited as one of the first jazz-rock albums and the best entry to Zappa. Some fans still debate whether the long solos or the composed miniatures are the heart of it; the 1987 CD remix altered some tracks and is still disputed.","Start with Peaches en Regalia and count how many instruments you hear, then remember it was mostly one player. Hear Willie the Pimp for Zappa the guitarist. Next, play Stravinsky's Octet: the same clear, bright wind textures."],"captain-beefheart-trout-mask-replica":["Twenty-eight tracks on a double album, credited to Captain Beefheart and his Magic Band. The guitars play interlocking, jagged lines that seem to ignore each other, in different metres and tempos, while the drummer follows none of them yet holds it together. Over this Don Van Vliet roars, recites, growls and croons surreal poetry in a deep blues voice. There are a cappella poems, field recordings, and bursts of free-jazz saxophone and bass clarinet.","Van Vliet, from California's Mojave Desert, was a friend of Frank Zappa since high school. He and the band lived for months in a small house in Woodland Hills, rehearsing under exhausting, often abusive conditions. Drummer John French transcribed Van Vliet's ideas, played on piano or whistled, into parts. Zappa produced it for his Straight label, largely recording the band live in one intensive session.","Listen closer: the parts are not improvised. For all their apparent chaos, the guitar lines were written, taught and learned note for note over months, so the collisions are composed. Van Vliet sang many of his vocals without headphones, following the faint leakage from the studio, which is one reason voice and band so often seem to drift apart.","It sold little and baffled many; some thought it a joke. It has gradually become a central text of experimental rock, admired by punk, post-punk and art-rock musicians. The debate over whether it is great music or an ordeal continues, and critics now also weigh the album against accounts of how Van Vliet treated the band.","Start with Ella Guru, Moonlight on Vermont and Pachuco Cadaver, the more song-like tracks. Try hearing each guitar separately. Then listen to Ives's Putnam's Camp, where two marching bands collide, and hear a similar idea of music as simultaneous, independent layers."],"roxy-music-roxy-music":["Nine tracks that sound like no-one else in 1972. Re-make/Re-model opens with party chatter, then a brash, chaotic rock band in which each player takes a brief solo break. Ladytron starts in near silence with oboe and synthesizer before exploding. Bryan Ferry's voice is arch, theatrical and vulnerable at once. Andy Mackay's saxophone and oboe, Phil Manzanera's guitar, and Brian Eno's synthesizer treatments give it an unsettling, collage-like texture.","Recorded in London in 1972 and produced by Peter Sinfield, lyricist of King Crimson. Ferry had studied fine art at Newcastle under the pop artist Richard Hamilton, and conceived Roxy Music as a work of art: glamorous, retro and modern at once. Eno had no training as a player; he treated the band's sound with a synthesizer and tape machines at the mixing desk.","Listen closer: in Re-make/Re-model the chorus spells out a car registration number, CPL 593H, the number plate of a car driven by a woman Ferry admired in the song. During the solo breaks each player quotes something, and the bassist plays the riff of the Beatles' Day Tripper. The sleeve looks like a glamour magazine, part of the art-school concept.","It surprised critics who found it both absurd and brilliant; it reached the UK top ten, and the single Virginia Plain followed. It is now often seen as the start of art rock's glam wing, a key influence on punk and new wave. Arguments centre on whether the pastiche is ironic or sincere.","Start with Re-make/Re-model and listen for each solo break in turn. Then hear Ladytron and notice the quiet opening. Compare with David Bowie's Ziggy Stardust from the same year: both use glam as a costume for something stranger."],"roxy-music-for-your-pleasure":["Eight tracks, darker than the debut. Do the Strand is a manic invitation to a new dance that does not exist. Editions of You is fast, with a sharp synthesizer solo from Eno. In Every Dream Home a Heartache is a slow, menacing love song to an inflatable doll that seems to end, then returns in a blast of guitar. The Bogus Man is nine minutes of repetitive, sinister groove, and the title track fades out on a haze of tapes.","Recorded in London in early 1973 and released that March. It was Brian Eno's last album with the band: his friction with Ferry and his growing stage profile led him to leave that summer. Ferry's lyrics had become more sardonic, about consumer luxury and loneliness. The cover shows the model Amanda Lear leading a black panther on a leash against a nighttime city skyline.","Listen closer: the false ending of In Every Dream Home a Heartache lasts several seconds before the band returns, now drenched in phasing, as if the song had come back from the dead. The Bogus Man's steady, trancelike groove is closer to Can than to British rock, and it points to the direction Eno would take on his solo albums.","Reviews were strong and it reached the UK top five. It is now often ranked as Roxy's finest album, the peak of Ferry and Eno together. Some prefer the lighter, more commercial Roxy that followed; most critics hear this as the band at its most daring.","Start with Editions of You for energy, then hear In Every Dream Home a Heartache in the dark and wait for the return. Then play The Bogus Man straight after Can's Ege Bamyasi and hear the kinship."],"david-bowie-space-oddity":["A five-minute song told as a dialogue between Ground Control and the astronaut Major Tom, who drifts away into space. It opens with strummed acoustic guitar and a countdown, then swells with strings and a lift-off. The small, buzzing electronic sound is a stylophone, a toy keyboard played with a stylus. The ending leaves Major Tom floating, cut off from Earth, as the orchestra rises and falls away.","Released as a single in July 1969, days before the launch of Apollo 11. Bowie had seen Stanley Kubrick's 2001: A Space Odyssey, and the title is a pun on it. Producer Gus Dudgeon took charge because Bowie's usual producer, Tony Visconti, disliked the song as a novelty. Rick Wakeman, later of Yes, played Mellotron on the session.","Listen closer: the song is less about space than about isolation; Major Tom's calm voice as he loses contact is chilling. In the middle eight, the chord sequence climbs as the strings swell, mimicking lift-off. Kubrick's 2001 had opened with Richard Strauss's Also sprach Zarathustra, and the song borrows its grand, slowly rising sense of cosmic awe.","It became Bowie's first hit and was reportedly played during British coverage of the moon landing, ironic given the lyric's ending. It was later re-released and reached number one in Britain in 1975. Critics now treat it as the first great Bowie song and a founding myth of his work; he returned to Major Tom in later songs.","Listen for the stylophone and the countdown. Then hear the opening of Also sprach Zarathustra and imagine the film Bowie had just seen. Follow Major Tom forward to Ashes to Ashes and Blackstar to see how the character haunted Bowie's work."],"david-bowie-ziggy-stardust":["Eleven songs loosely about Ziggy, a bisexual alien rock star who comes to an Earth with five years left to live, becomes a messiah, and is destroyed by his fans and his ego. Five Years opens it on a lonely drum pattern; Starman is a radio-ready anthem; Ziggy Stardust tells the story in a riff; Suffragette City is hard rock and roll; Rock 'n' Roll Suicide ends in a desperate, swelling crescendo: 'You're not alone.'","Recorded in London in late 1971 and early 1972 with the Spiders from Mars — Mick Ronson on guitar, Trevor Bolder on bass, Woody Woodmansey on drums — and co-producer Ken Scott. Glam rock was flourishing. Bowie's Starman performance on Top of the Pops in July 1972, his arm around Ronson, became a defining moment for a generation of British teenagers.","Listen closer: Mick Ronson arranged the strings, and his orchestral touches are everywhere. Starman's leap on the word 'star-man' echoes the octave leap of 'Somewhere' in Over the Rainbow. Ziggy drew on real figures, including the eccentric rock singer Vince Taylor and the Legendary Stardust Cowboy. Bowie killed Ziggy on stage at the Hammersmith Odeon in July 1973.","It made Bowie a star and has never really fallen from critical favour. Some find the concept thin — the album isn't a strict narrative — and the rock 'n' roll pastiche obvious. Others credit it with making theatrical identity and ambiguous sexuality central to rock. Its influence on punk, new wave and pop spectacle is hard to overstate.","Hear the whole album in order: the drum of Five Years at the start, the drama of Rock 'n' Roll Suicide at the end. Notice Ronson's guitar and strings. Then compare with Roxy Music's debut, released the same month, for the other face of glam."],"david-bowie-low":["Two halves. Side one is seven short, fragmentary songs and instrumentals, with processed drums that crack like gunshots, bright synthesizers and minimal lyrics about retreat and recovery: Sound and Vision, Always Crashing in the Same Car. Side two is four slow instrumentals. Warszawa is a dark, spacious piece with a wordless choir sung by Bowie in an invented language; Subterraneans ends with a lonely saxophone.","Recorded in the autumn of 1976 at the Château d'Hérouville near Paris and at Hansa Studios in West Berlin, after Bowie had moved to Berlin to escape cocaine addiction in Los Angeles. Tony Visconti co-produced; Brian Eno contributed synthesizers, treatments and ideas. Bowie and Eno both admired Neu! and Kraftwerk. RCA, expecting a commercial successor to Station to Station, was dismayed.","Listen closer: the drum sound came from an Eventide Harmonizer, a new digital effect, which Visconti used to drop the pitch of the snare and create a thudding crack. Bowie said that Weeping Wall drew on the interlocking mallet patterns of Steve Reich. Warszawa was built largely by Eno from a piano phrase during a few days when Bowie was away.","Reviews were mixed and RCA was unhappy, but it sold respectably. Its reputation rose quickly among musicians: post-punk, new wave, synth-pop and ambient all borrowed from it. It is now often named one of Bowie's greatest albums. In 1992 Philip Glass wrote his Symphony No. 1 on its themes, a rare classical tribute to a rock album.","Hear side one as a set of fragments, then sit with side two in the dark. Listen closely to the drum sound, which influenced a decade of production. Then hear Glass's Symphony No. 1 'Low' to see how a minimalist composer read it, and Reich's Drumming for Weeping Wall's source."],"david-bowie-heroes":["Ten tracks following Low's pattern: songs on the first side, instrumentals mostly on the second. The title song builds for six minutes on a droning organ, a chugging rhythm, and a sustained, feedback-laden guitar line, while Bowie's voice rises from calm to a desperate shout. Beauty and the Beast and Joe the Lion are jagged rock; Moss Garden features Bowie on Japanese koto, and V-2 Schneider salutes Kraftwerk's Florian Schneider.","Recorded at Hansa Studios in West Berlin in the summer of 1977, within sight of the Berlin Wall, with Tony Visconti and Brian Eno. Robert Fripp of King Crimson flew in and recorded his guitar parts rapidly, playing over backing tracks he had barely heard. Eno's Oblique Strategies cards shaped some decisions. It was the only one of the three Berlin albums made entirely in the city.","Listen closer: Visconti recorded the vocal of the title song with microphones at different distances and gated them so that the more Bowie shouted, the more the distant microphones opened, making his voice seem to fill the room. The lovers kissing by the Wall were Visconti and the singer Antonia Maass, a detail Bowie revealed only years later.","The title song was not a big hit on release but became one of Bowie's defining anthems; he performed it in West Berlin in 1987 near the Wall, and it was widely cited after his death. The album is sometimes seen as a more extroverted but less radical sequel to Low. Philip Glass also based a symphony on it.","Hear the title song on headphones and follow Fripp's guitar and the voice as they rise. Then listen to the instrumentals, especially Moss Garden. Compare with King Crimson's Red to hear Fripp in another setting, and with Low for the first panel of the diptych."],"brian-eno-here-come-the-warm-jets":["Ten songs of bright, strange, loud pop. Needles in the Camel's Eye opens on a wall of layered, droning guitars and a cheerful melody. Baby's on Fire is mocking and sinister, with a long, searing guitar solo by Robert Fripp. Cindy Tells Me and On Some Faraway Beach are sweet and elegiac. The title track ends it with a blissful wash of distorted guitars and faint voices, which prefigures shoegaze.","Recorded in London in 1973, soon after Eno left Roxy Music. He assembled players who would not normally work together, including Fripp, John Wetton, Phil Manzanera and Andy Mackay, and directed them by gestures and by dancing rather than by instructions. Eno, a non-musician who had studied art at Winchester, treated the studio and the band as raw material to shape.","Listen closer: Eno often wrote lyrics by singing nonsense syllables over the backing tracks, then finding words that fit the sounds, so the lyrics carry more rhythm than meaning. On Some Faraway Beach delays its vocal for more than two minutes. The layered guitars came partly from recording and treating a guitar many times over.","It sold modestly but reached the British top thirty and was praised by critics as witty and adventurous. It is now often seen as a model for art pop, new wave and later indie rock. Debate centres on the tension between the prettiness of the tunes and the abrasive textures, which some think Eno resolved better on later records.","Start with Needles in the Camel's Eye and Baby's on Fire. Then hear the title track as a picture of light rather than a song. Compare with Roxy Music's For Your Pleasure to hear what Eno took from and left behind in the band."],"brian-eno-another-green-world":["Fourteen short tracks, only five of them songs. The instrumentals are small, still landscapes of synthesizer, treated guitar and piano: the title piece, Sombre Reptiles, Becalmed. The songs are gentle and strange: St. Elmo's Fire with Robert Fripp's fast, twisting guitar solo, I'll Come Running, Everything Merges with the Night. The whole feels like a series of paintings rather than a rock record.","Recorded in London in 1975 with a few collaborators, including Phil Collins on drums, Percy Jones on bass, Fripp and John Cale on viola. Eno arrived at the studio without songs. He used Oblique Strategies, a deck of cards with instructions such as 'Honour thy error as a hidden intention', created with the painter Peter Schmidt, to make decisions and break deadlock.","Listen closer: the Oblique Strategies owe a clear debt to John Cage's use of chance procedures, which Eno had studied at art school. The title piece is barely more than a minute and a half and became the theme tune for the BBC arts programme Arena. Listen for the treated guitars, often recorded and slowed or altered so they no longer sound like guitars.","Warmly received, it has grown to be regarded as one of Eno's greatest records and a bridge between his song albums and his ambient work. Critics debate whether it is rock at all, and many hear it as the start of a new, texture-first kind of pop. Its influence on David Bowie's Low was immediate.","Listen from start to finish without skipping: the short tracks form a single journey. Notice the sudden change of mood between songs and instrumentals. Then hear Satie's Gymnopédie No. 1, which Eno admired, and Cage's Music of Changes to see the principles behind his methods."],"led-zeppelin-led-zeppelin":["Nine tracks that swing between Chicago blues played at enormous volume and moments of folk-guitar quiet. Good Times Bad Times opens with a clipped riff and a bass drum that seems to fire in triplets; You Shook Me and I Can't Quit You Baby slow the blues to a crawl; Dazed and Confused descends into a long, eerie middle section; Black Mountain Side is a solo acoustic guitar instrumental with tabla. The sound you recognise it by is space: loud, dry, and physically close.","Jimmy Page formed the band in 1968 from the wreckage of the Yardbirds, with singer Robert Plant, drummer John Bonham and the session bassist and arranger John Paul Jones. They recorded the album at Olympic Studios in London in the autumn of 1968, in roughly thirty-six hours of studio time, with Page producing and paying for it himself. That ownership let him negotiate an unusually generous deal with Atlantic Records before anyone at the label had heard a note.","Listen closer: during Dazed and Confused Page plays his guitar with a violin bow, producing moans and glissandi no pick can make. The record is also a lesson in borrowing. Two songs are by the Chicago bassist and writer Willie Dixon; Babe I'm Gonna Leave You and Dazed and Confused were later re-credited to include the folk writers Anne Bredon and Jake Holmes, whose versions the band had heard.","Rolling Stone's first review was scornful, calling the band a weaker cousin of Jeff Beck's group, and many rock critics held the line for years. Audiences paid no attention; the album sold steadily on the strength of the band's ferocious American tours. The borrowing question has never gone away: admirers hear transformation, detractors hear uncredited appropriation of Black and folk songwriters, and both have a case.","Start with Good Times Bad Times and listen only to Bonham's right foot. Then play Muddy Waters's or Otis Rush's versions of the Dixon songs next to Zeppelin's to hear what volume and slowness do to the blues. Black Mountain Side rewards headphones. The 2014 remaster is clean without being flattened."],"led-zeppelin-ii":["Nine tracks built around riffs rather than songs. Whole Lotta Love opens on a two-bar guitar figure, then breaks down into a middle section of theremin whoops, echoing drums and voice before the riff returns. Heartbreaker stops dead for an unaccompanied guitar solo; Moby Dick is a drum feature for Bonham; Ramble On and Thank You show the gentler, acoustic side. The record is tighter and harder than the first, and its riffs became the vocabulary of hard rock guitar for a decade.","Made on the run in 1969, between tour dates in Britain and America, in studios in London, Los Angeles, New York and Vancouver, then mixed by Page with engineer Eddie Kramer in New York. The band were already selling out large halls in the United States, and the album was built partly from riffs worked out on stage. Tolkien, whom Plant was reading, drifts into Ramble On.","Listen closer: the eerie delayed voice in Whole Lotta Love comes from studio echo that bled onto the tape and was kept rather than erased. Its words are closely modelled on Willie Dixon's You Need Love, recorded by Muddy Waters in 1962, and The Lemon Song reworks Howlin' Wolf's Killing Floor; both debts were eventually settled in court and credited.","It replaced the Beatles' Abbey Road at the top of the American album chart at the end of 1969, which critics read as a changing of the guard. Reviewers still divided: some heard empty bombast, others the most exciting guitar record since Hendrix. The later lawsuits made it a central text in arguments about how British rock treated the Black musicians it learned from.","Play Whole Lotta Love loud and follow the middle section as a piece of sound collage rather than a song. Then hear Muddy Waters's You Need Love and Howlin' Wolf's Killing Floor. Heartbreaker's unaccompanied solo is the moment many guitarists of the next generation started practising."],"led-zeppelin-iv":["Eight tracks with no title on the sleeve, only four symbols. Black Dog trades unaccompanied vocal lines with a riff that keeps wrong-footing the beat; The Battle of Evermore is a mandolin duet with the folk singer Sandy Denny; Stairway to Heaven grows over eight minutes from acoustic guitar and recorders to a full electric climax, getting faster as it goes. The album ends with When the Levee Breaks, a slow, enormous blues built on one of the most sampled drum sounds ever recorded.","Much of it was recorded in early 1971 at Headley Grange, a draughty former workhouse in Hampshire, using the Rolling Stones' mobile recording truck parked outside. Stung by reviewers who called them a hype, the band removed their name from the cover and let the music stand on its own. Page, a collector of occult books, designed his own symbol; the others chose theirs.","Listen closer: Bonham's drums on When the Levee Breaks were recorded in the stairwell of Headley Grange, with microphones hung high above the kit, which is why they boom like artillery. The song itself is a 1929 blues by Memphis Minnie and Kansas Joe McCoy about the great Mississippi flood. And the recorders at the start of Stairway are John Paul Jones, overdubbing several parts himself.","The album sold in the tens of millions and Stairway became the most requested song on American rock radio without ever being a single there. It later attracted mockery as the ultimate emblem of rock excess. In 2016 a Los Angeles jury rejected a claim that its opening copied the band Spirit's Taurus, a verdict upheld on appeal in 2020 in a case closely watched by songwriters.","Start with When the Levee Breaks on good speakers, then hear Memphis Minnie's original to measure the distance. Listen to Stairway as a single long crescendo, noticing where the drums first enter. Then play Going to California to hear how quiet this band could be."],"black-sabbath-black-sabbath":["The record opens with rain, thunder and a distant tolling bell, and then a guitar plays three notes: a low one, the same note an octave up, and a note a tritone away, the interval medieval theorists avoided. That slow, dragging riff is the title track and, for many, the birth of heavy metal. The rest mixes blues jams, a harmonica-driven song about a wizard, the bass-led N.I.B. and a long closing cover, all played at a crushing, deliberate pace.","Four working-class musicians from Aston in Birmingham, a city of factories and bomb sites, who had been playing blues as Earth. They renamed themselves after a horror film and recorded the album in about a day in October 1969 at Regent Sound in London, with producer Rodger Bain, essentially playing their live set. Guitarist Tony Iommi had lost two fingertips in a sheet-metal factory accident and played with homemade plastic caps and slackened strings.","Listen closer: the tritone at the heart of the title track is the same interval Saint-Saëns built into the retuned solo violin of Danse macabre, the classical shorthand for the devil. Bassist Geezer Butler has linked the band's darkness to his love of Holst's Mars, with its grinding ostinato. The album was released in Britain on Friday the 13th of February 1970.","Critics were contemptuous; Lester Bangs dismissed it in Rolling Stone and British reviewers treated it as a novelty. It sold regardless, through word of mouth among young listeners who recognised something new. Its reputation has only risen, and it now stands at the head of every genealogy of metal, doom and stoner rock.","Play the title track in the dark and let the first minute of rain run its course. Then play Holst's Mars and Saint-Saëns's Danse macabre and hear the family resemblance. N.I.B. shows Butler's bass leading rather than following. The original Vertigo vinyl mix is the one to seek."],"black-sabbath-paranoid":["Eight tracks, three of which became metal's bedrock. War Pigs opens with an air-raid siren and a slow, lurching riff before accelerating into an assault on the generals who send the poor to die. Paranoid is under three minutes of fast, driving guitar. Iron Man lumbers in on a distorted voice and a riff anyone can hum. Between them sit Planet Caravan, a hushed, jazzy drift with bongos, and Hand of Doom, a slow study of a soldier's heroin addiction.","Recorded in June 1970, only months after the debut, again with Rodger Bain, at Regent Sound and Island Studios in London. The Vietnam War was at its height and the band's audience was young, working class and draftable. The album was going to be called War Pigs; the label, nervous about the war, renamed it after the single, leaving a sleeve illustration of a warrior that no longer matched the title.","Listen closer: the title song was written in minutes as filler because the album was short, and became the band's biggest hit. Ozzy Osbourne's voice at the start of Iron Man is fed through an electronic modulator, and on Planet Caravan it is run through a rotating Leslie organ speaker, which is why it seems to swim around the room.","Critics again sneered and audiences again ignored them; it was a British number-one album. Its standing grew as punk, thrash and grunge musicians all named it as a source. War Pigs is now heard as one of the most direct anti-war statements in rock, a reading the band's satanic reputation long obscured.","Start with War Pigs and notice how the band shift tempo as one body, with no click track. Play Planet Caravan straight after Iron Man to hear the range. Compare Bill Ward's swing, learned from jazz drummers, with the stiffer metal drumming that followed."],"black-sabbath-master-of-reality":["The shortest and heaviest of the early Sabbath albums. Sweet Leaf, Children of the Grave, Lord of This World and Into the Void are built on riffs pitched lower than anything the band had played, thick and sludgy, with Bill Ward's drumming galloping underneath. Between them sit two short instrumentals, the classical-guitar miniature Orchid and the tiny Embryo, and Solitude, a quiet ballad with flute. After Forever is, unexpectedly, a defence of Christian faith.","Recorded in 1971 with producer Rodger Bain after a year of relentless touring. Iommi, playing through pain in his damaged fingertips, tuned his guitar down by three semitones to slacken the strings, and Butler followed on bass. The band were now hugely popular in America and held in contempt by most critics, a pattern that would define metal for twenty years.","Listen closer: the coughing that opens Sweet Leaf is Iommi, caught on tape after smoking in the studio, and the song is an unambiguous love letter to cannabis. The downtuning, born of an injury, is the album's real legacy: it gave the riffs their low, muddy weight, and nearly every doom and stoner band since has tuned down in imitation.","Reviewers were dismissive at release. Its influence surfaced in the 1990s, when Kyuss, Sleep, Monster Magnet and a whole scene of desert and doom bands treated it as scripture, and when grunge guitarists named it as a favourite. After Forever continues to complicate the lazy picture of Sabbath as a satanic band.","Play Children of the Grave loud and follow Ward's galloping drums. Then Orchid, to hear the classical guitar Iommi loved. Into the Void, the closer, is the template for doom metal: listen for the moment the slow riff finally doubles its speed."],"deep-purple-concerto-for-group-and-orchestra":["A three-movement concerto by Deep Purple's organist Jon Lord, in which the rock band takes the part of the solo instrument. The first movement sets band and orchestra against each other, taking turns and rarely combining; the second, slow movement is the most lyrical, with a sung section; the finale finally brings the forces together and features an extended drum solo inside the orchestral texture. The sound is part Hammond organ and electric guitar, part English film score.","Performed on 24 September 1969 at the Royal Albert Hall in London by Deep Purple and the Royal Philharmonic Orchestra, conducted by the composer Malcolm Arnold, who defended the project to sceptical players. Lord was classically trained and wanted to test whether the two worlds could meet on equal terms. It was one of the first concerts by the band's new line-up with Ian Gillan and Roger Glover.","Listen closer: the orchestra and band rarely play the same material at once; Lord wrote it as a debate, and much of the first movement is each side answering the other. The original score was lost; for a 1999 revival at the Albert Hall it was reconstructed by ear from the recording, and Lord took the chance to revise it.","Classical critics were polite to patronising, and some orchestra members openly resented the evening. Rock fans bought it, but the band soon turned hard toward the riff-based rock of In Rock, partly in reaction. Today it is valued as a sincere pioneer of rock-and-orchestra collaboration, flawed as a concerto but not as an idea.","Start with the second movement, where the blend works best. Then hear the finale for the moment when the whole orchestra and the band finally play together. Compare Metallica's 1999 concert with the San Francisco Symphony to see how far the idea travelled."],"deep-purple-machine-head":["Seven tracks in which Ritchie Blackmore's guitar and Jon Lord's distorted Hammond organ duel like two soloists in a Baroque concerto. Highway Star opens at full speed with solos for both; Smoke on the Water rides on a four-note riff in parallel fourths that every beginner guitarist learns; Lazy starts as a long organ improvisation and slides into a shuffle; Space Truckin' closes on a relentless riff. It is hard rock with a keyboard as heavy as the guitar.","Deep Purple went to Montreux in December 1971 to record in the town's casino with the Rolling Stones' mobile studio. During a Frank Zappa concert on 4 December, someone fired a flare gun, and the casino burned down. The band watched the smoke drift over Lake Geneva, then recorded the album in the corridors of the nearly empty Grand Hotel, and wrote a song about the whole affair.","Listen closer: Blackmore has said the guitar solo on Highway Star was built on a Bach-style chord sequence, and it moves through its harmonies in running patterns like a Baroque violin line, a decade before neoclassical metal made this a school. Lord put his Hammond organ through a Marshall guitar amplifier, which is why it growls rather than sings.","It was a British number-one album and Smoke on the Water became one of the most recognised riffs in rock. Critics treated the band as a lesser Zeppelin; guitarists never did. Its reputation rests less on the words than on the playing, and Highway Star is now cited as a founding text of speed and neoclassical metal.","Start with Highway Star and hear the organ solo and the guitar solo as a contest. Notice that the Smoke on the Water riff is two notes played together, not one. For the Bach connection, play the Toccata and Fugue in D minor on a big organ straight afterwards."],"queen-bohemian-rhapsody":["A six-minute song in sections with no chorus. It opens with unaccompanied multitracked voices, turns into a piano ballad in which a young man confesses a killing to his mother, gives way to Brian May's guitar solo, then bursts into a mock-operatic scene of dozens of voices trading names like Scaramouche, Galileo, Figaro and Beelzebub. A furious hard-rock section follows before the piano returns for a quiet, resigned coda and a single gong stroke.","Written by Freddie Mercury and recorded in 1975 for the album A Night at the Opera, named after a Marx Brothers film, with producer Roy Thomas Baker, across several studios including Rockfield in Wales. EMI thought a six-minute single unplayable on radio; the DJ Kenny Everett broadcast it repeatedly anyway. A cheaply made promotional film helped make it a hit and anticipated the music video.","Listen closer: the operatic section was built from dozens of overdubbed vocal passes by Mercury, May and Roger Taylor on sixteen-track tape, bounced down so many times that the tape was said to have worn nearly transparent. The name Figaro nods to Rossini and Mozart, and the section is a pastiche of Italian opera buffa ensemble, as much comedy as drama.","It spent nine weeks at number one in Britain and returned there after Mercury's death in 1991. Some critics thought it pretentious nonsense and still do. Mercury refused to explain the words; readings range from a confession of his sexuality to pure invention. Its appearance in the 1992 film Wayne's World made it a hit for a second generation of Americans.","Listen once straight through, then again counting the sections and noticing that none repeats. Follow May's guitar, which sings like a second voice. Then play the overture to Rossini's Barber of Seville and hear the comic energy Queen were parodying."],"rush-2112":["The first side is a twenty-minute suite in seven parts, from Overture to Grand Finale, telling a science-fiction story: in a future society run by the priests of the Temples of Syrinx, a young man finds an old guitar, discovers music, and is crushed when the priests reject it. Geddy Lee's high voice, Alex Lifeson's guitar and Neil Peart's elaborate drumming carry the drama. The second side holds five shorter, unrelated songs.","The Toronto trio had been told by their label, Mercury, that their previous album had sold badly and they should make something more commercial. They did the opposite, with producer Terry Brown, and it became their breakthrough. Peart, the band's lyricist, credited the story's inspiration in the liner notes to the writings of Ayn Rand, a dedication that dogged the band for decades.","Listen closer: near the end of the Overture the band briefly quote Tchaikovsky's 1812 Overture, a nod to the grandiose classical programme music the suite imitates. The Discovery section is Lifeson tuning and exploring an acoustic guitar, played as if for the first time, so the story's central event happens in sound rather than in words.","It saved the band's career and gave them a loyal audience that critics could never dent. Reviewers mocked the ambition and, especially in Britain, attacked the Rand dedication as politically suspect. Peart later distanced himself from Rand's politics while defending the story's point about individual creativity. Among musicians it is one of the most studied records in rock.","Hear the first side in one sitting as a single piece. Follow Peart's drums, which change pattern with each section. Then play the 1812 Overture for the quotation, and Yes's Close to the Edge for the side-long suite Rush were answering."],"ac-dc-highway-to-hell":["Ten songs built from the simplest materials in rock: open chords, a steady mid-tempo beat, and space. Malcolm Young's rhythm guitar and Cliff Williams's bass lock with Phil Rudd's drums; Angus Young's lead guitar stabs and squeals above; Bon Scott sings with a leering, joking rasp. The title track opens on a riff of three chords and a pause. Touch Too Much and Shot Down in Flames show the new, cleaner production that gave the band a larger audience.","The Australian band, led by the Scottish-born brothers Malcolm and Angus Young, had toured relentlessly in Britain and America. Atlantic wanted a radio hit, and after sessions with another producer were abandoned, the band worked in London with the South African producer Robert John 'Mutt' Lange, whose precise, layered backing vocals and clean separation of the instruments changed their sound. It was Bon Scott's last album; he died in London in February 1980.","Listen closer: the power of the band lies in what is left out. Malcolm's rhythm guitar mostly plays open chords with the notes let ring, and the drums rarely fill. Lange's gang choruses are stacked from many takes of the band's voices, a pop technique that makes the refrains feel like a crowd singing along.","It was the band's first American success, and its reputation as the best record of the Bon Scott years has only grown. Critics long treated AC/DC as crude; musicians, from punk to hip-hop producers, praised the groove. The title, meant as a joke about life on tour, drew protests from religious groups in America.","Start with the title track and count how much silence sits inside the riff. Listen to the rhythm section alone for a verse. Then play Back in Black to hear what the same producer did with a new singer a year later."],"ac-dc-back-in-black":["Ten tracks that open with the slow tolling of a bell. Hells Bells unfolds over a picked guitar figure before the band crash in; the title track rides a riff built on stops and hard accents; You Shook Me All Night Long has a chorus designed for stadiums. Brian Johnson's voice is higher and harsher than Bon Scott's. The production is spacious and exact, each guitar in its own speaker, the drums dry and enormous.","Made in the months after Bon Scott's death, when the band considered stopping altogether. They hired Brian Johnson, a singer from Newcastle, and recorded with Mutt Lange at Compass Point Studios in Nassau, in the Bahamas, in the spring of 1980. The all-black sleeve was a mourning band for Scott. Atlantic released it only five months after his death.","Listen closer: the bell on Hells Bells was cast specially for the band, and the whole record is shaped as a memorial, from the opening toll to the title song, which the Youngs framed as a tribute. The rhythm guitar is often recorded without heavy distortion; it is the precision, not the gain, that makes it sound huge.","It is among the best-selling albums in history. Critics who had dismissed the band grudgingly admitted its craft, and it is now a standard reference for rock production. Some fans still argue that the Scott era had more wit; almost no one argues with the riffs.","Play Hells Bells from the first toll and notice how long the band wait before coming in. Compare Johnson on Shoot to Thrill with Scott on Highway to Hell. For a cross-tradition view, set the bell beside Mussorgsky's Great Gate of Kiev, another piece that uses bells as monument."],"judas-priest-sad-wings-of-destiny":["Nine tracks in which Birmingham blues-rock turns into something new. Victim of Changes, nearly eight minutes long, moves through a stomping riff, a ballad section and a climax where Rob Halford's voice climbs to an operatic scream. The Ripper is a short gothic horror tale; Tyrant and Genocide are fast and harmonised; Epitaph is a piano ballad in the style of Queen. The two guitars of Glenn Tipton and K. K. Downing trade and harmonise lines throughout.","Judas Priest came from the same industrial West Midlands as Black Sabbath, and recorded this, their second album, for the small Gull label on a tiny budget in 1975. Hard rock in Britain was losing ground to punk and pub rock; the band pointed in the opposite direction, toward drama, precision and range, with Halford's classically inflected voice at the centre.","Listen closer: the twin-guitar harmony, two lead guitars playing the same line a third apart, is the sound that defined heavy metal for the next decade, from Iron Maiden to Metallica. Victim of Changes stitches together two earlier songs from different band members, which is why its halves feel like different pieces.","It sold modestly and the band left Gull, losing the rights to the recordings. Its reputation was made retrospectively, by the New Wave of British Heavy Metal bands and American thrash musicians who treated it as a textbook. Critics now often call it the first album of pure heavy metal, stripped of the blues.","Start with Victim of Changes and follow Halford's voice from croon to scream. Listen for the passages where both guitars play in harmony. Then play Iron Maiden's Hallowed Be Thy Name to hear where the sound went."],"judas-priest-british-steel":["Nine concise, hard-edged songs, most around four minutes, built on marching tempos and chanted refrains. Breaking the Law and Living After Midnight are short, catchy and simple; Metal Gods is a slow, mechanical stomp about robots taking over; Rapid Fire opens the record at a sprint. Compared with the band's earlier epics, everything is stripped down: the twin guitars play tight, clipped riffs and the production is dry and hard.","Recorded early in 1980 with producer Tom Allom at Tittenhurst Park, the country house near Ascot that had belonged to John Lennon and then Ringo Starr. The New Wave of British Heavy Metal was at its height, and Britain was entering the unemployment and industrial decline of the early Thatcher years; the title and the razor-blade sleeve pointed to the steel industry of the band's native Midlands.","Listen closer: many of the sound effects were homemade in the house. The band smashed milk bottles for the breaking glass in Breaking the Law and shook trays of cutlery to make the marching robots in Metal Gods. Halford's leather-and-studs stage image, adopted a little earlier, was fixed as metal's uniform around this record.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and made them stars in Britain and America. Some fans felt the short songs were a sell-out after the ambition of the 1970s records; the band defended them as focus. It is now regarded as the record that set heavy metal's visual and sonic image for the 1980s.","Start with Metal Gods and listen for the cutlery. Then play Breaking the Law and notice how few notes the riff uses. Compare it with Sad Wings of Destiny to hear a band trading drama for punch."],"motorhead-ace-of-spades":["Under three minutes of speed. Lemmy Kilmister's bass opens alone, a distorted, galloping rumble played more like a rhythm guitar than a bass, before 'Fast' Eddie Clarke's guitar and Phil Taylor's drums hit at full tilt. Lemmy sings in a gravelly shout about gambling as a way of life, and midway the whole band stops dead for a moment before tearing back in. It is loud, fast and rough, with no ballad softness anywhere.","Lemmy had been a roadie for Jimi Hendrix and bassist in the space-rock band Hawkwind before forming Motörhead in 1975. The song, recorded with producer Vic Maile for the album of the same name, came out in 1980 as punk and the New Wave of British Heavy Metal were both on the rise, and Motörhead were one of the few bands both audiences accepted.","Listen closer: Lemmy plays chords on a Rickenbacker bass through a Marshall guitar amplifier, with the treble turned up, which is why it is so hard to tell bass from guitar. He always said Motörhead played rock and roll, not metal, and underneath the noise the song's shape is closer to Little Richard than to Black Sabbath.","It became Motörhead's signature and a staple of British pop culture. Critics were slow to take it seriously, but thrash musicians, notably Metallica's Lars Ulrich, a devoted fan, credited Motörhead with showing that metal could be as fast and raw as punk. It is now heard as the bridge between the two.","Play it loud and listen to the bass in the opening seconds. Then play Little Richard's Long Tall Sally and hear the same forward drive at half the volume. Follow it with Metallica's Master of Puppets to hear what the next generation took."],"van-halen-van-halen":["Eleven tracks, mostly short and bright, played with the energy of a bar band. Runnin' with the Devil opens on a throbbing bass and car horns; Eruption, under two minutes, is a solo guitar showpiece full of sounds no one had heard; a cover of the Kinks' You Really Got Me follows straight on. Ain't Talkin' 'bout Love and Atomic Punk show the heavier side. David Lee Roth's showman vocals and the high harmonies of Michael Anthony frame Eddie Van Halen's guitar.","The band came from Pasadena, California, where they had spent years playing parties and clubs. Produced by Ted Templeman for Warner Bros., the album was recorded quickly in 1977, mostly live in the studio with few overdubs. Eddie, son of a Dutch jazz clarinettist, had trained as a classical pianist as a child and built his own guitar from spare parts.","Listen closer: in Eruption, Eddie taps the fretboard with a finger of his picking hand, producing rapid cascades of notes. A passage near the end is often identified as coming from a violin étude by Rodolphe Kreutzer, a trace of his classical training. The piece was reportedly recorded almost by accident, when the producer heard him warming up.","It sold enormously and critics were largely charmed, if puzzled by the combination of party music and virtuosity. Guitar magazines treated Eruption as a revolution, and two-handed tapping swept through rock guitar within a few years. The debate since is whether the imitators diluted it; the original still sounds new.","Play Eruption and You Really Got Me as one piece, as the album does. Then hear the Kinks' original. Listen to Eruption next to Paganini's Twenty-Fourth Caprice to hear two instrumentalists re-inventing what their instrument can do."],"iron-maiden-the-number-of-the-beast":["Eight tracks driven by Steve Harris's galloping bass, a rapid three-note pattern like horses' hooves, and the harmonised guitars of Dave Murray and Adrian Smith. Run to the Hills tells the colonisation of America from both sides; the title track opens with a spoken passage from the Book of Revelation before Bruce Dickinson screams in; Hallowed Be Thy Name, seven minutes long, follows a condemned prisoner's last hour through slow opening, mounting speed and a long instrumental climax.","Iron Maiden came out of the East London pub scene as leaders of the New Wave of British Heavy Metal. This was their first album with Bruce Dickinson, a trained, operatic singer, recorded quickly in early 1982 with producer Martin Birch. Their mascot, a grinning corpse called Eddie drawn by Derek Riggs, appears on the sleeve as a puppet master controlling the devil.","Listen closer: Harris has said the title song grew from a nightmare after watching the film Damien: Omen II, and its imagery also draws on Robert Burns's Tam o' Shanter. The spoken introduction is by the actor Barry Clayton. The gallop in the bass is so central that it is now called, simply, the Maiden gallop.","It was the band's first British number-one album. In America, religious groups accused them of Satanism and held record burnings, which the band noted mostly helped sales; the songs, as Harris pointed out, warn against evil rather than praise it. It is regarded as one of the defining metal albums of the 1980s.","Start with Hallowed Be Thy Name and follow the tempo as it builds. Listen to the bass alone on Run to the Hills. Then hear Holst's Mars, whose relentless rhythm Maiden's gallop resembles."],"iron-maiden-rime-of-the-ancient-mariner":["A fourteen-minute retelling of Coleridge's 1798 poem, the closing track of the album Powerslave. It moves in sections: a driving opening, verses that paraphrase the poem's story of the sailor who shoots an albatross and brings a curse on his ship, a becalmed middle section of creaking timbers and a slow bass line under a spoken passage from the poem itself, then a rebuilding of speed through guitar solos to a final return of the opening theme.","Written by bassist Steve Harris, the band's main composer, and recorded with Martin Birch for Powerslave, released in 1984 at the height of the band's world touring. Harris had studied the poem at school. Heavy metal was by then a mass youth culture in Britain, Europe and America, and Maiden brought unashamedly literary subject matter to it.","Listen closer: the creaking and wind in the middle section are sound effects, played under Harris's bass line to depict the ship becalmed, a direct piece of programme music. The band quote Coleridge's own verse in the spoken passage, making it one of the few rock songs that set a canonical poem nearly as written.","Fans treat it as one of the band's greatest achievements; critics have alternately praised its ambition and laughed at a metal band reading Coleridge. English teachers have been known to play it in class. It stands with Rush's 2112 as proof that long-form narrative did not die with 1970s progressive rock.","Read the poem first, or at least its outline. Then follow the song as a story, noticing where the music stops to depict stillness. Pair it with the overture to Wagner's Flying Dutchman, another cursed sailor told in sound."],"metallica-master-of-puppets":["Eight tracks, most over six minutes, built like suites. Battery opens with gentle Spanish-sounding acoustic guitars that are suddenly overrun by fast, down-picked riffing. The title track, eight and a half minutes, frames a slow, melodic middle section with relentless riffs about addiction's control. Welcome Home (Sanitarium) builds from a ballad to fury; Orion is a long instrumental featuring Cliff Burton's melodic bass. The playing is extremely fast, exact and tight.","Metallica emerged from the San Francisco Bay Area thrash scene, which combined British metal with the speed of punk. The album was recorded in 1985 in Copenhagen with Danish producer Flemming Rasmussen and released by Elektra without a radio single or video. Bassist Cliff Burton was killed in a tour-bus accident in Sweden in September 1986, six months after its release.","Listen closer: Burton had studied music theory and loved Bach, and his classical influence is audible in the harmonised, contrapuntal lines of Orion and the chord progressions of the slow sections. The Thing That Should Not Be borrows from H. P. Lovecraft, and Welcome Home takes its setting from One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest.","It sold steadily with almost no promotion and is widely ranked as the greatest thrash album. In 2016 it became the first metal recording selected for the US Library of Congress's National Recording Registry. Debate among fans centres on whether it or its predecessor, Ride the Lightning, is the peak; almost no one places anything later above it.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the riffing drops away to a quiet, clean guitar melody. Hear Orion for Burton. Then play a Bach organ fugue for the contrapuntal line of thinking that Burton brought to the band."],"metallica-metallica":["Twelve tracks, slower, heavier and simpler than the band's thrash albums. Enter Sandman builds from a quiet guitar figure to a huge, grinding riff; Sad but True is slower still; The Unforgiven and Nothing Else Matters are ballads, the latter with an orchestral string arrangement. The sound is massive and polished, with the drums and bass given more weight than ever. Known from its all-black cover as the Black Album.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1990 and 1991 with producer Bob Rock, known for commercial hard rock. The band wanted a record that would sound as good on radio as on stage, and the sessions were long and fractious. Released in August 1991, just before Nirvana's Nevermind, it arrived as hard rock was about to be remade by grunge.","Listen closer: the strings on Nothing Else Matters were arranged by the film composer Michael Kamen, who later conducted the band with the San Francisco Symphony in 1999. Much of the album's weight comes from what is removed: shorter songs, fewer riffs per song, and the bass, inaudible on the previous album, pushed well forward.","It sold enormously and became one of the best-selling albums in America. Many thrash fans accused the band of selling out, an argument that still runs. Critics who had ignored metal took it seriously. It is now seen as the point where metal entered the mainstream on its own terms.","Start with Enter Sandman and notice how slowly it builds. Then hear Nothing Else Matters for Kamen's strings. Compare with Master of Puppets to hear what the band gave up and gained."],"slayer-reign-in-blood":["Ten tracks in under half an hour, played at speeds that seemed barely possible. Angel of Death opens with a scream and a torrent of riffing; the songs run together with almost no pauses; the guitar solos of Kerry King and Jeff Hanneman are chaotic, deliberately atonal whirls. Raining Blood closes the record with a slow, pounding introduction, a burst of speed, and then the sound of heavy rain. Dave Lombardo's double-bass drumming drives everything.","Slayer came from the Los Angeles suburbs and were signed by Rick Rubin to Def Jam, a label then known for hip-hop. Rubin produced, giving them a clean, dry sound that made the speed intelligible. Columbia, Def Jam's distributor, refused to release it because of its content, and it came out through Geffen instead.","Listen closer: the album's chaotic guitar solos are not random; they use chromatic and whole-tone patterns more familiar from twentieth-century classical music than from blues. Angel of Death describes the crimes of Josef Mengele at Auschwitz; the band insisted it was a documentary account, and the accusations of sympathy it provoked were never supported.","Initially dismissed or ignored by critics and denounced by moral campaigners, it became a foundation stone of death metal and extreme music worldwide. Rubin's production has been praised as a model of clarity. Arguments continue over Angel of Death and whether depicting atrocity without comment is responsible.","Play it straight through in half an hour. Listen to Lombardo's drums alone for a minute. Then play Penderecki's Threnody for the Victims of Hiroshima, another work that finds a musical language for mass death."],"guns-n-roses-appetite-for-destruction":["Twelve tracks of hard, bluesy rock and roll. Welcome to the Jungle opens with a guitar echo like a siren and Axl Rose's scream; Paradise City builds from a singalong to a fast, frantic ending; Sweet Child o' Mine opens on a looping, bell-like guitar figure by Slash and becomes a love song. Elsewhere the band sing about addiction, violence and life on the Los Angeles streets with a sneer that recalls the Rolling Stones and punk.","Guns N' Roses formed in Los Angeles in 1985, living in poverty on the Sunset Strip, where glam metal bands were dominant. They were signed by Geffen and recorded the album with producer Mike Clink. Its original sleeve painting was withdrawn after complaints and replaced by a cross of skulls. For almost a year it sold slowly, until MTV began to play their videos.","Listen closer: the famous guitar figure that opens Sweet Child o' Mine began as a joke finger exercise Slash played in rehearsal, jumping between strings. The guitars of Slash and Izzy Stradlin are placed hard left and right, like the Stones' Keith Richards and Ron Wood, and trade roles constantly.","It became the best-selling debut album in American history and Sweet Child o' Mine reached number one. Critics were split between those who heard the return of dangerous rock and roll and those who heard misogyny and aggression, and both views persist. It is often credited with ending the reign of glam metal and preparing the way for grunge.","Start with Welcome to the Jungle and listen for the two guitars answering each other. Then Sweet Child o' Mine. Play it next to the Stones' Let It Bleed and Aerosmith, and you will hear where the swagger came from."],"ozzy-osbourne-blizzard-of-ozz":["Nine tracks built around the guitar of Randy Rhoads. Crazy Train opens on a fast, jagged riff and a solo full of classical-sounding runs; Mr. Crowley begins with a solemn church-organ introduction and has two long guitar solos; Dee is a short piece for classical guitar alone; Revelation (Mother Earth) builds from a piano ballad to a fast, dramatic climax. Ozzy Osbourne's plaintive voice is the same, but the music around it is lighter, faster and more ornate than Sabbath.","Osbourne was fired from Black Sabbath in 1979 and was thought finished. He formed a new band with Rhoads, a young Californian guitarist who taught at his mother's music school in Burbank, and recorded the album in England in 1980 with bassist Bob Daisley and drummer Lee Kerslake. Rhoads was killed in a light-aircraft crash in Florida in March 1982, aged twenty-five.","Listen closer: Dee, written for Rhoads's mother Delores, is a small piece of classical guitar music in the style of the Spanish Romantics, and Rhoads's electric solos draw on scales and harmonic sequences from the Baroque and Romantic periods. Mr. Crowley takes its subject from the occultist Aleister Crowley, and its organ introduction was played by Don Airey.","It restored Osbourne's career and made Rhoads a guitar hero; his early death froze his reputation at its height. It is now seen as a key record in bringing classical technique into metal, preparing the neoclassical school. Suicide Solution was the subject of a 1980s lawsuit blaming it for a death; the case was dismissed.","Start with Mr. Crowley, and listen to the solos as composed pieces with a beginning, middle and end. Then play Dee and set it beside Tárrega's Recuerdos de la Alhambra. Finish with Crazy Train for the riff."],"yngwie-malmsteen-rising-force":["Eight tracks, mostly instrumental, in which the Swedish guitarist plays at speeds that set a new standard. Black Star opens with a short classical guitar introduction before the electric theme; Far Beyond the Sun is a long, fast instrumental in the harmonic minor scale; Icarus' Dream Suite, which Malmsteen gave a mock opus number, is built on the famous Adagio in G minor. His guitar sounds like a violin: bright, vibrato-heavy, and full of arpeggios swept across the strings.","Malmsteen grew up in Stockholm and moved to Los Angeles in 1983, where he played briefly with two American metal bands before this debut under his own name. Its line-up included the keyboard player Jens Johansson and drummer Barriemore Barlow, formerly of Jethro Tull. It arrived when guitar virtuosity was the subject of magazines, schools and instructional videos.","Listen closer: Malmsteen's models were not guitarists but Paganini and Bach; he has said he learned to hear the guitar as a violin after watching a television programme about Paganini. The Adagio in Icarus' Dream Suite is usually attributed to Albinoni but was largely composed in the twentieth century by Remo Giazotto, so the neoclassical source is itself a neoclassical piece.","Guitarists were overwhelmed; critics accused Malmsteen of empty speed and excess, and the charge has followed him. It founded neoclassical metal and a school of 'shred' players. Opinion has settled that the debut is his most musical record and that his influence on technique is beyond dispute.","Start with Icarus' Dream Suite and play the Adagio in G minor straight after. Then hear Paganini's Twenty-Fourth Caprice and listen for the arpeggios in both. Far Beyond the Sun is the showpiece; listen for the harmonic minor scale's exotic step."],"megadeth-rust-in-peace":["Nine tracks of fast, intricate thrash, packed with riffs that change every few bars. Holy Wars... The Punishment Due opens with a rapid, twisting riff and moves through a Spanish-sounding interlude; Hangar 18, about alien bodies hidden by the US government, is almost all guitar solo trading between Dave Mustaine and Marty Friedman; Tornado of Souls holds one of the most admired guitar solos in metal. The playing is precise, the tempos restless.","Mustaine, fired from Metallica in 1983, formed Megadeth and fought addiction through the 1980s. For this album he assembled a new line-up with guitarist Marty Friedman, a shred player who drew on Japanese and Middle Eastern scales, and drummer Nick Menza. It was released in 1990, at the end of the Cold War, and much of it is about nuclear weapons, religious conflict and government secrecy.","Listen closer: Holy Wars was prompted by the conflict in Northern Ireland, which Mustaine saw on a visit. Friedman's solos use exotic scales and bent notes that sound almost like a koto or an oud rather than blues guitar, which is why they stand out so sharply from Mustaine's more chromatic playing.","Critics praised it as the band's most disciplined album and it remains widely regarded as one of the high points of thrash. It is often set against Metallica's Black Album of the following year: one band refining thrash's complexity, the other stripping it away.","Start with Holy Wars and follow the riffs as they change. Listen to Tornado of Souls for Friedman's solo. Then play Metallica's Master of Puppets to hear two ex-bandmates' rival versions of thrash."],"pantera-vulgar-display-of-power":["Eleven tracks at mid-tempo rather than thrash speed, built on stop-start, syncopated riffs that hit like punches. Mouth for War and A New Level open with heavy, grinding grooves; Walk stomps on a lurching riff that the whole band plays together; This Love and Hollow move from quiet ballad passages into explosions. Phil Anselmo's voice ranges from a clean croon to a hardcore shout; Dimebag Darrell's guitar is thick, precise and full of squealing harmonics.","Pantera came from Arlington, Texas, where they had spent the 1980s as a glam metal band before changing direction with Anselmo on vocals. Produced by Terry Date and released in 1992, just as grunge was overturning hard rock, the album offered a harder alternative. Dimebag Darrell was shot dead on stage in Ohio in 2004, and his brother, drummer Vinnie Paul, died in 2018.","Listen closer: much of the heaviness comes from rhythm rather than speed. Walk's riff sits in a lopsided shuffle that seems to drag against the beat, and the band often stop dead for a beat before hitting again, a technique borrowed from hardcore punk. Dimebag's guitar had its mid-range scooped out, which gives it a hard, dry bite.","It sold strongly without radio support and made Pantera one of the most influential metal bands of the 1990s. Critics credit it with founding groove metal and influencing nu-metal. Its aggression and some of Anselmo's later public conduct have complicated the band's legacy.","Start with Walk and follow the riff's lurch. Then play Mouth for War for the stop-start rhythm. Compare with Black Sabbath's Master of Reality to hear how slowness and weight came back into metal after thrash."],"emperor-in-the-nightside-eclipse":["Eight long tracks in which fast, tremolo-picked guitars and blasting drums are wrapped in layers of synthesizer choirs and strings. The result is less a riff than a wall of sound: the guitars shimmer rather than chug, the voice is a high shriek buried in the mix, and above it all the keyboards play slow, grand chord sequences like a church organ or film score. Into the Infinity of Thoughts opens with a long, atmospheric build before the storm breaks.","Emperor were teenagers in the Norwegian black metal scene of the early 1990s, which rejected commercial death metal and was marked by church arsons and violence; two members were later imprisoned. The album was recorded in 1993 at the Grieghallen studio in Bergen with the engineer Eirik 'Pytten' Hundvin, who recorded much of that scene. Ihsahn, the principal composer, was eighteen.","Listen closer: the keyboards carry most of the melodic content, often moving in slow chords that resemble late-Romantic orchestral music, while the guitars supply a blur of texture. The studio sits inside the concert hall named after Edvard Grieg, and Ihsahn has cited classical and film music, as well as Bathory and Slayer, among his models.","Initially an underground cult record, it came to be seen as the founding text of symphonic black metal. Critics now regard it as one of the key extreme metal albums; the criminal history of the scene around it remains a dark shadow over any appreciation of its music.","Listen on headphones and let the texture wash over you rather than following the riffs. Focus on the keyboards and hear the harmonic movement beneath the noise. Then play Sibelius's Tapiola for another Nordic forest in sound."],"meshuggah-destroy-erase-improve":["Tracks built on riffs that repeat in odd, shifting lengths while the drummer's cymbal keeps a steady four-beat pulse, so the music seems to fold over itself. Future Breed Machine opens the album with a mechanical, stuttering riff and robotic samples. Between the riffs, Fredrik Thordendal plays fluid, jazz-fusion solos that sound utterly unlike metal. Jens Kidman's voice is a flat, rhythmic bark, used almost like another drum.","Meshuggah came from Umeå in northern Sweden and recorded this, their second album, for the German label Nuclear Blast. It appeared as extreme metal was fragmenting into subgenres. The band's interest lay in rhythm and structure more than speed or darkness, and they drew on jazz fusion, especially the guitarist Allan Holdsworth, as much as on metal.","Listen closer: tap your foot to the cymbal, then try to count the guitar riff. The riff's length does not divide into the bar, so it drifts against the pulse until the two realign many bars later, a technique closer to Steve Reich's phasing or Conlon Nancarrow's player-piano studies than to metal convention.","Little noticed at first outside metal circles, it is now seen as the seed of the 2000s 'djent' movement and of progressive metal's rhythmic turn. Musicians and theorists study the band's polymeters, and critics who once found them cold now praise their rigour.","Start with Future Breed Machine and follow the cymbal, not the guitar. Then listen for Thordendal's solos, which sound like Mahavishnu Orchestra dropped into a metal record. Compare with Reich's Clapping Music, another piece built on patterns sliding against one another."],"opeth-blackwater-park":["Eight long pieces, most around ten minutes, that alternate death-metal fury with acoustic folk and progressive-rock passages. Mikael Åkerfeldt switches between a death growl and a clean, melancholy singing voice; distorted riffs give way to fingerpicked guitars and quiet piano, then return. The Drapery Falls and the title track are built like extended suites with recurring themes, and Harvest is a gentle, almost pastoral song in the middle of the storm.","Opeth came from Stockholm and had spent the 1990s mixing death metal with 1970s progressive rock. For this, their fifth album, they worked with producer Steven Wilson of the British prog band Porcupine Tree, who added keyboards and studio textures. The title comes from an obscure early-1970s progressive band that Åkerfeldt admired.","Listen closer: Åkerfeldt's acoustic passages borrow from Swedish folk melody and 1970s English progressive rock, and themes introduced quietly early in a song often return later with full distortion. Wilson's production smooths the switches between extremes, so the joins between fury and calm are almost invisible.","It brought Opeth international recognition and is widely regarded as one of the key progressive-metal albums. Critics who disliked death growls found it a gateway, while metal purists sometimes found it too soft. Its approach influenced a generation of bands mixing extreme and progressive styles.","Start with Harvest, then play The Drapery Falls and notice how the acoustic theme at the start returns. Listen to the title track as a whole suite. Compare with King Crimson's Red for the progressive rock ancestry."],"tool-lateralus":["Thirteen tracks, some long and interlinked, in which Danny Carey's intricate drumming, Justin Chancellor's melodic bass and Adam Jones's textured guitar build slowly shifting structures in unusual meters. Schism moves through changing time signatures; the title track, nearly ten minutes, grows from a quiet bass line to a vast climax. Maynard James Keenan's voice floats above in a calm, measured way rather than shouting. Short ambient pieces link the longer songs.","Tool came from Los Angeles and were one of the most successful hard-rock bands of the 1990s while avoiding conventional promotion. Lateralus, released in May 2001, entered the American charts at the top despite its length and complexity. The band toured with King Crimson later that year, acknowledging a debt to that band's interlocking guitar and rhythm parts.","Listen closer: the title track is built on the Fibonacci sequence. Its main pattern cycles through meters of nine, eight and seven beats, and 987 is itself a Fibonacci number; the syllables of the vocal lines follow the sequence too. The same numbers have been traced, controversially, in Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta.","Schism won a Grammy for best metal performance. Critics were impressed by the ambition and some found it overlong. Among musicians it is a standard text for odd meters in rock. Its mathematical structures have been debated in countless articles and fan analyses.","Start with Schism and try to count the beats as they change. Then play the title track and notice how it grows. Compare with Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta and King Crimson's Red, both built from the same instinct for pattern."],"the-stooges-fun-house":["Seven tracks, recorded almost as the band played them live: four men in a room, turned up past the point of politeness. Side one is a run of blunt, grinding riff songs, Down on the Street, Loose, T.V. Eye and the slow, smouldering Dirt. Side two adds Steve Mackay's honking tenor saxophone and falls apart on purpose, ending in L.A. Blues, five minutes of pure feedback, screaming and free-jazz squall. The sound you remember is Ron Asheton's wah-soaked guitar grinding against Iggy Pop's howl.","The Stooges came out of Ann Arbor, Michigan, a college town near Detroit, where their neighbours the MC5 were preaching revolution. Their first album, produced by John Cale in 1969, had sold poorly. For the second, Elektra sent them to Los Angeles in May 1970 with Don Gallucci, once the organist of the Kingsmen, who had the sense to strip the studio bare and record the band standing together at full volume, much as they sounded on stage in a Detroit ballroom.","Every song was taken many times over, and in 1999 an archive box set released the complete sessions: take after take of the same handful of songs. Listening to it reveals how deliberate the chaos was. The band played these riffs night after night, and the album takes were chosen from runs that sound nearly identical. What seems like a band collapsing is a band holding a very tight groove very hard.","It sold even worse than the debut, and by 1974 the Stooges had broken up amid drugs and debt. Mainstream critics largely ignored it. Its reputation was built by the people it inspired: the London and New York punks of 1976, who treated it as scripture, and later writers such as Lester Bangs. Today it is often ranked above the debut, though some still prefer the leaner songs of the first record or the rawer Raw Power.","Start with T.V. Eye at a volume that slightly worries you; listen to how the drums and bass lock into one repeated figure while the guitar sprays over the top. Then play Fun House itself and follow the saxophone, which turns a rock band into something close to a free-jazz session. Compare it with Coltrane's Ascension to hear what Iggy was reaching for."],"patti-smith-horses":["Eight tracks in which a poet takes over a garage band. Patti Smith half-sings, half-chants, drifting from verse into free association while Lenny Kaye's guitar, Richard Sohl's piano and a plain rhythm section build slowly underneath. Short songs such as Redondo Beach, a lopsided reggae lilt, sit beside long improvised pieces: Birdland and the nine-minute Land, which swerves into an old dance-craze hit. The opening, where her own poem collides with the rock standard Gloria, is the moment everyone knows.","Smith had spent the early seventies in New York as a poet, playwright and rock critic, reading her verse over Kaye's guitar at St Mark's Church. By 1975 her band was a fixture at CBGB alongside Television, and Clive Davis signed her to his new Arista label. The album was recorded at Electric Lady Studios with John Cale of the Velvet Underground producing, a relationship both later described as combative. Robert Mapplethorpe took the plain black-and-white cover photograph.","Birdland grew from a book, Peter Reich's memoir of his father, the psychoanalyst Wilhelm Reich, and a boy's fantasy of being lifted away by a spaceship; Smith improvised it largely in the studio. Elegie, the closing track, is a quiet memorial to Jimi Hendrix, whose studio they were working in. Tom Verlaine of Television plays guitar on Break It Up, a song about a dream of Jim Morrison.","Critics received it as an event: a woman fronting a rock record with the authority of Dylan and the rawness of the garage bands, and doing so as a poet. It did modestly in the charts but became a founding text for the punk scene in both New York and London. The only lasting dispute is over Cale's production, which some hear as too polished for what the band did on stage.","Play Gloria first and listen for the point where the band finally catches up with her voice and the song ignites. Then give Land its full length without interruption; it is built like a jazz solo, rising and falling on its own logic. For comparison, hear Dylan's Bringing It All Back Home, the record that first taught rock how to sound like poetry."],"ramones-ramones":["Fourteen songs in under half an hour, almost none longer than two and a half minutes, no guitar solos, no slow numbers, every one counted in with a shouted one-two-three-four. Johnny Ramone plays nothing but fast downstroked chords, a steady buzz like a machine; Dee Dee's bass doubles him; Tommy's drums never show off. Over it, Joey sings sweet, simple bubblegum melodies about boredom, glue-sniffing, girls and comic-book violence. Blitzkrieg Bop, with its stadium chant, is the calling card.","Four young men from Forest Hills, Queens, all taking the surname Ramone, had honed their act at CBGB on the Bowery since 1974. Seymour Stein signed them to Sire, and the album was cut in February 1976 at Plaza Sound, inside the Radio City Music Hall building, for a few thousand dollars. It arrived at the height of long progressive-rock albums and polished soft rock, and on purpose it sounded like the opposite of both.","Producer Craig Leon mixed the record like an early Beatles album, with the guitar hard in one speaker and the bass in the other. Put on headphones and you can hear the band pulled apart into its parts. Among the originals sits a cover of Let's Dance, a 1962 hit for Chris Montez: the Ramones saw themselves as a sixties pop band played too fast, not as vandals.","It sold poorly in America and got little radio play, but critics in New York and London took notice at once. When the band played the Roundhouse in London on 4 July 1976, much of the future British punk scene was reportedly in the audience or nearby. Its standing has only risen since; the debate now is whether its best songs are simply great pop, which the band always claimed.","Listen straight through; the album is designed to be heard as one breathless rush, and the sameness is the point. Then listen for Joey's melodies, which are gentler and more tuneful than the noise suggests; I Wanna Be Your Boyfriend is practically a girl-group song. Try it on headphones to hear the split-channel mix, then play Marquee Moon to hear the other road out of CBGB."],"ramones-rocket-to-russia":["The Ramones' third album keeps the formula, fourteen short songs at a gallop, but gives it a cleaner, brighter sound and a much stronger pop hook. Rockaway Beach is a surf song, Sheena Is a Punk Rocker a teen anthem, and the band covers two early-sixties dance records, Do You Wanna Dance? and the Trashmen's Surfin' Bird. Between them come comic songs about lobotomies, family misery and a dance called the Cretin Hop. The guitar still buzzes, but the melodies now come first.","Recorded in the summer of 1977 and produced by Tommy Ramone with Tony Bongiovi, it came out as British punk was peaking and Sire was pushing hard for an American radio hit. The band wanted to be the Beach Boys and the Ronettes as much as the Stooges, and this is the record where that ambition is clearest. Its release was overshadowed by Elvis Presley's death that August, and it never became the hit the label hoped for.","Rockaway Beach was written by Dee Dee Ramone, the band's surfer, about the beach at the end of the A train in Queens; it is a deliberate tribute to the Beach Boys, with the handclaps and the stacked harmony vocals, played at double speed. Joey sings every harmony himself, overdubbed, which is why the backing vocals sound so eerily like him.","Reviews were warm and many critics now call it the band's most complete album, the point where the joke and the craft balance perfectly. It sold better than the first two but still disappointed the band. The central argument about the Ramones, whether they were a primitive gesture or a great songwriting band, is usually settled in favour of songwriting on the strength of this record.","Start with Rockaway Beach and Sheena Is a Punk Rocker back to back, and hear how much sixties pop is hiding inside the noise. Then compare their Surfin' Bird with the 1963 original: the Ramones barely changed it, which says everything about where they came from. Pair it with the Buzzcocks' singles for the British cousin."],"television-marquee-moon":["Eight songs built around two guitars that talk to each other rather than one leading and one strumming. Tom Verlaine and Richard Lloyd weave clean, bright, trebly lines in and out like a string duet, while Fred Smith and Billy Ficca play with a jazz lightness. Verlaine's voice is thin and strained, his words oblique. The ten-minute title track is the centrepiece: a clipped, interlocking riff that opens out into a long solo climbing steadily towards a shimmering peak, then settles back.","Television had helped invent the CBGB scene in 1974, persuading the club's owner to let them play original songs. Their early bassist Richard Hell left to form his own band, taking the torn-shirt look that London would copy. By the time Elektra signed them, they were the scene's most musically ambitious band. The album was recorded in New York in late 1976 with Andy Johns, known for his work with Led Zeppelin, co-producing with Verlaine.","Listen to how rarely the two guitars play the same thing. The parts are composed to interlock, so that each fills the rests in the other; the effect is closer to a Bach invention than to rock rhythm guitar. Verlaine loved Albert Ayler and Coltrane, and his long solos grow from a small motif the way a jazz improvisation does. The title track is reportedly a first take.","It sold modestly in America but reached the British top thirty, where the young post-punk bands heard it as proof that punk did not have to be crude. Critics' polls have kept it near the top of the decade's rock albums ever since. The argument it still provokes is whether Television were a punk band at all, or the last great guitar band of the sixties tradition.","Play the title track first and resist skipping: track the two guitars, one in each speaker, and notice when the solo begins to climb. Then try See No Evil and Venus for the tighter, song-sized version of the same interplay. Afterwards play the Ramones' debut, made a few blocks away the same year, to hear how wide the scene was."],"sex-pistols-never-mind-the-bollocks":["Twelve songs, including all four singles, played at mid-tempo rather than the breakneck speed people remember. Steve Jones's guitars are layered into a thick, glossy wall; Paul Cook's drums are steady and heavy. Over it, Johnny Rotten sneers, rolls his r's and spits consonants, turning songs about the monarchy, the record industry, abortion, boredom and bad holidays into accusations. Holidays in the Sun opens with the sound of marching boots; Anarchy in the U.K. is the declaration of intent.","Managed by Malcolm McLaren, who ran a clothes shop on the King's Road, the Pistols had been dropped by EMI after swearing on teatime television and by A&M after a week. Virgin finally released the album in October 1977, after the singles had already caused national scandal. Glen Matlock, who co-wrote most of the songs, had been replaced by Sid Vicious, who could barely play. Chris Thomas, who had worked with Roxy Music and on Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon, produced.","Most of the bass is not Sid Vicious but Steve Jones, who overdubbed guitar after guitar and much of the bass line as well; Vicious reportedly appears clearly only on Bodies. The album's power comes from careful studio work by a craftsman producer, which is the quiet joke under punk's amateur myth. The band's attack on the industry, E.M.I., is a meticulously polished product of it.","Record shops that displayed it were threatened with prosecution under indecency laws; a Nottingham case against a Virgin store failed. It went to number one in Britain regardless. Some punks found it too slick, and the band split within three months. Its standing as one of rock's most influential records is secure; the argument is whether its songs or its surrounding scandal mattered more.","Play Holidays in the Sun and Anarchy in the U.K. loud and listen to the guitars; there are far more of them than a four-piece should have. Pay attention to Rotten's phrasing, which bends every word into sarcasm. Then play Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon, made in the same studio culture four years earlier, and hear what the Pistols were attacking."],"sex-pistols-god-save-the-queen":["A three-minute single that borrows the title of the national anthem and turns it against the Crown. A chugging, mid-paced riff, doubled and redoubled on guitar, drives under Johnny Rotten's vocal, which treats the monarchy as a tourist attraction propping up a dead system and ends with a chant denying the country any future. The tune is catchy, almost a pub singalong, which is exactly what made it dangerous.","It was released in May 1977 to coincide with Queen Elizabeth II's Silver Jubilee, when Britain was hung with bunting. A&M had pressed copies and then dropped the band, destroying most of them; Virgin put it out instead. The BBC banned it, many shops refused to stock it, and on 7 June the band played it from a boat on the Thames, ending in arrests. Jamie Reid's sleeve defaced the Queen's official portrait with ransom-note lettering.","The song was written before the Jubilee was on anyone's mind, under the title No Future; the timing was McLaren's marketing coup. And the official chart is itself part of the story: the single stalled at number two behind Rod Stewart in Jubilee week, while many in the industry believed it was actually outselling him, and the suspicion of a fix became part of its legend.","Members of the band were attacked in the street in the weeks after release. For its defenders it was the most direct political statement a British pop record had made; for its critics it was provocation for sale. Decades later it is played on the BBC it was banned from, and the argument has shifted to how far its rebellion was a manager's shop-window stunt.","Listen once for the riff and the tune, which are much more conventional than their reputation. Then listen for Rotten's delivery, how he stretches and snarls single words. Afterwards play Walton's Crown Imperial or Elgar's first Pomp and Circumstance march, the ceremonial music of the same monarchy, to hear precisely the sound this record set out to puncture."],"the-clash-the-clash":["Fourteen fast, shouting songs about being young and broke in London: dead-end jobs, tower blocks, riot, boredom with American culture. Joe Strummer's hoarse bark alternates with Mick Jones's higher, more tuneful voice; the guitars are thin, trebly and urgent. Then, in the middle of side two, the band slows to a crawl and plays a six-minute cover of Police and Thieves, a Jamaican reggae song, and the whole record changes colour. White Riot and London's Burning are the anthems.","Formed in 1976 around Strummer, Jones and Paul Simonon, the Clash signed to CBS for a large advance, which some punks saw as betrayal. The album was cut in a few weekends at CBS's London studio with their sound engineer Mickey Foote producing, and Terry Chimes on drums. They lived in west London near the Notting Hill Carnival, where the 1976 clashes between young Black Londoners and police had shaped their politics, and they heard reggae constantly.","Police and Thieves was written and sung by Junior Murvin and produced by Lee Scratch Perry at his Black Ark studio in Kingston in 1976. The Clash made no attempt to play it like Jamaicans; they played it as a punk band feeling for a heavy offbeat, and the gap is audible. Perry reportedly disliked the cover at first, but it led directly to their working together within months.","British critics hailed it at once as the definitive punk album. CBS in America judged it too crude and held it back until 1979, releasing an altered version; meanwhile it became one of the best-selling import records in the United States. Arguments continue over which version is better and whether the band's later sophistication improved on this directness.","Start with Janie Jones and London's Burning to catch the rush, then go straight to Police and Thieves and hear the band switch gears. Listen to how Strummer and Jones trade lines like two street-corner speakers. For the source, hunt down Junior Murvin's original Police and Thieves, and hear how Perry's production floats where the Clash's version marches."],"the-clash-complete-control":["A furious three-minute single in which the Clash complain about their own record company and their own management. Mick Jones's guitar builds in sheets of chords, Topper Headon's drums push hard, and Strummer's shouted verses give way to a chorus the band yells together. Near the end the song boils over: Strummer calls out Jones's guitar by name, and Jones answers with a ragged, overdriven solo that sounds like the tape is about to split.","CBS had released Remote Control from the band's first album as a single without asking them, and the song is their public reply, aimed also at manager Bernie Rhodes and at the business of punk itself. It was produced by Lee Scratch Perry, the Jamaican producer, who was in London in the summer of 1977 working with Bob Marley. The Clash had covered Perry's Police and Thieves; this was the result of their meeting.","What you hear is not quite what Perry left. He reportedly tamed the guitars and brought up the drums and bass in the Jamaican manner, and the band and Mickey Foote later remixed it to push the guitars back to the front. Listen underneath, though, and the echo on the drums and the space around Simonon's bass still carry Perry's fingerprints: a dub producer's ears on a punk record.","Critics at the time called it one of the best singles of the year, and many still rank it as the Clash's finest. It reached the lower half of the British top forty. Strummer himself pointed to it as the moment the band stopped just reacting and started commenting on its own position. It is often cited as the first real collaboration between punk and reggae production.","Listen for the build: the song keeps promising a release and withholding it until the final minute. Pay attention to the drums, which have a boomy, roomy echo quite unlike the dry first album. Then compare with the Clash's Police and Thieves, and afterwards with London Calling, where this reggae sensibility becomes part of their whole language."],"the-clash-london-calling":["A double album of nineteen songs that treats punk as one style among many. There is rockabilly (Brand New Cadillac), ska (Rudie Can't Fail), reggae (Paul Simonon's own Guns of Brixton), New Orleans rhythm and blues with horns (Jimmy Jazz), soul, and pure pop (Train in Vain). The title track opens it: a marching, ominous rhythm, stabbing guitar and Strummer's warning voice announcing floods, meltdowns and war. The sound is fuller and warmer than before, with piano, organ and a brass section.","By 1979 the Clash had broken with their manager, were in debt to CBS and had lost their rehearsal room. They rehearsed in a garage-studio in Pimlico and recorded at Wessex Studios in north London with Guy Stevens, a wild veteran producer who threw chairs and ladders to raise the energy. Britain had just elected Margaret Thatcher; unemployment was rising; the Three Mile Island nuclear accident was fresh. They insisted the double album be sold for the price of a single.","Train in Vain, the most radio-friendly song, is not listed on the original sleeve: it was added so late that the artwork had already been printed. The cover photograph by Pennie Smith, Simonon smashing his bass on stage in New York, sits under pink and green lettering copied from Elvis Presley's 1956 debut album, a deliberate claim to the whole rock and roll lineage.","It was a critical triumph on both sides of the Atlantic and gave the band their first American hit. Because it was released in the United States in January 1980, Rolling Stone later named it the best album of the 1980s. Some early punks saw its range as a retreat into rock tradition; that view has almost vanished, and it is now the band's most celebrated record.","Hear it as a double album, a side at a time. Begin with the title track, then Guns of Brixton for the reggae bass, then Lost in the Supermarket for Mick Jones's softer pop. Notice how the horns and piano change what a punk band can be. Afterwards play the band's first album to hear how far they travelled in two and a half years."],"buzzcocks-spiral-scratch":["A four-song seven-inch EP, about ten minutes long, recorded in an afternoon. Howard Devoto's flat, arch voice sneers over Pete Shelley's scratchy guitar and a rhythm section that barely holds together. Breakdown and Time's Up are tight bursts of nerves; Boredom is the famous one, its whole guitar solo made from two notes repeated over and over, a joke on the virtuoso rock solo that is also genuinely thrilling. Friends of Mine closes it at a sprint.","Devoto and Shelley, students in Bolton, had travelled to London to see the Sex Pistols in early 1976 and then booked them into Manchester's Lesser Free Trade Hall that summer, gigs now credited with starting the city's music scene. With money borrowed from family and friends they recorded Spiral Scratch in December 1976 and released it themselves on their own label, New Hormones, in January 1977. Martin Hannett, credited as Martin Zero, produced.","The back cover credits list how many takes and overdubs each song needed, as if the band were proud of how little work had gone into it. That honesty was part of the message: here was a record anyone could make. Hannett, just beginning, would go on to produce Joy Division, and Devoto left the Buzzcocks within weeks to form Magazine.","The first thousand copies sold out by mail order and through a handful of shops, and it was repressed many times. It is now regarded less for its music than for what it proved: that a band could bypass the record industry entirely. The independent labels of the next decade, Factory and Rough Trade among them, are its direct descendants.","Play Boredom and count the notes in the solo; then notice how exciting two notes can be when the band behind them is this nervous. Listen to how thin and cheap the recording sounds, and hear that as part of the point. Follow it with Ever Fallen in Love, made after Devoto left, to hear Shelley's pop gift take over."],"buzzcocks-ever-fallen-in-love":["A two-and-a-half-minute love song at punk speed. John Maher's drums open with a rolling tom pattern, the guitars kick in with a descending hook, and Pete Shelley sings in a high, pleading voice about loving someone who is wrong for you, circling back to a title question he cannot answer. There is no solo to speak of; the song is chorus, verse, chorus, with the guitar hook repeated between, and it ends almost before you notice.","Released in September 1978 and included on the band's second album, Love Bites, it was produced by Martin Rushent for United Artists, part of a run of singles that made the Buzzcocks the great pop band of British punk. Shelley, now the singer, wrote about love from a deliberately unspecified point of view; he was openly bisexual, and his songs rarely say whether the beloved is a man or a woman.","The title phrase reportedly came from the 1955 film musical Guys and Dolls, which Shelley caught on television while staying in a guesthouse in Edinburgh; a line of dialogue lodged in his head. Listen, too, to the guitars: there are two parts, one chiming, one chugging, and the tension between them gives the song its sense of being pulled two ways.","It reached the British top twenty and became the band's best-known song, later covered by Fine Young Cannibals and many others. Critics now treat Buzzcocks' singles as proof that punk could make perfect pop, and Shelley's ungendered love songs are increasingly written about as a quiet landmark in queer pop history.","Listen for the drum pattern at the start, which is as memorable as the tune. Then follow Shelley's voice as it climbs in the chorus, straining just enough to sound sincere. Put the Buzzcocks' singles compilation Singles Going Steady on next, then the Ramones' Rocket to Russia, for the American version of the same fast, sweet pop."],"wire-pink-flag":["Twenty-one songs in thirty-five minutes, several under a minute, one under thirty seconds. Wire take punk's economy to the point of abstraction: a song states an idea, repeats it once and stops, often mid-thought. Colin Newman's clipped voice, Bruce Gilbert's blocky guitar chords and Robert Gotobed's dead-straight drumming leave a lot of air. Some songs are pop (Mannequin), some are drones (the title track), some are just a riff and a shout (12XU). The abrupt endings are the thing you remember.","Wire came together among art-school students in London in 1976, arriving at punk from painting, graphic design and conceptual art rather than from rock and roll. EMI's Harvest label, home of Pink Floyd, signed them and assigned the producer Mike Thorne. They played at the Roxy club in Covent Garden, where the scene was already hardening into a uniform, and they set out, in their own account, to remove everything from rock that was not necessary.","The shortness is a method, not a mood: songs end the moment their idea is used up, which makes the album feel like a sketchbook of possible punk songs. Listen to Three Girl Rhumba; its opening guitar figure became, nearly twenty years later, the basis of Elastica's hit Connection, a borrowing settled out of court.","It sold little on release, and some critics found it too cold. Its reputation grew steadily as American hardcore bands took up its brevity (Minor Threat covered 12XU) and as later British bands cited it. It is now often named as the first great art-punk album and the bridge from punk to post-punk.","Listen straight through without pausing; the effect accumulates. Notice how each song ends: rarely on a big chord, usually just stopping. Try Ex Lion Tamer and Mannequin for the pop side, then the title track for the drone. Then play Pink Floyd's Echoes, twenty-three minutes long on the same label, to hear the idea Wire were arguing against."],"wire-chairs-missing":["Wire's second album keeps the short-song discipline but slows down and opens up. Synthesizers and keyboard washes appear; guitars are treated with echo and chorus; songs such as French Film Blurred and Mercy stretch into slow, circling moods. Outdoor Miner, a gentle, strange pop song about an insect that burrows through leaves, is the heart of it, with a piano line added for the single. I Am the Fly and Practice Makes Perfect keep the angular bite.","Made at Advision Studios in London in 1978, again with Mike Thorne, who now played keyboards on the record and effectively became a fifth member. By then punk's first wave had burned out and the British post-punk scene, with Joy Division, Magazine and Siouxsie and the Banshees, was forming. Wire, never comfortable in punk's uniform, used the studio as an instrument, influenced by the art-rock records of Roxy Music and Brian Eno.","The single of Outdoor Miner was lengthened for radio by adding a new middle section, a rippling piano solo played by Thorne, because the album version was judged too short to be played on air. It is a nice irony for the band of the twenty-eight-second song: they had to pad their best pop single to make it respectable.","Reviews were mixed at first, with some punks hearing betrayal in the synthesizers and slower tempos; others heard progress. It now stands with Pink Flag and 154 as one of a trio of albums often called the most inventive run of post-punk records. Later bands from R.E.M. to My Bloody Valentine cited it.","Start with Outdoor Miner, then French Film Blurred, and listen to how much space is left in the sound. Notice the synthesizer colours Thorne adds without ever taking over. Compare the sound world with Brian Eno's Another Green World, released three years earlier, which showed rock musicians how to build songs from texture."],"joy-division-unknown-pleasures":["Ten songs that sound as though recorded in an empty concrete building at night. Stephen Morris's drums are clipped and precise, Peter Hook's bass plays melody high up the neck, Bernard Sumner's guitar comes and goes in bursts, and Ian Curtis sings in a deep, level baritone about loss of control, isolation and dread. Disorder opens at a nervous gallop; She's Lost Control, with its hissing machine-like drums, and Shadowplay are the best known. Silence and echo are as present as the instruments.","Joy Division formed in Salford and Manchester after the Sex Pistols' 1976 gigs there, and first called themselves Warsaw after a track on David Bowie's Low. Tony Wilson's new independent label Factory released the album in June 1979, recorded at Strawberry Studios in Stockport with Martin Hannett. Manchester was a city of empty mills and redevelopment; Curtis, working at a job centre, had recently been diagnosed with epilepsy.","Hannett treated the band as raw material. He had Morris record drums piece by piece, fed them through a new digital delay unit, added sounds of breaking glass and a lift shaft, and pushed everything into cavernous space. The band, who were loud and heavy on stage, reportedly disliked the result. Peter Saville's sleeve shows radio signals from the first pulsar ever discovered, stacked into a white-on-black mountain range.","Reviews were strong and it sold steadily through independent shops, though it was not a hit. Its reputation grew enormously after Curtis's death in 1980. The long-running argument is Hannett's: whether he made the record or stole it from the band. Most listeners now accept that the eerie space he created is inseparable from what the album means.","Start with Disorder and pay attention to Hook's bass, which carries the tune while the guitar does the rhythm. Then play She's Lost Control and New Dawn Fades. Listen on good headphones to hear Hannett's space around each instrument. Next, try Bowie's Low, the record that named them, and Closer, where the sound grows darker still."],"joy-division-closer":["Nine songs, darker and more varied than the debut. Atrocity Exhibition opens with tribal tom-toms and a guitar that sounds like metal being scraped; Isolation is driven by a thin, bright synthesizer pulse; Heart and Soul is a slow, circling trance. The album ends with two slow pieces, The Eternal and Decades, built on hushed keyboards and a funeral pace, with Curtis's voice deeper and more resigned than ever. The last side is the part people remember.","Recorded in March 1980 at Britannia Row in London, a studio owned by Pink Floyd, again with Martin Hannett. The band had toured hard; Curtis's epilepsy was worsening under heavy medication, and his marriage was failing. On 18 May 1980, the night before Joy Division were due to fly to their first American tour, he took his own life at twenty-three. Closer was released two months later, and everyone heard it as a farewell.","The sleeve, designed by Peter Saville with a photograph of a tomb in Staglieno Cemetery in Genoa, was chosen before Curtis died; the funereal image that seems to speak so directly was an accident of timing that upset the band. Listen too for the synthesizers, which Sumner and Morris were just beginning to use; they point straight towards the electronic music of New Order.","It was received with reverence, shaded by grief, and reached the British top ten. Critics have long debated how much to read the lyrics as a suicide note; the band and Curtis's family have resisted that reading, pointing out that the songs were written as art. It is often placed beside Unknown Pleasures as one half of post-punk's central statement.","Hear side two as one piece: Heart and Soul through Decades, without interruption. Then go back to Isolation and notice how close the rhythm is to dance music, the seed of what came next. Listen to Siouxsie and the Banshees' Juju, made a year later, for another take on the same gothic space."],"joy-division-love-will-tear-us-apart":["A three-and-a-half-minute single in which a love song is turned inside out. The drums rattle fast and bright, Peter Hook's bass plays the melody, and a thin, high synthesizer line hangs over it like a second voice. Curtis sings in a calm, tired baritone about a marriage going cold, about routine and resentment, and the title line comes back like a verdict. It is the most tuneful thing Joy Division made, and the saddest.","Recorded at Strawberry Studios in early 1980 with Martin Hannett, and released by Factory in June, a month after Curtis's death. Curtis was then married, with a baby daughter, and had begun a relationship with another woman; the song is widely heard as being about his own marriage. It reached the British top twenty, the band's first real hit, and Curtis never saw it.","The title is usually taken as a bitter reply to Love Will Keep Us Together, a sunny 1975 hit by the Captain and Tennille. The title is also the inscription on Curtis's memorial stone in Macclesfield, chosen by his widow. There are two different recorded versions, and the one on the single is not the one the band first cut.","It has become Joy Division's signature, voted repeatedly among the best singles ever made in British polls and covered by artists from Paul Young to Swans. Some fans prefer the band's more severe album tracks, but few dispute that this is where Curtis's writing and the band's playing meet most perfectly.","Listen for how the song is built: the bass carries the tune, the guitar is almost incidental, and the synthesizer line is what stays in your head. Then listen to Curtis, who never raises his voice. Next hear New Order's Blue Monday, made by the same three survivors three years later, and notice the thread of Hook's bass running straight through."],"public-image-ltd-metal-box":["An hour of music built on bass. Jah Wobble's deep, round bass lines repeat for minutes on end, like Jamaican dub; the drums lock onto them; over the top, Keith Levene's guitar clangs, scratches and shimmers like broken glass, and John Lydon wails and chants. Albatross opens with ten minutes of this; Poptones rocks gently while Lydon narrates something grim; Swan Lake, also known as Death Disco, twists a famous ballet tune into a howl of grief. Songs dissolve into long grooves rather than choruses.","After the Sex Pistols collapsed in early 1978, Lydon, dropping the name Rotten, formed Public Image Ltd with Levene, who had been an early member of the Clash, and Wobble, a non-musician friend who learned bass from reggae records. Lydon had already chosen reggae and dub records for a well-known London radio show. Virgin released the album in November 1979 as three twelve-inch discs at 45 rpm inside a film-canister tin, to get the deepest possible bass.","In Swan Lake, Levene's guitar plays, distorted and out of shape, the famous oboe theme from Tchaikovsky's ballet, while Lydon sings about watching his mother die of cancer. Listen, too, for the drums: the band went through several drummers during the sessions and Levene played some parts himself, which is why the kit sounds different from track to track.","Critics called it a radical break, and it quickly became the model for a whole strand of post-punk that treated rock as a groove to be taken apart. The tin was impractical and damaged the records, and it was soon reissued as a double album, Second Edition. The main debate now is which of the three players was most responsible for its sound.","Put on Albatross, turn up the bass until you can feel it, and let it run; listen to how little the bass line changes. Then play Swan Lake and try to catch the Tchaikovsky theme inside the noise. Afterwards go back to Can's Tago Mago, a record Lydon admired, and to any King Tubby dub album you can find."],"gang-of-four-entertainment":["Twelve songs that sound like funk played by a band that distrusts pleasure. Dave Allen's bass and Hugo Burnham's drums lay down tight, danceable grooves; Andy Gill's guitar cuts across them in short, metallic slashes, then stops dead, leaving holes. Jon King's lyrics, often argued between two voices, pick apart love, leisure, advertising and the Northern Ireland conflict. Damaged Goods and At Home He's a Tourist are the singles; Anthrax, which ends it, opens with a minute of pure feedback.","The band met as students at Leeds University, where the art school was steeped in Marxist theory, Situationism and the politics of Rock Against Racism. Leeds in the late seventies was marked by unemployment and National Front marches. Their first EP came out on the small Fast Product label in 1978; EMI signed them, and this album followed in September 1979, taking its title as an ironic comment on what a pop record is supposed to be.","On Anthrax, two vocals run at once in separate channels: in one, King sings a love song that refuses to be a love song; in the other, Gill speaks flatly about why the band do not write love songs. Listen on headphones and you can follow either. The band walked away from a Top of the Pops appearance rather than change a single word in At Home He's a Tourist about contraceptives.","Reviews were excellent, sales modest. Its influence proved enormous: American bands from R.E.M. and the Red Hot Chili Peppers to the dance-punk revival of the 2000s cited Gill's guitar and Allen's bass. Some find its politics heavy-handed; the usual defence is that the music makes the argument, since the gaps in the rhythm are as political as the lyrics.","Start with Damaged Goods and listen to the guitar: count the silences. Then play Anthrax on headphones and switch your attention between left and right. For the kinship, hear Weill and Brecht's Threepenny Opera, whose method of making the audience think rather than swoon is the same idea in theatre."],"talking-heads-fear-of-music":["Eleven songs, most titled with a single plain word: Mind, Paper, Cities, Air, Heaven, Animals, Drugs. David Byrne's anxious, yelping voice worries at ordinary things as if they were threats. The music is tense and twitchy, full of stiff funk rhythms and odd textures. I Zimbra opens it with African-style percussion and a chant of nonsense syllables; Life During Wartime, a nervous dance song about living underground in a city at war, is the best known; Heaven is a slow, quiet ballad about a place where nothing happens.","Talking Heads came out of the Rhode Island School of Design and the CBGB scene of 1975. This was their second album produced with Brian Eno, and it was recorded partly in the loft of drummer Chris Frantz and bassist Tina Weymouth, using a mobile recording truck parked outside. New York in 1979 was a city of arson, near-bankruptcy and blackouts, and that unease runs through the lyrics.","The words of I Zimbra are not an African language at all: they come from Gadji beri bimba, a sound poem by the Zurich Dada poet Hugo Ball, written in 1916. Robert Fripp plays the chiming guitar on it. The sleeve is black and embossed to feel like the diamond-plate metal of a subway grating, an idea of keyboardist Jerry Harrison.","Reviews were strongly positive and it did respectably in America. Critics have often treated it as the transitional record between the art-punk of the early Heads and the polyrhythmic Remain in Light, and it is sometimes overshadowed by both. More recent writers have argued that it is their most original and unsettling album.","Start with Life During Wartime, then I Zimbra, then Heaven, to hear the album's three moods: panic, rhythm and stillness. Listen to how Eno places odd sounds at the edges of the mix. Then play Eno's Another Green World and Bowie's Heroes, where Fripp's guitar does something similar."],"talking-heads-remain-in-light":["Eight tracks, each built from many short, interlocking parts, guitars, bass, percussion, keyboards, that repeat without changing chords, stacked until they form a dense, shifting pattern like a West African drum ensemble. Byrne sings in a stream of half-sermon, half-list over the top, often answered by a chorus of voices. Born Under Punches and Crosseyed and Painless are driving funk; Once in a Lifetime, with its preacher's cadence and floating chorus, is the famous one; the last side turns dark and slow.","Talking Heads and Brian Eno went to Compass Point Studios in the Bahamas in 1980 with no songs, only the idea of building tracks from grooves. Byrne and Eno had been listening to Fela Kuti's Afrobeat and reading John Miller Chernoff's book on African rhythm. The band was expanded with guitarist Adrian Belew, trumpeter Jon Hassell and singer Nona Hendryx, and the tracks were assembled by looping and editing tape.","Byrne wrote most of the words after the music was finished, improvising nonsense syllables over the grooves and then finding words to fit their shapes; much of Once in a Lifetime came from imitating radio evangelists. Listen to the trumpet on The Overload and Houses in Motion: that blurred, electronically treated sound is Jon Hassell, whose Fourth World ideas shaped the whole record.","Critics hailed it at once as a breakthrough, and it now sits near the top of lists of the best albums of the 1980s. It also caused lasting rifts: Frantz and Weymouth felt the songwriting credits favoured Byrne and Eno. Some writers have questioned the ethics of borrowing African music, while others note that the band credited their sources openly and were widely heard in Africa itself.","Play Born Under Punches and try following a single part, the bass, then one guitar, then the percussion, noticing how each is simple and the sum is not. Then play Once in a Lifetime and The Overload. Afterwards, hear Steve Reich's Drumming, built on the same West African principle of interlocking cells, and Miles Davis's On the Corner."],"siouxsie-and-the-banshees-the-scream":["Ten songs of stark, jagged sound. John McKay's guitar grinds and shrieks, Steven Severin's bass pounds, Kenny Morris plays mostly on the tom-toms rather than the cymbals, and Siouxsie Sioux sings in a cold, commanding voice, never pleading. Songs deal with suburban breakdown, a butcher's shop, anonymity in the crowd. Jigsaw Feeling and Mirage are fast and cutting; Switch closes it in shifting sections. Their version of the Beatles' Helter Skelter, slowed and stripped, turns the song menacing.","Siouxsie and Severin were part of the Bromley contingent, young Bowie and Roxy Music fans who followed the Sex Pistols in 1976. The Banshees first played at the 100 Club Punk Festival that September, improvising for twenty minutes with Sid Vicious on drums. Record labels were slow to sign them; fans sprayed graffiti demanding it. Polydor finally did in 1978, and the album was co-produced with a young Steve Lillywhite.","Metal Postcard is about John Heartfield, the German artist whose photomontages mocked the Nazis in the 1930s; the song is an anti-fascist statement at a time when punk's flirtation with Nazi imagery was a live controversy, including in Siouxsie's own early wardrobe. Listen, too, for the drums: Morris's avoidance of cymbals gives the whole album its hollow, tribal thud.","Reviews were largely enthusiastic and the album reached the British top twenty. Critics quickly heard it as one of the first records to move past punk into something new; later writers credit it as a founding record of gothic rock, a label the band always rejected. Some have argued that its reputation has been overshadowed by later, more polished Banshees albums.","Start with Jigsaw Feeling and Mirage for the edge, then Helter Skelter for the menace; compare it with the Beatles' original. Listen for the drums, which avoid the cymbals almost entirely. Follow with Joy Division's Unknown Pleasures, released the next year, to hear another way of making punk cold and spacious."],"siouxsie-and-the-banshees-juju":["Nine songs of dark, glittering sound built around John McGeoch's guitar, which ripples in fast, chiming patterns and flanged swirls rather than chords, and Budgie's tumbling tom-tom drumming. Spellbound opens it at a gallop, guitars cascading; Arabian Knights sways over an Eastern-sounding figure; Halloween and Night Shift turn slow and menacing; Voodoo Dolly closes with seven minutes of building tension. Siouxsie's voice is stronger and more theatrical than before, now riding the band rather than cutting through it.","By 1980 the original guitarist and drummer had walked out, and the band had rebuilt with Budgie, formerly of the Slits, and McGeoch, formerly of Magazine. Juju, released in June 1981 and co-produced with Nigel Gray, was their third album with Polydor and their most commercially successful to that point. Britain was in recession and riots broke out in several cities that summer.","Listen to McGeoch's guitar on Spellbound: he often used a fast acoustic-like strum alongside flanged electric arpeggios, so that the guitar seems to glitter. Many guitarists of the next decade, including Johnny Marr and the Edge, cited him. The songs lean into horror films and folk tales, a playful theatre of darkness rather than despair, which is where they differ from Joy Division.","Critics praised it and it reached the British top ten. Its reputation has grown as the gothic rock it anticipated became a vast international scene; the band continued to reject the label. Many fans and writers now consider it the Banshees' finest record, especially for McGeoch's playing.","Start with Spellbound and follow McGeoch's guitar, then Arabian Knights and Night Shift. Notice how Budgie's drums roll around the kit rather than keeping a plain beat. Then compare with The Scream to hear how far the band travelled in three years, and with Joy Division's Closer for the darker cousin."],"the-fall-hex-enduction-hour":["An hour of relentless, repetitive rock with two drummers pounding at once, a bass locked on one figure, guitars scraping and droning, and Mark E. Smith's voice ranting, sneering and muttering over it all. Songs rarely change chord and go on for many minutes. The Classical opens it at full tilt; Hip Priest is slow, creeping and funny; Winter is a long hypnotic drone; And This Day ends the album in ten minutes of controlled chaos. Smith's voice, part poet, part pub bore, holds it together.","The Fall formed in Prestwich, north Manchester, in 1976 around Smith, the only permanent member through dozens of line-ups. By 1982 they had left Rough Trade and put this out on the small Kamera label. Part of it was recorded in a studio in Reykjavík during an Icelandic tour and part in a disused cinema in Hitchin, Hertfordshire. Thatcher's Britain, with its unemployment and the Falklands conflict brewing, supplied Smith's targets.","Smith's reference points were not other rock bands but Captain Beefheart and the German group Can, whose singer he later saluted in the song I Am Damo Suzuki. The two drummers do not simply double each other; they play slightly different patterns, which gives the grooves their lurching, lumbering feel. Hip Priest later turned up on the soundtrack of The Silence of the Lambs.","It sold better than expected and the disc jockey John Peel, the band's great champion, played it constantly. Critics now often name it as the Fall's best album, though with more than thirty studio records there is no consensus. The perennial argument about the Fall is whether Smith was a great writer or a great provocateur; this album is the strongest case for both.","Start with Hip Priest, then The Classical; follow the two drummers, one in each channel. Do not expect choruses; listen to Smith as you would a stand-up comic or a street preacher. Then hear Can's Tago Mago and Beefheart's Trout Mask Replica, the two records behind the Fall's method."],"devo-q-are-we-not-men":["Eleven songs of jerky, robotic rock. The rhythms are stiff and stop-start, the guitars chop in short spasms, synthesizers bleep and burble, and Mark Mothersbaugh's voice yelps and hiccups. The Devo version of (I Can't Get No) Satisfaction, with the famous riff removed and replaced by a twitching new pattern, is the record's calling card; Jocko Homo is the manifesto, a chant-and-response about humanity going backwards; Mongoloid and Uncontrollable Urge are the other favourites.","Devo came out of Kent State University in Akron, Ohio, where Gerald Casale had been a student during the 1970 National Guard shootings. From that, and from the collapse of Akron's rubber industry, came their theory of de-evolution: humanity was regressing. David Bowie and Iggy Pop championed them; Brian Eno produced the album at Conny Plank's studio near Cologne in West Germany, the room where Kraftwerk's and Neu!'s early records were made.","The name Jocko Homo comes from a 1924 anti-evolution pamphlet, one of the found objects Devo loved. The album is quieter about its synthesizers than you might expect: Eno reportedly added many electronic sounds that the band then turned down or removed, and the working relationship was tense. Their Satisfaction works as a critique of the original, draining the swagger from it.","Reviews were divided, some critics finding them a novelty act; others heard a sharp satire of American conformity. It reached the British top twenty. Their breakthrough came later with Whip It, but this debut is now usually rated their best, and its fusion of rock, electronics and concept has come to look prophetic.","Play Satisfaction beside the Rolling Stones' original and listen for what has been removed. Then play Jocko Homo, which switches between an odd meter and a straight beat. Listen for the synthesizer sounds at the edges. Next, try Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, released the same year, for the European version of the same idea."],"blondie-parallel-lines":["Twelve songs that turn the CBGB scene into polished pop. Hanging on the Telephone opens with a ringing phone and a breathless rush; One Way or Another is a stalking rocker; Sunday Girl is sixties girl-group pop; Picture This is sweet and wry. Heart of Glass, the album's biggest hit, rides a disco beat locked to a drum machine and a bubbling synthesizer. Debbie Harry's cool, knowing voice ties it all together, playful and detached at once.","Blondie, formed by Harry and guitarist Chris Stein, had been a CBGB band since 1974 but were regarded as lightweight next to Television. For their third album Chrysalis brought in the Australian producer Mike Chapman, a hit-maker for glam bands, who drilled them hard at the Record Plant in New York. Disco was at its commercial peak, and punk purists were appalled that a CBGB band would touch it.","Heart of Glass began years earlier as a slower song, and its final form owes much to Kraftwerk and to Giorgio Moroder's disco: the band used a Roland drum machine and synchronised synthesizer, then very new. The album also covers two older songs: Hanging on the Telephone, by the little-known Los Angeles band the Nerves, and I'm Gonna Love You Too, a Buddy Holly number.","It sold many millions and made Blondie stars around the world; Heart of Glass reached number one in both Britain and the United States. Some punk loyalists saw it as a sell-out; critics today mostly regard it as one of the great pop albums and the proof that new wave could conquer the charts without losing its wit.","Start with Hanging on the Telephone and One Way or Another for the rock side, then Heart of Glass; listen to the steady, mechanical pulse under the drums. Notice how Harry's voice stays cool even when the music gets hot. Then play Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine for the electronic source, and Buddy Holly for the pop one."],"blondie-rapture":["A slow, slinky disco-funk groove, with swooning backing vocals and a drifting saxophone, over which Debbie Harry first sings a dreamy, half-whispered verse and then launches into a long rap. The rap itself is playful nonsense: a surreal tale about a man from Mars who eats cars and people, delivered in a deadpan sing-song. In the middle she name-checks two real figures of the New York hip-hop scene. The groove, not the melody, is the hook.","Harry and Chris Stein had been going uptown to Bronx and Harlem parties with the graffiti artist Fab 5 Freddy, where they saw DJs and rappers at work before hip-hop had reached the radio. Rapture was on the album Autoamerican, produced by Mike Chapman and released in late 1980, and became a single early in 1981. The Sugarhill Gang's Rapper's Delight had been the first rap hit only a year before.","The rap names Fab 5 Freddy and Grandmaster Flash; in the video Freddy appears, and so does the painter Jean-Michel Basquiat, standing in as the DJ when Flash did not turn up. The debt was repaid almost at once: Flash cut Rapture into his own landmark turntable mix, The Adventures of Grandmaster Flash on the Wheels of Steel, later that year.","It went to number one in the United States, making it the first chart-topping single there to feature rap, and for many listeners it was their first taste of the form. Some hip-hop artists have praised it as a generous introduction; others have found Harry's rap amateurish. It is now seen as an important, if awkward, bridge between downtown new wave and uptown hip-hop.","Listen for the change in the middle, when the singing stops and the rap begins, and notice how the groove underneath does not change at all. Then play the Sugarhill Gang's Rapper's Delight for comparison, and afterwards Grandmaster Flash's Adventures on the Wheels of Steel to hear Rapture being cut up and used."],"the-slits-cut":["Ten songs of loose, springy, reggae-rooted post-punk. Tessa Pollitt's bass is deep and round, the drums are sparse, and Viv Albertine's guitar scratches and chimes in short bursts, leaving space. Ari Up sings, yelps, laughs and whoops, in a voice untouched by rock convention. Songs mock shopping, dating, housework and the expectations placed on young women. Typical Girls, the single, lurches through several tempos; Shoplifting is a riot; Instant Hit and Newtown drift with dub echoes.","The Slits were among the first all-female punk bands, formed in London in 1976 when Ari Up was fourteen. They toured with the Clash and faced open hostility and violence. By 1979 their original drummer had left and they had embraced reggae. Island signed them and paired them with Dennis Bovell, a leading British reggae and lovers-rock producer, who recorded them at Ridge Farm in Surrey, with Budgie, later of Siouxsie and the Banshees, on drums.","Listen for Bovell's dub craft: instruments drop out suddenly, echoes trail from a single snare hit, and the bass is mixed louder than the guitar, as on a Jamaican record. The cover photograph, the three members naked but for loincloths and mud, was a deliberate challenge to the male gaze, and remains controversial. Ari Up was the stepdaughter of John Lydon.","Reviews were mostly warm, some bewildered, and it reached the British top thirty. Its reputation has grown immensely; it is now seen as one of the key post-punk records and a founding document of feminist rock, cited by the riot grrrl movement. The cover still divides opinion, some reading it as empowerment, others as provocation.","Start with Typical Girls and Instant Hit, and follow the bass line, which leads everything. Listen for the moments when instruments vanish and echo takes over. Then hear PiL's Metal Box, from the same year, for another post-punk band in thrall to dub, and seek out a Dennis Bovell dub record to hear the source."],"suicide-suicide":["Seven songs made by two men and some cheap machines. Martin Rev plays a battered electric organ fed through effects, riffing on one or two chords over a primitive rhythm box; Alan Vega croons, whispers, hiccups and screams like a rockabilly singer lost in an echo chamber. Ghost Rider and Rocket U.S.A. are minimal, pulsing rock; Cheree is a tender lullaby; Frankie Teardrop is ten minutes about a factory worker driven to violence and despair, punctuated by screams that are genuinely hard to hear.","Vega, a sculptor, and Rev, a jazz keyboard player, formed Suicide in New York in 1970, playing in art spaces and later at CBGB and Max's Kansas City. They were the scene's outsiders: no guitars, no drummer. The album was recorded quickly for the small Red Star label, co-produced by Marty Thau and Craig Leon, who had made the Ramones' debut. Touring with the Clash and Elvis Costello in Britain in 1978, they faced furious, violent audiences.","Vega's model was not a punk singer but Elvis Presley and the rockabilly echo of Sun Records; listen to his hiccups and reverb and Ghost Rider becomes a 1950s record played by robots. Vega had seen the Stooges and credited Iggy with showing him what a performer could do. Rev's rhythm box was a cheap preset unit, and its tinny limitations are the sound.","Few people bought it and many critics dismissed it. Its influence has grown out of all proportion: synth-pop duos such as Soft Cell, industrial music, Bruce Springsteen's bleak Nebraska and later electronic rock all owe it something. The main argument now is whether Frankie Teardrop is art or ordeal.","Start with Ghost Rider and Cheree, and notice how little is happening and how much atmosphere it makes. Listen for Vega's rockabilly echoes. Approach Frankie Teardrop when you are ready for it. Then play Neu!'s first album for another kind of hypnotic repetition, and Kraftwerk's Autobahn for machines played with a very different temperament."],"new-order-blue-monday":["Seven and a half minutes of machine-driven dance music made by a rock band. It opens with a stuttering, programmed kick drum, then a pulsing synthesizer bass sequence and a chord of choir-like voices enter. Peter Hook's high, melodic bass guitar comes in over the machines, and Bernard Sumner sings a detached, understated vocal about emotional coldness. The drum pattern, with its rapid-fire bursts, is instantly recognisable.","After Ian Curtis's death, the three surviving members of Joy Division formed New Order with keyboard player Gillian Gilbert, and threw themselves into the electronic dance music they heard in New York clubs. Blue Monday was released on Factory in March 1983 as a twelve-inch single only, with Peter Saville's die-cut sleeve modelled on a computer floppy disk. It became one of the best-selling twelve-inch singles ever made.","The song began as a machine that could play the band's encores for them; the programming was so elaborate that it effectively ran by itself. The choir sound is reportedly taken from Kraftwerk's Radio-Activity, and the drum pattern owes something to disco productions of the time. The sleeve was so costly to make that Factory is said to have lost money on every early copy sold.","It was a hit around the world, and remixed and rereleased more than once. Critics hailed it as the moment post-punk met club music; it influenced house and techno producers, and New Order's crossover from rock to dance shaped the whole of British music in the following decade. Some Joy Division fans took it as a betrayal of what the band had been.","Listen to the opening minute as the parts are introduced one by one; notice how Hook's bass guitar is the only obviously human instrument. Then play Joy Division's Love Will Tear Us Apart to hear what survived the transition. Afterwards hear Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock, the other great fusions of European electronics and the dance floor."],"rem-murmur":["Twelve songs in about forty-four minutes, built on Peter Buck's ringing, arpeggiated Rickenbacker, Mike Mills's melodic bass and Bill Berry's light, tidy drumming. Over the top, Michael Stipe sings half-buried in the mix, vowels smeared so that the words become texture. 'Radio Free Europe' and 'Talk About the Passion' are the handholds, but the record's identity is its haze: acoustic guitars, piano and backing harmonies layered into a warm, humid Southern murk that sounds both old and new.","R.E.M. formed in Athens, Georgia, in 1980, a college town whose scene also produced the B-52's. After a self-released single on the tiny Hib-Tone label and the Chronic Town EP, they recorded their debut album for I.R.S. Records with Mitch Easter and Don Dixon in Charlotte, North Carolina, early in 1983. The band had resisted the label's attempt to pair them with a slicker producer. In a year dominated by synthesisers and MTV, a guitar band from the South singing indistinctly was an unlikely proposition.","Listen to how often Mills's bass carries the actual tune while Buck plays chords around it, and how his high backing vocal sings a second, different melody against Stipe's. The guitar rarely takes a solo; Buck said he disliked them. 'Radio Free Europe' is a re-recording of the Hib-Tone single, faster and cleaner, and comparing the two shows how deliberately Easter and Dixon softened the band into something dreamier.","Rolling Stone named Murmur its album of the year for 1983, ahead of Michael Jackson's Thriller, which says much about how critics were hungry for an alternative. It sold modestly at first but became the founding document of American college rock, the network of campus radio stations and small clubs that R.E.M. toured relentlessly. Arguments about what Stipe was actually singing have never been settled; he has suggested that was partly the point.","Start with 'Perfect Circle', the slow song with two pianos, where the fog is at its most beautiful. Then play 'Radio Free Europe' loud and notice how the bass pushes the song forward. Don't strain to catch the words; let the voice work as another instrument. Afterwards, hear the Byrds' jangle it inherited and the Smiths' Hatful of Hollow from across the Atlantic."],"rem-automatic-for-the-people":["Twelve songs, mostly slow, mostly acoustic, about ageing, loss and memory. Mandolin, piano, accordion and a string section replace the electric jangle; 'Drive' opens in a hush, 'Everybody Hurts' builds from an arpeggio in the manner of an old soul ballad, and 'Nightswimming' is just piano, strings and voice recalling teenage summers. Only 'Ignoreland' and 'Man on the Moon', a song about the comedian Andy Kaufman, raise the pulse. It is a quiet record made by the biggest band in the world.","After Out of Time (1991) and 'Losing My Religion' had made R.E.M. global stars, the band set out to make a harder rock album and ended up with the opposite. Recorded in several studios including Criteria in Miami and Bearsville in New York, with Scott Litt producing, it drew string arrangements for several songs from John Paul Jones, once of Led Zeppelin. The title comes from the slogan of a soul-food restaurant in Athens, Georgia. The band did not tour it.","'Nightswimming' was built on a circling piano figure by Mike Mills; listen for the oboe that enters late and shadows the strings, one of the few wind sounds on the record. Listen to how 'Man on the Moon' quotes Elvis's vocal hiccups and wanders through Kaufman's world of wrestling and hoaxes. The record's closing trio moves from Kaufman's death-defying jokes to a funeral-quiet goodbye.","Released in October 1992, just as grunge was peaking, it was received as a brave swerve and sold many millions. Many critics and the band members themselves came to rank it as their finest record. 'Everybody Hurts' took on a long second life as a song of comfort, used in suicide-prevention campaigns. Some early fans missed the Southern haze of the 1980s albums; the album's stature has grown steadily since.","Hear it in order, at night. Notice how 'Drive' refuses to explode, and how 'Everybody Hurts' uses the simplest means to reach people at their lowest. 'Nightswimming' is the centre; listen to the piano and strings and the way Stipe's voice is, for once, perfectly clear. Compare Nick Drake's Bryter Layter and its string-lit melancholy."],"pixies-surfer-rosa":["Thirteen short songs, raw and room-shaped: David Lovering's drums sound as though recorded in a concrete hall, Joey Santiago's guitar squeals and scrapes, Kim Deal's bass is plain and propulsive, and Black Francis switches from a croon to a throat-tearing scream within a line. 'Bone Machine', 'Where Is My Mind?', 'Gigantic' (sung by Deal) and 'Cactus' are its landmarks. Studio chatter and giggles are left between songs, so the record feels like overhearing a band at work.","Pixies formed in Boston in 1986 after Charles Thompson (Black Francis) and Santiago met at university. Their first full album was recorded in about ten days late in 1987 by Steve Albini, then of the band Big Black, who disliked the term producer and aimed simply to capture the band in a room. The British label 4AD released it in March 1988; in America it was an import. Spanish phrases and surreal images drew on Francis's time as a student in Puerto Rico.","The lyrics mix Catholic imagery, UFOs, sex and violence into a private mythology; Francis has said much of it is sound as much as sense. Listen for the ambient spill: Albini placed microphones to pick up the room itself, which is why the snare explodes. 'Gigantic', co-written by Deal and Francis, is the only Pixies single led by her voice, and the one that hints at the Breeders she would later form.","It barely sold at first but became a musicians' record. Kurt Cobain said it was the model for Nirvana's sound and hired Albini for In Utero because of it. 'Where Is My Mind?' found a mass audience when it closed the film Fight Club in 1999. Albini publicly disparaged the band not long after, which only adds to the legend. It is now widely treated as one of the formative American rock albums of the decade.","Play 'Bone Machine' first and notice the drum sound before anything else. Then 'Where Is My Mind?': the ghostly wordless voice floating over the chords is Kim Deal. Listen for the lurch from whisper to howl in almost every song, then play Nirvana's In Utero, made by the same engineer five years later."],"pixies-doolittle":["Fifteen songs, most under three minutes, that alternate verses sung almost under the breath with choruses that crash in at full volume. 'Debaser' opens with a bass line and a manic shout; 'Wave of Mutilation' is surf rock gone strange; 'Here Comes Your Man' is a sunny pop song; 'Monkey Gone to Heaven' adds cellos and violins to a song about the environment and numerology. It is tighter, brighter and more melodic than Surfer Rosa without losing its oddness.","Recorded in late 1988 in Boston with the English producer Gil Norton, who pushed the band towards cleaner, shorter arrangements, much to Black Francis's irritation. It was released in April 1989 by 4AD in Britain and Elektra in America, the band's first major-label distribution there. Vaughan Oliver's sepia cover and booklet, pairing each song with an image, were part of 4AD's house style. Tensions between Francis and Kim Deal were already growing.","'Debaser' is a song about the 1929 surrealist short film Un Chien Andalou by Luis Buñuel and Salvador Dalí, which Francis admired. 'Monkey Gone to Heaven' assigns the numbers five, six and seven to man, the devil and God, a bit of home-made numerology dropped into an environmental song. Listen to how often the band stops dead just before a chorus, so that silence becomes the loudest thing in the song.","It reached the UK top ten and became Pixies' best-selling record, a college-radio standard in America. Kurt Cobain admitted that 'Smells Like Teen Spirit' was his attempt to write a Pixies song, borrowing the quiet-verse, loud-chorus pattern. Critics still debate whether Norton's polish improved the band or tamed it; many rank Doolittle and Surfer Rosa as a pair rather than choose.","Play 'Debaser', 'Tame' and 'Hey' in a row and track the dynamic switch each time. 'Tame' is the loud-quiet idea in its purest form, breath and scream alternating. Then put on Nevermind's first track and hear the template reused. Vinyl or the 2014 remaster both keep the dry, punchy sound."],"sonic-youth-evol":["Nine songs where noise begins to take the shape of pop. Guitars tuned to odd open chords chime, drone and clang; 'Tom Violence' and 'Shadow of a Doubt' drift like dream sequences, while 'Starpower', sung by Kim Gordon, is nearly a pop song with a warped undertow. The record ends with 'Expressway to Yr Skull', a long build that collapses into feedback. Compared with their earlier records, the atmosphere is spookier and more cinematic.","Sonic Youth came out of New York's downtown art and no-wave scene of the early 1980s. EVOL was their first album for the Californian punk label SST and the first with drummer Steve Shelley, whose steadier playing gave the songs a spine. Its imagery drew on B-movies and underground film: the cover is a still of Lydia Lunch from a Richard Kern film. The title is love spelled backwards.","On the original vinyl, 'Expressway to Yr Skull' ends in a locked groove, so the final drone repeats until the listener lifts the needle; the sleeve listed its duration as infinite. The guitar tunings were often achieved with cheap instruments and screwdrivers or drumsticks jammed under the strings, making each guitar a prepared instrument in the manner of Cage's prepared piano.","Received in the underground press as a step towards songcraft without surrendering noise. It sold modestly but established the band's college-radio following and the growing respect of musicians. Critics now tend to hear it as the start of the run that led to Daydream Nation, with 'Expressway to Yr Skull' as a live showpiece that anticipated much of the post-rock of the next decade.","Start with 'Expressway to Yr Skull' and listen to how chords are made from guitars that ring in sympathy rather than being played in the usual way. Then try 'Starpower' for the pop side. Hear it alongside Glenn Branca's The Ascension to catch the harmonic clouds Thurston Moore and Lee Ranaldo absorbed in his guitar orchestras."],"sonic-youth-daydream-nation":["A double album of fourteen songs and more than seventy minutes, built on two guitars in unusual tunings that shimmer, grind and chime in a way no standard-tuned band can match. 'Teen Age Riot' opens with Kim Gordon murmuring before a clean, driving riff kicks in; 'Silver Rocket' breaks into a tunnel of noise; 'The Sprawl' and ''Cross the Breeze' stretch out; the closing 'Trilogy' runs together three linked pieces. Long instrumental passages feel like weather moving over the city.","Recorded in the summer of 1988 in New York with Nick Sansano and released that October on the independent Enigma label, it was the band's last album before signing to Geffen. It caught New York in the late Reagan years: the Lower East Side, suburban sprawl, the underground's contempt for mainstream culture. The cover reproduces Gerhard Richter's painting of a single candle, a strikingly still image for such loud music.","'Teen Age Riot' imagines a fantasy America in which J Mascis of Dinosaur Jr is president. The inner sleeves assign each member a symbol, a knowing joke at Led Zeppelin's fourth album. Listen to the guitars: each song often needed its own instrument set to a different tuning, sometimes with objects under the strings, so the band toured with a rack of guitars. The harmonies you hear are frequently overtones rather than played notes.","Critics received it as the peak of American underground rock, and it topped many year-end polls. It has since been added to the Library of Congress National Recording Registry. Some find its length self-indulgent; others argue the long, drifting passages are precisely the point, where rock borrows the time scale of minimalism. Its influence on shoegaze, post-rock and indie guitar has been wide and lasting.","Start with 'Teen Age Riot' and listen to how the clean guitar melody rides above a bed of ringing overtones. Then play 'The Sprawl' through to its long instrumental coda. Compare Terry Riley's In C or La Monte Young's drones, which Moore and Ranaldo knew well, and notice how both use repetition to let harmonics bloom."],"glenn-branca-the-ascension":["Five instrumental pieces for a small guitar orchestra: four electric guitars, bass and drums. The guitars are strummed hard and fast on strings often tuned to a single pitch or a few, so that dense waves of sound build and pile up until the overtones begin to sing on their own. Pieces such as 'Lesson No. 2' and the title track climb steadily in intensity for many minutes, more like a slow, loud chord change than a rock song.","Branca came to New York from Pennsylvania in the mid-1970s through experimental theatre and the no-wave scene, where punk energy met gallery art. He formed bands and then larger ensembles of electric guitars, playing in clubs rather than concert halls. This first full album appeared on the small 99 Records label in 1981, with a cover drawing by the artist Robert Longo. His players included Lee Ranaldo, later of Sonic Youth; Thurston Moore also played in Branca's ensembles.","Many of the harmonies you hear are not being played by anyone: when many loud guitars ring on related pitches, the overtones combine to produce phantom tones and choirs. Branca rethought the guitar as a source of the harmonic series, a thread that runs from La Monte Young's sustained tones. Listen to the title track's last minutes, where the ear begins to hear bells that no one struck.","Rock critics heard it as a noise record; classical listeners argued over whether it belonged to minimalism. Branca went on to write symphonies for up to a hundred guitars, and John Cage reportedly found one of them oppressive, a notorious exchange that shows how far Branca stood from Cage's cool. Its main legacy runs through the players who passed through his ensembles, above all Sonic Youth.","Play the title track loud, sit still and wait for the phantom tones to appear. Then play Sonic Youth's 'Expressway to Yr Skull' and hear the idea shrunk to rock-song scale. Compare Reich's Drumming, where repetition also lets new patterns emerge that nobody is playing."],"husker-du-zen-arcade":["A double album of twenty-three tracks that follows, loosely, a boy who leaves an unhappy home and finds the outside world no better. Hardcore punk at breakneck speed sits beside acoustic songs, a piano interlude, backwards tapes and the fourteen-minute instrumental 'Reoccurring Dreams' that closes it. Bob Mould's guitar is a roaring wall of distortion that still carries melody; Grant Hart's drumming is frantic yet tuneful. 'Pink Turns to Blue' and 'Something I Learned Today' are the easiest ways in.","Hüsker Dü came from Minneapolis and toured constantly on the American hardcore circuit. They recorded Zen Arcade for SST in a few days in 1983, mostly in single takes because money and time were short. It appeared in July 1984, the same month SST released the Minutemen's double album Double Nickels on the Dime. A punk band making a double concept album was close to heresy in a scene that despised excess.","Most of the songs are first takes, which is why small mistakes and bleed survive; the band valued momentum over polish. The story ends by revealing that the whole journey was a dream, which is why the long instrumental is titled as it is. Hart and Mould split the songwriting, and learning to tell their voices apart is part of the fun: Hart the melodist, Mould the storm.","Critics outside punk took notice, and it won unusually strong reviews for a hardcore record, proof that the form could hold ambition. Some punks found it pretentious. Its influence has been long: melodic songs buried in distortion anticipate the Pixies, Nirvana and a whole generation of emotional punk. Many listeners find the story vague; the emotional arc carries the record better than the plot.","Try 'Something I Learned Today', then the Hart-written 'Pink Turns to Blue', to hear the melodies inside the noise. Then take the whole second side as a single sweep. Compare the Who's rock operas or Pink Floyd's concept records, and notice how Hüsker Dü does it in half the studio time."],"the-smiths-how-soon-is-now":["Almost seven minutes of throbbing, swampy guitar. Johnny Marr's rhythm part pulses on a heavy tremolo effect, bass and drums lock into a slow, hypnotic groove, and eerie slide-guitar harmonics wail above like sirens. Morrissey sings calmly, almost conversationally, about crippling shyness and a night at a club where no one talks to him. It sounds nothing like the jangle the Smiths were known for: dark, heavy and circular.","The Smiths, formed in Manchester in 1982, were the defining band of British independent rock in the 1980s, signed to Rough Trade. The song was recorded in 1984 with the producer John Porter and first appeared as a B-side, then on the compilation Hatful of Hollow that November, before Rough Trade issued it as a single early in 1985. Many felt it was wasted as a flip-side.","The tremolo effect was created by running the guitar through several amplifiers and matching their wobble to the song's tempo, a laborious process in a pre-digital studio; Marr has linked the idea to the shimmering tremolo of Bo Diddley. The wailing guitar is several slide parts layered with harmonics. The song's quiet, steady vocal against the churning music is its whole drama.","As a single it reached only the UK top thirty, partly because fans already owned it, but it became their most famous song in America, a staple of college and alternative radio, and a cover version later became a television theme. The band itself was divided about it. It is often cited as the moment British indie absorbed dance-floor hypnosis; its guitar sound has been endlessly analysed and imitated.","Listen on headphones to separate the layers: the throbbing rhythm guitar in the centre, the slide wailing at the edges, the bass pulling the whole thing forward. Then compare Bo Diddley's 'Who Do You Love?' and hear the old tremolo idea turned into a haunted engine."],"the-smiths-the-queen-is-dead":["Ten songs that swing from fury to tenderness to broad comedy. The title track storms in on Mike Joyce's rolling tom-toms and a wah-wah guitar; 'Bigmouth Strikes Again' gallops; 'I Know It's Over' is a slow, bleak ballad; 'Cemetry Gates' and 'Frankly, Mr Shankly' are witty and light. The close, 'There Is a Light That Never Goes Out', pairs a lush string arrangement with a romantic and morbid fantasy. Johnny Marr's guitars shimmer throughout; Andy Rourke's bass is unusually melodic.","Recorded in 1985 with engineer Stephen Street, the album was delayed for months by a legal battle between the Smiths and Rough Trade, and released in June 1986. Britain was in the middle of Thatcherism, the miners' strike just ended; Morrissey's title and lyrics jabbed at monarchy, the Church and the music industry. Rourke was fighting addiction and was briefly sacked during this period.","The album opens with a brief fragment of Cicely Courtneidge singing a wartime music-hall song, taken from the 1962 film The L-Shaped Room. The strings on 'There Is a Light' and elsewhere were played by Marr on an Emulator sampling keyboard, not by an orchestra, despite the band's anti-synthesiser reputation. The cover photograph shows the French actor Alain Delon.","Reaching number two in Britain, it was hailed on release and has regularly topped lists of the greatest British albums. Morrissey's later public statements have made the record harder for some to love, a debate about separating art from artist that continues. Musically, the consensus is firm: it is the Smiths' most complete statement, balancing Marr's arrangements with Morrissey's wit.","Start with 'There Is a Light That Never Goes Out', the song most fans name first. Then go back to the title track and notice how the drums, bass and guitar feedback make a single tidal sound. Enjoy the humour: 'Frankly, Mr Shankly' is a music-hall number at heart. Compare the Kinks' Village Green for a different vision of English decline."],"my-bloody-valentine-isnt-anything":["Twelve songs of warped, woozy guitar rock. Kevin Shields's guitar bends in and out of tune in slow waves; the male and female voices, his and Bilinda Butcher's, are sung softly and mixed low; Colm Ó Cíosóig's drums are fast and busy. 'Soft as Snow (But Warm Inside)', 'Lose My Breath' and 'Feed Me with Your Kiss' show the range, from dreamy drift to punk ferocity. The sound is sensual and disorienting, like hearing music through a fever.","My Bloody Valentine, formed in Dublin and settled in London, spent several years as a fairly conventional indie band before the 1988 EP You Made Me Realise introduced their new sound. Isn't Anything, their first full album for Alan McGee's Creation label, was recorded that year in Wales. Its release in November 1988 pointed the way towards the scene the British music press would call shoegaze.","Shields had begun to develop his 'glide guitar' technique, strumming while holding the tremolo arm so that every chord slides slightly off pitch. The effect is audible everywhere here, but not yet as dominant as on Loveless. Listen for how the vocals are pushed back so far that they sit inside the guitars, making the singers part of the texture rather than the focus.","The British weeklies received it with enthusiasm, and the album has stayed in the shadow of Loveless while being admired in its own right; some fans prefer its rawer, more rhythmic energy. Musicians in the early 1990s treated it as a toolbox. Its reputation grew further when Loveless took years to follow and the band vanished for two decades.","Put on 'Soft as Snow (But Warm Inside)' and listen for the queasy pitch-bending of the guitar. Then 'Feed Me with Your Kiss', which is far louder and faster. Compare Dinosaur Jr's You're Living All Over Me, which Shields admired, and hear how two bands made fuzz tender."],"my-bloody-valentine-loveless":["Eleven songs in which guitars melt into a single, swooning mass of sound. The chords seem to warp and bend like vinyl left in the sun; voices float above or inside, almost wordless; drums are often loops. 'Only Shallow' opens with a blast of noise, 'When You Sleep' is a woozy pop song, 'To Here Knows When' dissolves into a haze, and 'Soon', driven by a dance beat, ends the album in euphoria. It is loud but strangely soft.","Recorded between 1989 and 1991 in a long list of studios, as Kevin Shields searched for exactly the sounds he heard in his head, Loveless became notorious for its cost to Creation Records; figures of around a quarter of a million pounds have been quoted, though Shields disputes them. It came out in November 1991 at the high point of the British shoegaze scene. The band then went silent for over twenty years.","Many of the album's drum parts are samples and loops rather than live playing, because Ó Cíosóig was ill and unsettled for much of the sessions. Shields also sampled his own feedback and played it back on keyboards, so some of what sounds like guitar is a sampler. Butcher's vocals were reportedly often recorded soon after she woke, which helps explain their sleepy intimacy. Listen closely and the bending chords are always moving.","Critics were dazzled, but sales could not cover the cost, and Creation dropped the band. Its reputation grew steadily, and it is now widely ranked among the most influential albums of its era, a touchstone for shoegaze, dream pop and ambient-leaning rock. Its absence of successors for twenty-two years made it a legend. Arguments continue about the best mastering; Shields supervised remasters in 2012 and 2021.","Play 'Only Shallow' loud and let the noise wash over you, then 'To Here Knows When' at lower volume, when it seems to breathe. Don't try to find words. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères or Eno's Discreet Music, where sound also becomes a slowly shifting cloud; Loveless is their rock cousin."],"slint-spiderland":["Six long songs that move between near silence and sudden eruptions. Clean, interlocking guitar figures repeat and shift in odd rhythmic patterns; Brian McMahan speaks or whispers stories more than sings them; bass and drums hold back until the music explodes. 'Breadcrumb Trail' describes a day at a carnival, 'Nosferatu Man' is jagged and heavy, and 'Good Morning, Captain', a sea story in fragments, ends in a scream. The mood is tense, lonely and unresolved.","Slint were four young musicians from Louisville, Kentucky, who emerged from the local hardcore scene. They recorded Spiderland with producer Brian Paulson in Chicago in 1990, and it was released by the Touch and Go label in March 1991. By then the band had already split up, so there was no tour, no press campaign and very little explanation. The cover photograph, taken by their friend Will Oldham, shows the members' heads above water in a quarry.","The songs are carefully composed, not jammed: guitar parts in unusual metres that fit together like clockwork, with repeats that change by one note or beat. The spoken passages are mixed very low, so you have to lean in, which makes the explosions more shocking. The sleeve included a brief notice seeking female vocalists, a quiet hint of the band's direction had it continued.","Barely noticed on release, it built a reputation by word of mouth and is now regarded as a founding text of post-rock and math rock, a word-of-mouth classic of the kind that the pre-internet decade produced. Critics have debated whether its bleakness is a pose or authentic adolescent unease. Bands such as Mogwai have cited it directly, and it was reissued in a large box set in 2014.","Play it from the start in a quiet room at a volume that lets you hear the whispers, and let the climaxes arrive as a shock. Focus on 'Good Morning, Captain': notice how long it waits before the full band comes in. Compare King Crimson's Red for another heavy, angular instrumental language."],"nirvana-smells-like-teen-spirit":["Five minutes built on four power chords. Kurt Cobain plays the riff alone, clean, before Dave Grohl's drums crash in and the band goes full volume; the verses drop to a quiet, sly bass-and-guitar pattern; the chorus explodes again with Cobain's voice shredding. A strange, sloppy guitar solo simply repeats the vocal melody. The lyric is a mocking jumble of apathy and slogans, hard to decode and designed to be.","Released as the first single from Nevermind in September 1991, the song was expected to be a college-radio track. MTV put its video, directed by Samuel Bayer and set at a chaotic high-school pep rally, on heavy rotation, and the song went global. Its title came from graffiti a friend, Kathleen Hanna of Bikini Kill, scrawled on Cobain's wall, referring to a deodorant brand he didn't know existed.","Cobain said he was trying to write a Pixies song, taking their quiet-verse, loud-chorus pattern. The guitar solo is almost exactly the vocal melody, a punk refusal of virtuosity. Listen to Krist Novoselic's bass in the verses: it carries the song, while the guitar plays just two scraped notes. Cobain also joked about the riff's resemblance to Boston's 'More Than a Feeling'.","It reached the top ten in the US and is widely held to have ended hair metal and brought alternative rock to the mainstream in one stroke. Cobain grew to resent it and at times refused to play it. Rolling Stone and others have placed it near the top of lists of the greatest songs. Its fame has made it easy to underrate how odd and ironic it actually is.","Listen for the switch from the clean opening to the full band at about seven seconds. Then focus on the verses: how little happens, and how much tension that creates. Compare 'Debaser' or 'Tame' from Pixies' Doolittle to hear the model, and notice what Nirvana added: heavier drums and a much bigger chorus."],"nirvana-nevermind":["Twelve songs of punk energy and pop melody, with 'Smells Like Teen Spirit', 'In Bloom', 'Come as You Are' and 'Lithium' making up the first half. Dave Grohl's drums are huge and precise, Krist Novoselic's bass is solid, and Kurt Cobain's guitar is often clean and chorused in the verses and heavily distorted in the choruses. 'Polly' is acoustic, 'Territorial Pissings' pure punk, and 'Something in the Way' closes with hushed guitar and cello.","Recorded at Sound City in Van Nuys in May 1991 with producer Butch Vig and mixed by Andy Wallace for Geffen's DGC label, the album aimed modestly to sell a few hundred thousand copies. Nirvana came from Aberdeen and Olympia, Washington, and had released Bleach on Sub Pop in 1989; Grohl had joined only months earlier. The album arrived in September 1991 and knocked Michael Jackson's Dangerous from number one on the American chart in January 1992.","'Something in the Way' was recorded with Cobain lying on a couch in the control room, because he could not get the quiet feel he wanted in the live room. Later pressings contain a hidden track, 'Endless, Nameless', a chaotic noise jam that begins after about ten minutes of silence after the last song. 'Polly' is a disturbing story sung from a criminal's point of view, handled with unsettling calm.","Its success reshaped the industry, sending labels searching for Seattle bands and turning underground rock into the mainstream. Cobain later complained that the mix was too slick, comparing it to a commercial record, and turned to Steve Albini for the next album. Debates about whether its polish betrayed punk continue, but its songwriting is widely admired, and it sits near the top of most greatest-albums lists.","Hear the first four tracks together to understand the album's impact, then skip to 'Drain You', which Cobain named as a favourite. Notice how nearly every song follows the quiet-loud template, yet each has a distinct melody. Afterwards play Pixies' Doolittle and Hüsker Dü's Zen Arcade to hear what Cobain loved."],"nirvana-in-utero":["Twelve songs, rawer and angrier than Nevermind. Grohl's drums sound enormous and live in the room, the guitars are more jagged, and Cobain's voice swings between despair and fury. 'Heart-Shaped Box' is slow and heavy, 'Scentless Apprentice' is a relentless assault, 'Dumb' and 'All Apologies' add cello and tenderness, and 'Pennyroyal Tea' is a quiet song that erupts. The songs mix confessional pain with sardonic comments on fame and the press.","After the vast success of Nevermind, Cobain wanted to return to a rougher sound and hired Steve Albini, who had made Pixies' Surfer Rosa. The band recorded at Pachyderm Studio in Cannon Falls, Minnesota, in February 1993, working quickly. Their label worried the record was uncommercial, and two songs were remixed by R.E.M.'s producer Scott Litt. The album was released in September 1993, less than a year before Cobain's death.","Albini recorded the band live and placed many microphones around the drums to capture the room; Grohl's snare seems to explode because of it. Cobain's lyrics refer to his medical troubles, the pressures of fame and his marriage. Some retailers stocked a version with a title altered on the back cover. 'Frances Farmer Will Have Her Revenge on Seattle' takes up the story of the Hollywood actress mistreated by her home city.","Released amid public argument between band, label and producer over its sound, it reached number one and has since been praised as Nirvana's bravest record, often preferred by critics to Nevermind. Albini's original mixes, later released on anniversary editions, are still debated against Litt's remixes. Cobain's death in April 1994 made the album's themes of pain and exposure impossible to hear neutrally.","Start with 'Heart-Shaped Box' and notice how the verses creep while the chorus explodes. Then 'All Apologies', whose cello makes it a farewell. Compare Pixies' Surfer Rosa for the same engineer's room sound, and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me, recorded at the same studio months earlier."],"nirvana-mtv-unplugged-in-new-york":["Fourteen songs recorded live in one set, acoustic and hushed. Nirvana play only a few of their hits, choosing instead deep cuts and covers: David Bowie's 'The Man Who Sold the World', the Vaselines' 'Jesus Doesn't Want Me for a Sunbeam', three songs by the Meat Puppets with two of that band's members joining, and, to finish, the traditional song Lead Belly made famous, 'Where Did You Sleep Last Night'. Grohl plays with brushes, a cellist adds weight, and Cobain's voice cracks and roars.","Recorded on 18 November 1993 at Sony Music Studios in New York for MTV's acoustic concert series, the performance was broadcast that December. Cobain insisted the stage be decorated with lilies and black candles, like a funeral. After his death in April 1994, the recording was released that November and topped the American chart. The set list was his choice, much to MTV's frustration at the lack of hits.","Listen to the final song, where Cobain pauses before the last line and his voice breaks into a howl; the performance was done in one take, and he refused an encore because he said he could not top it. Cobain had learned the song from Lead Belly's recordings, and he spoke of Lead Belly as his favourite performer. The Meat Puppets songs were a deliberate gesture to share his audience with a band he loved.","It won a Grammy for best alternative album and has often been ranked with Nevermind as Nirvana's best record. The funeral atmosphere, noticed at the time, became almost unbearable after his death. Some argue it is his finest singing. It was also a key moment in bringing Lead Belly and early folk blues to a new generation of listeners.","Hear the Lead Belly song first, then go back and listen to Lead Belly's own recording to hear how the old folk tune travelled. Notice Grohl's brushes and the cello's weight on 'Pennyroyal Tea' and 'Something in the Way'. Compare Johnny Cash's American Recordings, made the same year, another stripped-down record haunted by mortality."],"pearl-jam-ten":["Eleven songs of big, classic-rock-rooted grunge. Stone Gossard's riffs and Mike McCready's long, bluesy solos draw on Led Zeppelin and Hendrix more than punk; Jeff Ament's bass is fluid; Eddie Vedder's deep baritone, full of vibrato, is the signature. 'Once', 'Even Flow' and 'Alive' come early, 'Jeremy' tells the story of a troubled boy, and 'Black' is a slow, aching breakup song. The mood is earnest and anguished rather than ironic.","Gossard and Ament had been in Mother Love Bone, whose singer Andrew Wood died in 1990. They sent instrumental demos to a San Diego surfer and petrol-station attendant, Vedder, who wrote lyrics over them and was invited to Seattle. The album was recorded early in 1991 at London Bridge Studios and released by Epic in August, before Nevermind. It sold slowly at first before becoming one of the best-selling rock albums of the decade.","Vedder's first demo lyrics formed a loose trilogy about a son's discovery of family secrets, of which 'Alive' is the first part; he has said the story drew on his own life. 'Jeremy' is based on a real incident in a Texas school, handled in the song with restraint. Listen to McCready's solo in 'Alive', a long, soaring piece of classic rock that punk-minded listeners found almost shocking in 1991.","Its success made Pearl Jam one of the biggest bands in the world, and Kurt Cobain publicly dismissed them as careerists, though the two later made peace. Critics were divided at first between those who heard old-fashioned arena rock and those who heard genuine emotion. It was remixed by Brendan O'Brien in 2009, and fans still argue about which mix to prefer.","Start with 'Black', where Vedder's voice carries a slow build to a cathartic end. Then 'Alive' for the guitar solo. Notice how much of the sound comes from the 1970s rather than from punk; set it beside Nirvana's Nevermind to hear grunge's two faces in the same year."],"dinosaur-jr-youre-living-all-over-me":["Nine songs where extreme volume meets lazy, melodic singing. J Mascis drawls in a voice close to Neil Young's while his guitar roars through fuzz and Marshall amplifiers, erupting into long, melodic solos; Lou Barlow's bass is heavy and busy; Murph's drums are punk fast. 'Little Fury Things', 'Sludgefeast' and 'The Lung' define the sound, and the album closes with 'Poledo', a strange home-taped collage by Barlow that points towards lo-fi.","Dinosaur Jr came from Amherst, Massachusetts, from the hardcore punk scene, and moved towards a mix of punk energy and 1970s guitar rock. Their second album was recorded with Wharton Tiers in New York and released by SST late in 1987. They had been forced to add 'Jr' after a legal complaint from an older band called the Dinosaurs. Sonic Youth championed them.","Lee Ranaldo of Sonic Youth adds backing vocals to 'Little Fury Things'. Listen to the contrast between Mascis's guitar, which is enormously loud, and his voice, which barely rises above a murmur; the tension between the two is the band's whole character. 'Poledo', recorded by Barlow on cassette at home, shows the sound of the band he would form next, Sebadoh.","Received in the underground as a new kind of noisy melodic rock, the album inspired musicians more than it sold; Kevin Shields of My Bloody Valentine cited its influence. Internal tensions led to Barlow's departure after the next record. It is now seen as a key bridge from hardcore to alternative rock and a forerunner of grunge's sound.","Play 'Little Fury Things' loud and notice how the guitar solo sings a tune rather than showing off speed. Then compare My Bloody Valentine's Isn't Anything to hear the fuzz being turned dreamy, and Nirvana's Nevermind to hear the same mix made bigger."],"pavement-slanted-and-enchanted":["Fourteen songs recorded cheaply and messily, with out-of-tune guitars, drums that wobble in and out of time and Stephen Malkmus's half-sung, half-spoken vocals. Underneath the scruff are sharp melodies: 'Summer Babe (Winter Version)', 'Trigger Cut' and 'Here' are almost sweet, while 'Conduit for Sale!' and 'No Life Singed Her' are noisy and absurd. The lyrics are witty, fractured and allusive, full of odd phrases, and the album sounds casual yet carefully built.","Pavement began as a studio project for Malkmus and Scott Kannberg in Stockton, California. They recorded in the home studio of drummer Gary Young, for very little money, around the end of 1990 and into 1991. Cassettes circulated among critics before Matador Records released it in April 1992. It arrived just as grunge was going mainstream, and its offhand, low-budget sound was an alternative to the new corporate alternative.","Listen to Young's drumming: loose, occasionally falling out of time, and part of the band's appeal. The strongest influence is the English post-punk band the Fall, whose tape-hiss sound and declamatory vocals Malkmus admired. The guitars are often tuned slightly apart, making the chords sound blurry and bittersweet. Many fans first heard it on a dubbed cassette, which suits the sound.","Critics adored it, and it became a defining record of the lo-fi movement and 1990s indie rock. Some found its sloppiness affected; defenders argued that it was a rejection of rock's heroics. It has been widely reissued and remains a model for bands that want to sound unpolished and literate at the same time.","Start with 'Summer Babe' and 'Here', and notice how the melodies survive the noise. Then play 'Trigger Cut' and listen for the backing chants. Compare Pearl Jam's Ten from the year before to hear two opposite versions of American guitar rock in the early 1990s."],"pavement-crooked-rain-crooked-rain":["Twelve songs, cleaner and more tuneful than Slanted and Enchanted but still casual and witty. 'Cut Your Hair' is a catchy sendup of the music business, 'Gold Soundz' a wistful pop song, 'Range Life' a country-tinged daydream that mocks other bands, 'Stop Breathin' a slow drift, and 'Fillmore Jive' a long, sardonic farewell to classic rock. There is even a short jazz pastiche, '5-4=Unity'. The guitars are loose and bright; Malkmus's singing is relaxed and knowing.","By 1993 Gary Young had left, replaced on drums by Steve West, and percussionist Bob Nastanovich was more firmly part of the band. The album was recorded in New York and released by Matador in February 1994. That was the high point of grunge, two months before Kurt Cobain's death, and Pavement found themselves on MTV and in the mainstream conversation despite themselves.","'5-4=Unity' is a playful tribute to Dave Brubeck's 'Take Five', including its lopsided five-beat meter. 'Range Life' pokes fun at the Smashing Pumpkins and Stone Temple Pilots, which led to a real feud. The album's title and imagery nod to the California landscape and suburban life. Listen for the classic-rock guitar clichés Malkmus slips in ironically, especially in 'Fillmore Jive'.","Received as the band's most accessible record, it brought them their only real hit with 'Cut Your Hair'. Some early fans missed the noise, but it is generally ranked alongside or above Slanted and Enchanted. Critics debated whether its irony was a way of holding back feeling; others heard real tenderness in 'Gold Soundz'. It remains a touchstone for American indie rock.","Start with 'Gold Soundz' and notice how its melody is at once sad and cheerful. Then play '5-4=Unity' right after Brubeck's 'Take Five'. Finish with 'Fillmore Jive', whose long fade feels like the end of rock's golden age seen from a skate park."],"radiohead-the-bends":["Twelve songs of guitar rock, soaring and anxious. Three guitars (Thom Yorke, Jonny Greenwood and Ed O'Brien) interlock in shimmering layers; Yorke's falsetto rises over them. 'Planet Telex' and the title track are loud, 'Fake Plastic Trees' builds from acoustic guitar to a full band, 'High and Dry' and 'Just' became hits, and 'Street Spirit (Fade Out)' ends the album in quiet despair. It is bigger and more varied than their debut, and full of dread about modern life.","After their 1993 hit 'Creep', Radiohead feared being a one-hit band. They recorded The Bends in 1994 with the producer John Leckie, at Abbey Road and other studios, with Nigel Godrich engineering; Godrich would become their longtime producer. Parlophone released it in March 1995, in the middle of Britpop, but Radiohead's gloom set them apart from the celebratory mood of Oasis and Blur.","Yorke recorded the vocal for 'Fake Plastic Trees' in a single session after reportedly seeing Jeff Buckley perform in London, and Buckley's high, vulnerable style colours the album's singing. Greenwood's guitar on 'Just' is a dizzying run of notes climbing through the scale. 'Planet Telex' began as a late-night session with a drum loop, a hint of the electronic turns to come.","It sold slowly in America but was acclaimed in Britain and by fellow musicians, and its sound shaped a generation of British guitar bands from Coldplay on. Some fans still prefer it to OK Computer for its directness. It is usually treated as the bridge from their early grunge-influenced rock to the experiments of the later albums.","Start with 'Fake Plastic Trees' and listen to how the song grows, instrument by instrument. Then 'Street Spirit', whose arpeggiated guitar never changes. Compare R.E.M.'s Automatic for the People, which Yorke admired, for another major rock band exploring quiet despair."],"radiohead-ok-computer":["Twelve songs about technology, travel, alienation and dread. 'Airbag' opens with chopped-up drums; 'Paranoid Android' is a six-minute suite in several sections; 'Exit Music (for a Film)' moves from acoustic guitar to crushing distortion; 'Karma Police' and 'No Surprises' are gentle melodies with an edge. 'Fitter Happier', a list of lifestyle slogans read by a computer voice, sits in the middle. The sound is dense and spacious, guitars and keyboards blended into shimmering textures.","Recorded mostly in 1996 and early 1997 at St Catherine's Court, a Tudor mansion near Bath, with Nigel Godrich now co-producing, the album was released by Parlophone in May 1997. Britpop was at its height and the New Labour government was days old; the band's fears of globalisation, the internet and consumer life seemed prophetic. 'Exit Music' was written for Baz Luhrmann's film Romeo + Juliet.","'Climbing Up the Walls' ends with a cluster of strings written by Jonny Greenwood for sixteen players, each set a quarter-tone apart, a debt to Penderecki's Threnody. The drums on 'Airbag' were sampled and chopped, inspired by DJ Shadow. 'Fitter Happier' was spoken by the Macintosh text-to-speech voice. Listen to 'Let Down': the guitars play in a different meter from the drums.","Critics hailed it immediately, and it won a Grammy and was nominated for the Mercury Prize. It was added to the Library of Congress National Recording Registry in 2015. Some found it portentous; others heard the defining rock album of the late 1990s, anticipating the digital age. Its influence on rock, and its reputation as a concept album, remain lively debates.","Play 'Paranoid Android' and follow its sections, as you might the movements of a symphony. Then play 'Climbing Up the Walls' right after Penderecki's Threnody to hear the debt. Notice the details: harpsichord-like guitars, Mellotron, backward sounds, all of which reward headphones."],"radiohead-kid-a":["Ten tracks that trade guitar rock for electronics, jazz and orchestral sound. 'Everything in Its Right Place' opens with warm synthesiser chords and fractured vocals; 'The National Anthem' has a bass riff and a chaotic brass section; 'How to Disappear Completely' floats on strings and ondes Martenot; 'Treefingers' is ambient; 'Idioteque' is a glitchy dance track. Yorke's voice is often processed until it becomes another instrument.","Exhausted by the fame of OK Computer, Thom Yorke had grown tired of rock and immersed himself in electronic music from the Warp label. The band recorded in Paris, Copenhagen, Gloucestershire and Oxford in 1999 and 2000, with Nigel Godrich producing, and released Kid A in October 2000 without singles or conventional videos. The album went to number one in America despite this. Early internet leaks and short animated clips promoted it.","Jonny Greenwood plays the ondes Martenot, the early electronic instrument heard in Messiaen's Turangalîla-Symphonie, on 'How to Disappear Completely', where his string writing slides in clusters descended from Penderecki. 'Idioteque' is built on samples from early computer music by Paul Lansky and Arthur Kreiger. The brass on 'The National Anthem' was told to play like a free-jazz band; Yorke cut up words and drew lines from a hat.","Critics and fans were divided at first; some called it a wilful retreat, others a landmark. Within a decade it was topping lists of the best albums of the 2000s. It is often credited with leading rock towards electronic music, and with showing that a big band could abandon a formula. Debate continues about whether it is truly radical or a smart fusion of existing ideas.","Start with 'How to Disappear Completely' and listen for the ondes Martenot's wavering tone over the strings; then hear it in Messiaen's Turangalîla. Play 'Idioteque' loud for the beat. Let 'Treefingers' be a pause. Greenwood's There Will Be Blood score is the next step."],"radiohead-in-rainbows":["Ten songs, warmer and more intimate than Kid A, often built on interlocking patterns. '15 Step' starts with a skittering beat in five; 'Nude' floats on strings; 'Weird Fishes/Arpeggi' builds from overlapping guitar arpeggios; 'Reckoner' has a falsetto melody over tambourine and piano; 'House of Cards' is slow and hazy; 'Videotape' closes with piano and quietly shifting drums. The mood is sensual and melancholy, closer to love songs than the band had come in years.","Out of contract with EMI, Radiohead announced In Rainbows with ten days' notice and released it as a download on 10 October 2007, letting buyers pay whatever they chose. A physical version followed at the end of the year. Produced by Nigel Godrich over long sessions in several places, including a country house in Wiltshire, it was a sensation in debates about digital music, piracy and the future of the record industry.","'Nude' had been played live since 1997 and was only now completed. The interlocking guitar lines of 'Weird Fishes' use the same principle of phase and pattern that runs through Steve Reich's music; Reich later wrote Radio Rewrite (2012) on songs from Radiohead, and Greenwood recorded Reich's Electric Counterpoint. Listen to '15 Step' for the sound of children cheering, and to 'Videotape' for a drum pattern that shifts its accent.","The pay-what-you-want release dominated news coverage, sometimes obscuring the music, but critics praised it as one of the band's best records and it later won two Grammys. Industry arguments about whether it helped or harmed musicians continue. Many fans rank it as their favourite Radiohead album, more human and approachable than Kid A.","Play 'Weird Fishes/Arpeggi' and follow the guitar lines as they overlap; then hear Reich's Electric Counterpoint, where one guitar is layered many times. 'Reckoner' is the heart of the record; notice how the falsetto floats over the rhythm. Listen late at night."],"pj-harvey-rid-of-me":["Fourteen songs, raw, violent and extremely dynamic. The title track opens with a whispered verse and a hammering chorus; '50ft Queenie' is a boastful, fast stomp; 'Man-Size Sextet' replaces the band with scraping strings; 'Dry' is slow and aching; and there is a snarling cover of Bob Dylan's 'Highway 61 Revisited'. Polly Harvey's voice ranges from a whisper to a howl, and her guitar is jagged. The songs explore sex, power and gender with dark humour.","Harvey grew up in rural Dorset, and her first album, Dry, appeared in 1992 on the independent label Too Pure. For her second, on Island, the trio of Harvey, bassist Steve Vaughan and drummer Rob Ellis recorded at Pachyderm Studio in Minnesota with Steve Albini late in 1992. The album was released in 1993; within months the band had broken up and Harvey continued alone.","Albini recorded the quiet passages very quietly and the loud ones very loud, so that the volume leaps can be startling at home; the vocals are often low in the mix. Harvey's love of Captain Beefheart shows in the jagged rhythms and bluesy growl, and she has spoken of growing up on her parents' records of Beefheart and the blues. Listen to how Ellis's backing vocals add ghostly falsetto.","Critics praised it as ferocious and original, though some thought Albini's production made it hard to hear. It was nominated for the Mercury Prize. Debate about whether Harvey was a feminist artist began here; she resisted labels, preferring to play characters. It remains one of the most intense rock albums of the 1990s and was made months before Nirvana's In Utero at the same studio.","Play the title track and let the quiet verses draw you in before the chorus erupts. Then hear 'Man-Size Sextet' for the strings, and '50ft Queenie' for sheer energy. Compare Captain Beefheart's Trout Mask Replica for the blues and angles she loved."],"pj-harvey-let-england-shake":["Twelve songs about war, England and its landscape, many of them centred on the Gallipoli campaign of 1915. Harvey plays autoharp and sings in a high, clear voice, far from her 1990s howl; the arrangements are sparse, with guitar, keyboards, drums and occasional samples of bugles and old records. 'The Words That Maketh Murder', 'The Glorious Land' and 'On Battleship Hill' are standouts. The tunes are catchy, even jaunty, against lyrics describing carnage.","Harvey spent several years writing, reading war poetry, history and soldiers' testimony from Gallipoli to Iraq and Afghanistan. The album was recorded in 2010 in a nineteenth-century church in Eype, Dorset, near her home, with longtime collaborators John Parish, Mick Harvey and Jean-Marc Butty. It was released in February 2011, while British troops were fighting in Afghanistan. The photographer Seamus Murphy made a short film for each song.","Harvey borrows from old records: the title track uses a sample of the 1950s novelty song 'Istanbul (Not Constantinople)', one song briefly quotes Eddie Cochran's 'Summertime Blues', and 'The Glorious Land' has a bugle call. Listen to how brisk tunes and a bright autoharp are set against descriptions of dead soldiers, a contrast that makes the horror more vivid. The recording church adds natural reverb.","It won the Mercury Prize in 2011, making Harvey the first artist to win it twice, and topped many critics' lists for the year. Critics compared it to the war poets such as Wilfred Owen. Some found its tone too detached; most heard it as a searching study of patriotism and violence and Harvey's most ambitious work.","Hear the title track and notice the sample running under the autoharp. Then 'On Battleship Hill', whose high, fragile vocal evokes a haunted landscape. Compare Britten's War Requiem, which sets Wilfred Owen, and Butterworth's A Shropshire Lad rhapsody, written by a composer who died at the Somme."],"neutral-milk-hotel-in-the-aeroplane-over-the-sea":["Eleven songs built on Jeff Mangum's strummed acoustic guitar and fervent, nasal voice, surrounded by fuzzed bass, flugelhorn and trumpets, singing saw, accordion and organ, all recorded with lots of distortion. 'The King of Carrot Flowers' opens in three parts, 'In the Aeroplane Over the Sea' is a sing-along waltz, 'Holland, 1945' is a loud rush, and 'Oh Comely' is an eight-minute solo performance. The songs mix love, death and surreal imagery.","Mangum was part of the Elephant 6 collective, a group of friends from Ruston, Louisiana, who made psychedelic-tinged indie pop. The album was recorded in 1997 in Denver with Robert Schneider of the Apples in Stereo, with Julian Koster, Jeremy Barnes and Scott Spillane, and released by Merge in February 1998. Mangum had been reading The Diary of Anne Frank, which affected him deeply and runs through the lyrics.","Anne Frank appears in several songs, though never named in a way that explains the album; Mangum imagines her as a child who might be reborn. The singing saw, played by Koster, is the eerie whistling sound. At the end of 'Oh Comely', someone in the room can be heard reacting to Mangum's performance, which was reportedly captured in one take.","Modestly received at first, it grew into a cult classic through word of mouth and the early internet, and by the 2000s was widely considered one of the best indie albums ever made. Mangum largely withdrew from public life, adding to its mystique. Some find his voice grating and the imagery overwrought; devotees hear a rare sincerity.","Start with the title track: notice the singing saw and the horns. Then 'Holland, 1945' for its joyful noise. Hear 'Oh Comely' alone at night. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon for an intimate voice and guitar, and the Beatles' Sgt Pepper for the brass-band psychedelia."],"the-strokes-is-this-it":["Eleven songs, taut and minimal, in about thirty-six minutes. Two guitars, Nick Valensi's and Albert Hammond Jr.'s, play interlocking, clean, choppy lines; Nikolai Fraiture's bass pulses; Fabrizio Moretti's drums are precise and almost machine-like; Julian Casablancas sings through a distorted, lo-fi filter with a bored drawl. 'Last Nite', 'Someday', 'Hard to Explain' and 'The Modern Age' are the hits. It sounds casual, cool and effortless.","The Strokes were five friends from Manhattan who played Lower East Side clubs. Their debut EP created a buzz in Britain, and Rough Trade released the album in the summer of 2001, before RCA's American release in October. It arrived just after 9/11 in America, where 'New York City Cops' was dropped and the cover changed. Produced by Gordon Raphael in a small basement studio, it marked a return to lean guitar rock after years of nu-metal and electronica.","Casablancas recorded his vocals through a small amplifier to get the distorted, far-off sound; Raphael deliberately avoided modern gloss. Moretti's drums were recorded to sound like a drum machine, part of the band's goal of making rock sound like 1970s New York filtered through electronic music. 'Last Nite' so resembled Tom Petty's 'American Girl' that Petty later acknowledged it with amusement.","It was hailed as the saviour of rock and topped many polls of the decade's best albums, launching the garage-rock revival along with the White Stripes. Some critics found it derivative of the Velvet Underground and Television; others argued it was a synthesis rather than a copy. Its style influenced many British bands, including the Arctic Monkeys and the Libertines.","Play 'Last Nite' and listen to how the two guitars fit together like gears. Then 'Hard to Explain', with its machine-like drums. Hear it after Radiohead's Kid A, released a year earlier, to understand the two directions rock split into at the turn of the century."],"arcade-fire-funeral":["Ten songs, big, anthemic and emotional. Violins, accordion, piano and organ are woven around guitars and drums; many voices chant together. The four 'Neighborhood' songs imagine children escaping into snowstorms and tunnels; 'Wake Up' is a huge wordless chant; 'Rebellion (Lies)' drives on a pulsing bass; 'Une année sans lumière' is in French; 'In the Backseat', sung by Régine Chassagne, closes in grief. The mood is childlike, desperate and joyful at once.","Arcade Fire formed in Montreal around Win Butler and Chassagne, a married couple. While they were recording the album in 2003 and 2004, several relatives of band members died, including Butler's grandfather, the bandleader and steel-guitar pioneer Alvino Rey; hence the title. Merge released it in September 2004. Its success, driven by blogs and a glowing Pitchfork review, showed how the internet could make an indie band famous.","The strings were arranged by Owen Pallett and Sarah Neufeld, bringing a chamber-music sound. The band recorded in a Montreal studio with all members shouting and singing to create the choral effect. Listen for how the French-language song reflects Montreal's bilingualism and Chassagne's Haitian heritage. The 'Neighborhood' songs form a loose narrative of children growing up in a world of blackouts.","It was widely hailed as one of the best albums of the decade; David Bowie became a fan and performed with the band. Some critics found its emotion overblown; others heard a rare sincerity. It paved the way for 2000s indie rock's taste for big choruses and many-member bands, and led to later Grammy success with The Suburbs.","Start with 'Wake Up', and notice how the whole band sings the melody. Then 'Neighborhood #1 (Tunnels)', whose piano builds slowly. Compare Neutral Milk Hotel's In the Aeroplane Over the Sea for another cathartic, homemade indie sound, and Bowie's Heroes for the grandeur."],"bob-dylan-like-a-rolling-stone":["Six minutes of four verses and a chorus that keeps growing. It opens with a single crack of the snare drum, then piano, Mike Bloomfield's stinging guitar and, floating over everything, Al Kooper's organ, a high wavering line that answers each phrase. Dylan does not so much sing as sneer, pitching every chorus as a question thrown at a fallen socialite who has lost her place in the world. The song never resolves; it simply fades while still at full pressure.","Dylan wrote it in June 1965, back from a draining English tour, out of what he described as a long page of angry prose. Producer Tom Wilson cut it at Columbia's Studio A in New York over two days. Columbia hesitated to issue a six-minute single, since radio wanted three, but it went out in July and climbed to number two in America, kept from the top by the Beatles' 'Help!'. Five days after release Dylan played it, loud and electric, at the Newport Folk Festival.","Kooper was a guitarist who talked his way to the organ bench and did not really know the chords, so he watched the other players' hands and came in a fraction late on each change. That hesitation is the organ sound everyone remembers. The first day's takes tried the song as a waltz; the session tapes, since issued, let you hear it turn from three beats to four and find its swagger.","It was heard at once as a rupture: a pop single with the length, bile and imagery of a poem. Rolling Stone put it at the top of its list of the greatest songs in 2004 and again in 2021. The argument now is less about its worth than about blame: whether it ended the folk revival's innocence or simply told the truth about it. Its handwritten draft sold at auction in 2014 for about two million dollars.","Listen first for the opening drum shot and the organ entering behind the voice; then follow how each chorus gets angrier than the last. Hear it next to 'Blowin' in the Wind' to measure how far he travelled in two years, and next to the Rolling Stones' 'Satisfaction', released weeks earlier, for the other great rock single of that summer."],"bob-dylan-highway-61-revisited":["Nine songs, eight of them fully electric, with a blues band sound that is rough, bright and fast. 'Like a Rolling Stone' opens; 'Tombstone Blues' and the title track race; 'Ballad of a Thin Man' creeps over a slow piano and organ; 'Just Like Tom Thumb's Blues' is a weary border-town lament. The record closes with 'Desolation Row', eleven minutes of acoustic guitar and a Spanish-tinged second guitar, a carnival parade of figures from history, scripture and literature.","Made in New York in the summer of 1965, the weeks around Newport, with Mike Bloomfield on lead guitar and Al Kooper on keyboards; Bob Johnston took over as producer from Tom Wilson after the first single. U.S. Route 61 runs from New Orleans up through the Mississippi Delta to Duluth, Minnesota, where Dylan was born: the road of the blues migration, of Delta crossroads legends, of the pilgrimage north that made Chicago blues.","The title song begins with a comic retelling of Abraham ordered to sacrifice his son (Dylan's own father was named Abram), and its manic bursts come from a toy police siren credited to Dylan himself. 'Desolation Row' was recorded with Nashville guitarist Charlie McCoy, who happened to be visiting New York and improvised the lacework second guitar line on the spot.","Critics and fellow musicians took it as proof that rock could be art, and the Beatles, the Byrds and the Stones all listened closely. Some folk loyalists felt betrayed. Its standing has never really wavered; the debate is whether it or 'Blonde on Blonde' is the summit of the 1965 to 1966 run, and whether its surreal imagery is profound or brilliantly arbitrary.","Take side one straight through, then sit still for 'Desolation Row' as a single long journey. Notice how the band plays like a Chicago blues outfit that has read too many French poets. Follow with Robert Johnson's 'Cross Road Blues' and Charley Patton's 'High Water Everywhere' to hear the highway the album is named for. The mono mix is punchier."],"bob-dylan-blonde-on-blonde":["A double album of fourteen songs, softer and stranger than its predecessor. Organ and electric guitar swirl together into a silvery, nocturnal glow. 'Visions of Johanna' drifts through a sleepless night; 'I Want You' and 'Just Like a Woman' are sharp pop; 'Rainy Day Women #12 & 35' is a raucous, laughing brass-band romp; and the whole fourth side is given to 'Sad Eyed Lady of the Lowlands', an eleven-minute slow hymn to one woman.","Sessions began in New York in January 1966 with the Hawks, the group later known as the Band, and stalled. Producer Bob Johnston moved them to Nashville, where Dylan wrote in the studio while crack country session players such as Charlie McCoy, Kenny Buttrey and Wayne Moss waited, sometimes until dawn, playing cards. Robbie Robertson and Al Kooper came along from New York. It appeared in June 1966, among the first double albums in rock.","'4th Time Around' is a sly answer to the Beatles' 'Norwegian Wood', which had borrowed Dylan's own manner; John Lennon admitted feeling uneasy about it. Kenny Buttrey's drumming on 'Sad Eyed Lady' swells in near-panic because nobody knew how long the song would go on. Dylan later described the sound he was chasing as thin and wild and mercurial, which is exactly what the organ and harmonica do together.","Hailed at once and still widely ranked his finest record. Its reputation also made Nashville respectable to rock musicians, opening the road to country rock. Arguments continue about how much of the imagery is private, how much Sara Lownds, whom he had just married, lies behind 'Sad Eyed Lady', and whether the rambling side four is devotion or indulgence.","Start with 'Visions of Johanna' in the dark, then 'I Want You' for the bright pop Dylan. Listen for Garth Hudson-style organ and McCoy's bass harmonica in the corners. Play the Beatles' 'Rubber Soul' beside it to hear two camps trading ideas in real time. The 2010s mono reissues are worth seeking out."],"bob-dylan-live-1966":["A whole concert on two discs. The first half is Dylan alone with acoustic guitar and harmonica, playing long, hushed songs such as 'Visions of Johanna' and 'Desolation Row' to an attentive, reverent hall. The second half brings on the Hawks, loud and ferocious, for 'Tell Me, Momma', 'Ballad of a Thin Man' and more, while the audience slow-handclaps and heckles. It ends with a snarling 'Like a Rolling Stone'.","Recorded at the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, on 17 May 1966, during a British tour on which Dylan was booed nightly by folk audiences who felt he had sold out. The Hawks were Robbie Robertson, Rick Danko, Richard Manuel and Garth Hudson, with Mickey Jones on drums. A tape circulated for three decades as one of rock's most famous bootlegs, wrongly labelled as the Royal Albert Hall show; Columbia finally issued it officially in 1998, keeping the wrong name in quotation marks.","Just before the last song a voice from the audience shouts 'Judas!'. Dylan answers that he doesn't believe him and calls him a liar, then turns and tells the band, profanely, to play loud. At least two men have since claimed to be the heckler. Listen too to the acoustic half: the silence of the crowd there is part of the drama, because it is the same people.","Long before release, critics treated the bootleg as a founding document of rock's live power and of the folk-rock schism. Its official issue confirmed it; many rank it the greatest live rock recording. The debate now is about whose side history takes: the hecklers are usually cast as philistines, though some writers argue they were defending a real community Dylan had left behind.","Play one acoustic song, then jump to the electric 'Ballad of a Thin Man', and feel the shock the audience felt. Then save the last track for the end. Compare with Muddy Waters at Newport in 1960, and with Neil Young's 'Rust Never Sleeps', which stages the same acoustic-then-electric split as a choice rather than a fight."],"bob-dylan-blood-on-the-tracks":["Ten songs of love gone wrong, mostly built on Dylan's acoustic guitar with a light band. 'Tangled Up in Blue' opens, a story that jumps between times and pronouns; 'Simple Twist of Fate' is quiet and rueful; 'Idiot Wind' is a long, organ-driven storm of accusation; 'Shelter from the Storm' is a patient, circling ballad; 'Buckets of Rain' closes almost in a whisper. The long narrative 'Lily, Rosemary and the Jack of Hearts' is a Western told in fifteen verses.","Dylan recorded the album in New York in September 1974, then, dissatisfied, re-cut five songs in Minneapolis that December with local players gathered by his brother. His marriage to Sara was coming apart. He had also spent weeks that year in a painting class with Norman Raeben, who, he said, taught him to see past, present and future at once, which he credited for the album's shifting sense of time.","Test pressings of the New York version reached reviewers before the Minneapolis re-recordings replaced half of it, and the full September sessions were released in 2018 as 'More Blood, More Tracks'. Compare the two 'Idiot Wind's: in New York it is weary and hushed, in Minneapolis it is furious. Dylan has denied the songs are autobiography and pointed to Chekhov's short stories instead.","Initial reviews were mixed, some finding it slight, but within a few years it was ranked among his best, and it is often called the great breakup album. The autobiography question remains open: his son Jakob said it sounded like his parents talking, while Dylan insists on fiction. Fans still argue whether the New York takes were the better record.","Start with 'Tangled Up in Blue' and track who 'I' and 'she' are in each verse; the confusion is the point. Then hear 'Idiot Wind' and 'You're Gonna Make Me Lonesome When You Go' back to back: rage and tenderness a track apart. Schubert's 'Winterreise' is a surprisingly close cousin, a cycle of a jilted man walking through his memories."],"bob-dylan-time-out-of-mind":["Eleven songs in a murky, reverberant sound: organ, twanging guitars, doubled drums and a voice now cracked and worn, pushed through echo as if recorded in a swamp. The songs are slow blues and ballads about walking, waiting and ageing: 'Love Sick' creeps in over a ticking organ; 'Not Dark Yet' is a stately meditation on fading; 'Make You Feel My Love' is a plain ballad; and 'Highlands' closes the album with sixteen shambling minutes of wry monologue.","Recorded in early 1997 at Criteria Studios in Miami with producer Daniel Lanois, who had made Dylan's 'Oh Mercy' in 1989, and a crowded room of musicians. Dylan had issued no original songs for seven years. Just before release he was hospitalised with a serious heart infection, and the album's preoccupation with mortality was read through that, though it was finished beforehand. It won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","Dylan was frustrated by the sessions and later said he wanted a sound closer to the old records he loved; the album is full of borrowed phrases and melodies from pre-war blues and country, a practice that grew on his later records. 'Make You Feel My Love' became a standard through covers by Billy Joel, Garth Brooks and Adele, the rare Dylan song others sing more than he does.","It was hailed as a comeback, the start of a late period many now rank alongside the 1960s. Lanois's atmospheric production divides listeners: for some it is the album's soul, for others a fog. Dylan went on to produce himself, under the alias Jack Frost. The borrowings, later more explicit, opened a lasting argument over his methods.","Begin with 'Not Dark Yet' at night. Then try 'Highlands' as you would a long short story. Listen for the old blues shapes under the fog; Muddy Waters and Charley Patton are close by. Schubert's 'Winterreise', a wanderer's cycle in a cold landscape, is an unexpected but apt companion."],"neil-young-after-the-gold-rush":["Eleven short songs alternating between fragile piano-and-voice ballads and loose, loud electric rock. Young's thin, high tenor is the signature. The title track is only piano, voice and a closing flugelhorn solo, a dream of knights, a burning world and silver spaceships. 'Southern Man' is a furious electric guitar assault on racism; 'Only Love Can Break Your Heart' is a gentle waltz; 'Don't Let It Bring You Down' is bleak and consoling at once.","Young made it in 1970, partly in a basement studio at his house in Topanga Canyon outside Los Angeles, between tours with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young, whose 'Déjà Vu' had just been a huge success. He used members of Crazy Horse and an eighteen-year-old Nils Lofgren, a guitarist, whom he put on piano. Several songs grew from a screenplay by the actor Dean Stockwell for a film that was never made.","Lofgren's piano is simple because he had barely played the instrument; its plainness is part of the charm. 'Southern Man' drew a reply four years later from Lynyrd Skynyrd's 'Sweet Home Alabama', which names Young directly; he took it good-naturedly. The album also includes a cover, Don Gibson's country hit 'Oh, Lonesome Me', slowed into a dirge.","Rolling Stone's first review was cool, calling it uneven, but the album sold well and its reputation climbed steadily. It is now considered one of the defining records of the Laurel Canyon era. The title song's cryptic ecological vision has been widely covered, notably by Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Emmylou Harris on 'Trio II'.","Play the title track first with nothing else going on, then 'Southern Man' straight after to hear Young's two voices, the choirboy and the wrecker. Compare Joni Mitchell's 'Blue' from the next year for the same canyon, a different temperament. Then hear 'Tonight's the Night' to see where this fragility went."],"neil-young-tonights-the-night":["A wake on record. Twelve songs played loose and ragged, slurred and off-key by design, with piano, guitars and Ben Keith's crying pedal steel. The title song opens and closes the album in two versions, an unsettling chant naming a dead friend. Between them come shambling barroom ballads such as 'Mellow My Mind', 'Tired Eyes' and 'Albuquerque', with voices cracking and tempos dragging throughout. It sounds like a late night that never quite ended.","Crazy Horse guitarist Danny Whitten died of an overdose in November 1972, days after Young had sent him home from rehearsals; roadie Bruce Berry died the same way the following year. In August and September 1973 Young gathered a band he called the Santa Monica Flyers at a rehearsal studio in Los Angeles and recorded late at night, often after heavy drinking. Reprise shelved the album; it came out only in June 1975.","One track, 'Come On Baby Let's Go Downtown', is a 1970 live recording from the Fillmore East with Whitten himself singing lead; the dead man is on his own memorial. Young kept the flubbed notes and cracked high notes on purpose. It belongs to what fans call the 'ditch trilogy', with 'Time Fades Away' and 'On the Beach', his deliberate swerve away from the success of 'Harvest'.","Critics were divided on release; some heard sloppiness, others unbearable honesty. Its reputation grew steadily, and it is now often called his best album and a precursor to punk's and grunge's distrust of polish. Young himself said it was the closest he came to art. The argument over whether rawness is a style or a truth starts here.","Listen straight through, late, in one sitting, as one long wake. Hear 'Tired Eyes', half-spoken, and notice how the pedal steel does the weeping the voice will not. Compare Hank Williams, whose broken-hearted country it drinks from, and the grief of Nick Cave's 'Skeleton Tree' forty years later."],"neil-young-rust-never-sleeps":["An album of two halves. Side one is Young alone with acoustic guitar and harmonica: 'Thrasher', about leaving old companions, and 'Pocahontas', a dreamy history of dispossession. Side two brings on Crazy Horse at deafening volume: 'Powderfinger', a doomed young man defending his family's river, 'Welfare Mothers', 'Sedan Delivery'. The same song bookends it, as the acoustic 'My My, Hey Hey (Out of the Blue)' and the distorted, grinding 'Hey Hey, My My (Into the Black)'.","Most of it was recorded live on Young's 1978 tour with Crazy Horse, with the audience largely removed and some overdubs added. Punk had just arrived, and many 1960s stars felt pushed aside; Young instead embraced it, naming Johnny Rotten of the Sex Pistols in the bookend song. The title came from the band Devo, who adapted it from an advertising slogan for a rust-proofing paint.","The bookend songs argue that it is better to burn out than to fade away, an idea taken up by a whole generation; Kurt Cobain borrowed a line from it in his suicide note in 1994, which haunted Young. Listen to how the feedback on 'Into the Black' is played almost melodically: Crazy Horse's sludge is the sound grunge would later adopt as its own.","Hailed on release, it topped the Village Voice critics' poll for 1979, and a concert film of the tour followed. It is regularly cited as a bridge between 1960s rock and punk, and Young as the 'godfather of grunge'. Debate lingers over whether the 'burn out' ethic is inspiring or dangerous, especially after Cobain.","Play the first track, then skip to the last, and hear one song change from a campfire tune to a wall of noise. Then give 'Powderfinger' full attention as a short story. Compare Dylan's 1966 Manchester concert, which stages the same acoustic-then-electric split under protest."],"joni-mitchell-blue":["Ten songs, spare and close-miked, sung in a voice that leaps without warning from low murmur to high clear octave. Mitchell accompanies herself on Appalachian dulcimer on 'All I Want', 'Carey', 'California' and 'A Case of You', on piano for the title song, 'River' and 'The Last Time I Saw Richard', and on open-tuned guitar elsewhere. The sound is intimate and unguarded; the songs are about travel, love affairs and a longing for home.","Recorded at A&M Studios in Hollywood in early 1971 and released that June, it followed a European journey in 1970, including a stay among hippies in the caves at Matala on Crete, the setting of 'Carey'. Mitchell was living in Laurel Canyon, the Los Angeles hillside community of songwriters, and had just ended relationships with Graham Nash and James Taylor, who plays guitar on three songs; Stephen Stills and pedal steel player Sneaky Pete Kleinow also appear.","'Little Green' is about the daughter Mitchell gave up for adoption in 1965, a secret that became public only in the 1990s, before they were reunited. 'River' opens and closes with the piano playing 'Jingle Bells' in a minor, melancholy key. Her open tunings, partly a way round the weakness polio left in her left hand, create chords no standard-tuned guitarist would find.","Reviews were warm but some were uneasy at its nakedness; Mitchell herself said she had no defences left while making it. Its standing only grew, and in 2020 Rolling Stone ranked it third among all albums. It is now the model of confessional songwriting, though Mitchell has resisted the word 'confessional' and insisted on craft over diary.","Start with 'A Case of You' and listen to the dulcimer's bright drone against her low voice. Then 'River' at Christmas time. Hear it beside Nick Drake's 'Pink Moon' for the other great solitary record of the moment, and beside Schumann's 'Dichterliebe', a song cycle of love recalled and lost, for its oldest ancestor."],"joni-mitchell-court-and-spark":["Eleven songs, most of them with a full band of jazz players, polished and sophisticated, with woodwinds, electric piano and supple drums. The title song opens on solo piano; 'Help Me' is a lithe pop hit with jazz harmonies; 'Free Man in Paris' is a breezy portrait of a music-business friend longing to escape; 'People's Parties' and 'Down to You' are introspective; the record ends, unexpectedly, with a jazz vocalese cover, 'Twisted'.","Released in January 1974 on David Geffen's Asylum label, it was made with Tom Scott's L.A. Express, a group of Los Angeles jazz and studio musicians. It became the best-selling album of Mitchell's career, and 'Help Me' was her only top ten single in America. 'Free Man in Paris' is widely understood to be about Geffen himself. Mitchell was at the peak of her commercial power.","'Twisted' was written in the early 1950s by the jazz singer Annie Ross, setting words to a saxophone solo by Wardell Gray; Mitchell's version even includes a comic cameo from the duo Cheech and Chong. The arrangements' jazz harmonies, not the pop hooks, are the real signal: from here on she would move steadily into jazz, working with Jaco Pastorius, Wayne Shorter and eventually Charles Mingus.","Warmly received and commercially enormous, it topped the Village Voice critics' poll for 1974. Some early fans missed the stark dulcimer of 'Blue'. It is now regarded as a model of how a songwriter can move into jazz-inflected pop without losing edge, and its polish now sounds less like compromise than like a new kind of precision.","Listen first to 'Help Me' for the way the melody leaps unexpectedly between registers, then to 'Down to You' for the orchestral side. Then hear 'Twisted' and seek out the Lambert, Hendricks and Ross version. Follow with 'Hejira' to see where the jazz leads."],"joni-mitchell-hejira":["Nine long, wandering songs of the road, built on Mitchell's chiming open-tuned guitar with very little else. On four of them Jaco Pastorius's fretless bass sings like a second voice, sliding, harmonising, chiming harmonics. There are no choruses in the usual sense; the melodies flow like speech over steady rolling guitar. 'Coyote' opens with a portrait of a restless man; 'Amelia' addresses the lost aviator Amelia Earhart; 'Refuge of the Roads' ends it.","Mitchell wrote most of the songs in 1976 while driving alone across America, from Maine back to Los Angeles, after touring with Bob Dylan's Rolling Thunder Revue. 'Coyote' is usually read as a portrait of the playwright Sam Shepard, also on that tour. 'Hejira' is the Arabic word for the Prophet Muhammad's flight from Mecca to Medina; she chose it for its sense of honourable escape.","'Furry Sings the Blues' is about visiting the aged Memphis blues man Furry Lewis on Beale Street, then being demolished; Neil Young plays harmonica on it. Lewis was reportedly unhappy with the song. Listen to Pastorius on 'Hejira' itself: he recorded his parts afterward, overdubbing melody lines that treat the bass as a lead instrument, a first in her work.","Received respectfully but with less excitement than its predecessors, it sold modestly. Over time it has become a musicians' favourite and for many critics her finest record, the point where her folk roots and jazz ambitions fully merge. Some still find its long, hookless songs monotonous; its admirers call that monotony the hypnotic hum of a highway.","Play 'Coyote' then 'Amelia' on a long drive. Focus on Pastorius's bass as a second singer. To hear the jazz he brought, try Miles Davis's 'Bitches Brew'; for the blues she was visiting, Mississippi John Hurt's 'Avalon Blues' is close in spirit to Furry Lewis."],"leonard-cohen-songs-of-leonard-cohen":["Ten songs sung in a low, level, almost unmusical voice over steady nylon-string fingerpicking, with discreet strings, soft female backing voices and odd touches such as a jaw harp. 'Suzanne', a slow, circling portrait of a woman by the Montreal waterfront, opens; 'Sisters of Mercy', 'So Long, Marianne' and 'Hey, That's No Way to Say Goodbye' follow. The songs are long, literary and patient; the voice never raises itself, and that restraint is the spell.","Cohen was thirty-three and already a known Canadian poet and novelist, whose 'Beautiful Losers' appeared in 1966. He turned to songs partly because books did not pay. Judy Collins recorded 'Suzanne' first, in 1966, and John Hammond signed him to Columbia. Producer John Simon completed the album in New York in 1967, over Cohen's objections to some of his arrangements. 'So Long, Marianne' is for Marianne Ihlen, with whom he had lived on the Greek island of Hydra.","The rolling guitar pattern owes a debt to flamenco: Cohen said a young Spanish guitarist in Montreal taught him a few chords before dying, not long after. Listen to how the melodies stay within a narrow range, like chanted verse, and how the backing voices sometimes carry the melody while Cohen intones below it, an arrangement idea he kept for fifty years.","It sold modestly in America and better in Britain and Europe, where Cohen quickly became a cult figure. Early critics found his voice dreary; later listeners heard gravity. Robert Altman's film 'McCabe & Mrs. Miller' (1971) used three of its songs. It is now regarded as a founding document of the literary singer-songwriter, and its songs are sung widely.","Hear 'Suzanne' first, paying attention to the guitar more than the words, then 'Master Song', and notice how the voice never hurries even when the song runs past five minutes. Compare Nick Drake's 'Five Leaves Left', also dark and hushed, and Hugo Wolf's Mörike songs, where the poem similarly governs the music."],"leonard-cohen-hallelujah":["A slow song in a rocking triple time, built on simple chords and a chorus of one word repeated. In Cohen's own recording, his deep, weathered voice is set against a synthesiser, a plodding rhythm and a gospel-tinged choir. The verses move between biblical stories (David the harpist, Samson and Delilah) and a love affair, mixing reverence and sensuality, with praise and doubt sitting side by side.","It first appeared on the album 'Various Positions' in 1984, produced by John Lissauer. Columbia's chief declined to release the album in America, and it came out there through a small label. Cohen said he wrote dozens of verses over several years, and he changed the verses he sang in concert, moving it from sacred to more worldly. He was fifty, his career in the doldrums, his record company unsure what to do with him.","The first verse describes its own harmony: as the words name the chord changes, the music makes them. John Cale's 1991 recording for a tribute album chose different verses and put it on piano; Jeff Buckley's 1994 version was based on Cale's, and it was Cale's version that played in the film 'Shrek' in 2001. The song everyone knows is a collaboration across three recordings.","Hardly noticed in 1984, it became over the next three decades one of the most covered songs in the world, sung at funerals, in talent shows and at Christmas. That ubiquity has its critics, including, at times, Cohen, who suggested it should be rested. The debate over which verses make the true song is unanswerable, since Cohen himself never fixed it.","Hear Cohen's original first, with its slightly dated synthesiser, then Cale's, then Buckley's, and notice how the meaning shifts with the verses chosen. Compare Mahalia Jackson's gospel for the tradition of praise it borrows from. Then return to the start and hear the song describe its own chords."],"leonard-cohen-im-your-man":["Eight songs framed in cheap synthesisers and drum machines, with Cohen's voice now a deep, dry bass murmur. 'First We Take Manhattan' is a menacing synth-pop march; 'Ain't No Cure for Love' a sly ballad; 'Everybody Knows' a grim catalogue of the world's rigged game; 'Take This Waltz' a Viennese waltz with violin; 'Tower of Song' a wry self-portrait of the songwriter's trade. Female backing voices, especially Jennifer Warnes and Anjani Thomas, carry the tunes.","Released in early 1988, it followed Jennifer Warnes's 1986 album of Cohen songs, 'Famous Blue Raincoat', which revived interest in him. Cohen co-produced with several collaborators, and co-wrote 'Everybody Knows' with his longtime singer Sharon Robinson. He was in his fifties and embracing irony, sharp suits and cheap keyboards, the opposite of his 1960s image as a mournful troubadour, and his voice had dropped to a register that made every line sound like a verdict.","'Take This Waltz' adapts a poem by Federico García Lorca, the poet after whom Cohen named his daughter; it first appeared on a Spanish tribute album for the fiftieth anniversary of Lorca's death. In 'Tower of Song' he imagines Hank Williams in the same building, a hundred floors above: a songwriter's lineage placed in a joke. The cheap keyboard solo there is played by Cohen himself, deliberately clumsy.","A critical and commercial comeback, especially in Europe and Canada. Some old fans found the synthesisers a betrayal; others heard wit and menace they had missed. Now it is often ranked among his best, and its songs, especially 'Everybody Knows' and 'Tower of Song', are cited as late-career models of irony and gravity combined.","Start with 'Tower of Song' and hear the jokes; then 'Everybody Knows' for the dread. Notice how the women's voices sing the melody while Cohen speaks under it. Then hear Hank Williams's 'I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry' to meet the man upstairs."],"van-morrison-astral-weeks":["Eight long songs that feel like one improvised dream. Morrison's acoustic guitar and voice are surrounded by jazz players: Richard Davis's double bass wanders freely, Connie Kay's drums whisper, vibraphone and flute drift in, strings float on top. There are no hooks and few choruses; songs like 'Madame George' and 'Cyprus Avenue' stretch to nine or ten minutes, circling, repeating a phrase until it becomes an incantation. The voice stammers, soars and scats.","Morrison, a young singer from Belfast who had fronted the band Them and had a hit with 'Brown Eyed Girl', was stuck in a contract dispute after his producer Bert Berns died in 1967. Living near Boston, he signed with Warner Bros. and recorded the album in three sessions in New York in autumn 1968 with producer Lewis Merenstein and jazz musicians he had just met. Strings and horns were added afterward.","The musicians say Morrison sang and played in a vocal booth, barely speaking, and they simply followed; most songs were done in one or two takes, so what you hear is them discovering the song. The streets are real: Cyprus Avenue is a leafy road in east Belfast. Listen for Davis's bass: he played as freely as in a jazz trio, and the whole record hangs on him.","It sold poorly at first and reviews were few, but it became a cult treasure. Lester Bangs's 1979 essay made the case that it had saved him. Now it routinely appears near the top of all-time album lists, though Morrison has been dismissive of the record's mystique and of the way it was made.","Hear it whole, in one sitting, perhaps on a long walk. Start with 'Sweet Thing' for joy, 'Madame George' for longing. Follow the bass rather than the voice. Compare Miles Davis's 'In a Silent Way', from months later, and Joni Mitchell's 'Hejira' for other open-ended musical journeys."],"van-morrison-moondance":["Ten tight, warm songs, all under five minutes, with horns, piano and organ, a joyful soul-and-rhythm-and-blues record. 'And It Stoned Me' recalls a childhood day of rain; the title song swings like a jazz standard with walking bass and flute; 'Crazy Love' is a falsetto ballad; 'Caravan' is a radio-worship stomp; 'Into the Mystic' is a quietly ecstatic song about sailing home. The mood is unforced celebration.","After 'Astral Weeks' failed commercially, Morrison produced this himself in New York in late 1969, with a regular band and a horn section. He was living in Woodstock, in upstate New York, where Dylan and the Band were neighbours. Released early in 1970, it was his first big album success and set the pattern for his long career. Where the earlier album had been improvised by strangers, this one was rehearsed and arranged, with Morrison firmly in charge.","Morrison has said he wanted to sound like the rhythm-and-blues and soul singers he grew up with in Belfast, Ray Charles especially, and listen to how he bends phrases like a soul shouter. The swinging title song, now a jazz-club standard, was originally conceived around a saxophone riff; Morrison was a saxophonist before he was a singer.","Well reviewed and popular, it has always stood slightly in the shadow of 'Astral Weeks' for critics, while being the better loved for many listeners. It shows that Morrison could be both mystic and hit-maker. Its classic mixture of soul, jazz and Irish folk was later called 'Celtic soul', and 'Into the Mystic' has become one of his most covered songs.","Put on 'Into the Mystic' first, then 'Caravan', loud. Notice how the horns answer his voice the way a gospel choir answers a preacher. Hear Otis Redding's 'Otis Blue' and Sam Cooke's 'Live at the Harlem Square Club' for his models."],"bruce-springsteen-born-to-run":["Eight songs, layered and huge, full of piano, glockenspiel, surging guitars and Clarence Clemons's saxophone. 'Thunder Road' opens on harmonica and piano, a young man urging a girl into his car; 'Backstreets' is operatic betrayal; the title song is a wall of sound with a four-count countdown; 'Jungleland' closes with nine minutes of street drama and a long saxophone solo. The tone is romantic, cinematic, desperate: escape from small-town New Jersey at any cost.","Springsteen's first two albums had sold poorly and Columbia was losing patience. The title song alone took months to record in 1974, at a studio in Blauvelt, New York, as he tried to pack in the dense Phil Spector sound of his youth. Critic Jon Landau, who had written that he had seen rock and roll's future in Springsteen, came in as co-producer. In October 1975 Springsteen was on the covers of Time and Newsweek the same week.","The title song has dozens of overdubbed guitars, several keyboards and strings stacked into a single roar, with the glockenspiel cutting through at the top; Springsteen has said he wanted Roy Orbison's voice and Dylan's words over Spector's sound. On the cover photograph he leans on Clemons: the image of a Black and white partnership was part of the message.","It was a hit and a critical triumph, though the media hype prompted a backlash and doubts about whether he was a record-company creation. Its reputation has never slipped; it is regularly ranked among rock's greatest albums. Some critics find its romanticism overwrought; others hear it as the last flourish of 1950s rock's teenage dream.","Play 'Thunder Road' first; then 'Jungleland', listening for Clemons's solo as the emotional climax. Follow how Roy Bittan's piano carries the melodic line. Compare Dylan's 'Like a Rolling Stone' and Elvis's 'Mystery Train' for the lineage, then his own stark 'Nebraska' for the opposite response to the same streets."],"bruce-springsteen-nebraska":["Ten songs recorded alone on a four-track cassette machine: voice, acoustic guitar, harmonica, a bit of mandolin and glockenspiel, all bathed in tape hiss and echo. The title song is told by a young killer on death row, calm and unrepentant; 'Atlantic City' is a doomed man's plea; 'Highway Patrolman' a brother torn between duty and blood; 'Reason to Believe' ends the album with a bleak half-smile. The sound is lonely, stark, American gothic.","Springsteen recorded these songs at home in Colts Neck, New Jersey, in early January 1982, as demos for the E Street Band. The band versions felt wrong, so he released the cassette itself, after struggling to master it for vinyl. He was reading Flannery O'Connor and had seen Terrence Malick's film 'Badlands', about the 1958 killings by Charles Starkweather, the subject of the title song. Reagan's America was in recession.","Some songs recorded at the same full-band sessions became the album 'Born in the U.S.A.', whose title song began life in this acoustic set. 'Mansion on the Hill' borrows a Hank Williams title. Warren Zanes's 2023 book 'Deliver Me from Nowhere' describes how dark Springsteen's state of mind was during the recording.","Critics were impressed but puzzled; it sold modestly without a tour or single. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now seen as a founding document of lo-fi home recording and of the modern Americana revival. Johnny Cash recorded two of its songs within a year. Some prefer the bright E Street Springsteen; many now think this is his best.","Hear the title song alone, late at night. Notice how the hiss and echo make the room part of the song. Then hear Woody Guthrie's 'Dust Bowl Ballads', its ancestor, and Johnny Cash's 'American Recordings', its descendant. Play 'Born to Run' straight after to hear the same highways without hope."],"tom-waits-swordfishtrombones":["Fifteen short songs for a bizarre junkyard band: marimba, pump organ, bass, bagpipes, clanging metal, muted brass and a double bass slapped like a drum. Waits growls, croons and barks. 'Underground' is a stomping march of subterranean creatures; 'In the Neighborhood' is a funeral brass-band hymn to his street; 'Frank's Wild Years' is a deadpan spoken story over organ; 'Soldier's Things' is a tender piano ballad of army junk for sale.","Waits had spent the 1970s as a jazzy, beatnik piano balladeer on Asylum Records. In 1980 he married Kathleen Brennan, who introduced him to Captain Beefheart and to the instrument-builder Harry Partch. Asylum reportedly would not release this new direction; Island Records did, in 1983. Waits produced it himself, a first, and wrote many of the arrangements around sounds he found rather than chords he knew.","Harry Partch's home-built instruments and hobo songs are the key: Waits hunted down odd percussion and chromelodeon-like organs to get that sound. 'Johnsburg, Illinois' is a tiny love song for Brennan's hometown. The album is the first of a trilogy about the character Frank, continued in 'Rain Dogs' and 'Franks Wild Years'.","Critics hailed a reinvention; some old fans were bewildered. It is now regarded as the turn that made Waits one of the most original voices in rock, and its 'junkyard orchestra' was widely imitated. Few argue now about its importance; the question is whether it or 'Rain Dogs' is the peak.","Start with 'In the Neighborhood' for the brass, then 'Underground' for the percussion. Listen for what is being hit. Then hear Harry Partch's 'Barstow' and Captain Beefheart's 'Trout Mask Replica' for the sources, and Kurt Weill's 'Threepenny Opera' for the cabaret in his voice."],"tom-waits-rain-dogs":["Nineteen songs in a rattling, lurching sound, a carnival of the urban lost. Marc Ribot's angular guitar leads, with marimba, accordion, trombone and banging percussion. 'Singapore' opens as a sailors' shanty; 'Clap Hands' is dark ritual; 'Cemetery Polka' a family portrait; 'Tango Till They're Sore' a drunken tango; 'Time' and 'Hang Down Your Head' are tender ballads; 'Downtown Train' is a straight rock love song. Waits's voice ranges from growl to lullaby.","Waits had moved to New York, and he recorded the album there in 1985 with a band including Ribot, Robert Quine, and Keith Richards of the Rolling Stones on three songs. It continues the sound of 'Swordfishtrombones' and the tale of lost wanderers in the city, the 'rain dogs' of the title, strays who can't find their way home after rain washes away the scent.","The cover photograph, by Anders Petersen, was taken in a Hamburg bar in the late 1960s. 'Downtown Train' became a hit for Rod Stewart in 1989, a telling measure of how good a pop song hides under the clatter. Listen for Richards's slack, bluesy guitar on 'Big Black Mariah': a Stones sound in a very un-Stones room.","It was widely hailed as his best work, and it often appears on lists of the great albums of the 1980s. Critics praised its variety and unity; a few found it too long. Its sound, with Ribot's guitar and the junkyard percussion, influenced a generation of alternative and Americana musicians.","Start with 'Time' for the balladeer, then 'Singapore' and 'Clap Hands' for the carnival. Listen for Ribot's guitar, never quite where you expect, and for the percussion, which is often something other than a drum. Hear Kurt Weill's 'Threepenny Opera' and the Rolling Stones' 'Let It Bleed' beside it."],"carole-king-tapestry":["Twelve songs, with King's warm, slightly husky voice and propulsive gospel-style piano at the centre and a small, relaxed band around them. 'I Feel the Earth Move' opens with a stomping piano riff; 'It's Too Late' is a gentle breakup song; 'You've Got a Friend' a hymn of loyalty; 'Beautiful' a morning pep talk. King also reclaims her own 1960s songs for other singers, 'Will You Love Me Tomorrow' and '(You Make Me Feel Like) A Natural Woman', slowed and made personal.","King had been a hit songwriter in New York's Brill Building world of the 1960s, writing with Gerry Goffin for the Shirelles, Aretha Franklin and others. After their divorce she moved to Laurel Canyon in Los Angeles and started singing her own songs. She recorded 'Tapestry' in January 1971 at A&M Studios with producer Lou Adler and friends including James Taylor, Joni Mitchell and her husband Charles Larkey.","Toni Stern wrote the words for 'It's Too Late' and 'Where You Lead'. Listen to the piano: King's left hand plays gospel bass lines that give the songs their drive. The cat on the cover, named Telemachus, was her own. James Taylor released his own version of 'You've Got a Friend' the same year, and it was his that topped the charts.","It topped the American album chart for fifteen weeks and won four Grammys, including Album of the Year. For years it was the best-selling album by a woman. Critics then and now praise its warmth; some find it too comfortable beside Mitchell's sharper self-exposure. It remains the model of the songwriter reclaiming her own songs.","Hear 'Natural Woman' in King's version and then Aretha Franklin's: the same song as private confession and public declaration. Then play 'I Feel the Earth Move' for the piano, and 'So Far Away' for the loneliness under the warmth. Compare Joni Mitchell's 'Blue', made the same year in the same studio."],"randy-newman-sail-away":["Twelve short songs, most for Newman's lazy, drawling voice and rolling New Orleans piano, many framed by a lush orchestra he arranged himself. The title song, with sweeping strings, is a slave trader's cheerful sales pitch luring Africans to America. 'Political Science' is a jaunty plan to bomb the world flat; 'God's Song' has God mocking humanity; 'Old Man' is a hushed deathbed farewell; 'You Can Leave Your Hat On' is a sly striptease.","Newman was the nephew of the Hollywood film composers Alfred, Lionel and Emil Newman, and grew up around studio orchestras; he had been a staff songwriter before recording. His 1968 debut had been orchestral and unsuccessful. This album, produced by Lenny Waronker and Russ Titelman for Reprise and released in 1972, was his breakthrough with critics, though not with the public.","Newman's great device is the unreliable narrator: nearly every song is sung in the voice of someone he despises or pities, and the lovely orchestral writing is the lure. Listen to the title track's strings, which echo the hymn-like Americana of Aaron Copland; the prettiness is the trap. Joe Cocker's rock hit version of 'You Can Leave Your Hat On' in 1986 turned irony into sincerity.","Critics adored it and it is often called his best album; sales were modest. The irony has always been a risk: listeners sometimes take the narrators at face value. Newman later found wide success writing film scores, especially for Pixar, which some see as a return to his family's trade and others as a waste of his bite.","Hear the title song, then 'Political Science' and 'God's Song', and ask who is speaking and whether Newman agrees with a word of it. Then listen to Copland's 'Appalachian Spring' and Gershwin's 'Porgy and Bess' for the American orchestral idiom he uses against itself."],"randy-newman-good-old-boys":["Twelve songs making a loose portrait of the American South, sung by Newman in character over piano, rhythm section and his own orchestral arrangements. 'Rednecks' opens with a Southern narrator mocking Northern liberals' hypocrisy about race; 'Birmingham' is a steelworker's proud hymn to his city; 'Louisiana 1927' recalls the Great Mississippi Flood with a slow, hymn-like orchestra; 'Kingfish' and 'Every Man a King' evoke Governor Huey Long; 'Marie' is a drunk man's tender apology.","Newman, who spent some childhood years in New Orleans, first conceived the album as a story about one man, Johnny Cutler, a demo version of which was later released. He said 'Rednecks' was sparked by seeing the segregationist Lester Maddox mocked on a television talk show. Released by Reprise in 1974 amid the Watergate crisis, it was his most commercially successful album so far.","'Every Man a King' was written in 1935 by Huey Long himself with the bandleader Castro Carazo; Newman quotes the populist anthem straight. 'Rednecks' uses a racial slur repeatedly as its narrator's own word, which is the point of the satire and a reason the song is rarely played. 'Louisiana 1927' became an anthem of mourning after Hurricane Katrina in 2005.","Critics praised its ambition and nuance, though 'Rednecks' has made some uncomfortable from the start. It is now seen as one of the most penetrating portraits of the South in popular music, mixing sympathy and scorn. Debate continues over whether his irony can be heard correctly without explanation, and over who is entitled to sing it.","Start with 'Louisiana 1927' and listen to the orchestra, a sort of Copland hymn gone mournful. Then hear 'Rednecks' and 'Birmingham', and notice the shift in voice. Play Charley Patton's 'High Water Everywhere', recorded after the same 1927 flood, for the Delta's own account."],"nick-cave-the-boatmans-call":["Twelve slow, spare songs, most built on Cave's piano and deep baritone with the Bad Seeds playing softly behind: brushed drums, organ, bass, a little violin. 'Into My Arms' opens with a prayer to a God the singer does not believe in; 'Brompton Oratory' sets a love song in a London church; 'People Ain't No Good' is a ballad of disillusion; 'West Country Girl' and 'Black Hair' are intimate portraits. There is almost no volume; everything is close.","Cave, an Australian who had fronted the violent post-punk band the Birthday Party and made a name with dark gothic ballads, was living in London. He had just released 'Murder Ballads' (1996), gleefully bloodthirsty. This record followed the end of relationships with Viviane Carneiro, mother of his son, and with the singer PJ Harvey. Released by Mute in March 1997.","Listen for the religious language: Cave was reading the Bible seriously, and many songs are addressed to God as much as to a lover. 'Into My Arms' was sung by Cave at the funeral of his friend Michael Hutchence of INXS later that year; he asked cameras to be turned off. The songs are in effect a cycle in the lieder tradition, one voice and a piano.","Hailed by many as a brave change of direction, and a surprise from the author of 'Murder Ballads'; a few old fans missed the noise. It is now widely considered his finest record and the beginning of his mature style. The debate about whether his religiosity is sincere or a literary pose has run through his career since.","Play 'Into My Arms' first, late, and notice how few notes the piano uses. Then hear it beside Leonard Cohen's early records, the obvious ancestor, and Schubert's 'Die schöne Müllerin', a cycle of hope turning to loss for one voice and piano."],"nick-cave-skeleton-tree":["Eight songs with almost no conventional structure: floating synthesiser drones, loops and electronic pulses by Warren Ellis, with Cave's voice speaking, half-singing, sometimes cracking. 'Jesus Alone' opens with a harsh electronic drone and a litany of images; 'Girl in Amber' circles around a frozen moment; 'I Need You' is a raw, repetitive plea; 'Distant Sky' is a duet with the Danish soprano Else Torp; the title track ends with an almost tender acceptance.","Recording began in late 2014. In July 2015 Cave's fifteen-year-old son Arthur died after falling from a cliff near their home in Brighton. Much of the writing had been done beforehand, but the album was finished in grief, partly in France. It was released in September 2016, along with Andrew Dominik's documentary 'One More Time with Feeling', about the making of it.","Many lyrics were written before the death and now seem uncannily predictive; Cave has written about how uncomfortable that is. Listen to how many vocals are improvised, with the slips left in. Else Torp is best known for early and sacred music, including Arvo Pärt; her pure voice in 'Distant Sky' is like a visitor from a different tradition.","Reviewers were overwhelmed, many calling it one of the most powerful records ever made about grief. Some found it too painful to revisit. It began a later, ambient-leaning phase in Cave's work, continued on 'Ghosteen' (2019). The difficulty of writing about it critically, given its circumstances, is itself part of its reception.","Hear it whole once, then return to 'Distant Sky'. Notice how the electronics hold everything in suspension, like breath held. Hear it beside Mahler's 'Kindertotenlieder', songs on the deaths of children, and Arvo Pärt's 'Cantus in memoriam Benjamin Britten', for other music made from loss."],"elliott-smith-either-or":["Twelve songs, most built on intricate fingerpicked acoustic guitar and a hushed, double-tracked voice, some filled out with bass, drums and electric guitar that Smith played himself. 'Speed Trials' opens; 'Between the Bars' is a slow, whispered waltz; 'Ballad of Big Nothing' and 'Rose Parade' have Beatles-style melodies; 'Say Yes' ends the album on a small, hopeful note. The melodies are complex and graceful even as the words are bleak.","Smith, who had played in the Portland, Oregon, band Heatmiser, recorded the album largely himself in Portland and released it on the independent label Kill Rock Stars in February 1997. The title comes from the philosopher Søren Kierkegaard. That year Gus Van Sant used several of its songs in the film 'Good Will Hunting', along with Smith's 'Miss Misery', which was nominated for an Oscar.","Listen to the doubled vocals: Smith sang each line twice and layered them, so the whisper is actually two voices. The chord progressions often move in unexpected directions, closer to the Beatles and to classical harmony than to folk. Smith's appearance at the 1998 Oscars in a white suit, performing to a huge audience, made him briefly and uncomfortably famous.","Critics praised it as his breakthrough; it sold modestly until 'Good Will Hunting'. After Smith's death in 2003 it gained a sadder aura. It is now regarded as a defining record of 1990s indie music, and his intimate, multitracked style influenced many later songwriters, including Sufjan Stevens and Phoebe Bridgers.","Start with 'Between the Bars' and 'Say Yes'. Pay attention to the guitar picking and to how the melody moves away from the obvious note. Compare Nick Drake's 'Pink Moon' and the Beatles' 'Revolver', then Sufjan Stevens's 'Carrie & Lowell' for the next generation."],"elliott-smith-xo":["Fourteen songs with fuller arrangements than before: piano, strings, horns, drums, bright electric guitars, alongside the familiar whispered voice. 'Sweet Adeline' begins with just guitar and voice and bursts into a full band; 'Waltz #2 (XO)' is a stately waltz about a family's fracture; 'Baby Britain' and 'Independence Day' are bright pop; 'I Didn't Understand' ends the album with Smith's voice alone, multitracked into a choir with no instruments.","After the attention from 'Good Will Hunting', Smith signed to DreamWorks Records and made XO in Los Angeles with producers Rob Schnapf and Tom Rothrock, released in August 1998. He had more money and time than ever before and used them for Beatles-style arrangements with real strings and brass. It sold better than anything he had done, though he remained uneasy with the attention.","'Sweet Adeline' takes its title from an old barbershop song Smith's grandmother sang. 'Pitseleh' is a Yiddish endearment for a little one. Listen to 'I Didn't Understand': the close vocal harmony recalls the Beach Boys and the Beatles, with Smith singing every part. On 'Waltz #2', notice how the stately triple time sits against words about a mother and a stepfather, a sore subject he returned to often.","Critics welcomed the fuller sound; a few missed the bedroom intimacy. It is sometimes considered his most accomplished record, sometimes a slightly overproduced step away from his strengths. Its mixture of whispered voice and orchestral pop has been influential on a generation of indie songwriters, and the argument over bedroom versus studio Smith has never settled.","Hear 'Waltz #2' first, then 'Sweet Adeline', waiting for the moment the band crashes in after the quiet opening. Listen for how the voice stays whispered even when the arrangement is loud. Compare the Beatles' 'Abbey Road' for the arrangements, and 'Either/Or' for the same songwriter with less."],"sufjan-stevens-illinois":["Twenty-two tracks, many with long, whimsical titles, making a sprawling portrait of the state of Illinois through history, myth and personal memory. Stevens plays banjo, piano, guitar, woodwinds and glockenspiel, with brass, strings and a choir of cheerleaders. 'Chicago' is a joyous road song; 'John Wayne Gacy, Jr.' a quiet, disturbing portrait of a serial killer; 'Casimir Pulaski Day' a hushed song about a friend's death. Short instrumental interludes stitch it together.","Released by Stevens's own label, Asthmatic Kitty, in July 2005, it was the second of what he announced as a 'Fifty States' project, after 'Michigan' (2003); he later admitted it was mostly a joke. Stevens, from Detroit and living in Brooklyn, drew on history books, family stories and his Christian faith. The original cover showed Superman, which had to be changed after a legal complaint.","The piano, marimba and woodwind patterns repeat and shift like Steve Reich's and Philip Glass's minimalism, which Stevens admired; he also studied the oboe. 'Come On! Feel the Illinoise!' is partly in five beats to the bar. In 2024 the album became a Broadway dance-theatre show, 'Illinoise', directed and choreographed by Justin Peck, which won a Tony for its choreography.","It topped many critics' lists for 2005 and became one of the defining records of indie music in that decade. Some found it overlong and too twee. Its mixture of folk, orchestral pop and minimalism was widely imitated. Stevens's religious sincerity, rare in indie rock, remains part of the discussion.","Start with 'Chicago', then 'Casimir Pulaski Day', a song that sounds gentle until you follow the story. Listen for the repeating patterns under the melodies, and the brass that breaks in like a school band. Hear Steve Reich's 'Music for 18 Musicians' and Copland's 'Appalachian Spring' for its two American ancestors."],"sufjan-stevens-carrie-and-lowell":["Eleven quiet songs, mostly just fingerpicked acoustic guitar or banjo, with piano and soft synthesiser haze, and Stevens's voice doubled and whispered. 'Death with Dignity' opens; 'Should Have Known Better' moves from grief to a small light; 'Fourth of July' is a conversation with his dying mother; 'No Shade in the Shadow of the Cross' is almost unbearable; 'Blue Bucket of Gold' ends in a long, fading drone.","Stevens's mother, Carrie, who had left the family when he was a small child and struggled with mental illness and addiction, died of cancer in 2012. For a few summers in his childhood she had been married to Lowell Brams, who later co-founded Stevens's record label, Asthmatic Kitty. Stevens made the album in several places, some of it on a phone, and released it in March 2015.","The songs are full of Oregon place names from those summers with Carrie and Lowell. The doubled, whispered vocals are close to Elliott Smith's technique. The album's photographs, including the cover, come from the family's own snapshots. Listen to the closing drone of 'Blue Bucket of Gold': after all the words, the album dissolves into a long wash of sound, grief with nothing left to say.","It was received as one of the most moving albums of the decade, and appeared near the top of many year-end lists. Some found its sparseness a relief after the complexity of 'Illinois'. It is now often compared with Nick Drake's 'Pink Moon' and Springsteen's 'Nebraska' as a classic of solitary, intimate recording.","Hear it whole, quietly, preferably on headphones so the doubled whisper sits close. Start with 'Should Have Known Better' and notice the shift in mood at the end, when a brighter figure enters. Compare Elliott Smith's 'Either/Or', and Fauré's 'Requiem' for a gentle, unfrightened music of death."],"the-teddy-bears-to-know-him-is-to-love-him":["A slow, hushed ballad in a lilting triple time, barely louder than a lullaby. Annette Kleinbard sings the lead in a small, pure voice close to the microphone while two young men hum and croon beneath her, and a guitar picks out gentle broken chords. There is no drama and no climax: the whole record is one devoted confession repeated, its title returning like a refrain in a hymn, and it is over in about two and a half minutes.","Phil Spector was a Los Angeles high-school student of eighteen when he wrote this, formed a trio with two friends, and paid for a session at Gold Star Studios. It came out on the small Doré label as a B-side, was flipped by radio, and reached number one in the United States late in 1958. For a teenager with no connections it was a staggering start, and it taught Spector that the producer, not the singer, could be the real author of a record.","The title came from the inscription on the gravestone of Spector's father, who had taken his own life when Phil was nine; Spector changed only the tense. Knowing that, the song stops being a teenage crush and becomes something odder: a son's private grief dressed in the clothes of a love song, sung by a girl who did not know where the words came from.","At the time it was one more soft teen ballad in a year full of them, and the Teddy Bears could not follow it up; the group was finished within a year. Its standing rose with Spector's. Historians now hear it as the seed of his method, a producer treating the recording studio as the instrument. The Beatles sang it in their early club and audition sets, and Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Emmylou Harris recorded a fine trio version in 1987.","Listen for how dry and close the lead voice is: this is the opposite of the later Wall of Sound, with almost no echo at all. Then play it straight after Be My Baby, five years later, and hear the same producer's ear for a girl's voice swallowed by a cathedral of noise. The single mix, in mono, is the one to hear."],"the-drifters-there-goes-my-baby":["A rhythm-and-blues vocal group record with violins on it, which in 1959 was the whole shock. Ben E. King's lead is raw and pleading, the group moans behind him, and over a heavy, loping beat a string section plays a swaying, faintly Middle Eastern figure while a kettledrum thuds away underneath. The effect is lush and a little seasick at once, as if a doo-wop group had wandered into a film score.","The original Drifters had been sacked wholesale by their manager in 1958 and replaced by a Harlem group called the Five Crowns, with the young Ben E. King among them. Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller, the writers of Hound Dog, were producing for Atlantic in New York and decided to dress this new line-up in strings, a device then reserved for white pop crooners. It reached the top of the rhythm-and-blues chart and the pop top ten.","Listen to the timpani: it is noticeably out of tune with the band, and nobody fixed it. Atlantic's Jerry Wexler reportedly thought the record was a mess and did not want it released. That woozy mismatch between the kettledrum, the strings and the group is exactly what makes it sound new; the accident became the texture that Phil Spector, who apprenticed with Leiber and Stoller soon after, would build a career on.","Listeners bought it in large numbers while some in the business found it pretentious for an R&B record. History has sided with its ambition. It is now routinely cited as the first rhythm-and-blues record with a real string arrangement, the bridge between doo-wop and the orchestral pop of the Brill Building years, and the model for Stand by Me, Spanish Harlem and every uptown soul record that followed.","Start by ignoring the singer and following the strings, which never settle into ordinary accompaniment. Then listen to the drummer and timpani fight over the beat. Compare it with Hound Dog, by the same writers three years earlier, to hear how far they travelled. A good mono Atlantic compilation of the Drifters is the way in."],"the-shirelles-will-you-love-me-tomorrow":["A mid-tempo ballad sung by Shirley Owens over a light Latin-tinged beat, with strings sweeping in behind and the other three Shirelles answering in soft harmony. The song is a single question, asked in the dark by a girl weighing whether to trust a boy for one night: will this still be love in the morning? The melody climbs to that question and leaves it hanging each time, and the record fades without an answer.","Gerry Goffin and Carole King, a young married couple writing for Don Kirshner's Aldon Music near the Brill Building, wrote it when King was eighteen. The Shirelles were four schoolfriends from Passaic, New Jersey, signed to Florence Greenberg's small Scepter label and produced by Luther Dixon. Owens at first thought the song too white and too country for the group. It went to number one in the United States in early 1961, the first by a Black girl group.","The strings were King's own arrangement, and she is often said to have played the kettledrum on the session herself. Listen too for what the song refuses to say: the subject is plainly sex, yet every line is a question about the future rather than a statement about tonight, which is how it passed radio censors in 1960 while every teenager understood it perfectly.","Some stations did balk at the subject, but it sold vastly and made the girl-group sound a commercial category. Its reputation has only grown: it is now heard as the first great statement of female uncertainty in rock-era pop, and as the song that made Goffin and King the model Brill Building team. King's own slower version on Tapestry in 1971 invited a long argument about which is the truer reading.","Listen for the strings first entering, and how Owens's voice is lighter than you expect, almost conversational. Then compare King's 1971 piano version, which turns a teenage question into a grown woman's memory. A clean mono Scepter transfer keeps the strings and the voices in their original balance."],"the-shirelles-baby-its-you":["A slow, aching ballad built on a wordless 'sha-la-la' chant from the group that opens the record and returns between verses like a sigh. Shirley Owens sings of loving a boy whom everyone warns her against, her voice cracking slightly at the top, over a thin, churchy electric organ and a patient shuffle. It is small and unpolished beside later girl-group records, and that intimacy is its charm.","It is an early Burt Bacharach song, written with Mack David, Hal David's elder brother, and Luther Dixon, who produced for Scepter and took his credit under a pseudonym. Bacharach was still best known as Marlene Dietrich's musical director and was only beginning his Brill Building career. The record reached the American top ten in 1962 and became a staple of the British beat groups' repertoire.","The session was reportedly built over Bacharach's own demo track, with the Shirelles' voices added on top, which may explain why the backing sounds so bare and different from their other records. Listen for the harmonic swerve in the middle section, a slightly unexpected chord change that already has Bacharach's fingerprint: melody that turns a corner just where your ear expects it to go straight.","It was a solid hit rather than a sensation, and its long afterlife came through covers. The Beatles recorded it on their first album in 1963, one of several Shirelles songs in their set, and that made it known to millions who never heard the original. Critics now use it to show how deep the Beatles' debt to American girl groups ran, a debt they paid openly in interviews.","Hear the opening chant as the record's real hook, then listen for how little is happening beneath Owens: organ, drums, bass, hardly anything else. Then play the Beatles' version with John Lennon singing and notice how closely they copied the arrangement, down to the chant. A good Shirelles anthology collects it with the other Scepter hits."],"the-marvelettes-please-mr-postman":["A bright, stomping two and a half minutes in which a girl begs the mailman to check his bag once more for a letter from her boyfriend. Gladys Horton's lead is urgent and a little rough, the group chants the title behind her, and the rhythm section pounds a heavy backbeat with handclaps and a honking saxophone. It sounds less like a polished pop record than a schoolyard game that got very loud.","The Marvelettes were high-school girls from Inkster, Michigan, who entered a talent contest and were sent to audition for Berry Gordy's young Motown company in Detroit. The song grew out of a blues by a local writer, William Garrett, reworked by the group and Motown staff. Released on Tamla, it became the company's first number one on the American pop chart in December 1961, proof that a Black-owned Detroit label could top the national market.","The drummer on the session is widely reported to have been the young Marvin Gaye, then a Motown session player hoping for a solo career; listen for how hard and simple the backbeat is. And notice that the song is a girl-group record from a label that would soon perfect a very different, more polished sound: the rough edges here are the raw material the Supremes would later smooth away.","It was a hit at once and has never needed rescuing. The Beatles recorded it on With the Beatles in 1963 and the Carpenters took it to number one again in 1975, which made it one of the few songs to top the chart in two different decades. Historians of Motown place it at the start of the company's national story, before the house style had settled.","Count how many times you hear the word of the title before the first verse ends; the song is almost all hook. Then listen to the handclaps and piano, the Motown engine in its crudest form. Compare the Beatles' cover, sung by Lennon at a harder tempo, and then the Supremes' Where Did Our Love Go three years later."],"the-drifters-up-on-the-roof":["A gentle, unhurried song about escaping a crowded tenement by climbing to the rooftop, where the city noise falls away and the stars are close. Rudy Lewis sings it softly, almost privately, the group harmonising behind him, and the strings rise and fall like a breeze across the tar. The melody lifts with the singer on the chorus, and the verses sink back to street level, so the song's shape acts out its story.","Gerry Goffin and Carole King wrote it in 1962, King composing the melody on the drive home, as a song of urban refuge for the Drifters, then Atlantic's most sophisticated vocal group. By now Rudy Lewis had replaced Ben E. King as lead. The production kept the strings-and-baion style Leiber and Stoller had developed for the group, and the record reached the American top five early in 1963.","Listen to how the arrangement opens up at the chorus, strings climbing as the singer climbs the stairs; the orchestration is a small piece of scene painting. The song also carries a quiet social point: its rooftop is the only private place available to a working-class Black New Yorker in a crowded building, a detail sung so softly that most listeners hear only a pretty love song.","It was a major hit and has become one of the most covered songs of the Goffin and King catalogue, recorded by Laura Nyro, James Taylor and King herself on Tapestry. Critics often name it the most lyrical of the Brill Building songs, the point where teenage pop began to write about place, class and solitude rather than dates and heartbreak alone.","Listen for the lift into the chorus and the way the strings follow the singer upwards. Then play Carole King's own version from 1971 for the writer's view, plainer and more wistful. Put it beside On Broadway, the Drifters' next great city song, to hear two sides of the same New York."],"francoise-hardy-tous-les-garcons-et-les-filles":["A slow, melancholy pop song in which a teenage girl watches all the couples of her age walking hand in hand and wonders when she will know what that feels like. Françoise Hardy sings in a low, breathy, almost toneless voice, never pushing, over a plain strummed guitar, a soft backbeat and a ghostly chorus of backing voices. It sounds shy rather than sad, and that shyness is its whole character.","Hardy was eighteen, a Paris student who had taught herself guitar and written the song herself, when Disques Vogue released it in 1962. The French youth craze called yé-yé was built on translated American hits; Hardy's own song stood apart. She sang it on television during the results broadcast of the October 1962 referendum, a vast audience, and the record went on to sell well over a million copies.","Listen to how little the voice does: Hardy barely varies her volume, and the melody moves in small steps within a narrow range. That flatness, which French critics at first took for inexperience, is exactly what made her the opposite of the brassy American girl groups and of the chanson tradition's big theatrical singers, and it prefigures a whole later line of cool, withdrawn female pop vocalists.","It made her a star overnight and an icon of French youth, which Hardy herself later found embarrassing, dismissing her early records as naive. Critics have since reversed her verdict: the song is now heard as a model of understated pop songwriting by a young woman writing her own material at a time when few did. English-speaking pop, from Bob Dylan to Blur, took notice of her.","Listen once to the words for the feeling, even without French: the loneliness is in the voice. Then notice how the backing voices sit far behind her, like the crowd she is watching. Compare Lesley Gore's American records from the next year for a completely different model of the teenage girl in pop."],"the-crystals-da-doo-ron-ron":["Two minutes and twenty seconds of joy at full pelt. A girl tells how she met a boy on a Monday, and the group answers every line with the nonsense syllables of the title, while behind them pianos, guitars, saxophones, castanets and handclaps pile into one roaring, echoing mass. Hal Blaine's drums hammer the beat, the saxophone takes a honking solo, and the whole thing sounds as if it were recorded in a stone hall.","Phil Spector wrote it with the husband-and-wife team Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich, and recorded it at Gold Star Studios in Los Angeles with the session players later nicknamed the Wrecking Crew. It came out on Philles, his own label, and was a top-three hit in America and Britain in 1963. By now Spector used the Crystals' name as a brand rather than a group; he could put any voice on the record he chose.","The lead is not by an original Crystal at first: Darlene Love reportedly sang a guide vocal, and Spector then had sixteen-year-old La La Brooks of the Crystals record the lead that was released. And the nonsense title is a placeholder that Barry and Greenwich never replaced. Listen too for the echo chamber on everything, drums included, which is what turns ordinary instruments into one blurred wall.","It was an immediate hit and has never lost its place as one of the purest examples of Spector's style. Some later critics find the Crystals records the most enjoyable of all his productions precisely because they are less grand than the Ronettes' or the Righteous Brothers'. The treatment of the group as interchangeable voices has become part of the darker reassessment of Spector the man.","Listen with the volume up, and try to pick out individual instruments; you mostly cannot, which is the point. Then find the saxophone solo, one of the few things that stands out of the mass. Hear it alongside Be My Baby from the same summer to feel Spector's two moods, the romp and the ache."],"the-ronettes-be-my-baby":["It begins with a drum alone: a heavy kick, two beats, then a snare crack, the most imitated opening in pop. Ronnie Bennett's voice enters, nasal and yearning with a little tremor, promising devotion to a boy she has just met, and around her the Wall of Sound rises: pianos and guitars doubled many times, castanets, strings, horns, a choir of backing voices, all washed in echo. The chorus arrives like a wave breaking.","Phil Spector wrote it with Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich and recorded it at Gold Star in Los Angeles with his session players, among them Hal Blaine on drums, and with Sonny Bono and the young Cher reportedly among the backing voices. The Ronettes were three girls from Spanish Harlem, two sisters and their cousin, admired for their beehive hair and eyeliner. It reached number two in America in 1963. Ronnie later married Spector.","Listen to the fade: the strings keep climbing while the voices repeat the chorus, and the record seems to fall away into its own echo rather than ending. And listen to how the drum pattern of the opening returns every time the chorus lands. Brian Wilson has told of hearing it on a car radio and having to pull over; he spent years trying to answer it, and Don't Worry Baby and Pet Sounds are the result.","It was a huge hit at once, and its reputation has grown into near-universal agreement that it is one of the greatest pop singles ever made. The drum intro has been copied by everyone from the Jesus and Mary Chain to Amy Winehouse. The later accounts of Spector's cruelty to Ronnie, and his conviction for murder in 2009, now shadow how the record is heard, but not whether it is heard.","Start by listening only to the drums through the whole song, then play it again for the voice, and a third time just to feel the chorus arrive. Then listen to the Beach Boys' God Only Knows or Good Vibrations to hear what Brian Wilson learned. The original mono single mix is the only way it was meant to sound."],"phil-spector-a-christmas-gift-for-you":["Thirteen tracks: familiar seasonal songs such as White Christmas, Frosty the Snowman, Sleigh Ride and Santa Claus Is Coming to Town, each dragged into the Wall of Sound with sleigh bells, castanets, pounding drums and massed pianos, sung in turn by the Ronettes, the Crystals, Darlene Love and Bob B. Soxx and the Blue Jeans. One new song, Christmas (Baby Please Come Home), is the emotional centre, and the album ends with Spector himself speaking over Silent Night.","Spector recorded it through the late summer and autumn of 1963 at Gold Star, treating an album, then usually a few hits and filler, as a single designed whole. It was released on 22 November 1963, the day President Kennedy was shot in Dallas. In a country in mourning a jolly Christmas record sold poorly, and Spector was bitter about the failure for years.","Listen to Darlene Love on Christmas (Baby Please Come Home): the only original song on the record, written with Barry and Greenwich, and the one track where the Wall of Sound becomes truly desperate, the piano hammering like a fist on a door. And at the end, Spector's spoken thanks to his artists over Silent Night reveals how completely he thought of the album as his own.","Its commercial failure was reversed by reissues from the 1970s onward, most famously on the Beatles' Apple label in 1972, and it is now counted among the best Christmas albums in pop and a standard of seasonal radio. Critics note it as one of the first rock-era albums conceived as a whole. Darlene Love sang her song on American late-night television every December for nearly thirty years.","Start with Darlene Love's Christmas (Baby Please Come Home), then go to the Ronettes' Sleigh Ride with its horse whinny and the Crystals' Santa Claus Is Coming to Town. Listen for the sleigh bells mixed into the Wall. The original mono mix is preferable to later fake-stereo versions."],"lesley-gore-its-my-party":["A teenage girl's birthday party ruined because her boyfriend has gone off with another girl, and she insists on her right to cry about it. Lesley Gore sings in a bright, double-tracked voice that makes her sound both stronger and younger, over a shuffling beat with blaring horns and a group of backing singers. It is barely two minutes long, and its melodrama is played with a straight face and a wink at once.","Gore was a sixteen-year-old schoolgirl from New Jersey when Quincy Jones, then an executive at Mercury Records, chose the song from a pile of demos and produced it in New York in March 1963. He heard that Phil Spector was about to record it with the Crystals and rushed his version out within days. It reached number one in America, and Gore was performing on national television within weeks.","Listen to the double-tracking of Gore's voice, two takes laid on top of each other, a studio trick that thickens a young voice and gives it the slightly unreal shine of the record. Jones, who would later produce Michael Jackson's Thriller, was already using the studio to build a star rather than simply capture one. The race with Spector over the same song shows how fast the Brill Building machine moved.","Critics at the time treated it as disposable teen pop, and for years it was cited as the very model of sixties bubblegum. Later writers have been kinder, noting the precision of Jones's production and Gore's surprisingly assured voice. It spawned a sequel in Judy's Turn to Cry, and it is now often heard as the setup to which her next single, You Don't Own Me, delivered the punchline.","Listen to the horns in the intro and how quickly the song reaches its chorus. Then play You Don't Own Me, released just months later, and hear the same singer and producer turn from self-pity to defiance. A good mono Mercury compilation will have both."],"lesley-gore-you-dont-own-me":["A dark, slow-building declaration: a young woman telling a boyfriend that he does not possess her, cannot tell her what to do or how to dress, and that she will go her own way. It begins almost in a whisper over a minor-key vamp, then swells with each verse into a full orchestral climax, Gore's voice rising from level calm to open anger. Everything about it, from the melody to the arrangement, pushes forward.","Written by two Philadelphia songwriters, John Madara and David White, and produced by Quincy Jones with an arrangement by Claus Ogerman, it was released at the end of 1963. It reached number two in America early in 1964, held off the top only by the Beatles' I Want to Hold Your Hand, at the moment when Beatlemania was sweeping the American charts and the teen-girl market was changing.","Listen to the melody in the verses: it hovers over a few notes like someone controlling her temper, and only in the chorus does it leap. That structure is the song's argument. Gore herself later said she heard it as a humanist song rather than a narrowly feminist one; she came out as gay publicly only in 2005, which gives its insistence on independence a second meaning many listeners now hear.","It was a hit without being taken seriously as a statement until the women's movement of the late sixties and seventies adopted it, and it has been revived at political marches ever since, notably in a 2016 voting campaign video. It is now regarded as one of the first feminist anthems in American pop, a record whose meaning grew as the society around it caught up.","Hear the first verse as a quiet warning and follow how the strings and voices pile in behind Gore; the final chorus sounds nothing like the opening. Then play It's My Party, from the same year, and hear how far she travels. The mono single is still the most direct version."],"dionne-warwick-anyone-who-had-a-heart":["A torch song that refuses to sit still. The singer tells a lover that anyone with a heart would see how she loves him, and the music keeps catching and shifting under her, bars of uneven length making the rhythm stumble like a halting breath. Dionne Warwick sings it cool and exact, never oversinging, while Bacharach's arrangement moves from bare piano to sighing strings and massed voices at the climax.","Burt Bacharach and Hal David wrote it for Warwick, a church-trained singer from East Orange, New Jersey, whom they had discovered singing backing vocals on Drifters sessions in New York. They were writing almost exclusively for her by 1963, and Bacharach produced and arranged her records for Scepter. It reached the American top ten in early 1964, while Cilla Black's cover topped the British chart.","Try tapping your foot and it will fail you: the verse changes metre repeatedly, from four beats to five to seven-and-a-half, following the natural stress of David's words. Session musicians reportedly struggled to play it. Bacharach did this not for show but because he wrote the melody first as a vocal line, then let it take the time it needed, which is why the song feels so much like speech.","Pop audiences took the irregular rhythm in their stride and the record was a hit on both sides of the Atlantic. Musicians and critics came to treat it as the proof that Bacharach was a composer of real sophistication rather than a hitmaker; jazz players adopted it and later it was recorded by artists from Dusty Springfield to Luther Vandross. The British argument over whose version was better, Warwick's or Black's, ran for years.","Try counting the beats in the first verse; you will lose the count, and that is how the song works on you. Then listen for Warwick's restraint on the high notes. Compare Cilla Black's more dramatic version, and Brubeck's Take Five for another popular record that made an odd metre feel natural."],"dionne-warwick-walk-on-by":["A woman asks a former lover who passes her in the street to keep walking, so he will not see her cry. Warwick sings with complete control, pride holding grief in check, while the accompaniment keeps interrupting: a mournful two-trumpet figure answering her lines, a light piano, strings that swell and fall back, and backing singers who echo her like an inner voice. It is sad and elegant at once, and very cool in temperature.","Bacharach and David wrote it for Warwick and recorded it in New York in 1963; it was released in spring 1964 and went to the American top ten. Bacharach produced and arranged it himself, one of the first pop songwriters to control the whole record. The Beatles had arrived in America weeks earlier, and this was one of the few American records of that year to sound entirely independent of them.","Listen to the chord under the title phrase: a harmony that sounds unresolved and a little jazzy, which leaves the song hanging just as the singer's composure is. And notice the trumpets, which play a small falling phrase instead of a normal horn fanfare; they sound like someone sighing. Isaac Hayes's twelve-minute version in 1969 took that single chord and stretched it into a whole symphonic soul epic.","It was an instant hit and has only risen in estimation. It is regularly listed among the finest pop singles of the decade and is the central example critics use for Bacharach's distinctive harmonic language. Its afterlife is extraordinary: Isaac Hayes's version helped invent symphonic soul, and the song has been reinterpreted by the Stranglers, Cyndi Lauper and many others.","Start by listening for the trumpets answering Warwick, then for the hanging chord on the title. Then play Isaac Hayes's version on Hot Buttered Soul for a radical reimagining. For comparison with an older school of heartbreak, try Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours; this is the sixties answer to it."],"the-righteous-brothers-youve-lost-that-lovin-feelin":["It opens with a deep, slow baritone almost alone, Bill Medley accusing a lover of going cold, and builds over nearly four minutes into one of the largest sounds on any pop record. Strings, brass, massed pianos, drums and choir climb steadily until the middle section, where the music drops away to a stark, pounding beat and Medley and Bobby Hatfield trade desperate pleas, then everything rises again to a shouting climax.","Phil Spector wrote it with Barry Mann and Cynthia Weil, and recorded it in Los Angeles for Philles. The Righteous Brothers were two white singers from Orange County who sounded Black enough on radio that they were first heard as a soul act. Released at the end of 1964, it went to number one in America and Britain early the next year, just as British groups were dominating the charts.","It was far longer than radio then allowed, so Spector had the label print a false running time of 3:05, shaving nearly a minute. When Mann first heard the playback at the slow tempo he reportedly thought it was playing at the wrong speed. Listen to the middle section: the orchestra drops out almost completely, the drums pound alone, and the two voices overlap in something like church call and response.","DJs were at first bewildered by its length and slowness, and some thought it too gloomy for pop radio; it became a colossal hit anyway. It was later judged by the broadcasting-rights organisation BMI to be the most-played song on American radio and television in the twentieth century. Critics now treat it as Spector's grandest achievement and a template for the soul ballad as epic.","Listen to the opening with nothing else in your head, just the baritone and space. Then track how the arrangement accumulates, and brace for the breakdown in the middle. Compare the Walker Brothers' The Sun Ain't Gonna Shine Anymore, its direct British descendant. A mono transfer from the original master is essential."],"the-shangri-las-remember-walking-in-the-sand":["A girl remembers walking on a beach with a boy who has left her by letter, and the record plays the memory as if it were a film. It opens with seagull cries and crashing surf, a slow piano and the group's hushed voices, then Mary Weiss's lead, flat and unadorned, enters over a sparse rhythm. The song keeps collapsing into silence and rebuilding, and the gulls and waves return between the sections like flashbacks.","George 'Shadow' Morton, a self-invented producer with no formal training, wrote it and recorded it with the Shangri-Las, two pairs of sisters from Cambridge Heights in Queens, New York. It came out on Red Bird, the label newly set up by Jerry Leiber, Mike Stoller and George Goldner, and reached the American top five in 1964. The girls were teenagers still at school.","The seagulls and surf are the heart of the record's invention: sound effects, not instruments, carry the emotional weight, turning a pop single into a small radio play. Morton's method was cinema rather than music theory. The demo of the song is said to have had the teenage Billy Joel on piano, one of those details that sound invented but may well be true.","It was a hit and made the group stars, and its theatricality divided critics at first between those who found it melodramatic kitsch and those who heard real feeling. Opinion has swung strongly toward the latter. The Shangri-Las' records are now claimed as ancestors by punk and new wave; the New York Dolls and Blondie cited them, and Amy Winehouse drew heavily on their look and sound.","Listen in the dark, with the effects doing their work. Notice the long pauses: the song trusts silence. Then play Leader of the Pack, from later the same year, to hear Morton's method at its boldest. Compare Brian Wilson's use of found sound on Pet Sounds two years later."],"the-shangri-las-leader-of-the-pack":["A tragic teenage drama compressed into under three minutes. The girls question Mary Weiss about her boyfriend, she answers in spoken asides, then the song tells how her parents forced her to end it with the motorcycle-riding boy from the wrong side of town, how he rode off into the rain, and how he was killed. Revving engines, screeching tyres and a crash break into the music, and a thumping piano drives it to an anguished end.","Shadow Morton wrote it with Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich for Red Bird, and it went to number one in America late in 1964. It belongs to a brief teen-tragedy craze of songs about young lovers dying, but it also plugged into a moral panic about motorcycle gangs and juvenile delinquency. The BBC declined to play it for a time, and it still reached the British top twenty.","Listen to the spoken dialogue at the start: the other girls ask the questions, and Weiss answers in a flat, unexcited speaking voice, a Queens teenager rather than a singer. That deadpan realism, against the lurid sound effects, is what keeps the record from being just a joke. The motorcycle noise was reportedly recorded with a real bike brought into or just outside the studio.","It was a massive hit and an easy target for parody, which it has attracted ever since. For years it stood as shorthand for sixties pop kitsch. Later critics have heard something more in it, a portrait of class and parental control in which the girl has no power at all, and punk bands from the Ramones onward adopted its leather-and-attitude image wholesale.","Hear it as a three-minute film, not a song: the setup, the conflict, the crash. Listen for the piano that drives everything. Then compare Remember (Walking in the Sand) for Morton's quieter method, and Lesley Gore's You Don't Own Me for a very different girl facing a very different pressure."],"dusty-springfield-i-just-dont-know-what-to-do-with-myself":["A woman alone after a break-up, unable to do anything that does not remind her of the lover who left. Dusty Springfield begins quietly and intimately over a sparse piano and a soft beat, then the orchestra and backing voices build behind her until, on the climactic chorus, she lets go with the huge, husky sound that was her trademark. The song is a Bacharach-David melody that keeps rising and falling like someone trying not to cry.","Bacharach and David had written it for Tommy Hunt in America; Springfield heard it and recorded it in London with producer Johnny Franz and arranger Ivor Raymonde for Philips. It reached number three in Britain in 1964. She had left the folk trio the Springfields the year before and was fast becoming the country's leading female pop singer, fiercely devoted to American soul and Brill Building writing.","Listen to the breath Springfield takes before the big chorus; she sings with a microphone technique borrowed from the soul records she loved rather than from British variety tradition, which is why she sounds so unlike other British pop singers of the day. Behind the lavish sound she was famously insecure about her own voice, fussing over every take, and her perfectionism shows in how exact the phrasing is.","It established her firmly as a singer of American material and the British rival to Dionne Warwick in Bacharach songs, a rivalry Warwick reportedly did not enjoy. Its reputation has endured; the White Stripes' bare garage-rock version in 2003 introduced it to a new generation and showed how strong the song is stripped of its orchestra.","Listen for the moment in the final chorus when Springfield opens up. Then compare Warwick's cooler Walk On By to hear two schools of singing the same writers. Finish with the White Stripes' version for contrast. The original mono single mix gives the voice its full weight."],"petula-clark-downtown":["An invitation to the lonely to go downtown, where the lights and noise and people will make them forget their troubles. It opens with a bright piano figure and a quiet verse, then climbs to a chorus that explodes with brass, strings and backing voices, the title shouted with delight. Petula Clark sings it with clear, warm optimism, and the arrangement keeps adding colour, like neon switching on street by street.","Tony Hatch, a staff producer at Pye Records in London, wrote it after a first trip to New York, when he stood in Times Square and was struck by its energy. Clark had been a British child star since the war and was then living in France. Released in late 1964, it reached the top of the American chart early in 1965, making her the first British woman to do so in the rock era, at the peak of the British Invasion.","Listen to the verse against the chorus: the verse is almost muted and tentative, the chorus loud and bright, so the song acts out the move from a dark room into the city lights. Hatch borrowed the scale of American orchestral pop, and the record is essentially a London answer to the Brill Building, written by a British staff songwriter working in the American way.","It was a worldwide hit and won a Grammy. Critics sometimes dismissed Clark as a family entertainer, and the song as easy listening, but its craft is widely admired now, and its vision of the city as refuge has made it a recurring choice in film and television. Hatch's later work with Clark is often counted among the best British songwriting of the decade.","Listen for the first entry of the brass at the chorus; the arrangement is built around that moment. Then compare the Drifters' Up on the Roof, where the city is something to escape. The original mono Pye single is punchier than later stereo mixes."],"france-gall-poupee-de-cire-poupee-de-son":["A bright, bouncing song in which a teenage singer describes herself as a wax doll stuffed with sound, singing of love she has never felt. France Gall's voice is high and girlish, the orchestra jaunty with brassy stabs and a driving beat, and the melody leaps about cheerfully. On the surface it is pure yé-yé candy; underneath, the words question the very pop industry that made the record.","Serge Gainsbourg wrote it for Gall, then seventeen, and it won the Eurovision Song Contest for Luxembourg in Naples in March 1965. Gainsbourg was then a respected but unfashionable songwriter of adult chanson looking for commercial success in the youth market. The win made both of them rich, and the song became a hit across Europe and beyond, recorded in several languages.","Listen to the title image: a doll of wax and sound, a singer who is merely a mouthpiece. Gainsbourg was writing an ironic comment on Gall herself, and on yé-yé stars in general, and giving it to her to sing to a huge television audience. The double meaning passed almost everyone by at the time, though not the knowing listeners who soon realised Gainsbourg was toying with his singers.","It won and it sold, but the song has also drawn lasting criticism for how Gainsbourg used a teenage singer, especially after his next song for her, Les sucettes, carried a double meaning she did not understand when she recorded it. Critics today admire the song's cleverness while treating its authorship as part of a troubling pattern. Gall moved on to a long, serious career.","Enjoy the song first as pure pop, with its bouncing orchestra. Then think about the title and hear the irony. Compare Françoise Hardy's Tous les garçons et les filles for a yé-yé singer writing her own feeling, and Gainsbourg's later Histoire de Melody Nelson for where his fascination with young muses went."],"the-walker-brothers-the-sun-aint-gonna-shine-anymore":["A vast, doom-laden ballad of loss. Scott Engel's rich baritone leads, joined by John Maus's harmony, over an arrangement that heaps strings, brass, choir and thundering drums into a wall of echo. The song says that without the lost lover the world has lost its light, and the arrangement makes that sound cosmic rather than personal: each chorus arrives like a storm front, and the singing never loses its stately, controlled gloom.","The Walker Brothers were three Americans, none named Walker, who moved to London in 1965 and found success there that had eluded them at home. The song, by Bob Crewe and Bob Gaudio of the Four Seasons, had been a minor American single for Frankie Valli. Produced by Johnny Franz with arranger Ivor Raymonde, it went to number one in Britain in March 1966 amid scenes of fan hysteria.","Listen to how closely the production copies Phil Spector's Righteous Brothers sound, from the slow tempo to the pounding drums and choir; the group's early British hits were deliberately modelled on it. But listen too to Engel's phrasing, already more detached and theatrical than a soul singer's; the future Scott Walker, the solo artist of deathly orchestral albums, is audible in embryo here.","It was the group's peak, and for a year they rivalled the Beatles for teenage adulation in Britain. Critics have sometimes seen it as a mere Spector copy, but its reputation has grown along with Scott Walker's, and it is now heard as the moment British orchestral pop reached its biggest and darkest.","Put it straight after You've Lost That Lovin' Feelin' and hear the debt. Then listen closely to Engel's voice, and follow it to Scott 4 three years later. The original mono single mix carries the full weight of the arrangement."],"dusty-springfield-you-dont-have-to-say-you-love-me":["A woman who once turned her lover away now asks only that he stay near, with no need to declare love. The song begins softly over strings and a slow beat, then Springfield's voice swells through each verse to a final chorus of enormous force, backed by a choir and a full orchestra. It is a torch song on the scale of Italian opera, and Springfield holds nothing back.","The melody is Italian: Io che non vivo (senza te), by Pino Donaggio, which Springfield heard at the Sanremo festival of 1965. Her friend Vicki Wickham and the manager Simon Napier-Bell, neither a professional lyricist, wrote English words to it in an evening. Produced by Johnny Franz in London, it became her only British number one in 1966 and a top-five hit in America.","Springfield was unhappy with the sound of the studio and reportedly recorded her vocal in a stairwell at the Philips building to get the echo she wanted. Listen to the natural reverb on her voice at the climax: that is concrete stairs, not a machine. Donaggio later became a film composer, working often with Brian De Palma, which is a nice irony for so cinematic a pop song.","It was her biggest British hit and remains her signature song. Some critics find its bombast too much beside the subtlety of Dusty in Memphis, three years later; others hear it as her greatest vocal performance. Elvis Presley recorded it in 1970, which says something about the reach of its melodrama.","Wait for the last chorus and listen for the stairwell echo on the voice. Then compare I Just Don't Know What to Do with Myself for her gentler style, and Dusty in Memphis for her quietest. The mono single is essential; stereo mixes lose the punch."],"ike-and-tina-turner-river-deep-mountain-high":["The last and largest of Phil Spector's great productions. Tina Turner sings of a love as deep as a river and high as a mountain, her voice raw and gospel-fierce, over an arrangement so thick it barely has edges: massed pianos, guitars, strings, horns, choir and pounding drums in a churning, galloping rhythm. In the middle the music breaks down to a staccato vocal passage before the whole mass rises again.","Spector wrote it with Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich and recorded it at Gold Star in 1966 in sessions reputed to have cost a fortune and involved dozens of musicians. Only Tina sang; Ike Turner was reportedly paid to take the credit and stay away. It reached only number 88 in America, a failure Spector took as a personal rejection, though it made the British top three.","Tina Turner was reportedly made to sing the lead over and over for hours, until she was exhausted and drenched. Listen to her voice in the last chorus, fraying at the edges: that is the sound of a singer being pushed past control. Spector largely withdrew from the business after its American failure, so this is effectively the end point of his Wall of Sound era.","American radio found it neither Black enough for R&B stations nor white enough for pop, the usual explanation of its failure. In Britain it was admired at once, and George Harrison reportedly called it a perfect record. Its reputation has since become enormous, and Spector himself counted it his best work, though some listeners find it overbearing rather than overwhelming.","Listen once at high volume without trying to separate anything. Then listen again only for Tina's voice, especially in the breakdown. Compare the Beach Boys' Good Vibrations from the same year, the other studio epic of 1966, and Be My Baby for the style in its lighter youth."],"the-shangri-las-past-present-and-future":["Almost no singing at all. Mary Weiss speaks, slowly and coldly, about a love that is over and the fact that she will never let herself be touched by it again, while a piano and strings play the opening movement of Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata beneath her. The group hums and chants softly behind, and the music swells into a short, ballroom-like dance passage before sinking back into Beethoven's triplets and silence.","Shadow Morton wrote and produced it in 1966 as one of the last Shangri-Las records for Red Bird, which was already failing. The girl groups were being swept aside by the British Invasion and by folk-rock, and the Shangri-Las themselves were weeks from dissolving. Released as a single, it was a minor hit, and some American stations would not play it.","Listen to the music: it is Beethoven's first movement of the Moonlight Sonata, adapted almost literally, so a teenage pop single is built on one of the most famous piano pieces ever written. The chill in Weiss's voice suggests some trauma behind the words, which radio programmers found unsettling; the song never says what happened, and that silence is what makes it haunting.","It was barely noticed at the time, but has become a cult favourite, admired as one of the strangest and most daring records of the girl-group era, as close to a spoken-word art piece as to pop. Later writers have seen it as an ancestor of the gloomier strands of post-punk and of the darkest of Scott Walker's work.","Listen first for the Beethoven in the piano, then for the coldness of Weiss's voice against it. Then play the original sonata movement and hear what Morton borrowed. Compare Leader of the Pack from two years earlier to hear how far the Shangri-Las travelled."],"dionne-warwick-i-say-a-little-prayer":["A woman describing her day, from waking to dressing to riding the bus, and how at every moment she says a small prayer for her lover. The song moves quickly and lightly, Warwick singing precisely over a busy piano and a brisk beat, while the backing singers answer with their own gospel-flavoured refrain. The melody keeps changing its step, bar lengths shifting, so the song feels both rushing and graceful.","Bacharach and David wrote it for Warwick in 1966; the lyric was widely heard as the voice of a woman whose man is away in the Vietnam War, though David never said so directly. Bacharach reportedly thought the take too fast and did not want it issued as a single; Scepter released it anyway from an album in 1967, and it reached the American top five.","Listen for bars that are longer or shorter than normal, a Bacharach trademark: the song has measures of ten and eleven beats in places, disguised by the easy flow. And notice that the backing singers' refrain is the true hook, as in church. Aretha Franklin's version in 1968 took that gospel element and made it the whole song, a transatlantic hit that many prefer.","It was a hit, but Bacharach's own doubts and Franklin's quick cover mean it is often discussed as the song Aretha made her own. Warwick's version is now respected for its clarity and poise; the argument between the two readings, one composed and precise, one churchy and free, neatly sums up two ways of singing sixties pop.","Listen for the backing singers' refrain and how it overlaps Warwick's lines. Then play Aretha Franklin's version for the gospel reading. Try counting the beats in a verse and see where the pattern breaks. The original mono Scepter single is best."],"francoise-hardy-comment-te-dire-adieu":["A woman trying and failing to find the words to leave a lover. Françoise Hardy sings in her cool, low voice over a bright, bouncy orchestral arrangement with a clipped, almost comic rhythm, the gaiety of the music playing against the sadness of the words. Each line of the French lyric is packed with the same hard, clicking syllable, and the song sounds almost like a tongue-twister sung with a straight face.","The tune is American, from It Hurts to Say Goodbye by Arnold Goland and Jack Gold, first recorded by Vera Lynn. Hardy wanted to record it in French and asked Serge Gainsbourg to write the lyric; he took the challenge as wordplay. Released in 1968, it was a large French hit and became one of her signature songs, just as she was moving away from the yé-yé image toward more adult material.","Listen to the rhymes: Gainsbourg built the lyric around the sound of 'ex', repeated in word after word, so the song's sound itself is a stammer, a woman stuck on one syllable because she cannot say the one word she needs. It is a virtuoso piece of lyric writing hidden inside a light pop song, and only really audible once you know it is there.","It was a hit and has grown in reputation as one of the best Gainsbourg lyrics written for another singer. Critics now hear it as a meeting of two very different talents, Hardy's reserve and Gainsbourg's wordplay, and it has been widely covered, including by Jimmy Somerville in English and French versions.","Even without French, listen for the repeated clicking sound in the lyric. Then enjoy the contrast of the cheerful arrangement with the cool voice. Compare Tous les garçons et les filles for Hardy at eighteen, and Je t'aime moi non plus from the next year for Gainsbourg in a very different mood."],"serge-gainsbourg-je-t-aime-moi-non-plus":["A slow, languid duet over a softly swaying organ melody, bass and strings. Gainsbourg murmurs and Jane Birkin sighs in a high, breathy voice, the two exchanging fragments of a lovers' conversation in which each says the opposite of what they mean. The music barely changes over four minutes, the organ repeating its lazy tune while the voices become more intimate; it is a record of mood rather than of melody.","Gainsbourg wrote it in 1967 for Brigitte Bardot and recorded it with her, but at her request that version was held back. He re-recorded it with Birkin, his English partner, in London in 1968, and it was released in 1969. It was banned by the BBC and radio in several countries and condemned by the Vatican's newspaper, which only helped it to become a number one in Britain after Fontana withdrew it and a smaller label took it over.","The title itself is the key: 'I love you, me neither' is a paradox, and the song's joke is that the lovers' words contradict each other throughout. Listen, too, to the organ tune, which is sweet and almost churchy, a deliberate clash with the erotic content. Bardot's original version, finally released in 1986, is harder and more businesslike than Birkin's.","It was a scandal, which was the point, and a huge seller. For years it was treated as a novelty record, and its content is still discussed with unease. But critics have increasingly admired the craft of the arrangement and its influence on slow, sensual pop and later trip-hop. Its reputation now rests more on sound than on scandal.","Listen to the organ and the bass before paying attention to the voices, and hear how simple and hypnotic the music is. Then compare Histoire de Melody Nelson, two years later, where Gainsbourg pushes the same mood into a whole orchestral album."],"scott-walker-scott-4":["Ten songs, all Scott Walker's own, sung in his deep, rich baritone over arrangements that range from full orchestra and choir to spare guitar and harmonica. The album opens with The Seventh Seal, a song retelling Ingmar Bergman's film of a knight playing chess with Death, with a Morricone-like Western twang. Elsewhere there are songs about a Soviet leader's return, a war hero's hollow homecoming, and a dying old man, all delivered with calm, sombre grandeur.","By 1969 Walker, formerly the lead singer of the Walker Brothers, had released three solo albums mixing his own songs with covers, notably of Jacques Brel. He released Scott 4 under his real name, Noel Scott Engel, perhaps to escape his teen-idol past. It was his first album of entirely original songs. It failed to chart at all and was soon deleted, and Walker spent the next years singing others' material.","The song The Old Man's Back Again was prompted by the Soviet invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968; the record gives it a funky, ominous bass groove that makes the politics feel sinister rather than preachy. Listen too to the arrangements, much of the orchestral writing by Wally Stott, who later worked as Angela Morley: they mix film-score strings with pop rhythm in a way almost nobody else attempted.","It was a commercial failure and largely forgotten until the 1980s, when musicians including Julian Cope and David Bowie championed Walker, and it was reissued. It is now widely regarded as one of the finest British albums of its decade and the bridge between Walker's pop career and the avant-garde work of his later years. Some prefer Scott 3 for its orchestral sweep.","Start with The Seventh Seal, then The Old Man's Back Again. Listen to the voice: calm, grave, never raised. Then compare Nick Drake's Five Leaves Left from the same year, another orchestral singer-songwriter album ignored in its time, and David Bowie's Low for how Walker's influence travelled."],"serge-gainsbourg-histoire-de-melody-nelson":["Seven tracks in about twenty-eight minutes, telling a single story. Gainsbourg speaks rather than sings, in a low, smoky voice, over a slinky, prominent bass guitar, sparse drums and a swirling orchestral score. The narrator, driving his Rolls-Royce, knocks a red-haired English girl off her bicycle; a brief affair follows, and the album ends with her death in a plane crash and a vast finale for choir and orchestra.","Gainsbourg made it in 1970 and 1971 with the arranger Jean-Claude Vannier, who wrote the orchestral and choral parts, recording in Paris and London. Jane Birkin, then his partner, is the Melody Nelson of the story and appears on the cover. The album was a commercial disappointment when released in 1971, though admired by some French critics. Rock in France was then largely imported, and a forty-two-year-old chansonnier making a narrative rock album with an orchestra had no obvious audience.","Listen to the bass guitar: it carries the melody much of the time, placed far forward in the mix, while the orchestra swells and retreats around it. The last track, Cargo culte, uses the idea of Melanesian islanders building replica airstrips to summon supply planes, and makes it an image of grief and longing, with Vannier's choir giving it a sacred, almost requiem-like weight.","It has become Gainsbourg's most admired record, a cult album that found a new audience from the 1990s when Beck, Air, Portishead and others cited it. Its subject, a middle-aged man's obsession with a teenage girl, is now discussed with more discomfort than before. Critics generally treat it as a landmark in the concept album and in orchestral pop.","Listen to it in one sitting; it is short. Follow the bass through the first track, and wait for the choir in the final one. Then compare Scott Walker's Scott 4 for another orchestral auteur record of the period, and Je t'aime moi non plus for the same voice in a simpler setting."],"francoise-hardy-la-question":["A quiet, dreamlike album of love songs, most written by or with the Brazilian guitarist and singer Tuca. Hardy's low voice floats over nylon-string guitar, soft bass and strings that drift in and out like mist. The songs are slow and intimate, touched by bossa nova, with melodies that wander rather than resolve. The album is a single mood from start to finish: private, sensual, withdrawn.","By 1971 Hardy had left behind the yé-yé image and was recording more adult material. She worked with Tuca, a Brazilian musician living in Paris, and the album was recorded with a small group and string arrangements. It was released on Sonopresse and did not sell especially well, though it became treasured by her admirers. In France the singer-songwriter mood of the early seventies favoured quieter, more personal records, and this one pushed further into privacy than almost any of them.","Listen to how the strings never simply accompany; they hover and swell, sometimes in unusual harmonies, so the album sounds at once Brazilian and French. And notice how rarely Hardy raises her voice. The Brazilian bossa nova influence ties the record to Getz and Gilberto, whose cool, murmured style reached Europe in the early 1960s.","It was little noticed at the time, but has grown into Hardy's most admired album, often cited by musicians and critics as a model of intimate, atmospheric pop. Its reputation outside France rose after 2000, when a new generation discovered her through reissues. Some listeners still find it too uniform in mood, one long sigh; its admirers answer that the sameness is the design, an album meant to be heard whole rather than raided for singles.","Listen with headphones in a quiet room, from start to finish. Notice the guitar and the strings working together. Compare Getz/Gilberto for the Brazilian roots, and Tous les garçons et les filles to hear how far Hardy's voice travelled in nine years."],"sam-cooke-touch-the-hem-of-his-garment":["Two and a half minutes of quartet gospel, taken slowly. Cooke sings the story of the woman in the crowd who reached for Christ's robe, and the four Soul Stirrers behind him answer in close harmony on a single repeated phrase while a guitar keeps a soft shuffle. What you notice is the tone: light, unforced, with a little catch at the top of a phrase and a yodel-like turn that Cooke could place anywhere he wanted.","Cooke joined the Soul Stirrers in 1950, aged nineteen, replacing R. H. Harris, and by 1956 he was the biggest draw on the gospel circuit, singing to churches full of women who screamed. He wrote this himself for Specialty Records in Los Angeles. Within two years he had crossed to secular pop under a pseudonym, then under his own name, and the gospel world treated it as a defection. Almost every soul singer who followed made the same walk.","Listen to what Cooke does not do. Where a gospel shouter would tear the last chorus open, he stays inside a narrow, conversational range and lets the quartet supply the heat behind him. That restraint is the invention: he proved a gospel lead could hold a crowd by pulling back, and every soul ballad singer from Otis Redding to Al Green built on it.","In the churches it was simply a Soul Stirrers record among many. Judgement changed once critics went looking for the roots of soul and found that Cooke's gospel sides already contained the whole vocabulary — the melisma, the moan, the delayed entry — with no secular subject attached. The old argument about whether taking that voice to pop was betrayal or liberation has quietly been settled in favour of liberation.","Play it directly before 'A Change Is Gonna Come' and hear the same voice thirty seconds into the same technique with a different text. Notice the quartet's answering phrase, which never varies; the whole drama sits in Cooke's timing against it. Specialty's mono transfers are close and dry, which is the way to hear how little he is doing."],"sam-cooke-harlem-square-club":["Forty minutes recorded in a small Miami club, and it sounds like it: the room is packed, the band plays loud and slightly ragged, and the audience answers every line. Cooke works the crowd like a preacher, stretching 'Bring It On Home to Me' into a call and response that will not end, roughening his voice until it cracks. The polished crooner of the hit singles is nowhere present; this is a man sweating through a set.","Cooke was, by 1963, a pop star with an immaculate reputation on white television, and a chitlin-circuit headliner on Black stages where he sang entirely differently. RCA sent a mobile unit to Miami's Harlem Square Club in January to capture the second version. Hearing the tape, the label decided it could damage the crossover career it had built and shelved it. It stayed in the vault for twenty-two years, until 1985.","Listen for the moment he stops singing to talk to the room and the band keeps vamping under him, exactly as a gospel quartet lead would over a church organ. Also notice how little he uses the falsetto that made his records famous. The whole performance is pitched a shade lower and rougher, which tells you the studio smoothness was a decision, not a limit.","When it finally appeared in 1985 it reversed a critical consensus: Cooke had been filed as the great voice let down by soft arrangements, and here was proof that he could out-work Otis Redding on a stage. Some argued RCA's caution was ordinary commercial sense in 1963; others read it as the record industry sanding the Blackness off a Black artist. The second reading has largely won.","Start with 'Bring It On Home to Me' and count how many times he trades the phrase with the room before moving on. Then play the studio single of the same song and hear what was left out. The 1985 release is mono and close-miked, with the crowd nearly as loud as the band, which is the point."],"the-supremes-where-did-our-love-go":["Two and a half minutes built on one sound: a flat, wooden thump on every beat, like someone stamping on boards, with handclaps riding over it. Diana Ross sings low and almost without expression, half-spoken, while Florence Ballard and Mary Wilson answer in two-note sighs. A baritone saxophone doubles the bass. Nothing accelerates, nothing modulates, nothing is added after the first eight seconds; the record simply repeats until it stops.","Holland, Dozier and Holland wrote it for the Marvelettes, who turned it down. The Supremes, nicknamed the no-hit Supremes inside Motown after eight failed singles, got it by default and did not want it either. Ross disliked singing that low. It reached number one in August 1964 and was followed by four more consecutive number ones, which established both the group and Motown's claim to be the sound of young America rather than a Detroit race label.","The stamping is not a drum. It was made by musicians hitting boards with their feet in the studio, a trick Motown reused for years, and it is mixed almost as loud as the voice. Notice too that Ross's deadpan is a byproduct of the key: pitched where she is uncomfortable, she stops projecting, and that flatness became the sound everyone imitated.","Contemporary critics heard a novelty; rock writers of the seventies called Motown's assembly line the opposite of authenticity. The argument has inverted. The production line is now the point of interest, and this is the record where its method becomes audible — a hook, a rhythm and a hole where the emoting should be. Ballard's later removal from the group, and her death in poverty, colours every reissue.","Listen once for the stomp alone and you will hear how much of the record is percussion. Then follow the baritone saxophone, which is doing the work a guitar would do anywhere else. Compare it with the Temptations' 'My Girl' from the same months to hear the same studio making warmth instead of cold."],"the-temptations-my-girl":["It opens with a bass climbing four notes, then a guitar picking the same four, then finger snaps, and only after twenty seconds does David Ruffin come in, grainy and unhurried. The other four Temptations answer him in block harmony. Strings arrive for the second verse and lift the key at the end, a small shift that the whole record has been saving up for. Under three minutes, with no wasted bar.","Smokey Robinson wrote it with Ronald White as an answer to 'My Guy', which he had just given Mary Wells, and he built it for Ruffin's rough voice rather than for the group's smoother leads. Motown's writers were by 1964 competing internally for singles, and this was Robinson proving he could place a ballad against the Holland-Dozier-Holland dance records. It became the Temptations' first number one in early 1965.","The four-note bass phrase is the entire song in miniature — the tune, the guitar figure and the string line are all versions of it, which is why the record sounds inevitable rather than merely catchy. Listen also to the key change near the end: it happens under a held note, without a drum fill to announce it, so most listeners feel it before they notice it.","It was a hit at once and has never stopped being one, which has made it hard to hear. Critics who prefer the Temptations' later psychedelic records treat it as the pretty phase, and Ruffin's own later career, ended by addiction, gave the song a retrospective sadness. Scholarship on Motown now uses it as the standard example of an arrangement in which every part is a variation of one idea.","Try to ignore the singing for one play and follow only the bass. Then listen to how Ruffin lags fractionally behind the beat on every line while the backing voices sit exactly on it. Compare with the Supremes' 'Where Did Our Love Go' from the same studio, same season, entirely different temperature."],"sam-cooke-a-change-is-gonna-come":["A french horn opens alone, answered by strings and a slow drum, and the scale of the thing is orchestral before a word is sung. Cooke tells three verses of autobiography — born by the river, afraid to die, turned away from a cinema — each ending on the same phrase, and the arrangement grows behind him until the timpani are rolling. The bridge, where he asks his brother for help and is knocked back down, is the only place his voice breaks.","Cooke wrote it in 1963 after hearing Bob Dylan's 'Blowin' in the Wind' and asking himself why a white man had written the movement's song, and after he and his party were refused rooms at a Louisiana motel and arrested for the fuss they made. He recorded it in Los Angeles in December 1963 with an arrangement by René Hall. It was issued as a single after he was shot dead in December 1964.","The orchestration is deliberately un-soulful: strings, horn and timpani are the furniture of a film score, and Cooke uses them so the song cannot be filed as a race record. Notice that the melody barely moves within each line — almost everything is one repeated note — so that the few rising phrases carry all the weight. He performed it publicly only rarely; he seems to have been uneasy with it.","It sold modestly at first and grew into the anthem it is now over decades, sung at marches, funerals and inaugurations. The debate is about ownership: whether a song written by a wealthy pop star in a Beverly Hills frame of mind belongs to the movement that adopted it, and whether the orchestral dress is dignity or camouflage. Nobody argues about the singing.","Listen first for the french horn, which returns at each seam and is the only instrument that answers the voice. Then go to the bridge and notice it is the one passage without strings. Play William Grant Still's Afro-American Symphony after it to hear another attempt to carry the same feeling in orchestral clothes."],"smokey-robinson-tracks-of-my-tears":["A guitar figure that sounds almost Spanish opens it, and everything else is arranged around that one curling phrase. Robinson sings high, in a light falsetto that never pushes, telling you that the smile is a mask and asking you to look closer at his face. The Miracles answer with a single long held word. The chorus arrives without any change of volume; the record refuses to get louder, which is why the image lands.","Marv Tarplin, the Miracles' guitarist, brought Robinson the guitar figure with no song attached, and Robinson carried it around for weeks before the phrase about tracks — the marks tears leave — gave him the title. He was by then Motown's vice-president as well as its most prolific writer, turning out singles for the Temptations and Mary Wells between his own. The record reached the American top ten in 1965.","The verse and chorus use almost the same melody; what changes is the harmony underneath, so the song seems to darken without moving. Listen for the way the backing voices enter a fraction early on the title phrase, overlapping the lead, which makes the line sound like something Robinson cannot stop the others from saying. Tarplin's guitar plays the hook alone at every seam, never doubled.","Robinson's reputation as a lyricist has always run ahead of his reputation as a singer, helped by Bob Dylan's much-repeated description of him as a great American poet, a line Robinson himself has treated as a joke. Critics divide over whether the extended metaphors are elegant or a touch too neat. The song is generally exempted from that argument; the image and the melody arrive together.","Follow the guitar rather than the voice for the first thirty seconds so you learn the figure, then let it disappear behind Robinson and see if you can still hear it. Notice the record never raises its voice. Try it beside the Temptations' 'My Girl', which Robinson also wrote, to hear his two registers side by side."],"curtis-mayfield-people-get-ready":["Under three minutes, quiet throughout, with a guitar figure that rocks between two chords like a train on rails and strings that enter so softly you may miss them. Mayfield sings high and close, and the two other Impressions fold their voices under rather than over his, so the trio sounds like one instrument. There is no chorus in the usual sense; the same eight bars come round, and each time the words move further from the railway and closer to judgement.","Chicago in 1964 and 1965: Mayfield was writing for the Impressions on Chicago's own soul label, ABC-Paramount sessions arranged by Johnny Pate, while the civil-rights movement was moving north into his city. The train to Jordan is old spiritual imagery, and Mayfield used it with the coded double meaning intact — a gospel train and a movement bus at once. Martin Luther King's organisers took the song up, and it was sung on marches.","There is no drum fill and no key change in the entire record; the only dynamic event is the strings swelling for four bars near the end. That flatness is the gospel structure showing through — a hymn does not build, it circulates. Listen also for the line that warns there is no room for the hopeless sinner, which Mayfield later said he was uneasy about, and which he softened in performance.","It was a hit and became a standard, covered by Rod Stewart, Bob Marley and Eva Cassidy among many others, usually louder and worse. Critics of Mayfield's Impressions years sometimes find the gentleness evasive next to the confrontation of his solo records; others argue the movement needed songs that could be sung by a thousand untrained voices, and that this one was engineered for exactly that.","Listen for the guitar's two-chord rock and count how many times it comes round unchanged. Then attend to the vocal blend: try to pick out the two lower Impressions, which is harder than it sounds. Follow it with 'A Change Is Gonna Come' to hear the movement's two great songs, one orchestral and one almost bare."],"otis-redding-otis-blue":["Eleven songs in thirty-three minutes, played by a five-piece house band with two horns and almost no overdubbing. The sound is dry and forward: Steve Cropper's guitar answers in single notes, Booker T. Jones's organ holds the chords, Al Jackson's drums drag very slightly behind. Redding sings three Sam Cooke songs, his own 'Respect', and a version of 'Satisfaction' in which the guitar riff becomes a horn line. Everything is loud, and nothing is smooth.","Recorded at Stax in Memphis in July 1965, in a single stretch of about twenty-four hours broken only so the musicians could play their regular engagements. Stax's method was the opposite of Motown's: no written arrangements, a racially integrated house band working out the parts on the floor, and a former cinema for a studio with a sloping floor that shaped the sound. Cooke had been dead seven months, and the covers are a wake.","Listen for the horns, which are arranged but never notated — the two players worked out lines by singing them to each other, which is why they breathe with Redding rather than against him. The room itself is audible: the sloping floor of the old Capitol Theatre gave Stax a slightly boxy ambience that engineers have tried and failed to reproduce. 'Respect' here is a man's demand; two years later it was answered.","It was Redding's breakthrough with white audiences and is still the usual first recommendation. The old critical split between Memphis grit and Detroit gloss, which this album was used to prove, now looks overdrawn: both were factories with house bands, and both made records to a formula. What survives the argument is the singing, and the fact that a major album was cut in about a day.","Start with 'I've Been Loving You Too Long' and listen to how Redding delays the first syllable of nearly every line. Then play 'Satisfaction' and notice the Rolling Stones' guitar riff reassigned to horns. A good mono transfer beats stereo here; the stereo split scatters a band that was playing as one thing."],"the-supremes-you-keep-me-hangin-on":["A guitar stutters the same high note over and over, fast and metallic, like a signal being tapped out, and it never stops for the whole record. Diana Ross sings harder than usual over it, close to a shout on the repeated set-me-free line, and the other two Supremes chop in on the offbeat. Horns punch in the gaps. The tempo is quick, the mood is not romantic at all, and the fade leaves the tapping still going.","Holland, Dozier and Holland were by 1966 writing at industrial speed and beginning to darken the group's material; the Supremes' run of number ones had made Motown a corporation with a charm school and a choreographer. This was the seventh of their eventual twelve chart-toppers. Within two years the writing team had left Motown in a dispute over royalties, and Florence Ballard had been forced out of the group.","The tapped guitar is sometimes described as Morse code, and Holland has said the idea came from exactly that association. Notice what it does structurally: because one element never varies, the arrangers can cut everything else away without losing the beat, and they do — the bridge drops to almost nothing but voice and tapping. The trick was copied for the next twenty years, most audibly by Vanilla Fudge, who slowed it to a crawl.","It was the Supremes record that rock audiences accepted, and it has aged into the one that sounds least like 1966. Kim Wilde's 1986 version restored it to number one in a synthesiser arrangement that made the mechanical basis explicit. The criticism that Motown treated singers as interchangeable parts has some purchase here, though the hard edge in Ross's delivery is hers and appears nowhere on the earlier hits.","Listen to the bridge, where the production pulls back and you can hear how little is holding the record up. Compare Ross's phrasing with 'Where Did Our Love Go' two years earlier: the same singer, twice the pressure. Then try Vanilla Fudge's slow version to hear how much of the song is tempo."],"otis-redding-try-a-little-tenderness":["It begins as a ballad — horns laid out long and slow, Redding almost murmuring the first verse — and then, about halfway, the drums double the pace and never come back down. The last ninety seconds are a controlled collapse: Redding shouting the same four words, the band hammering a two-chord figure, the horns stabbing on top, the singer improvising a string of exclamations over the fade. One song containing two entirely different records.","The song was written in 1932 by three British songwriters and had been crooned by Bing Crosby and Frank Sinatra as a piece of gentlemanly advice. Redding recorded it at Stax in 1966 with Isaac Hayes, then a staff pianist and arranger, shaping the horn parts. Stax's band was used to building a track on the floor; the acceleration was worked out in the room rather than written down.","The tempo shift is not a cut between two takes. Al Jackson simply moves from the backbeat to a fast pattern on the same pulse, so the underlying speed never changes — what changes is how often you hear it. Listen also to how Redding's final ad-libs fall on unexpected parts of the bar, dragging the band with him rather than following it.","Contemporary reviewers noted the audacity of taking a white crooner's standard and destroying it. Later writers have used the performance to argue that soul's real subject is effort — that the interest lies in hearing a voice run out of reserve. A minority still prefer the restraint of the first half and find the ending mannered. Live films from 1967 suggest Redding could not sing it any other way.","Play the Sinatra or Crosby version first, then this; the point is unmissable. Listen for the exact bar where the drums double and note that nobody signals it. Then hear Isaac Hayes's own long-form records, where the same instinct for a slow build gets eighteen minutes instead of three."],"aretha-franklin-i-never-loved-a-man":["Eleven songs, thirty-four minutes, and the crucial instrument is Franklin's own piano: she plays it on most of the record, which locks the band to her phrasing rather than the reverse. The title song is slow and accusatory, 'Do Right Woman' is a hymn in disguise, 'Dr. Feelgood' is close to a blues, and 'Respect' ends side one. Her sisters Carolyn and Erma sing behind her, so the backing voices are family harmony.","Franklin had spent six years at Columbia being given show tunes and jazz standards. Jerry Wexler signed her to Atlantic and took her to FAME Studios in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, in January 1967, put her at a piano in front of a white Southern rhythm section and let her lead. The session ended in a drunken argument after one day; the rest of the album was finished in New York with the same players flown up.","Listen to how the band follows the piano into places no arrangement would go — the small rhythmic hesitations before a chorus are Franklin's hands, not a chart. On 'Do Right Woman' almost everything except the organ and voices was added later in New York, which is why it floats free of the drums. The gospel training is audible in the way she answers her own lines.","It was received immediately as a career resurrection and has stayed near the top of every soul album list since. The debates concern the Muscle Shoals session itself, now a founding myth of integrated Southern recording, and how much credit Wexler deserves for a transformation that was mostly a matter of removing the material Columbia had imposed. Franklin's own accounts and Wexler's differ on details.","Start with the title track and listen only to the piano; it is the argument the voice then makes. Move to 'Dr. Feelgood' for the church left hand. Then compare a Columbia-era recording of hers from 1962 and hear what four years and one rhythm section changed."],"aretha-franklin-respect":["Two and a half minutes, fast, with the drums and a guitar snapping together on every offbeat. Franklin sings it as an ultimatum rather than a plea, and her sisters chant the sock-it-to-me line under her like a playground taunt. In the middle King Curtis takes a tenor saxophone solo lifted in feel from the Stax horn style. Then comes the spelled-out chorus, seven letters shouted one at a time, and the record never recovers its composure.","Otis Redding wrote and recorded it in 1965 as a working man's complaint: he brings home the money, so he wants respect when he gets in. Franklin cut her version at Atlantic in New York in February 1967, rearranged it with her sisters, and reversed the sex of the demand. It went to number one that summer, in the months of the Detroit and Newark uprisings, and was immediately heard as a civil-rights and a women's song at once.","The spelling of the title and the sock-it-to-me chant are Franklin's additions, worked out with Carolyn and Erma, and neither exists in Redding's song. Notice also the key detail of the bridge, where she lists what she is prepared to give; the demand is conditional, not absolute, which is why the record sounds like a negotiation rather than a slogan.","Redding is said to have remarked, after hearing it, that the girl had taken his song. The record is now so thoroughly a political emblem that the sexual bargaining in the lyric is often edited out of the public memory of it; recent scholarship has pushed back, insisting the song is about a specific relationship before it is about anything larger. Nobody disputes that the arrangement is entirely hers.","Play Redding's original first — it is a completely different song at the same tempo. Then listen to the two backing voices rather than the lead, because they carry the jokes. Finally note how short it is: everything is over in the time a later soul record would spend on an introduction."],"marvin-gaye-i-heard-it-through-the-grapevine":["An electric piano plays four heavy chords in near-silence, a tambourine ticks, and the record is already tense before anything happens. Gaye sings higher than his usual range, sounding cornered, while female voices circle behind him with a wordless figure. Strings enter low and stay low. The bass moves constantly. Nothing about the arrangement is dense, yet the record sounds crowded, which is exactly the subject: a man learning his private life is public knowledge.","Norman Whitfield and Barrett Strong wrote it and pushed it through the Motown quality-control meetings repeatedly. Gladys Knight and the Pips had a hit with a faster, gospel-driven version in 1967. Gaye's take was held back, buried on an album, then released as a single in late 1968 after radio picked it up, and became the biggest-selling Motown single to that point. Gaye was at the time still mainly known for duets.","Whitfield pitched the key deliberately high for Gaye, who had to strain, and the strain is the performance. Listen for the tambourine, which is doing the job of a snare drum and is placed unusually far forward in the mix. The wordless backing figure is the same shape as the vocal melody, a half-bar behind — the gossip arriving just after the news.","Its success embarrassed Motown's release committee and strengthened Whitfield's hand, which led directly to the label's psychedelic soul phase. Creedence Clearwater Revival's eleven-minute rock version in 1970 opened a long argument about whether the song belongs to Gaye's paranoia or to a swampy guitar groove; the argument is settled every time the original's first four chords play.","Listen to the first fifteen seconds twice before going on, because the whole record's atmosphere is set there. Then follow the bass, which never repeats a bar exactly. Compare Gladys Knight's earlier version, which is faster and happier and shows how much of the menace is tempo and key."],"the-temptations-cloud-nine":["A wah-wah guitar scratches out a rhythm before any voice appears, congas come in under it, and the beat is harder and more broken than any Motown record before it. The five Temptations trade lines rather than harmonise — one voice takes two words, another takes the next two — so the lead keeps moving around the group. The song is about a childhood in poverty and the escape a high offers, and the chorus is left ambiguous.","Norman Whitfield had heard Sly and the Family Stone and decided Motown's polish was about to become a liability. David Ruffin had just left the group, so the Temptations needed a new identity anyway, and Whitfield gave them one: no single lead, a psychedelic rhythm section, and lyrics about the street rather than the dance. Motown's executives worried the song endorsed drugs. It won the label its first Grammy in 1969.","Listen for how the vocal lines are cut across the beat rather than laid along it — Whitfield is treating five singers as a percussion section. The wah-wah pedal, then a new device, is used for rhythm rather than solos, which is how funk guitar would be played for the next decade. The lyric never says what the cloud is, which was the compromise that got it released.","The Grammy legitimised the change and Motown spent the next four years chasing it. Older fans, and some of the Temptations, complained that the group's singing had been subordinated to a producer's studio ideas, a complaint that grew louder as the records grew longer. Historians now treat this as the hinge between sixties soul and seventies funk, and as evidence that Detroit was listening hard to San Francisco.","Listen for the guitar in the first ten seconds; everything that follows is built on it. Then try to track a single Temptation through a verse and notice how quickly you lose him. Play 'My Girl' immediately afterwards — same group, four years, an entirely different idea of what a vocal group is for."],"otis-redding-dock-of-the-bay":["Waves and a gull, then a guitar figure that rocks gently between two chords, and a voice that does none of the things Redding was famous for. He sings quietly, in long relaxed phrases, about doing nothing at all. There is no climax, no shouted coda; the horns are reduced to two soft punctuations, and the record ends with whistling over the fade while the waves come back in.","Redding wrote the first verse on a houseboat in Sausalito in the summer of 1967, weeks after the Monterey Pop Festival had introduced him to a young white rock audience. He finished it at Stax with Steve Cropper in November and December. On 10 December 1967 his plane came down in a Wisconsin lake, killing him at twenty-six and most of his band. The single was released in January and became his only number one.","The whistling is generally reported to be a placeholder: Redding had not written a final verse and whistled instead, intending to return to it. The waves and gull were added afterwards by Cropper. Listen also to the bridge, where the chord movement briefly resembles a folk-rock song rather than a soul record — the Sausalito influence is audible in the harmony, not only the lyric.","Stax was reportedly unsure about releasing something so unlike him; its success and the circumstances of his death fused into a single story that has been hard to separate from the music. Critics argue about whether it signalled a real new direction or was a one-off experiment. What is clear is that the singer of 'Try a Little Tenderness' had decided he could hold attention without raising his voice.","Notice how long the phrases are and how late he enters each one. Follow the second guitar, which answers the first throughout. Then play 'Try a Little Tenderness' back to back with it to hear the two extremes of the same singer within eighteen months, and stay for the whistling."],"aretha-franklin-lady-soul":["Under thirty minutes and almost no filler. 'Chain of Fools' opens on a single tremolo guitar note before the band drops in; 'Ain't No Way' is a slow ballad with a soprano floating a wordless line high above Franklin's; '(You Make Me Feel Like) A Natural Woman' builds from piano to full gospel chorus. The band is the Muscle Shoals rhythm section again, and Franklin's piano and her sisters' harmonies remain the spine of the sound.","1968: a year of assassinations and burning cities, and Franklin's second Atlantic year, in which she was on the cover of Time and being called the Queen of Soul in print. The material came from several directions at once — Don Covay wrote 'Chain of Fools', Carole King and Gerry Goffin wrote 'A Natural Woman' with Jerry Wexler, and her younger sister Carolyn wrote 'Ain't No Way'. Eric Clapton played guitar on one track.","The soprano soaring above Franklin on 'Ain't No Way' is Cissy Houston, singing a countermelody rather than harmony, and it is one of the few places on any Franklin record where another voice is allowed to go higher than hers. Listen also to 'Chain of Fools', where the guitar's opening note was reportedly meant as a test tone and was kept because it set the mood.","It was a commercial and critical success on arrival and has remained the album people reach for after the debut. Later criticism has focused less on the record than on what surrounded it — the Queen of Soul framing, the political weight placed on a singer who preferred not to make speeches. The performances themselves have never needed defending.","Begin with 'Ain't No Way' and listen for the second soprano entering above the lead. Then 'Chain of Fools' for the guitar and the drum pattern. If you want to hear where the phrasing comes from, follow it with Margaret Bonds's Montgomery Variations, written out of the same church music and the same years."],"isaac-hayes-hot-buttered-soul":["Four tracks in forty-five minutes, which in 1969 was almost a provocation. 'Walk On By' runs twelve minutes and opens with strings and a distorted guitar before Hayes sings a note. 'By the Time I Get to Phoenix' runs eighteen, of which the first eight are a spoken monologue over an organ chord, telling the back story the song leaves out. Hayes's voice is a low, unhurried baritone; the band never hurries either.","Stax had just lost its entire back catalogue to Atlantic in a contract dispute and lost Otis Redding in a plane crash. In 1969 it responded by releasing twenty-seven albums at once to rebuild a catalogue from nothing, and gave its staff writer Isaac Hayes complete control of one of them. He used it to ignore every rule about single length. It outsold almost everything else Stax issued that year.","Both long tracks are covers of songs by white pop writers — Burt Bacharach and Hal David, and Jimmy Webb — and Hayes treats them as material to be dismantled rather than interpreted. Listen for the way the monologue on 'Phoenix' never quite resolves into the song; the band vamps on one chord for so long that when the melody finally arrives it feels like a memory. The fuzz guitar on 'Walk On By' is mixed as a lead instrument.","It was received as an eccentric gamble that worked, and it changed the economics of Black albums: soul artists could now be sold as album acts. Critics have argued since about the monologues, which some find hypnotic and others find close to self-parody, an impression the later Shaft persona did nothing to dispel. The arrangements are no longer in dispute; they set the pattern for orchestral soul.","Give 'By the Time I Get to Phoenix' the full eighteen minutes without skipping the talking, and time how long the organ holds before the band moves. Then compare Ravel's Boléro, another long crescendo over a fixed pattern. Hear 'Walk On By' beside Dionne Warwick's original to see what was removed."],"dusty-springfield-dusty-in-memphis":["Eleven songs, thirty-four minutes, sung very quietly. Springfield's voice is breathy and set close to the microphone, so the record works at conversational volume while the band plays full Memphis soul underneath — organ, horns, a rhythm section that swings without pushing. 'Son of a Preacher Man' is the famous one, but the character of the album is in the slow tracks, where she sings behind the beat and lets the arrangement do the rising.","Jerry Wexler brought her to Atlantic and to the Memphis studio where Elvis Presley and Neil Diamond were also recording, using the house rhythm section known as the Memphis Boys. Springfield, a British pop star raised on American records, was so intimidated by singing where Aretha Franklin had sung that she could not perform in the room; the backing tracks were cut in Memphis and her vocals were added later in New York.","Almost none of the album is her singing live with the band, and it is the reason the record sounds the way it does — a close, isolated voice floating on a group performance recorded a thousand miles away. Listen to the song selection too: Randy Newman, Goffin and King, Bacharach and David, all pop writers, arranged as Southern soul. The seam between those two worlds is the album's whole idea.","It flopped on release; the soul audience did not want a white English singer and the pop audience had lost interest. Its reputation was rebuilt slowly by critics, then decisively by the use of 'Son of a Preacher Man' in Pulp Fiction in 1994. The residual argument is about the word authenticity: whether the record is an act of homage or appropriation, and whether the answer changes because she sings so well.","Start with 'Just a Little Lovin'' rather than the hit, and notice how low she keeps the volume. Then play Aretha Franklin's Muscle Shoals sides for the contrast in attack. The 1999 expanded reissue adds outtakes that show how many passes it took to get that apparent ease."],"smokey-robinson-tears-of-a-clown":["The introduction sounds like a fairground: a bassoon burbling a circus figure against a high piccolo, over a tumbling percussion pattern. Then Robinson's falsetto arrives with a lyric about hiding grief behind a public face. The verse is fast and busy, the chorus opens out, and the carnival figure keeps returning between sections, so the joke and the confession alternate every eight bars. Three minutes, and none of it is slow.","Stevie Wonder and Hank Cosby built the backing track as an instrumental and gave it to Robinson at a Motown Christmas party because they could not find words for it. He heard a circus in it, thought of Pagliacci and wrote the lyric around the clown. It sat on a 1967 album for three years until Motown's British office issued it as a single, where it went to number one and forced an American release in 1970.","The bassoon is the giveaway. Motown almost never used one, and it is there because Wonder was writing for an imagined circus band rather than a soul group. Notice how the lyric quotes the operatic clown directly, and how the melody of the verse crowds so many syllables in that Robinson has to sing it almost as patter — the strain is the mask slipping.","Its three-year delay is the standard example of Motown's quality-control system failing, and of the British market rescuing an American record. Critics divide on the arrangement: some hear it as the most inventive thing Motown released, others as a novelty whose orchestration undercuts a serious lyric. It was the Miracles' only American number one, which surprises people who know how many hits Robinson wrote.","Listen once to the introduction alone and decide what kind of record you think is coming. Follow the bassoon after that; it never leaves. Then hear Leoncavallo's Pagliacci, whose weeping clown Robinson is openly borrowing, and 'The Tracks of My Tears' for the same idea with no costume at all."],"curtis-mayfield-curtis":["The opening track begins with a whispered congregation of voices before the band crashes in, and for eight minutes it warns that everyone is going to hell. 'Move On Up' is the opposite: a long, elated drive over congas and a punching horn line that keeps going for almost nine minutes in its full version. In between are slow songs with strings. Mayfield's own guitar, tuned to an open chord, threads through everything, and the falsetto is unchanged.","Mayfield left the Impressions in 1970 after thirteen years, and set up his own label, Curtom, in Chicago — Black-owned, which very few soul labels were. The gentleness of 'People Get Ready' had stopped matching the moment; this record answers the Black Power years directly, and the long tracks answer the new album market that Hot Buttered Soul had opened the year before. Johnny Pate arranged the horns and strings.","Mayfield tuned his guitar to an open chord and played, self-taught, in a way no trained guitarist would, which is why his rhythm parts sit oddly against the beat and cannot be transcribed convincingly. Listen for the congas, which carry the groove more than the drum kit does. The nine-minute 'Move On Up' was cut to a fraction for the single; the long version is the one to hear.","It was a commercial success and established that a soul singer could run his own label and write his own politics without a producer between him and the record. Critics at the time found the hellfire opener heavy-handed; the judgement has softened as the album's influence on seventies soul, and later on sampling producers, became clear. The falsetto, once considered a limitation, is now the signature.","Play the long 'Move On Up' and stay past the four-minute mark, where the horns take over completely. Then go back to the opening track for the whispering before the band enters. If you want the argument the record is making, put 'People Get Ready' from five years earlier immediately before it."],"marvin-gaye-whats-going-on":["Nine songs that run into each other without a gap, thirty-five minutes, beginning with party chatter and a saxophone that wanders in as if already mid-thought. Gaye multi-tracks his own voice into a small choir, so lead, harmony and murmured asides all belong to one man. Underneath, Motown's house band plays softly — congas, vibraphone, a bass line that never stops moving — with Detroit Symphony strings floated on top. The record almost never raises its voice.","Gaye had been Motown's smooth hit-maker through the sixties. By 1970 his singing partner Tammi Terrell was dead at twenty-four, his marriage was failing and his brother Frankie had returned from Vietnam with stories. Obie Benson of the Four Tops brought him the title song after watching police beat protesters in Berkeley. Berry Gordy thought a protest record would finish him and refused to release it; Gaye recorded nothing else until Motown gave way in May 1971.","The saxophone that opens the album was an accident — Eli Fontaine was warming up, Gaye heard the phrase and told him he was done. The voices at the party are partly Detroit Lions players Gaye knew. Listen for how the songs are stitched: the last chord of one becomes the first of the next, so there are no gaps and nowhere to applaud, which is why the album cannot be heard in pieces.","It sold immediately and was recognised at once as proof that a soul singer could sustain an album-length argument; Gordy's resistance has since become part of the legend, and he has spent decades explaining it. Later debate concerns the mixes — the album exists in more than one, and reissues have made the case for each — and how much of the seamless flow was designed in advance rather than found at the desk.","Hear it whole, in order, once, without skipping. Then notice that the title song and 'Inner City Blues' bracket the sequence and share material, so the record closes a circle. Play 'Mercy Mercy Me' twice: once for the words, once for the vibraphone. It works best quietly, late."],"isaac-hayes-theme-from-shaft":["A hi-hat opens alone, hissing in even sixteenths, and stays there for a full minute. A wah-wah guitar joins, scratching a two-note figure; then flute, then horns, then strings, then a bass line, each entering on top of what is already running and none of them leaving. Hayes does not sing until the piece is three-quarters over, and then mostly talks, answered by female voices who supply the punchlines. Five minutes of accumulation.","Gordon Parks was directing Shaft for MGM, a detective film with a Black hero made largely by a Black crew, and Stax wanted the soundtrack. Hayes, whose Hot Buttered Soul had made him the label's biggest solo act, wrote and produced the score, expecting also to be cast in the lead. The single reached number one at the end of 1971 and won the Academy Award for Best Original Song the following spring.","The wah-wah pedal is played by Charles Pitts, and the figure he scratches is deliberately unmusical — two notes, no melody, pure rhythm — which is precisely why it became the sound of urban film scoring for a decade. Listen also to how the arrangement is nothing but layers added in sequence: no section is ever taken away until the very end.","The Oscar made Hayes the first Black composer to win in that category and confirmed that soul musicians could write for film, a door that stayed open. The blaxploitation cycle it helped launch has been argued over ever since, for trading in the images it also celebrated. The theme itself is now so widely parodied that its construction — a five-minute crescendo with no chorus — is easy to overlook.","Count the entries: hi-hat, guitar, flute, horns, strings, bass, voice, in that order, and notice that nothing ever drops out. Then hear Duke Ellington's Harlem for another orchestral portrait of the same streets thirty years earlier, and Superfly for the answer Curtis Mayfield gave a year later."],"al-green-lets-stay-together":["Thirty-three minutes of restraint. Al Jackson's drums are recorded dry and slightly muffled, always a shade behind the beat; the horns play soft sustained chords rather than stabs; the organ hums. Green sings in three registers — a soft baritone, a light falsetto and an unpitched sigh — and slips between them mid-word. The title song's opening phrase, sung almost under his breath before the band arrives, is the sound the whole record is built around.","Hi Records in Memphis, produced by Willie Mitchell in a converted cinema on South Lauderdale Street with the Hodges brothers as the rhythm section and Al Jackson, from Booker T. and the MG's, on drums. Mitchell had spent two years persuading Green to stop imitating Otis Redding and sing quietly instead. Stax was in decline; Hi became the city's second sound, and this album, with a number one single, made Green the biggest soul star in America.","The drum sound is a deliberate piece of engineering: Jackson's kit was damped with tape and towels and recorded with very few microphones, so the beat has no ring at all and the voice can float above it. Listen for the moment in the title track where Green abandons words and just breathes in rhythm; Mitchell kept those breaths in the mix rather than editing them out.","It was received as the year's most sensual record and has been treated ever since as the high point of Memphis soul after Stax. Criticism has centred on repetition — Green and Mitchell made several albums to a very similar recipe — and on how to weigh the later gospel career against it. Green himself has said the songs were always closer to church than they sounded.","Put the title song on and listen only to the drums; once you hear how far behind the beat they sit, the whole record opens. Then 'I've Never Found a Girl' for the falsetto. Compare with Otis Redding's 'Dock of the Bay' for two Memphis singers discovering what quiet does."],"the-temptations-papa-was-a-rollin-stone":["The album version runs nearly twelve minutes and no one sings for the first four. A bass figure repeats, hi-hat ticking above it; a wah-wah guitar enters, then a lone muted trumpet, then strings scraping high and dissonant, then handclaps — each layer added over an unchanging foundation. When the voices finally arrive they trade single lines about a father who died on the day the singer was born. The instrumental sections return between every verse.","Norman Whitfield had been extending Motown records for four years and here pushed the method to its limit, after first cutting the song with the Undisputed Truth. The Temptations reportedly resented being made to wait four minutes to sing on their own single. Motown had moved its headquarters to Los Angeles, the Detroit era was over, and the record's cinematic length matched the blaxploitation scores then dominating Black radio.","The strings are not doing what strings usually do: they are placed high and left unresolved, functioning as tension rather than sweetness, which is unusual anywhere in Motown. Listen for the trumpet, which states a three-note phrase and then vanishes for minutes at a time. The single edit cuts the song roughly in half and removes most of what makes it strange.","It won three Grammys, including one for the instrumental B-side, which rather made the Temptations' point about who the record belonged to. It is now cited as the end of Motown's Detroit period and the single most sampled Motown production. The argument that remains is the old one between producer and performer: whether a twelve-minute track with four minutes of vamping serves the group or the man at the desk.","Insist on the twelve-minute album version. Time the entries — bass, hi-hat, guitar, trumpet, strings — and notice nothing is ever removed. Then play 'My Girl' for the same group eight years earlier, and Isaac Hayes's Shaft theme for the other great exercise in pure accumulation."],"curtis-mayfield-superfly":["A film score that works as an album because Mayfield wrote songs rather than cues. Congas and a hi-hat set almost every track moving; horns answer the falsetto; strings arrive to make the street sound tragic rather than glamorous. 'Freddie's Dead' is an instrumental in the film and a song here. 'Pusherman' introduces the dealer in the first person, which is the record's central device: Mayfield sings as the people the film only shows.","Gordon Parks Jr. was making a film about a Harlem cocaine dealer trying to get out. Mayfield, running his own Curtom label in Chicago, saw the script, thought it glamorised the trade, and wrote a commentary rather than an accompaniment. He recorded it before the film was finished. The album was released ahead of the picture and became one of the very few soundtracks to earn more than the film it belonged to.","The lyrics contradict the images they accompany. In the film the dealer is stylish; in the songs he is a dead man walking, and the most seductive groove on the record carries the bleakest words. Listen for Mayfield's guitar, tuned to an open chord and played with his thumb, sitting slightly across the conga pattern. The strings quote the same three-note falling phrase in several tracks.","It was a huge commercial success and gave blaxploitation its conscience; critics who attacked the cycle for selling dealers and pimps generally exempted this record, because it says the opposite of the film. Its afterlife has been enormous in hip-hop, where the rhythm tracks have been sampled for four decades. The persistent question is whether a warning that grooves this well can function as a warning at all.","Play 'Pusherman' and read the words while it goes; the gap between the two is the whole album. Then 'Freddie's Dead' for the string writing. Gershwin's Porgy and Bess makes a useful companion — another ambitious portrait of a Black neighbourhood that drew the same argument about who it was for."],"stevie-wonder-talking-book":["Ten songs in which Wonder plays most of the instruments himself, including the drums. The sound is new: a clavinet, an electric keyboard with a hard plucked attack, played through effects until it functions as a rhythm guitar, with synthesiser bass underneath. 'Superstition' is the demonstration — layered clavinet parts, a horn section, and drums that Wonder recorded first, alone. 'You Are the Sunshine of My Life' sits at the other end, soft and jazz-inflected, sung partly by other voices.","Wonder turned twenty-one in 1971, let his Motown contract lapse, and renegotiated one that gave him ownership of his publishing and complete artistic control — almost unheard of for a Black artist at the label. He then worked with Malcolm Cecil and Robert Margouleff and their enormous custom synthesiser, TONTO, at a rate of several songs a week. He toured with the Rolling Stones that year, which took his music to a white rock audience.","The sleeve carries the album title in braille, the only major pop record of the period to do so, and it is not decoration: Wonder insisted on it. Musically, listen for how 'Superstition' has no bass guitar at all — the low end is a keyboard — and how the drum track was laid down before anything else, so the whole song hangs off a performance by the singer.","It was the record that established Wonder as an album artist rather than a singles act, and the beginning of the run of five albums usually treated as his classic period. Critics noted at the time how strange it was for one man to play everything; the counter-argument, that the records lose the friction of a band, is still occasionally made about the later albums and rarely about this one.","Listen to 'Superstition' for the drums first and the clavinet second, which reverses how everyone hears it. Then 'Maybe Your Baby' for seven minutes of the same instrument doing something dirtier. Follow with Innervisions, recorded within months, to hear the method turned on politics."],"marvin-gaye-lets-get-it-on":["Eight songs, thirty-two minutes, and a completely different climate from the album before it. A wah-wah guitar opens the title track with three notes that everyone recognises; congas and a loose drum pattern keep the tempo slow; strings and horns swell and retreat. Gaye stacks his own voice again, but now the overdubs are moans and encouragements behind the lead. 'Distant Lover' turns the mood inside out, a slow plea sung almost entirely in falsetto.","Gaye had moved to Los Angeles and was recording at Motown's West Coast studio. He was separating from Anna Gordy, Berry Gordy's sister, and had met Janis Hunter, who was seventeen. Ed Townsend, who co-wrote the title song, had drafted it about recovery from addiction; Gaye rewrote it as an argument for sex. Released two years after What's Going On, it outsold it, and fixed his public image for the rest of his life.","The three-note guitar hook is played with a wah-wah pedal held nearly still, so it sounds vocal rather than funky — the guitar is doing an impression of the singer. Listen also for how Gaye buries his own spoken and sighed overdubs low in the mix; on headphones there is a second, private performance running under the sung one. The record's sound is deliberately warmer and murkier than its predecessor's.","It was an enormous commercial success and was immediately used to file Gaye as a seduction artist, which flattened the reputation the previous album had built. Later writing has been more interested in the unease audible in the songs, and in how the marriage that was ending would produce the bitter divorce album of 1978. The title track's ubiquity has made it almost impossible to hear as music.","Skip the title track first time and start with 'Distant Lover', where the falsetto and the strings do the work. Then return to the opening for the guitar hook alone. Headphones matter here more than on any other Gaye record, because of what is hidden under the lead vocal."],"al-green-call-me":["Ten tracks, the same Hi Records band and the same damped drum sound, but the writing has widened. 'Here I Am (Come and Take Me)' rides a fast, light groove; 'Call Me (Come Back Home)' is a slow plea with horns sighing behind it; 'Jesus Is Waiting' ends the record with eight minutes of gospel that never quite becomes a sermon. Two country songs sit in the middle, sung straight. Green's falsetto is used more sparingly and lands harder.","Memphis, 1973, with Willie Mitchell producing and the Hi rhythm section intact. Green was at his commercial peak and beginning to be uneasy about it; the religious material on this album is not a late addition but the centre of it, and within a few years he would be ordained and largely leave secular music. Recording a Hank Williams song and a Willie Nelson song on a soul album was, in Memphis in 1973, a quiet act of border-crossing.","The two country covers are the key to the record. Green sings 'I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry' almost without ornament, which exposes how close honky-tonk phrasing and soul phrasing already were. Listen also to 'Jesus Is Waiting', where the band plays a secular groove under an entirely sacred lyric, and to the organ, which is playing church chords throughout the love songs.","It is frequently argued to be the better album than Let's Stay Together, more varied and less reliant on a single hit, though it has never been as famous. Critics have used it to show that the division between soul and country was a marketing arrangement rather than a musical one. The gospel material, once heard as a curiosity, now looks like the announcement of what came next.","Start with 'Here I Am' for the groove, then jump to the country covers and hear how little he changes them. Finish with 'Jesus Is Waiting' and its eight minutes. Dusty in Memphis makes the obvious pairing: two records made in the same city out of songs from somewhere else."],"stevie-wonder-innervisions":["Nine songs, forty-four minutes, most of them played entirely by one man. 'Too High' opens with scat singing over a jittery bass; 'Living for the City' runs seven minutes and breaks in the middle for a piece of street theatre with recorded traffic and a wrongful arrest; 'Higher Ground' is built on a clavinet run through a wah-wah pedal; 'He's Misstra Know-It-All' closes it gently. The synthesiser bass is everywhere and there is almost no guitar.","Wonder was twenty-three and working at enormous speed with the TONTO synthesiser system and its two operators. The subjects had changed: drugs, racism, the mechanics of poverty, and a portrait of a politician widely taken to be Richard Nixon. Three days after the album's release, in August 1973, a car he was travelling in struck a logging truck in North Carolina and he spent days in a coma; he lost his sense of smell and nearly his life.","The dramatised sequence in 'Living for the City' — a man arriving in New York, arrested within minutes — uses actors and street recordings and was cut from the single, so most people who know the song have never heard its point. Listen also to how much of the album's low end is synthesiser rather than bass guitar, and to 'Higher Ground', written and recorded within hours, weeks before the crash.","It won the Grammy for album of the year and consolidated the argument that Wonder was the most complete musician in popular music. The accident and the near-prophetic 'Higher Ground' attached a mythology to the record that critics have since tried to peel away. The lasting debate is about the one-man method: whether the absence of a band is the source of the album's coherence or the reason it sometimes sounds airless.","Hear 'Living for the City' in the full album version, including the spoken sequence; then hear the single edit and notice what was removed. 'Higher Ground' repays listening for the clavinet alone. Play it after Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, which it answers directly and rather less gently."],"stevie-wonder-songs-in-the-key-of-life":["Two long-playing records plus a bonus disc, over a hundred minutes, and the range is deliberate: big-band tribute ('Sir Duke'), a school-yard memoir over one of the hardest grooves he ever cut ('I Wish'), a synthesiser string quartet describing a slum ('Village Ghetto Land'), a fourteen-year-old's jazz-fusion instrumental, a lullaby for his newborn daughter, and a seven-minute declaration of love that changes key repeatedly. Wonder plays much of it and sings all of it.","He had threatened to retire and emigrate, then signed what was reported as the largest recording contract to that point, and spent two years in the studio with dozens of musicians passing through. It arrived in 1976, in the American bicentennial year, as a summing-up: Black American music surveyed from swing through gospel to synthesiser funk by a man who had been a Motown child star fourteen years earlier.","'Village Ghetto Land' has no real instruments at all: the string quartet is a synthesiser, and the mismatch between a polite classical minuet and a lyric about rats and rotting food is the entire device. 'Isn't She Lovely' includes a recording of his daughter being bathed. 'Sir Duke' names Ellington, Basie, Glenn Miller and Louis Armstrong outright, and the horn line imitates a swing band in unison.","It entered the American chart at number one, which almost nothing did in 1976, and won album of the year. The standing criticism is length: two records and an EP contain material most artists would have edited, and the sequencing asks a lot. The counter-case is that the sprawl is the subject, and that no shorter record could have held a survey of a whole tradition.","Do not attempt it in one sitting. Take side one for 'Love's in Need of Love Today' and 'Village Ghetto Land' back to back — the softest and the sharpest thing on the album. Then 'Sir Duke', and then Ellington himself, whom the song is paying for. Keep the bonus disc for later; 'All Day Sucker' is worth finding."],"james-brown-live-at-the-apollo":["A single show, captured whole: James Brown and the Famous Flames at the Apollo Theater in Harlem, fronted by a big R&B band with a hard horn section. It runs like a revue, not a playlist. Short songs are cut together with no gaps, the band hits every cue on the singer's signal, and the centre of the record is the long slow ballad Lost Someone, stretched to ten minutes of pleading, silence and screams from the crowd before the band tears into a medley of his hits.","Brown's label, King Records of Cincinnati, run by Syd Nathan, saw no point in a live album: live records did not sell, and singles were the business. Brown believed his stage show was the real product and paid for the recording himself. It was taped in October 1962 during a week of shows at the Apollo, the proving ground of Black entertainment in New York, at a time when rhythm and blues was still largely kept off white radio.","Listen to the opening. Before a note is sung, the master of ceremonies runs through Brown's song titles and billings in a rising incantation, and the band is already vamping underneath him: the show is built as a ritual of anticipation. Then notice the audience. The shrieks from women near the stage are part of the drama, and during Lost Someone Brown plays them like an instrument, holding the band back until the room answers him.","It was a surprise hit, climbing high on the pop album chart and staying there for over a year, bought by listeners who played it straight through at parties. That success forced the industry to take both live albums and Black album buyers seriously. It now appears on nearly every list of the great live records, and critics tend to agree it catches Brown at the hinge point, still a soul balladeer, already a bandleader of military precision.","Play it loud and from the start, without skipping: the whole point is momentum. Listen to how the band responds to Brown's shouted cues rather than to written bar lengths. Then compare Sam Cooke's Harlem Square Club set from the following year, rawer and gentler, and Brown's own later funk sides, where the song dissolves entirely into the groove."],"james-brown-papas-got-a-brand-new-bag":["Just over two minutes of a new idea. The horns stab short bright chords, the guitar scratches a tight choked rhythm high on the neck, and the whole band leans on the first beat of every bar instead of the backbeat that R&B had always stressed. Brown's voice is barked and percussive, the words almost secondary to the attack. The tune barely moves harmonically; the excitement comes from rhythm, from where the accents land and from the silences between them.","By 1965 Brown was the hardest-touring act in Black America, with a large, rigorously drilled band that he fined for missed cues. He was looking for a sound that would carry his stage show onto a three-minute single. The song, about an older man showing he can still keep up with new dances, arrived as soul was crossing into the pop charts, and it became Brown's first top-ten pop hit and earned him his first Grammy.","The record is faster than the band played it. The master tape was sped up for release, which raised the pitch and tightened the feel, giving Brown's voice its slightly strained edge. The full take was also longer and was split into two parts across the single. Listen too for the guitar: the scratchy muted strumming, played by Jimmy Nolen, would become the signature texture of funk guitar for the next decade.","Brown himself called it a turning point, and historians broadly agree, though they argue about whether this or Cold Sweat two years later is the first true funk record. The consensus now is that this is the hinge: still shaped like a twelve-bar song, but already built around rhythm rather than melody. Its influence on dance music, from Motown to Afrobeat, is hard to overstate.","Ignore the words and count along: feel how the weight lands hard on the first beat, then how much space follows. Listen to the guitar in isolation for one pass and the horns for another. Then play Cold Sweat and hear the same ideas with the song form stripped away."],"james-brown-cold-sweat":["Seven minutes built on almost nothing: a bass figure, a scratching guitar, a horn riff and a drum pattern that stutters around the beat, locked together and repeated. There is effectively one chord for long stretches. Brown sings, grunts and calls out, then hands the floor to a saxophone solo and, famously, to the drummer, who plays a short break alone. Everything that had been accompaniment in rhythm and blues has become the substance.","The arrangement came together with the band's musical director, the saxophonist Alfred 'Pee Wee' Ellis, who shaped it from an older Brown ballad. It was recorded at King's studio in Cincinnati in 1967, the year of the Detroit and Newark riots and of psychedelia's summer. Released as a two-part single, it went to the top of the R&B chart and into the pop top ten, a startling result for a record without a chorus to speak of.","Ellis said the horn line was inspired by Miles Davis's So What, from Kind of Blue: the same idea of a short riff answering a phrase, turned from cool jazz into something clipped and physical. Listen too for the moment Brown calls on the drummer, Clyde Stubblefield, to take a solo: it is one of the first isolated funk breaks on record, a template for the drum breaks later hip-hop producers would hunt for.","Many writers name it the first fully formed funk record, the point at which harmony stops moving and rhythm takes over. Musicians at the time were startled by how little happened harmonically and how much rhythmically. It is now taught as a foundation text of groove-based music, and Ellis's role, long under-credited, is increasingly acknowledged.","Put on the full seven-minute version rather than the single edit. Listen first to the bass and the kick drum together, then to the way the guitar and horns fill only the gaps they leave. Follow it with Kind of Blue's opening track to hear the borrowed idea, and with Funky Drummer to hear where it led."],"james-brown-say-it-loud":["A funk groove with a chant at its centre. Brown half-speaks, half-shouts verses about dignity, work and self-reliance, and a chorus of children answers him with the title, turning the song into a call-and-response rally. The band keeps the tight interlocking pattern of Cold Sweat, horns jabbing, guitar scratching, bass circling, so that the message rides on the body rather than on a melody. It is a protest song with no lament in it at all.","It was recorded in Los Angeles in the summer of 1968, months after the assassination of Martin Luther King. In April Brown had been credited with helping keep Boston calm by letting his concert be broadcast live on television the night after the killing. The children were recruited locally for the session. The song arrived as the phrase 'Black is beautiful' and the Black Power movement were reshaping how a generation spoke about itself.","Notice who sings the chorus: not the band or backing singers but kids, which makes the statement sound like something being taught to the next generation, not argued with the current one. The verses also say nothing about violence or grievance; they talk about building, owning and working. The pride is framed as economic self-determination, a theme Brown would return to throughout the next decade.","It topped the R&B chart for weeks and reached the pop top ten, yet Brown later said it cost him part of his white audience and some radio play. Some in the Black Power movement thought him too cosy with politicians; others heard the song as the moment soul music said the word Black with pride on national radio. That second view now dominates.","Listen to it as a chant, not a song: notice how short each exchange is, and how the children's answer lands on the beat like a drum. Then play Curtis Mayfield's People Get Ready, from only three years earlier, to hear how far and how fast the tone of Black popular music moved."],"james-brown-funky-drummer":["A long, loose jam in which the song is almost a pretext. The band settles into a slinky groove, Brown ad-libs and directs, a saxophone takes a turn, and then, near the end, everything drops away except the drummer. For about twenty seconds Clyde Stubblefield plays alone: a light, bouncing pattern with ghost notes skittering around a steady backbeat, relaxed and precise at once. It is those twenty seconds, not the song, that became one of the most recycled pieces of music ever recorded.","Recorded in Cincinnati in November 1969, it was released early in 1970 as a two-part single and did modestly on the R&B chart. It was, by Brown's standards, a minor record. Its second life began in 1986, when a Polydor compilation called In the Jungle Groove issued the full unedited version, just as affordable digital samplers were letting hip-hop producers loop short passages of old records.","Listen to what Stubblefield plays with his left hand on the snare: the quiet in-between strokes, not the loud ones, give the pattern its bounce, and that softness is what made it so easy to loop under a rapper. Brown can be heard naming the drummer and telling the band to hold back, arranging the famous break in real time rather than planning it.","The break has been sampled on hundreds or, by some counts, over a thousand records, from Public Enemy and Eric B. and Rakim to LL Cool J and much of 1990s dance music. It has become the central case in arguments about sampling and credit: Stubblefield, as a salaried sideman, received no royalties for its reuse, and said so often before his death in 2017.","Listen to the whole nine minutes first, so the break arrives as a surprise, then loop those twenty seconds and hear how much is in them. Then play It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back or Paid in Full and try to catch Stubblefield's pattern buried underneath."],"james-brown-sex-machine":["Two voices trading lines over a small, lean band. Brown calls, his long-time partner Bobby Byrd answers, and underneath a teenage bassist plays a rubbery, constantly moving line against a guitar that chops the same short figure over and over. Horns are nearly absent. Midway Brown asks the band to change section and they move to a new chord and back again, the only harmonic event in the track. The subject is the dance floor and desire, handled with a wink.","In March 1970 most of Brown's band walked out over pay and conditions. Within days he had hired a young Cincinnati group led by the brothers William 'Bootsy' and Phelps 'Catfish' Collins, then still in their teens, and renamed them the J.B.'s. This was one of the first records made with them, in Nashville that spring, with John 'Jabo' Starks on drums. It was released as a two-part single and later recut as a pseudo-live track.","Listen to Bootsy Collins's bass: it barely repeats itself, bubbling and sliding under the static guitar, and that restless line is what the young players brought that the old band did not. Brown's question to the band about going to the bridge, and Byrd's answers, are not lyrics so much as stage directions, a live rehearsal captured on tape and turned into the hook.","It was a big R&B and pop hit and has become one of Brown's signature pieces. Critics tend to rank it just behind Cold Sweat as a funk touchstone, and more than a few prefer its lighter, bouncier feel. Bootsy's short stay with Brown, only about a year, is now seen as the bridge that carried Brown's discipline into the looser world of Parliament-Funkadelic.","Follow the bass alone for one play, then the call-and-response between Brown and Byrd. Count how long the band stays on one chord. Then play Parliament's Mothership Connection and hear Bootsy and the Brown horn players, a few years later, let the same groove sprawl."],"james-brown-funky-president":["A mid-tempo, heavy-footed groove with a hard, dry drum sound, bright horn punctuations and Brown talking more than singing. The track is a state-of-the-nation address in funk form: hard times, rising prices, and a new man in the White House. Brown's advice is practical rather than angry, urging listeners to pool money, buy land and build businesses. The drums open the track bare, and that clean, weighty pattern is what later ears seized on.","It was released on Brown's 1974 album Reality, months after Gerald Ford replaced Richard Nixon, during recession and oil shock. Brown was by then a figure in national politics as well as music, courted by politicians of both parties and criticised for it. The early seventies were his most prolific funk years, working with the trombonist and arranger Fred Wesley, as disco was about to change what dance floors wanted.","Listen to the opening drum pattern on its own: short, clean, with space around each hit. That is why it was sampled for Eric B. and Rakim's debut single Eric B. Is President, whose title is itself a nod to this record. The economic message also echoes Say It Loud: pride expressed as ownership and self-help rather than protest.","It was a solid R&B hit but little noticed by the white press at the time, when Brown was often treated as a spent force. Its reputation grew with hip-hop, which mined its drums repeatedly. Critics now read it alongside Gil Scott-Heron and Curtis Mayfield as a sharp piece of mid-seventies social commentary, though some find the politics muddled.","Listen to the first few seconds several times and then play Eric B. Is President to hear what the producers kept and what they threw away. Then return to the whole track and follow Brown's talk as a sermon over a groove, not a song with verses."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-everyday-people":["A two-and-a-half-minute pop song that sounds like a nursery rhyme and is about prejudice. The band's voices, male and female, take turns and then join in unison over a bouncing piano and a steady beat. The song lists the small differences that divide people, long hair and short, rich and poor, and shrugs them off with a playground chant. Its tone is warm and teasing rather than preachy, which is exactly why it carried so far.","Released in late 1968, the year of the King and Kennedy assassinations and of riots in American cities, it reached number one on the pop chart early in 1969. Sly and the Family Stone were themselves the argument: a San Francisco band of Black and white musicians, men and women, which was still rare enough in 1968 to be a statement. It preceded the album Stand!, released that spring.","Listen to Larry Graham's bass: for most of the song it plays essentially one note, over and over, while the chords shift above it. It is a deliberately simple part that keeps the song grounded. The song also popularised a common American phrase about different strokes for different people, a saying that passed into everyday speech from this record.","It was the band's first number one and has hardly left the air since, used in advertising, children's television and campaigns. Some critics find it slight next to the band's heavier funk, but most admire the craft: an argument for tolerance with no sanctimony. It was reworked in 1992 by Arrested Development, keeping the spirit and changing the sound.","Listen for the order in which the voices enter and how they change each time. Notice the bass barely moving. Then play Stand! as a whole album to hear how the same band could be both this sunny and much tougher."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-stand":["Eight tracks that fuse soul vocals, rock guitar, gospel and the new funk rhythm into one sound. The title song is a short anthem of defiance; I Want to Take You Higher is an explosion of shouted voices and fuzz guitar; Everyday People is sunny pop. Side two ends with a thirteen-minute instrumental jam in which Sly sings through a wah-wah effect. The voices constantly swap lead, so the album sounds like a crowd rather than a star with a backing band.","Sly Stone was a former San Francisco radio DJ and producer who had built his band as a model of integration. Stand! came out in May 1969, a few months before the band's triumphant set at Woodstock, and made them stars with rock audiences as well as soul listeners. Its politics, about race, self-belief and mutual suspicion, reflected a year of assassinations, Black Power and the counterculture colliding.","The title track ends with a sudden, much slower gospel coda, and it sounds different because it was reportedly recorded later with outside session musicians rather than the whole band. Listen too for Cynthia Robinson's trumpet cries and ad-libs: she was a woman playing lead horn in a funk band in 1969, and her voice is one of the band's signatures.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and is often called their most complete statement, the moment their optimism and their anger were in balance. A few critics find the long jam self-indulgent; most hear it as a seed of later funk and hip-hop. Its fusion of rock and soul shaped Miles Davis, the Temptations' producer Norman Whitfield, George Clinton and, later, Prince.","Start with I Want to Take You Higher, loud, then go back to the start and take the album in order. Listen for how often the lead voice changes hands. Then play Jimi Hendrix's Electric Ladyland and the Temptations' Cloud Nine to hear the same rock-soul border crossed from each side."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-thank-you":["A lean, mid-tempo groove that feels both celebratory and weary. The guitar chops a clipped rhythm, the horns punctuate, and the band's voices sing a gratitude chorus with a slightly sardonic edge. The star is the bass: Larry Graham hits the strings with his thumb and pops them with his fingers, making the bass sound half like a drum. The misspelled title, written as it is sung, announces a band having fun with language.","Released at the end of 1969 as a single, it went to number one early in 1970, with Everybody Is a Star on the other side. It did not appear on a regular studio album at the time. The band were at the height of their fame after Woodstock, but Sly's growing drug use, pressure from Black activists and record-company demands were pulling the group apart.","The verses name-check the band's own earlier hits, turning the song into a wry look back at their success. Two years later Sly remade it as the slow, druggy Thank You for Talkin' to Me Africa on There's a Riot Goin' On, the same song drained of energy. Listen to Graham's bass technique, which he developed to cover missing drums in his mother's church band: it is one of the first recorded examples of slap bass.","Hugely popular at the time, it became a key text for bassists: slap bass was taken up across funk, disco and later rock, so Graham's playing here is often called one of the most influential bass parts on record. Janet Jackson sampled it for Rhythm Nation in 1989. Critics hear its mixed mood as the band's turn from optimism.","Follow the bass line from start to finish and notice when it slaps and when it glides. Then play Thank You for Talkin' to Me Africa from There's a Riot Goin' On to hear the same song seen through a darker lens."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-theres-a-riot-goin-on":["The sound of a party ending. The grooves are slower, the textures murky and dry, the voices slurred and half-buried. Family Affair, the hit single, is a quiet, eerie song built on a primitive drum machine and a sinuous electric piano. Elsewhere the funk is heavy and stripped down, and a long slow remake of the band's own Thank You closes the record. It is funk turned inward: claustrophobic, ambivalent, full of mood rather than slogans.","Two years after Stand! and Woodstock, Sly was holed up in Los Angeles, using heavy drugs, missing concerts, and under pressure from the label for new music. The band was fragmenting; much of the album was made by Sly alone, overdubbing parts at home and in the studio. It came out in November 1971, after the killings at Kent State and Jackson State, and as the optimism of the late 1960s curdled.","The title track is listed on the sleeve as lasting no time at all: it is silent, a joke and a statement, perhaps an answer to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On from earlier that year. The muddy sound is partly the result of Sly recording over the same tapes again and again until they wore. Listen for the Maestro Rhythm King drum machine ticking under Family Affair, one of the first uses of a drum box on a hit record.","Reviewers were baffled and some hostile, yet it went to number one on the album chart. Its reputation has only grown: it is now routinely named among the greatest albums of its era and a source for everything from Miles Davis's On the Corner to D'Angelo's Voodoo. The debate is whether its darkness is artistic vision or the sound of a collapse.","Listen at night, on headphones, from beginning to end. Start by noticing the drum machine on Family Affair, then compare the closing Thank You remake with the original single. Then play What's Going On from the same year: two very different answers to the same American moment."],"parliament-mothership-connection":["Seven tracks of spacious, slow-rolling funk presented as a broadcast from outer space. George Clinton plays a cosmic DJ, Star Child, arriving to bring the funk back to Earth. The grooves are thick with interlocking parts: Bootsy Collins's elastic bass, Bernie Worrell's synthesizers, a horn section led by former James Brown players, and a dozen voices chanting and joking. Give Up the Funk, with its crowd chant about tearing the roof off, is the best-known track.","Clinton ran two overlapping bands, Parliament and Funkadelic, with shared personnel and separate record deals. Parliament signed to Casablanca, the Los Angeles label that was also building disco. For this album he brought in the horn players Maceo Parker and Fred Wesley from James Brown's band. Released in December 1975, it was followed by a tour featuring a giant spaceship prop that descended onto the stage.","The title track borrows a line and melody from the spiritual Swing Low, Sweet Chariot, turning the chariot that carries souls home into a spaceship: an Afrofuturist image of deliverance and a link between Black sacred song and science fiction. Listen for Worrell's synthesizer in the background, bending and swooping like a theremin in a science-fiction film.","It was Parliament's first gold album and made P-Funk a mass phenomenon. Critics at the time found it silly; the fashion now is to read its science-fiction mythology as a serious strand of Afrofuturism, next to Sun Ra. It was heavily sampled in the late 1980s and early 1990s, from De La Soul to Dr. Dre's G-funk.","Start with Give Up the Funk and listen to how many separate parts are playing without cluttering. Then go back to the title track, and when you hear the spiritual, play the Fisk Jubilee Singers' Swing Low, Sweet Chariot. Finish with Sun Ra's Space Is the Place."],"parliament-flash-light":["A slow, heavy funk track whose bass line is played not on a bass guitar but on synthesizers. Bernie Worrell's Minimoog lines bounce and wobble low in the mix, stacked several at once, under a chanting chorus that trades nonsense syllables and a group sing-along. The drums are simple and steady, the guitar sparse; the energy comes from the synthetic bass and the chants passing between voices.","It appeared on the album Funkentelechy vs. the Placebo Syndrome, released in late 1977, part of Clinton's mythology in which Star Child defeats the villain Sir Nose, who refuses to dance. Issued as a single, it topped the R&B chart in 1978, knocking disco aside for a while. Synthesizers were becoming affordable, and this record showed how they could replace instruments rather than decorate them.","The bass line is built from several separately recorded Minimoog parts layered together, which gives it that thick, slightly unstable wobble no single instrument could produce. Bootsy Collins, normally the bassist, is reported to have played drums. Listen for the chanting in the chorus, which has almost no words, just rhythm and sound.","It became one of the most influential synthesizer bass lines ever recorded, a direct ancestor of 1980s electro and of the whining synth bass of West Coast G-funk. Critics now credit Worrell as one of the great under-recognised keyboard innovators of the era, a conservatory-trained pianist who brought classical technique and a sense of orchestral colour into funk.","Listen through headphones and try to separate the layered Moog lines, some low and round, some buzzing. Notice how little the drums do by comparison. Then play Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock and hear the same idea, electronic bass driving a dance record, taken a step further toward the machine."],"funkadelic-maggot-brain":["The album opens with its most famous track: a ten-minute guitar solo by Eddie Hazel, slow, grieving and increasingly ecstatic, over a quiet, repeated figure. After Clinton's brief spoken introduction the rest of the band drop almost out of hearing. The other six tracks move between gospel-inflected soul, hard rock riffing and chaotic, noise-laden psychedelia, ending with the sprawling Wars of Armageddon. It is funk at its most rock-minded and most experimental.","Funkadelic were George Clinton's Detroit-based band, recording for the small Westbound label. They were steeped in Hendrix and Sly Stone and played loud to both Black and white crowds. Released in 1971, the album came out as the counterculture fractured and the Vietnam War dragged on. The cover photograph, a woman's head apparently buried in soil and screaming, set a tone of apocalyptic dread.","Clinton later said he told Hazel to play as if he had just learned his mother had died, and then as if he discovered she was alive. The track was reportedly recorded with the full band, whose parts were then pulled down in the mix to leave Hazel almost alone. Listen for the echo and phasing on the guitar, which makes one player sound like an entire choir.","It sold modestly, and critics at the time were divided by its mix of genres. The title track has since become a touchstone for guitarists, often named among the greatest solos recorded, and the album is now regarded as the peak of Funkadelic's early work. Some find the second half uneven next to its opening.","Listen to the title track in a dark room, start to finish, without interruption, and follow how Hazel's phrases grow from sighs into cries. Then play Can You Get to That for the gospel side of the band. Afterwards try Hendrix's Electric Ladyland and Frank Zappa's Hot Rats, and ask how Hazel's lyricism differs from their virtuosity."],"funkadelic-one-nation-under-a-groove":["Funkadelic at its most polished and accessible. The title track is a seven-minute mid-tempo groove, with a gentle chant about a dance floor as a united nation and synthesizer lines weaving through the choir of voices. The rest moves from comic funk to heavy guitar rock, including a track whose title openly asks who says a funk band cannot play rock. It is funk as community, a sprawling party record with a utopian streak.","By 1978 Funkadelic had moved to Warner Bros. and was in competition with Clinton's other band, Parliament, and with disco, which dominated the charts. Walter 'Junie' Morrison, formerly of the Ohio Players, became a key writer and keyboard player. Released that autumn, the title track spent six weeks at number one on the R&B chart and the album became the band's biggest seller.","Original copies came with a bonus seven-inch record of extra tracks, including a live version of Maggot Brain: the band glancing back at its earlier self. Listen for Morrison's keyboard parts, which carry much of the title song's melodic work, a real shift from the guitar-driven sound of the early Funkadelic records.","It was praised on release and is widely regarded as Funkadelic's best album, or at least its most complete. Critics note how it answers disco without surrendering to it, keeping the groove danceable but loose and human. It has been sampled throughout hip-hop, and its title has passed into common use as shorthand for the ideal of the dance floor as a place of unity.","Start with the title track and listen to how many voices share the chant, then to the synthesizer threading through them. Then go back to Maggot Brain and hear how far the band travelled in seven years. Finish with Chic's Good Times, from the following year, to compare funk's sprawling unity with disco's cool elegance."],"earth-wind-and-fire-thats-the-way-of-the-world":["Eight tracks of bright, disciplined, jazz-inflected soul and funk. Shining Star is a tight, punchy funk song built on unison vocals and stabbing horns; the title track is a slow, warm anthem of optimism; Reasons is a falsetto ballad that shows off Philip Bailey's soaring voice. Throughout, the horns are arranged with jazz-like precision, the rhythm section is crisp, and Maurice White's kalimba, an African thumb piano, adds shimmering colour.","The album was recorded as the soundtrack to a film of the same name, about the music industry, starring Harvey Keitel. The film flopped; the album, released in March 1975 by Columbia, went to number one, and Shining Star topped the singles chart. The arranger Charles Stepney, from Chicago, helped shape the band's sophisticated sound, before his early death the next year.","Listen for the kalimba, often tucked behind the horns: White used it on stage and on record as a symbol of the band's African and spiritual aspirations. Shining Star ends with its title sung a cappella, the band suddenly naked after the full funk arrangement. The album was released well before the film, so most listeners never connected it with the movie at all.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and remains its most admired album, and Shining Star won a Grammy. Critics praised the craftsmanship; a few found the relentless positivity bland beside grittier, angrier funk. Over time the band's precision and spiritual aspiration have made them a touchstone for later soul and R&B producers, and the album is a staple of lists of the decade's best.","Start with Shining Star and listen for the horns hitting exactly with the voices. Then play Reasons and follow Bailey's falsetto as it climbs. Listen for the kalimba glinting in the background. Then compare Parliament's Mothership Connection, also from 1975: Earth, Wind & Fire's cosmos is spiritual and sleek, Parliament's comic and gloriously chaotic."],"earth-wind-and-fire-september":["Three and a half minutes of pure uplift. A bright guitar figure, a bouncing bass and swinging horns carry a song of remembered love and dancing, sung in a mix of Maurice White's warm lead and Philip Bailey's falsetto. The chorus is built on a nonsense syllable, repeated and harmonised, that anyone can sing. The mood is so relentlessly joyous that it has become a default soundtrack for celebrations.","Recorded in 1978 for the compilation The Best of Earth, Wind & Fire, Vol. 1, it was released as a single that autumn and became a major international hit. White wrote it with the band's guitarist Al McKay and the songwriter Allee Willis. Disco was at its peak, and the song's four-on-the-floor bounce and horn arrangements sit on the border between funk and disco.","Willis wanted to replace the nonsense syllable in the chorus with a real word; White refused, telling her, as she recalled, never to let the lyrics get in the way of the groove. The song names a date in September, and that day is now marked every year online as a kind of informal holiday. Listen to how the horns answer the vocals, almost like a second chorus.","It was a hit on release and has become one of the most streamed and licensed songs of its era, a fixture of weddings, films and sports arenas. Critics once dismissed it as lightweight; now many admire it as a perfectly engineered piece of joy. Some find its ubiquity exhausting, but few deny its craft.","Listen for the moment the horns first enter, then sing the chorus. Then play That's the Way of the World to hear the same band in a more serious mood, and Chic's Le Freak to hear another route from funk to disco."],"the-meters-cissy-strut":["A three-minute instrumental played by a four-piece band, with space as the main ingredient. The guitar and bass play a short, angular riff in unison, the organ fills in above, and the drums skip and syncopate around them with a looseness that comes straight from New Orleans street parades. Nothing is crowded; each player leaves gaps for the others. The result feels both lazy and precise, a groove that seems to walk rather than run.","The Meters were the house band for the New Orleans producer Allen Toussaint, playing on records by Lee Dorsey, Betty Harris and others. In 1969 they began releasing instrumentals under their own name on the Josie label. New Orleans had its own tradition of parade drumming and piano rhythm-and-blues, and the Meters brought these into funk at the same time as James Brown was developing his own version in Cincinnati.","Listen to Joseph 'Zigaboo' Modeliste's drumming: he often plays against the beat rather than on it, echoing the second-line rhythms of New Orleans brass bands. That rhythmic looseness is what makes the Meters sound different from Brown's tight band. The same unison riff structure, guitar and bass doubling each other, would become a funk staple.","It was a modest hit but made the Meters' reputation among musicians. Their records have since been sampled extensively in hip-hop, and they are regarded as one of the essential funk bands, sometimes called the originators of New Orleans funk. Many critics consider them underrated compared with Brown and Sly.","Listen to the drums first, and try to count along: notice where the accents fall. Then play Cold Sweat to compare Brown's tight version of funk with the Meters' looser one, and Fats Domino's The Fat Man for the New Orleans piano rhythm behind both."],"the-meters-rejuvenation":["The Meters with voices, a fuller sound and longer grooves. The album moves between tight, singing funk such as People Say and Hey Pocky A-Way, whose chant comes from Mardi Gras Indian tradition, and the eleven-minute It Ain't No Use, a slow-burning jam with extended guitar work. The drumming is still loose and syncopated, but the arrangements are richer and the songs have hooks. It feels like a band at the peak of its confidence.","By 1974 the Meters had moved to Reprise, a Warner Bros. label, and were recording with Allen Toussaint producing at his new Sea-Saint studio in New Orleans. They had backed Dr. John and Labelle and were soon opening for the Rolling Stones. The album came out as funk reached its commercial peak and disco was emerging, and the band hoped at last for a national hit of their own.","Hey Pocky A-Way draws on the chants of the Mardi Gras Indians, the Black carnival societies of New Orleans, and the Meters' Art and Cyril Neville had family ties to that tradition. Listen for the percussion that comes straight from parade music, and for the clavinet, a keyboard with a sharp, percussive tone that was central to 1970s funk.","Critics praised it on release, but it sold modestly, and the band never broke through nationally as its peers did. It is now frequently named the Meters' best album and one of the essential funk records. Some writers consider the Meters the most musically subtle of all the funk bands, and their influence is heard in New Orleans brass bands and jam bands to this day.","Start with Hey Pocky A-Way and listen to the percussion bubbling under the chant. Then give yourself eleven minutes for It Ain't No Use and let the long groove settle. Afterwards go back to Cissy Strut to hear how much the band's sound grew in five years, from four players and a riff to a full, singing band."],"chic-le-freak":["A sleek disco record with a funk band underneath. Nile Rodgers plays a tight, chiming guitar figure, Bernard Edwards a supple bass line, Tony Thompson steady drums, and strings glide above them. The chorus is a chant naming a new dance, sung by the band's vocalists in cool, precise unison. It is elegant rather than sweaty, with every part placed exactly.","Rodgers and Edwards wrote it after being turned away from Studio 54 in New York on New Year's Eve 1977, despite an invitation. They went home and wrote a riff with a much ruder chorus aimed at the club's door policy, then softened it into a dance-craze title. Released that autumn on Atlantic, it became the label's biggest-selling single to that date.","The song began as an angry reaction to the club's exclusivity, and it still carries a trace of that in its cool distance. Listen for Rodgers's guitar playing: the rhythmic 'chucking' technique, choked chords played in short bursts, would become his signature, later heard on records by David Bowie and Daft Punk.","It was a huge hit, topping the American chart, and remains a disco standard. Rock critics at the time often dismissed disco as faceless, but Chic's musicianship earned respect even then. It is now seen as one of disco's finest records and a model of how to make a dance record that is both simple and sophisticated, and its origin story has become part of disco legend.","Listen to the guitar and bass together first, then notice how the strings float above without crowding them. Count how few chords the song uses. Then play Good Times to hear Chic strip the formula down even further, and James Brown's Papa's Got a Brand New Bag to hear where that choked rhythm guitar came from."],"chic-good-times":["Eight minutes of cool, understated groove. Bernard Edwards's bass line walks and bounces through the song, carrying it; Nile Rodgers's guitar chops sparely above; strings and piano add colour. The vocals, sung in a detached, almost ironic tone, celebrate carefree fun. The track is built on repetition, with the bass line and the guitar pattern cycling almost unchanged, and a long instrumental break in the middle.","Released in June 1979 as the lead single from the album Risqué, it became Chic's second American number one. The country was sliding toward recession, with petrol queues and high inflation, so the song's celebration of good times carried an ironic undertone. Disco was at its commercial height and about to face a furious backlash, symbolised by the Disco Demolition Night riot at a Chicago baseball stadium that July.","The lyrics borrow phrases from Depression-era songs, a knowing nod to an earlier time of hardship masked by cheerful music. Within months the bass line was replayed by the Sugar Hill house band as the backing for Rapper's Delight, the first hip-hop hit; Rodgers and Edwards threatened legal action and won songwriting credit. Queen's Another One Bites the Dust, from 1980, is widely heard as borrowing its bass line.","It was a hit and has become one of the most influential dance records ever made. Critics now regard it as Chic's finest moment and its bass line as one of the most borrowed in pop. It is also a key text in debates over borrowing and credit, since the first rap hit was built on it before anyone had agreed the rules for such reuse.","Follow the bass line all the way through, and notice how it changes slightly each time while the guitar barely moves. Then play Rapper's Delight, where a studio band replays it for fifteen minutes under three rappers, and Grandmaster Flash's Adventures on the Wheels of Steel, where the original record is cut and scratched live on turntables."],"donna-summer-love-to-love-you-baby":["A seventeen-minute disco track built on a slow, steady groove and Donna Summer's breathy, intimate vocal. Strings swell and fall back, the rhythm section keeps a relaxed pulse, and the song drifts through repeated sections rather than following a verse-and-chorus plan. Its sensual tone is its defining feature, handled as mood and texture rather than explicit statement. It is disco as extended atmosphere.","Summer, an American singer living in Munich, recorded it with the producers Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte. A short version was released first in Europe. Neil Bogart of Casablanca Records in Los Angeles reportedly asked for a much longer version to play at parties, and the seventeen-minute track filled one side of the album. Released in late 1975, it became an international hit.","The long version was a new format in itself: a single piece that took up a whole side of a record, designed for dance floors where DJs needed long tracks. Listen for how little changes over seventeen minutes: the art is in the gradual build and release, a technique that would shape club music for decades.","It was a hit and a controversy, with some radio stations reluctant to play it because of its sensual tone. Many critics dismissed it as a novelty or a gimmick. Later writers see it as a pioneering extended dance track, the start of Summer and Moroder's partnership, and an early sign that Summer was a far more controlled and versatile singer than its reputation suggested.","Give it the full seventeen minutes, as intended, ideally in a dim room. Notice how the strings and rhythm change only slowly, and how the tension builds and releases. Then play I Feel Love, from two years later, to hear the same team replace the band with machines, and Isaac Hayes's Hot Buttered Soul for an earlier long slow burn."],"donna-summer-i-feel-love":["A pulsing electronic track built almost entirely on a Moog synthesizer. A rapid, repeating bass line, doubled by an echo, drives the song forward; shimmering synthesizer lines rise above it; and Donna Summer's voice floats high and airy over the machine. There is no chorus in the conventional sense, only a hypnotic cycle. It sounds futuristic even now: cold, sensual and relentless.","It was made at Musicland Studios in Munich in 1977 by Summer with the producers Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte, for I Remember Yesterday, a concept album whose songs moved through the decades of popular music; this closing track stood for the future. Everything except the kick drum was played on a Moog modular synthesizer, a room-sized instrument few pop producers dared use. Released as a single that summer, it became an international hit.","Keeping the Moog in time with the recording tape was a real technical problem; the studio's engineer devised a way to lock it to a click track, making the machine's pulse perfectly steady. The kick drum was played by a human drummer, Keith Forsey. By a much-repeated account, Brian Eno heard it and told David Bowie it was the sound of the future.","It was a hit and has come to be seen as one of the most important records ever made, the ancestor of electronic dance music, house, techno and synth-pop. Critics once treated disco as disposable; this record helped change that view. Some debate how much credit belongs to Summer and how much to Moroder, but most now emphasise the collaboration.","Listen on headphones and follow the bass line and its echo, which together make a gallop no human bassist could sustain. Notice how Summer's voice seems to float rather than drive. Then play Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express, from the same year, and Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians to hear other kinds of pulsing repetition."],"donna-summer-bad-girls":["A double album that moves from hard-edged disco to rock and ballads. Hot Stuff opens with a guitar riff and a rock beat; the title track is a brassy, driving disco number with whistles and chants; Dim All the Lights builds from a slow ballad to a fast disco climax. The album closes with synthesizer-heavy tracks in the style of I Feel Love. Summer's voice ranges from soft to powerful, proving her as a singer, not just a disco figure.","Released in April 1979 on Casablanca, at disco's peak and just before its backlash, it was Summer's most successful album, spending weeks at number one in America. It was made in Los Angeles with Moroder and Bellotte. Summer wrote the title song herself, about women working the streets; the record company reportedly considered giving it to another singer before she recorded it.","Dim All the Lights was written by Summer alone. Listen for the long note she holds at its turning point, one of the most famous sustained notes in pop. Hot Stuff's guitar solo is by Jeff Baxter, formerly of Steely Dan and the Doobie Brothers, a sign of the album's reach toward rock radio.","It was praised on release and is widely regarded as Summer's best album, the point where she became a full pop star rather than a disco voice. Critics admired its range and its rock crossover, and Hot Stuff won her a Grammy in a rock category. Some have debated the title track's handling of its subject; most now hear it as sympathetic rather than exploitative.","Start with Hot Stuff and hear how the rock guitar sits on a disco beat. Then play Dim All the Lights and wait for the long held note at its turning point. Finish with the synthesizer tracks at the end of the album, and then I Feel Love, to hear where their sound came from."],"giorgio-moroder-from-here-to-eternity":["A short album of synthesizer disco whose first side runs as one continuous suite, each song flowing into the next without a break for the DJ to cover. Sequenced bass lines pulse underneath, bright synthesizer melodies ride on top, and Moroder's own voice comes through a vocoder, flattened into something robotic. There are no strings, no horns and almost no guitar. It is disco remade from electronic sound, cool, clean and relentlessly steady.","Moroder, a South Tyrolean producer based in Munich, made it in 1977, the same year as I Feel Love, while synthesizers were moving from university studios and progressive rock into pop. Released on Casablanca, it found its audience mainly in European clubs. Moroder was already Donna Summer's producer and would soon win an Academy Award for his score to Alan Parker's film Midnight Express.","The sleeve carries a note announcing that only electronic keyboards were used on the recording, a boast at a time when synthesizers were widely treated as novelties or sound effects. Listen for the vocoder on Moroder's voice, and for the way the suite's seams are hidden: the album was designed to be played in clubs as a single unbroken piece.","It was a modest seller and is now valued mainly as a pioneering document, an early statement that dance music could be made without a band. Critics sometimes find it thin and a little mechanical next to I Feel Love, where Summer's voice gives the machine a heart, but its influence on synth-pop, Italo disco and electronic dance music is widely acknowledged.","Play side one straight through as one piece and notice the moments where a song changes without the beat ever stopping. Listen for the vocoder voice. Then play I Feel Love to hear the same machinery with a great singer on top, and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express for German electronic music with a very different temperament."],"prince-dirty-mind":["Thirty minutes of lean, punky funk, played almost entirely by Prince himself. The sound is spare and raw: clipped guitar, bright synthesizer lines, a drum machine or simple kit, and Prince's voice leaping from falsetto to growl. Songs are short and fast, combining funk rhythm with new-wave energy. The subject matter is frankly sexual, handled with provocation and humour, and the album closes with a call for unity.","Prince was twenty-two and based in Minneapolis, with two albums behind him. He made this one largely alone in a home studio, and Warner Bros. released the demos with little change in October 1980. It came out as disco was collapsing and new wave was rising, and Prince was looking for a sound that would cross the line between Black and white radio.","Many of the tracks were originally demos, and the rawness is the result: Prince decided the rough versions had more energy than polished remakes. Listen for the synthesizer lines, which take the place of horns in a traditional funk band. This new sound, spare and synth-driven, would be called the Minneapolis sound.","It was praised by rock critics, some of whom compared its brazenness to punk, but sold only modestly. Its sexual frankness was controversial, and some radio stations would not play it. It is now seen as the album where Prince found his own voice and as the founding record of the Minneapolis sound, the template for the Time, Vanity 6 and much 1980s pop-funk.","Listen to the whole album in one go; at thirty minutes it is over quickly. Notice the synthesizers standing in for horns and the way the guitar plays tight funk rather than rock solos. Start with When You Were Mine and Uptown. Then play 1999 to hear how Prince stretched the same sound into long grooves."],"prince-1999":["A double album of long, synthesizer-driven funk grooves. The title track is a party song about celebrating in the face of nuclear fear; Little Red Corvette is a pop-rock hit; Delirious is a rockabilly-funk romp. Many tracks stretch to seven or nine minutes, built on a Linn drum machine and layered synthesizers. The sound is bright, mechanical and sensual, the grooves hypnotic.","Released in October 1982 on Warner Bros., it was Prince's commercial breakthrough, helped when MTV, then criticised for ignoring Black artists, put the video for Little Red Corvette into regular rotation. Prince recorded much of it alone in Minneapolis and Los Angeles, playing most instruments himself. The Reagan-era arms race and a renewed fear of nuclear war were in the air, and the title song turns that dread into a party.","On the cover, the words 'and the Revolution' are written backwards inside the lettering of the title, the first hint of the band that would be fully credited on Purple Rain. Listen for the Linn LM-1 drum machine, whose crisp, dry claps and snares give the album its synthetic snap, and for the several voices trading lines in the title song, perhaps a nod to Sly and the Family Stone.","It was a big hit, and critics praised its ambition even as some found the double-album length indulgent. It is now seen as one of the defining albums of the 1980s and a template for the fusion of funk, pop and electronic sound. Its title song gained a second life as the millennium approached, when it became the obvious soundtrack for New Year's Eve 1999.","Start with the title track, then Little Red Corvette, and notice how the drum machine and synthesizers carry the groove. Then give time to one of the long tracks such as Automatic or Lady Cab Driver. Afterwards go back to Dirty Mind to hear the sound in its lean first form, and Donna Summer's I Feel Love for the machine pulse it builds on."],"prince-sign-o-the-times":["An eighty-minute double album that ranges across funk, soul, rock, pop, gospel and psychedelia. The title track is a spare drum-machine piece about social problems; If I Was Your Girlfriend explores gender and intimacy through a sped-up voice; The Cross is a gospel-rock hymn; Adore is a lush soul ballad. It is Prince's most wide-ranging album, a panorama of American music.","Prince disbanded the Revolution in 1986 and was working on several projects at once, including an album called Dream Factory, a record credited to an alter ego named Camille, and a triple album called Crystal Ball. Warner Bros. refused to release a triple; the double album that came out in March 1987 drew from all of them. It arrived amid the crack epidemic and the AIDS crisis, which the title song names directly.","Several songs were meant for Camille, a character sung with the tape sped up so Prince's voice sounds higher and androgynous. Listen for that voice on If I Was Your Girlfriend, where it deepens the song's play with gender. The title track, by contrast, is almost bare: a drum machine, a synthesizer bass line and short guitar interjections, the social report delivered with no decoration at all.","Critics hailed it as Prince's finest album, and it regularly appears near the top of lists of the greatest albums of the 1980s. It sold less than Purple Rain, and the accompanying concert film did poorly in America. Debate now centres on the vaults: the abandoned projects it came from have been partly released, and fans still argue which version would have been best.","Start with the title track and notice how little is playing. Then hear If I Was Your Girlfriend, Housequake for pure funk, and The Cross, which builds from a quiet folk-like chant to a roaring gospel-rock climax. Then play Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life, another sprawling double album that tries to hold all of Black American music."],"rick-james-street-songs":["An album of hard-hitting funk that its maker called punk funk. Super Freak is built on a fat, strutting bass line and stabbing synthesizers; Give It to Me Baby rides a thumping groove with horns shouting back; Ghetto Life sketches James's Buffalo childhood; Fire and Desire is a long, slow duet with Teena Marie. The sound is punchy and loud, horns and synthesizers jostling over a heavy rhythm section, and James's voice swaggers throughout.","Rick James, from Buffalo, New York, had signed to Motown in the late 1970s and was already a star. Street Songs, released in April 1981 on Motown's Gordy label with his Stone City Band, was his biggest success, topping the R&B album chart. It came out as disco's collapse left funk briefly dominant again, just before drum machines and MTV reshaped Black pop, and James would soon attack MTV publicly for ignoring Black artists.","Super Freak's bass line had a second life in 1990, when MC Hammer built U Can't Touch This on it; James sued and won a share of the credit. Listen closely to Fire and Desire, where Teena Marie, a white singer he produced and championed at Motown, matches him phrase for phrase: it is as much a contest as a love song.","It was a big hit and is widely regarded as James's best album. Critics praised its energy, though some found its boasting and swagger crude, and his later troubles have shadowed his reputation. It is now seen as a key record of early-1980s funk, a bridge between the P-Funk era and the electro and hip-hop that followed.","Start with Give It to Me Baby and listen to the horns answering the bass. Then Super Freak, and notice how simple the bass line is. Then hear Fire and Desire as a duet between equals. Afterwards play Prince's Dirty Mind to hear a leaner, stranger approach to the same punk-funk idea."],"betty-davis-they-say-im-different":["Eight tracks of raw, heavy funk with a hard-rock edge. Betty Davis's voice growls, rasps, shrieks and purrs over grinding, distorted guitars, a thick, prowling bass and drums that hit hard and dry. The songs are bold and sexually confident, told from the point of view of a woman who sets the terms, and the sound is deliberately rough rather than polished. The title track slows down to pay tribute to the blues singers she counted as ancestors.","Betty Davis, born Betty Mabry, had been married briefly to Miles Davis at the end of the 1960s and introduced him to the music of Jimi Hendrix and Sly Stone, nudging him toward the electric sound of Bitches Brew. This, her second album, was released in 1974 on the small Just Sunshine label. Her frankness about desire was too much for many radio stations of the day, and some shows were met with protests.","Davis largely produced the album herself, which was very rare for a woman in 1974, and she wrote every song. Listen to the title track's roll call of blues figures: she places her own shocking funk in a line of descent from earlier Black women and men who were also called too loud and too much. Her voice is often mixed as another rhythm instrument rather than floating above the band.","It sold poorly and was largely ignored or disapproved of at the time, and Davis had left music entirely by the end of the decade. Reissues from the 2000s onward transformed her reputation. She is now seen as a pioneer of funk-rock and of a woman's self-possession in pop, and a documentary and a stream of admiring younger musicians have given her the recognition she missed.","Start with the title track, then turn up something harder such as Shoo-B-Doop and Cop Him and notice how her voice attacks the beat. Then play Funkadelic's Maggot Brain and Sly and the Family Stone's There's a Riot Goin' On, the two records she sits most comfortably beside."],"michael-jackson-thriller":["Nine songs in forty-two minutes, and seven of them became American top-ten singles. The record moves from the churning Afro-disco of Wanna Be Startin’ Somethin’ through the horror-film camp of the title track, with Vincent Price’s spoken coda, to the hard-rock guitar of Beat It and the taut, minimal groove of Billie Jean. What binds it is Jackson’s voice used as percussion — hiccups, gasps and clipped yelps sitting exactly in the pocket of the beat — and Quincy Jones’s spotless, layered production.","Jackson was twenty-four, already a star after Off the Wall (1979) but determined to make an album with no filler. Sessions ran through 1982 at Westlake Recording Studios in Los Angeles with Jones producing and Bruce Swedien engineering; Rod Temperton wrote the title song, members of Toto played on several tracks and Eddie Van Halen dropped in a guitar solo. The release coincided with the rise of MTV, then playing almost no Black artists, and the Billie Jean video helped break that barrier.","The chant that closes Wanna Be Startin’ Somethin’ is adapted from Manu Dibango’s 1972 Soul Makossa, a Cameroonian makossa hit — a piece of African popular music hiding at the climax of the best-selling album ever made; the borrowing was later settled out of court. Listen too for how many songs fade on a vamp rather than ending: Jones built the record for dancing, and the grooves are allowed to keep turning.","It won eight Grammys in one night in 1984 and is generally cited as the best-selling album in history. Critics at the time admired its polish more than its depth, and some rock writers resented a Black pop star taking over rock radio with Beat It. Later judgment has settled on it as the record that reunited a racially segregated pop market; argument now centres on the man rather than the music.","Play Billie Jean, then Human Nature, back to back: one is all tension, the other all floating air, and together they show the album’s range. Then hear the side-one opener at high volume and follow the bass and handclaps. The original vinyl mix is warmer than several loud remasters; Swedien’s later reissues are a fair compromise."],"michael-jackson-billie-jean":["A song built from almost nothing: a dry, ticking drum pattern, a walking bass line that repeats for nearly five minutes, a few stabs of synthesizer and, later, a cushion of strings. Jackson sings in a tight, breathy murmur that swells into cries on the chorus. The story is a paternity accusation told by a narrator who insists the child is not his, and the music never resolves the unease — the groove simply keeps circling while the voice grows more agitated.","Jackson wrote it alone and made a detailed home demo before the Thriller sessions. Released as a single in January 1983, it went to number one in the United States and Britain. Its video, with pavement slabs lighting up under his feet, became one of the first by a Black artist in heavy rotation on MTV. In March 1983 he performed it at the Motown 25 television special and first showed the backward-gliding dance step later called the moonwalk.","The introduction runs for almost half a minute before a word is sung. Quincy Jones wanted it cut; Jackson refused, saying the introduction was what made him want to dance. Jones also reportedly pushed for a different title, worried listeners would think of the tennis player Billie Jean King. Notice how the bass and drum figure never change through the verses: all the drama comes from what is laid on top.","It is regularly ranked among the greatest pop recordings, and engineers still study Bruce Swedien’s drum sound, recorded with a specially built platform and covers to keep each hit separate. Some critics hear in the lyric a paranoid view of women and fame that recurs across Jackson’s work. The Motown 25 performance is often treated as the moment he stopped being a former child star and became the dominant entertainer of the decade.","Listen once only to the bass and once only to the hi-hat — each is a complete piece of music. Then notice where the strings enter and how they darken rather than sweeten the track. Watch the Motown 25 performance afterwards to see how the song was meant to be danced."],"madonna-like-a-prayer":["Madonna’s most personal album opens with its title song, where a chugging rock guitar gives way to a full gospel choir and a church organ, and closes with a track that plays a prayer backwards. Between them sit the brassy dance anthem Express Yourself, the bubbly Cherish, the brooding Oh Father about her father, Till Death Do Us Part about a failing marriage, and Promise to Try, a quiet piano song for her mother, who died when Madonna was five.","She was thirty, newly divorced from Sean Penn and rethinking her Catholic upbringing. Co-written and produced with Patrick Leonard and Stephen Bray, the album featured the Andraé Crouch choir on the title track and Prince duetting on Love Song. Pepsi had paid for the title song to launch in a television advertisement, but when the video — burning crosses, a Black saint, stigmata — appeared the next day, religious groups protested and Pepsi pulled the campaign.","The first pressings of the album were scented with patchouli oil, meant to evoke the incense of church, and came with an insert of facts about safe sex at the height of the AIDS crisis. Listen to the last seconds of the title song: the choir keeps going after the band drops away, as if the service has outlasted the pop song.","Reviewers who had dismissed Madonna as a clever marketer largely conceded that this was a serious, coherent album; Rolling Stone called it as close to art as pop music gets. The outrage over the video now looks like a case study in how she used controversy as form. Debate continues about whether the confessional songs or the dance tracks are its real centre.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the choir first answers her voice. Then play Promise to Try to hear how bare she was willing to be. Compare the gospel writing with Aretha Franklin’s church recordings to hear what she borrowed and what she changed."],"madonna-ray-of-light":["Thirteen songs that fold trance, ambient washes, trip-hop and acoustic guitar into mainstream pop. The title track is a hurtling rave song with a squealing synthesizer line; Frozen is slow and orchestral, a cold string riff over a crawling beat; Shanti/Ashtangi sets a Sanskrit yoga invocation to a club rhythm; Little Star is a lullaby for her daughter. Madonna’s voice is lower, rounder and steadier than on any earlier record.","Madonna had given birth to her daughter Lourdes in 1996, taken formal vocal lessons for the film of Evita and begun studying Kabbalah and yoga. She turned to the British producer William Orbit, whose home-made electronic records she admired, and the sessions in Los Angeles ran for months as Orbit’s ageing equipment broke down. Released in March 1998 on her own Maverick label, it arrived as electronic dance music was moving into the American mainstream.","The title song is built on Sepheryn, a 1971 folk song by the obscure British duo Curtiss Maldoon, credited on the sleeve — a hippie tune turned into a rave anthem. Drowned World/Substitute for Love samples an easy-listening record by the San Sebastian Strings, a Rod McKuen project. Listen for the gentle crackle and hiss Orbit left in: the machines are meant to sound handmade.","It won four Grammys and was received as a reinvention, the first Madonna album critics discussed chiefly as sound. Some electronic musicians grumbled that she had borrowed an underground style; many more credited her with carrying it to a mass audience. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is often named her best album, though admirers of Like a Prayer dispute that.","Hear Frozen on good headphones for the way the strings and beats occupy separate spaces. Then play the title track loud. Compare with Björk’s Homogenic from the previous year, which uses strings and beats to very different ends. Notice how rarely her voice is doubled or buried; Orbit keeps it dry and close."],"kate-bush-hounds-of-love":["Two halves. Side one, Hounds of Love, holds five songs, among them Running Up That Hill, driven by a booming drum machine and a synthesizer cello figure, and Cloudbusting, powered by marching strings. Side two, The Ninth Wave, is a continuous seven-part suite about a woman adrift at night in freezing water, moving through dream, hallucination, a witch trial and a view of the Earth from space before she is rescued at dawn.","Bush had built a studio at her family home in Kent and took full control of production. The Fairlight sampler, then an expensive novelty, let her play orchestral and found sounds from a keyboard. After the commercial disappointment of The Dreaming, EMI worried; Hounds of Love went to number one in Britain, displacing Madonna’s Like a Virgin. Running Up That Hill’s original title, A Deal with God, was changed at the label’s request.","Cloudbusting is drawn from Peter Reich’s memoir of his father, the psychoanalyst Wilhelm Reich, whose rain-making machine and arrest the song describes. Hello Earth quotes Tsintskaro, a Georgian folk song Bush heard on the soundtrack of Werner Herzog’s Nosferatu, sung here by a male choir. The title song opens with a line of dialogue sampled from the 1957 horror film Night of the Demon.","It is usually treated as her best album and one of the high points of 1980s art pop, with The Ninth Wave compared to a song cycle. In 2022 Running Up That Hill, used in the television series Stranger Things, returned to the top of the British chart thirty-seven years after release — a rare case of a new generation finding a song on its own terms.","Play side two in one sitting, in the dark if possible, as a single piece. Listen for the ice and radio voices in Under Ice and Waking the Witch. Then return to side one and hear how every song uses drums as the lead instrument."],"kate-bush-the-dreaming":["Ten densely packed songs in which Bush sings in a dozen voices — growling, shrieking, cockney, childlike. Sat in Your Lap opens with thunderous tom-toms and brass; There Goes a Tenner is a comic bank robbery in music-hall style; Pull Out the Pin sees the Vietnam War through a Viet Cong soldier’s eyes; Night of the Swallow brings in Irish pipes and fiddle; Houdini imagines the escapologist’s wife; Get Out of My House ends in braying animal noises.","This was the first album Bush produced herself, made over more than a year in several London studios with heavy use of the new Fairlight sampler. She was twenty-four, famous since Wuthering Heights in 1978, and tired of being marketed as a whimsical ingénue. EMI was alarmed by its density, and it sold far less than her earlier records, pushing her to build her own home studio for the next album.","Get Out of My House was inspired by Stephen King’s The Shining, and its layered voices act out a person and a haunted house fighting each other. Listen to how often the drums are the melody: the album is built more from rhythm and texture than from chords. The title song features a didgeridoo.","British reviewers were divided and some called it the sound of an artist losing control. Its reputation has risen sharply; many musicians, Björk among them, have named it a favourite, and it is now often ranked with Hounds of Love. A few of its portrayals of other cultures sit less comfortably today, and listeners still disagree about them.","Start with Sat in Your Lap for the drums, then Houdini for the drama. Expect to be disoriented on first hearing; the second time the shapes emerge. Hear it before Hounds of Love to understand where that album’s methods came from."],"prince-purple-rain":["Nine songs made as the soundtrack to Prince’s semi-autobiographical film. Let’s Go Crazy opens with a mock sermon over church organ before exploding into guitar rock; When Doves Cry is funk stripped to drum machine, keyboards and voice; Darling Nikki is lurid and theatrical; the closing Purple Rain is a slow gospel-rock ballad nearly nine minutes long, ending in a soaring guitar solo and a wordless falsetto cry.","Prince and his band the Revolution were based in Minneapolis, far from the music industry. Warner Bros. backed a film on the strength of his album 1999. Several songs, including the title track, were recorded live at a benefit concert at the First Avenue club in August 1983 and then overdubbed; it was guitarist Wendy Melvoin’s first show with the band. The album spent twenty-four weeks at number one in the United States.","When Doves Cry has no bass line. Prince recorded one, decided the track sounded too conventional, and removed it, leaving a hole at the bottom of the mix that radio had never heard in a hit. Darling Nikki led Tipper Gore to found the Parents Music Resource Center, whose campaign produced the Parental Advisory sticker on American records.","It won an Academy Award for original song score and made Prince a superstar on the scale of Michael Jackson. Critics praised its fusion of rock, funk and gospel; rock purists and funk purists each felt it belonged to the other side. It now appears near the top of most lists of the greatest albums, and the title song became his signature and his farewell anthem.","Play When Doves Cry and listen for the missing bass. Then hear the title track, waiting for the moment the guitar solo gives way to the strings and the falsetto. Watch the film’s concert scenes: it shows how the band played the music live."],"whitney-houston-i-will-always-love-you":["Houston’s recording of Dolly Parton’s 1974 country farewell song opens with her voice completely alone for about three-quarters of a minute. Piano and strings enter quietly, a saxophone solo bridges the middle, and then a single drum hit launches the final chorus, raised a step in key and sung at full power. The song is a gentle goodbye; Houston turns it into a slow, controlled build to one enormous climax.","It was recorded for The Bodyguard (1992), the film in which Houston starred opposite Kevin Costner, who is credited with suggesting the unaccompanied opening. The producer was David Foster; Kirk Whalum played the saxophone. The soundtrack became one of the best-selling albums ever, and the single spent fourteen weeks at number one in the United States, a record at the time.","It was not the first choice: the plan had been to record What Becomes of the Brokenhearted until that song turned up in another film. Parton’s version is a quiet, tender country waltz; listen for how Houston keeps the melody almost intact but stretches individual syllables into long ornamental runs rooted in her gospel training at New Hope Baptist Church in Newark.","It became the template for the big pop ballad and for a generation of televised singing competitions, where its final key change is a rite of passage. Some critics find the performance overwhelming or showy; others hear an extraordinary piece of breath control and pacing. Parton has said the royalties from it made her very comfortable.","Hear Parton’s original first, then Houston’s: notice what stays the same and how the arrangement pushes toward one moment. On a second listen, follow only the breathing and the dynamics in the unaccompanied opening. Seek the full soundtrack version rather than the shorter radio edit, which trims the unaccompanied opening."],"whitney-houston-i-wanna-dance-with-somebody":["A bright, bouncing dance-pop song with a cowbell-and-synthesizer introduction, a skipping drum-machine beat and an exuberant chorus that Houston hits with gospel force. The arrangement is dense and glossy — layered keyboards, handclaps, chattering backing voices — and in the closing minutes she improvises over the repeated hook, pushing higher each time. Underneath, a busy synthesizer bass line keeps the whole thing skipping forward at a brisk tempo.","It was the lead single from her second album, Whitney, released on Arista in 1987 under the guidance of label head Clive Davis. Written by George Merrill and Shannon Rubicam of the duo Boy Meets Girl, who had also written How Will I Know, and produced by Narada Michael Walden, it went to number one in the United States and Britain and won a Grammy. It was the fourth of seven consecutive American number ones, still a record.","Under the party surface the verses describe loneliness at the end of a day and a longing for a partner who will stay; the celebration is a wish, not a fact. Listen to how the ad-libbed ending changes the mood from bright to almost desperate. The song later lent its title to the 2022 film of Houston’s life.","Some critics thought it too close to How Will I Know and too slick, and her label’s crossover strategy was criticised by those who felt she was being steered away from Black audiences. Its standing has grown with time: it is now a wedding and karaoke staple and is often heard as a song of bittersweet longing.","Listen for the moment in the last chorus when she leaves the melody behind. Then compare it with Robyn’s Dancing On My Own, which takes the same lonely-dancer idea and plays it in a minor key. The extended single mix gives the ad-libs more room."],"abba-the-winner-takes-it-all":["A ballad sung by Agnetha Fältskog over a rolling piano figure that repeats almost unchanged. The verses are long and conversational, the chorus builds with stacked backing voices, and the song climbs slowly without ever breaking into a dance beat. It is essentially a monologue: one partner, after a divorce, speaking to the other with dignity and sadness. It lasts nearly five minutes.","Written by Benny Andersson and Björn Ulvaeus and recorded at their Polar Music studio in Stockholm, it was the first single from the album Super Trouper in 1980, reaching number one in Britain and many other countries. Ulvaeus and Fältskog had divorced the year before; Ulvaeus has said the song is not a literal account, but it was hard for audiences, and for Fältskog singing it, to hear it otherwise.","An early demo reportedly had a different title and lyric; Ulvaeus has said he wrote the final words very quickly, late at night, and handed them to Fältskog. Listen to how the melody of the verse lies low and then jumps: she has to reach for the top notes, and the strain is part of the effect. The piano figure never stops, like a clock.","Critics who had dismissed ABBA as bubblegum found themselves taking the song seriously; it is now often voted the group’s best. The revival of ABBA’s reputation from the 1990s onward, including the Mamma Mia! stage musical, placed it at the emotional centre of their catalogue. Some find the lyric’s even-handedness more devastating than any accusation.","Listen for how the backing voices enter one by one. Then hear it after Adele’s Someone Like You or Robyn’s Dancing On My Own, both of which descend from this kind of dignified heartbreak. Then play the full Super Trouper album to hear the song in its setting."],"abba-the-visitors":["Nine songs, cooler and darker than any earlier ABBA album. The title track is a tense synthesizer piece about a Soviet dissident waiting for a knock at the door; Soldiers is about the arms race; Slipping Through My Fingers is a mother watching her daughter grow up; When All Is Said and Done, sung by Anni-Frid Lyngstad, faces the end of a marriage; One of Us is a lonely, lilting single. Synthesizers dominate and the old bright harmonies are used sparingly.","By 1981 both couples in ABBA had divorced, and the group was barely functioning socially. The album was recorded at Polar Music in Stockholm, which had installed a digital recorder, and it is often cited as one of the first albums pressed on compact disc. It was their last album of new material until Voyage in 2021; the group stopped recording the following year.","When All Is Said and Done was written by Ulvaeus about Andersson and Lyngstad’s divorce and then sung by Lyngstad herself. Listen in the title track for how the melody stays trapped in a narrow range, the music as claustrophobic as the room it describes. Slipping Through My Fingers was prompted by Ulvaeus and Fältskog’s daughter heading to school.","It was their least commercially successful album at the time and was often called cold. Later critics have revalued it as their most mature and adventurous work, a bridge between their pop craft and the stage musicals Andersson and Ulvaeus went on to write. Some fans still prefer the sunnier albums.","Start with the title track and listen for the synthesizer’s ticking pulse, then play Slipping Through My Fingers for tenderness. Hear it next to Fleetwood Mac’s Tango in the Night, another band turning broken relationships into polished records. A good remaster will bring out the detail of the early digital recording."],"fleetwood-mac-tango-in-the-night":["Twelve glossy, shimmering songs shared among the band’s three writers. Lindsey Buckingham’s Big Love is a jittery guitar song with gasping vocal answers; Christine McVie’s Everywhere and Little Lies are bright, chiming love songs; Stevie Nicks’s Seven Wonders has a sweeping, airy chorus. Synthesizers, sampled marimbas and layered voices give the record a sheen very different from the band’s 1970s sound.","Fleetwood Mac had not released a studio album in five years. The project grew out of a Buckingham solo album and was recorded largely at his home studio in Los Angeles, with Buckingham and Richard Dashut producing. Band members were struggling with addiction and exhaustion, and Nicks was present only for short stretches. It became the group’s second-best-selling album, and Buckingham left the band shortly after its release.","The breathy gasps in Big Love that sound like a woman answering Buckingham are his own voice, sped up and processed, not Nicks’s. Listen too for how much of the percussion is sampled and played on keyboards rather than hit by Mick Fleetwood: the album is a studio construction more than a band performance.","Reviewers were polite rather than excited; its polish was often compared unfavourably with Rumours. Its reputation has since risen, especially among younger musicians who admire its production, and Everywhere has become one of the band’s most-streamed songs. The argument is whether it is a great band record or a great Buckingham record with guest vocalists.","Start with Everywhere for the chiming texture, then Big Love for Buckingham’s guitar. Listen on headphones to catch how many small sounds are tucked into each track. Compare with Kate Bush’s Hounds of Love, also made in a home studio with sampled sounds."],"bjork-debut":["Eleven songs that set Björk’s voice — whispering, yelping, then suddenly enormous — against club beats, strings, a harp and a saxophone ensemble. Human Behaviour watches people as an animal might; Venus as a Boy has lush, swooning strings with an Indian film-music feel; Big Time Sensuality is pure house music; Like Someone in Love is a jazz standard sung with harp alone. The mood is curious, playful and wide-eyed.","Björk had been the singer of the Icelandic band the Sugarcubes and moved to London in 1993 to make a solo record. She chose Nellee Hooper, producer for Massive Attack and Soul II Soul, and plunged into the city’s club culture. The album was released on the independent One Little Indian label and was a surprise commercial success across Europe and in the United States.","There’s More to Life Than This was recorded in the toilets of a London club during a night out: you can hear the party muffled behind a closed door, and the door opening and shutting. The Anchor Song closes the album with just voice and a brass choir of saxophones, a hint of the orchestral ambitions to come.","It was widely praised and won her two Brit Awards, but some critics treated her as an eccentric novelty and wrote about her accent and appearance rather than the music. The album now reads as a key document of the early-1990s moment when club culture, trip-hop and pop overlapped in London.","Start with Human Behaviour for the timpani and the voice, then Like Someone in Love to hear how she treats a standard. Listen to the club recording on headphones, following the door. Finish with The Anchor Song and its saxophones, which point toward the orchestral writing of her later albums."],"bjork-homogenic":["Ten songs built on a strict pairing: an eight-piece string ensemble and harsh, crackling electronic beats. Jóga opens with lush strings over beats that sound like rock being split; Bachelorette is a sweeping, cinematic ballad; Hunter is a cold march; Pluto is a burst of distorted noise; All Is Full of Love ends the album in stillness. Björk said she wanted a sound for Iceland itself: the strings as its landscape, the beats as its volcanic ground.","After a stalker mailed a bomb to her London home in 1996, Björk left the city and recorded in a studio in southern Spain. She worked with Mark Bell of the electronic group LFO, alongside Guy Sigsworth, Howie B and Markus Dravs, and the Brazilian arranger Eumir Deodato arranged the strings for the Icelandic String Octet. It was the first of her albums made as a single, unified concept.","Many of the beats were made by distorting and chopping acoustic sounds until they crack and hiss like ice or lava. Listen in Jóga for the moment the strings seem to hold still while the beat tears underneath them. The album’s title is ironic: its unity comes from putting two opposites side by side.","Critics received it as a bold turn away from the eclectic pop of her first albums, though its austerity puzzled some. It has since become many listeners’ favourite Björk record, and its strings-and-beats method was widely copied. The video for All Is Full of Love, with two robots embracing, is shown in art museums.","Listen on good speakers or headphones so the low beats and the strings are both clear. Start with Jóga, then Bachelorette. Compare with Sibelius’s Fifth Symphony for another Nordic attempt to turn landscape into sound. Then watch the All Is Full of Love video with the sound up."],"bjork-vespertine":["A hushed, wintry album of twelve songs about intimacy. Beats are tiny clicks and rustles — shuffled cards, footsteps in snow, crackling paper — surrounded by harp, celesta, custom-built music boxes, choir and strings. Hidden Place opens with a whisper; Cocoon is barely audible; Pagan Poetry builds to an overwhelming, exposed climax; Sun in My Mouth sets a poem by E. E. Cummings; Unison closes with an ensemble swell.","Björk wanted music for the private sphere, made for headphones and home computers at a moment when listening was moving from shops to laptops. She worked with the San Francisco electronic duo Matmos and the harpist Zeena Parkins, with strings arranged by Vince Mendoza. It followed her harrowing role in Lars von Trier’s Dancer in the Dark (2000) and was toured in opera houses and concert halls with a harp, choir and orchestra.","Listen for the rhythms made from household sounds: Aurora’s beat is built from someone walking in snow. Harm of Will sets words by the filmmaker Harmony Korine. The music boxes were specially made, with punched cards that had to be designed note by note. Cocoon is mixed so close that her breathing is part of the rhythm.","Critics loved its intimacy and detail, and it is often named her finest album. Some found it too quiet or too private after the big drama of Homogenic. It is now regarded as a key record in the crossover between glitch electronica and song, and between pop and the concert hall.","Listen alone, on headphones, at night. Follow one tiny sound through a whole song. Then hear Arvo Pärt’s Spiegel im Spiegel for another kind of hush, or Meredith Monk’s Dolmen Music for voices used as landscape. The live album from the tour shows how the songs grew in concert halls."],"amy-winehouse-back-to-black":["Eleven short songs in thirty-five minutes, in the sound of 1960s girl groups and soul — booming drums, baritone saxophone, tambourine, echoing piano — with a voice that sounds decades older than its twenty-three years. Rehab is a jaunty refusal; You Know I’m No Good is a sly confession; the title track is a funeral march for a relationship; Love Is a Losing Game is a spare, sad ballad. The lyrics are blunt, witty and specific.","Winehouse wrote the songs after a breakup, and recorded with two producers: Mark Ronson, who brought in the Brooklyn soul band the Dap-Kings, and Salaam Remi, who handled the jazzier tracks. Released on Island in October 2006, it made her famous worldwide and won five Grammys in 2008. Her struggles with alcohol and drugs, and relentless tabloid coverage, followed; she died in 2011 at twenty-seven.","Tears Dry on Their Own is built on the chords and rhythm of Ain’t No Mountain High Enough, the Marvin Gaye and Tammi Terrell hit, and credits its writers — the happiest Motown duet turned into self-reliance after heartbreak. Listen for the Dap-Kings’ deliberately old-fashioned recording: few microphones, lots of room sound, like a 1960s session.","It was hailed at once, and it opened the door to a wave of British soul singers including Adele and Duffy. Critics debate whether its retro sound was homage or pastiche, and whether its frankness about addiction was later misread as glamour. After her death it came to be heard partly as a document of her struggles, which admirers argue distorts its humour and craft.","Start with the title song and listen for the tolling piano and the reverb on the drums. Then play Love Is a Losing Game, the quietest track. Follow it with the Supremes and Dusty Springfield to hear her sources. The deluxe edition adds demos that show how the songs began."],"amy-winehouse-frank":["A debut of jazz-inflected songs with hip-hop beats underneath: Stronger Than Me scolds a passive boyfriend; In My Bed and Take the Box deal with a breakup; there is a version of the standard There Is No Greater Love and the vocalese showpiece Moody’s Mood for Love. Winehouse’s voice is supple and conversational, swinging behind the beat like a jazz singer, and the lyrics are sharp, funny and unguarded.","Winehouse was nineteen, a product of London’s stage schools and jazz clubs, and a fan of Sarah Vaughan and Dinah Washington. Island released the album in October 2003 after she worked mainly with the producer Salaam Remi. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and Stronger Than Me won an Ivor Novello award for songwriting. Her band played live in the studio on several tracks, and she co-wrote every original song on the record.","Moody’s Mood for Love is itself a remarkable object: King Pleasure’s 1950s vocalese setting of words to James Moody’s saxophone solo on I’m in the Mood for Love. Winehouse sings the solo line by line — a nineteen-year-old channelling a bebop saxophonist. She later said she was unhappy with the album’s final mixes and had not approved all of them.","It was well reviewed but sold modestly until Back to Black made her famous. Critics now hear it as the more jazz-rooted, less styled record, and some prefer it for that reason. Its hip-hop production is sometimes called dated beside the more carefully period-styled sound of its successor. Admirers point to its wit and to the ease of her phrasing.","Start with Stronger Than Me for the voice and the attitude. Then find Moody’s Mood for Love and try to hear the saxophone solo underneath the words. Then play Ella Fitzgerald for comparison. Notice how often a sly joke lands in the middle of a sad line."],"beyonce-crazy-in-love":["The song is built on a blazing horn fanfare sampled from a 1970 soul record, played over a crisp, slamming drum beat. Jay-Z raps the opening verse; Beyoncé enters with a stuttering, syllable-chopping hook and a dizzy, breathless verse about losing control in love. The track barely changes harmony: its energy comes from the horn stabs and the rhythm of her delivery.","It was the lead single from Dangerously in Love, Beyoncé’s first solo album, released while she was still a member of Destiny’s Child. The producer Rich Harrison had built the horn-sample beat months earlier; Beyoncé wrote the vocal hooks with him in the studio. It spent eight weeks at number one in the United States and won two Grammys, launching her solo career.","The horn loop comes from Are You My Woman (Tell Me So), a 1970 single by the Chicago soul group the Chi-Lites, credited on the record. Listen for how the sample is chopped and repeated so that a brief fanfare becomes a relentless riff. Beyoncé has said the hook was written in a couple of hours, the stuttering phrase coming from a comment about how she looked.","It was received as an instant classic, and many critics rank it among the best singles of the 2000s. Some wrote it off as a well-produced club hit; its reputation as the song that made her a solo artist has only grown. It is a model of the early-2000s blend of soul sampling and pop songcraft.","Listen for the moment the horns first drop out and the voice carries the rhythm alone. Then find the Chi-Lites original and hear how different it sounds at its own tempo. Then listen to the whole Dangerously in Love album, which sets the single among slower, smoother ballads, and compare with the Destiny’s Child records that came before."],"beyonce-lemonade":["Twelve songs that move through the stages of discovering a partner’s infidelity — suspicion, anger, apathy, forgiveness — and outward to Black women’s history and inheritance. The sound changes with each stage: breezy reggae-tinged pop (Hold Up), ferocious garage rock with Jack White (Don’t Hurt Yourself), brass-band country (Daddy Lessons), piano balladry (Sandcastles), a gospel-rock protest with Kendrick Lamar (Freedom) and the defiant bounce music of Formation.","Released in April 2016 alongside an hour-long film on HBO, it was made with a large team of collaborators including Diplo, Jack White, James Blake, Ezra Koenig and the poet Warsan Shire, whose verse links the film’s chapters. It came amid the Black Lives Matter movement; the film features the mothers of Trayvon Martin, Michael Brown and Eric Garner holding photographs of their sons.","Don’t Hurt Yourself samples the drums of Led Zeppelin’s When the Levee Breaks — itself a reworking of a 1929 Memphis Minnie blues — so a Black woman reclaims a rhythm first taken from Black music. Hold Up interpolates the Yeah Yeah Yeahs’ Maps. Listen for how the album moves from private betrayal to collective grief without ever announcing the shift.","It was greeted as her most ambitious work, winning a Peabody Award for the film. When Adele’s 25 beat it to the Grammy for Album of the Year, Adele said on stage that Lemonade should have won, and the award’s treatment of Black artists became a major controversy. Debate continues over how autobiographical it is.","Watch the film first, then listen to the album straight through. Notice how each genre carries a mood. Then play Marvin Gaye’s What’s Going On, another soul album that moves from private life to public crisis. Listen closely for the samples and interpolations, which carry the story of Black music across the whole album."],"beyonce-renaissance":["Sixteen tracks sequenced as a continuous DJ set, with no gaps between songs. It moves through house, disco, ballroom, Afrobeats and bounce: Break My Soul rides a classic 1990s house piano, Cuff It is sunny disco-funk, Alien Superstar is a strutting catwalk track, and Summer Renaissance closes by interpolating the pulsing synthesizer of Donna Summer’s I Feel Love. The album is about pleasure, release and the dance floor as a place of freedom.","Released in July 2022 after pandemic lockdowns, it was presented as the first act of a three-part project. Beyoncé dedicated it to her late Uncle Jonny, a gay man who introduced her to house and disco, and to the Black and queer pioneers who created those scenes in Chicago, Detroit and New York. It won the Grammy for Best Dance/Electronic Album.","Break My Soul samples Robin S.’s 1993 house hit Show Me Love and Big Freedia’s New Orleans bounce track Explode; the album credits dozens of writers and samples, a history lesson hidden in the liner notes. Listen for the joins between tracks: each transition is mixed like a club set, not a playlist.","It was widely praised as a celebration of Black queer dance culture, though some house-music veterans questioned whether a superstar record could honour an underground scene without flattening it. It lost Album of the Year to Harry Styles, renewing arguments about the Grammys. Its continuous form has been praised as a statement about how dance music is actually experienced.","Play it without shuffle, from start to finish, loud. Listen for the voices of ballroom emcees and bounce artists. Then play Robyn’s Dancing On My Own and Madonna’s Ray of Light for two other pop albums that owe everything to the club."],"taylor-swift-1989":["Thirteen synthesizer-driven pop songs, with gated drums, sparkling keyboards and layered vocals recalling the 1980s pop of the year Swift was born. Blank Space parodies her own media image as a serial romancer; Style is a slinky, guitar-funk song; Out of the Woods is a pounding, anxious chant; Shake It Off is a brassy dismissal of her critics; Clean closes quietly with a sober, healing ballad.","Swift had begun as a teenage country singer in Nashville; with 1989 she announced a complete move to pop. She worked with the Swedish hit-makers Max Martin and Shellback, with Jack Antonoff and Ryan Tedder, and with Imogen Heap on Clean. She had moved to New York, and the album opens with Welcome to New York. In 2014 she also pulled her catalogue from Spotify in a dispute over payments to musicians.","Swift has described Clean as written about sobriety from a relationship, using the language of addiction recovery. The re-recorded 1989 (Taylor’s Version), released in 2023, is part of her campaign to own her own recordings after her early masters were sold; listening to both shows how carefully the arrangements were rebuilt.","It won the Grammy for Album of the Year and was widely admired for its craft. Some critics missed the narrative detail of her country songs and felt the Max Martin production sanded her down. Ryan Adams covered the whole album in a folk-rock style in 2015, an early sign that serious critics were beginning to treat her songwriting as the point.","Start with Out of the Woods for the drums, then Style for the groove, then Clean. Compare with Madonna’s 1980s records or Fleetwood Mac’s Tango in the Night to hear what it borrows. Hear the 2023 re-recording next to the original and listen for the small differences in her voice."],"taylor-swift-folklore":["Sixteen quiet songs, mostly piano- or guitar-based, with soft strings, muted drums and hazy synthesizers. Many are told through characters rather than Swift herself: the last great american dynasty tells the story of the socialite Rebekah Harkness, whose Rhode Island house Swift bought; cardigan, august and betty form a three-song teenage love triangle heard from each side; exile is a duet with Bon Iver; epiphany imagines her grandfather in the Second World War.","Swift wrote and recorded the album during the Covid-19 lockdown of 2020, working remotely with Aaron Dessner of the indie band the National and Jack Antonoff. It was announced only hours before release in July 2020. Some songs were co-written by William Bowery, later revealed as a pseudonym of her then-partner Joe Alwyn. It won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","The triangle songs mirror each other: details from one reappear from a different character’s view in another, so a small phrase or image changes meaning as you move between them. Listen for how Dessner’s piano patterns, borrowed from his indie-rock work, drive the songs more than any beat. Several songs share images and phrases that return like motifs.","It was received as a bold artistic turn, and many critics who had resisted Swift praised its restraint and storytelling. Some indie-rock listeners felt it borrowed the National’s sound without its edge. It is often named her best album and was followed within months by a companion, evermore. Its sales showed that a quiet record could still dominate charts.","Listen to cardigan, august and betty in sequence as a story. Then play exile for the contrast of two voices. Follow with Nick Drake’s Pink Moon for another spare, intimate record made in a small room. Choose headphones: the mixes are soft and detailed."],"lady-gaga-bad-romance":["A pounding electropop song with a dark, minor-key synthesizer riff, a nonsense-syllable chant for a hook and a chorus that rises into a full-throated rock vocal. A spoken French bridge and a sudden gear change into the final chorus give it the drama of a stage number. The subject is a lover who is also bad for the singer: desire bound up with danger and obsession.","It was the lead single from The Fame Monster, a 2009 expansion of Gaga’s debut, co-written and produced with RedOne. Its video, directed by Francis Lawrence and set in a white bathhouse, became the most-viewed on YouTube at the time and helped establish the video platform as the new MTV. Gaga, a New York performance artist and songwriter, was building a theatrical persona in the tradition of Bowie and Madonna.","The lyric name-checks three Alfred Hitchcock films — Psycho, Vertigo and Rear Window — and Gaga has linked the song’s obsession to their themes of voyeurism and fatal desire; listen for how the synthesizer riff has the stabbing insistence of a Bernard Herrmann score. In the video she wears pieces from Alexander McQueen’s final runway collection, including his towering armadillo shoes.","It was a global hit and won two Grammys; critics hailed it as the best pop single of 2009. Some found her image more interesting than her music, while others argued that the persona was the art. It is now regarded as a defining song of late-2000s pop, and its video as a landmark of the YouTube age.","Listen for the change in key before the last chorus. Then watch the video with the sound up. Play Bernard Herrmann’s Psycho score afterwards to hear the family resemblance. Then hear the rest of The Fame Monster, a short, eight-song collection where each track dramatises a different fear, and compare it with Madonna’s Like a Prayer."],"robyn-dancing-on-my-own":["A song with a pulsing, insistent synthesizer line that runs from start to finish over a punchy dance beat. Robyn sings about standing alone in a club, watching the person she loves with someone else. The chorus builds with layered synthesizers and her voice pushing to the top of its range, and the song never becomes happy: the dancing is a way of surviving.","Robyn had been a Swedish teen pop star in the 1990s before leaving her major label and founding her own label, Konichiwa Records, in 2005. Dancing On My Own was the lead single from Body Talk Pt. 1, part of a series of three EPs released in 2010, co-written and produced with Patrik Berger. It was a hit in Europe and became a cult favourite in the United States.","Listen for how little the arrangement changes between verse and chorus: the lift comes almost entirely from her voice climbing and from extra synthesizer layers stacking up, so the song feels like the same night getting louder rather than going anywhere new. The song has been covered as a slow ballad by the British singer Calum Scott, revealing the grief beneath the dance track.","It was widely hailed as one of the best pop songs of its decade, topping at least one major magazine’s end-of-decade list. Critics praised its combination of dance-floor energy and heartbreak, sometimes called crying on the dance floor. It has influenced many younger pop artists, from Lorde to Charli XCX.","Play it loud and let the synthesizer pulse carry you. Then listen to Calum Scott’s ballad version to hear the lyric naked. Follow with ABBA’s The Winner Takes It All to hear the Swedish pop heartbreak tradition behind it. Listen to the whole of Body Talk afterwards."],"adele-21":["Eleven songs about the end of a relationship, sung in a powerful, raw voice. Rolling in the Deep opens with a stomping beat and a gospel-style chorus; Rumour Has It is a bluesy, handclapping put-down; Set Fire to the Rain is a big string-drenched ballad; Someone Like You closes the album with just piano and voice, a moment of resigned grief. The sound mixes soul, gospel, country and pop balladry.","Adele wrote the album at twenty-one, after a breakup, and worked with producers including Paul Epworth, Rick Rubin and Ryan Tedder; Dan Wilson co-wrote Someone Like You. She had heard a lot of American country and rockabilly — she has named Wanda Jackson — while touring. Released by XL Recordings in 2011, it became the best-selling album of the 21st century and won six Grammys.","Someone Like You was written with Dan Wilson in a day or two, with the piano figure played by Wilson. Listen for the tiny breaks in her voice on the album recording. Later in 2011 she had surgery on her vocal cords, and the voice on 21 is a snapshot of an instrument under strain.","It was universally acclaimed and sold beyond all expectations at a time when album sales were collapsing. Some critics called it old-fashioned, but most praised its directness. Her performance of Someone Like You at the 2011 Brit Awards, with only piano, is remembered as one of the most moving moments in British television pop.","Listen to Rolling in the Deep and Someone Like You back to back: one is all force, the other all restraint. Then hear Dusty Springfield and Etta James for her lineage. Then play the whole album in order, noticing how the mood shifts from anger to acceptance over its eleven songs."],"lorde-royals":["A startlingly spare track: deep bass, finger snaps, a slow kick drum and many overdubbed layers of Lorde’s own voice, singing in low, cool tones. The song is a teenager’s shrug at the luxury and excess paraded in mainstream pop and hip-hop, set against the ordinary life of her friends in a New Zealand suburb. It has no guitars or keyboards to speak of; voice and rhythm do all the work.","Lorde (Ella Yelich-O’Connor) was sixteen when she co-wrote the song with the Auckland producer Joel Little. It appeared first on an EP released free online and then on her debut album, Pure Heroine. It reached number one in the United States — the first solo New Zealand artist to do so — and won the Grammy for Song of the Year.","The title came from a National Geographic photograph of the baseball player George Brett, wearing a Kansas City Royals shirt, signing baseballs. Listen for how many vocal layers fill the track: she is essentially a choir of one, doing the work that synthesizers usually do. Joel Little built the track in his Auckland studio around her voice, leaving deliberate space in the mix.","It was hailed as a fresh voice against pop excess. Some critics argued that its list of luxury items singled out hip-hop imagery and carried an uncomfortable racial edge; others said it was simply a teenager noticing what was on the radio. The debate is still cited in discussions of pop and cultural critique.","Listen on headphones and count the layers of her voice. Then hear Grandmaster Flash’s The Message to hear what the song is reacting to in hip-hop’s own tradition of social realism. Then play the whole of Pure Heroine, which extends the same sound across an album."],"lorde-melodrama":["Eleven songs imagined as a single house party after a breakup, from the first rush to the lonely walk home. Green Light starts as a quiet piano ballad and bursts into a pounding house-piano anthem; Sober and Homemade Dynamite capture the giddiness of the night; Liability is an exposed piano confession; Writer in the Dark is almost a theatrical aria; Perfect Places closes with a wistful question about where happiness lives.","Lorde was twenty, living partly in New York after the success of Pure Heroine. She worked closely with the producer Jack Antonoff over two years. Released in June 2017, it topped the American album chart and was nominated for the Grammy for Album of the Year; many critics named it the best album of the year. She has described the album as a study of solitude and of the extremes of feeling that come after heartbreak.","Lorde has synaesthesia and has said she hears songs as colours; she chose sounds to fit those colours. Listen in Green Light for how the piano changes from a sad ballad instrument into a dance instrument in one beat. Hard Feelings/Loveless is two songs joined at a sudden shift in mood, like a party turning.","It was received with broad acclaim and is now regarded as one of the defining pop albums of the late 2010s, praised for its emotional honesty and ambitious structure. Some found the production too close to Antonoff’s work with other artists. Its reputation as a cult coming-of-age record has only grown.","Play it straight through, ideally late at night. Start with Green Light, then Liability. Then hear Kate Bush’s Hounds of Love for another album that shapes a night into a story. Listen for the returning idea of a party in the lyrics and the sounds."],"charli-xcx-pop-2":["Ten tracks of exaggerated, glittering, distorted pop, packed with guest singers and rappers. Voices are pitch-corrected until they sound like machines; synthesizers glitch and shriek; songs lurch between bubblegum melody and industrial noise. Backseat with Carly Rae Jepsen is a dreamy car-ride song; Femmebot is a robotic dance track; I Got It is a chaotic posse cut; Track 10 dissolves into distorted, melting vocals. The whole feels like pop music on fast-forward.","Charli XCX had been a mainstream hitmaker but grew frustrated with her label’s pressure. She released Pop 2 as a mixtape in December 2017 with A. G. Cook of the London label PC Music as executive producer, and producer SOPHIE among the collaborators. It became a founding text of what critics would call hyperpop, and a touchstone for young queer fans online.","Listen to Track 10, which begins as one song and warps into another as though a file were corrupting. Its guest list is a snapshot of the queer and underground pop world of 2017, including Kim Petras, Caroline Polachek, Mykki Blanco and Pabllo Vittar. Many of the voices are processed so heavily you cannot tell who is singing.","Initially a cult record, it was praised by critics for its daring and is now regarded as a blueprint for the hyperpop that followed. Some listeners found it abrasive and unfinished; admirers argue that is the point, a rebuke to over-polished pop. It is often called her artistic breakthrough. It also made a star of its producers.","Start with Backseat for its soft beauty, then Track 10 for its chaos. Listen on headphones to catch the details in the processing. Then hear Kraftwerk’s Computer World to trace the machine voice back to its roots. Hear Brat next to see where the ideas went."],"charli-xcx-brat":["Fifteen tracks of hard, bass-heavy club pop, bratty and confessional by turns. 360 and Club Classics are swaggering party anthems; Sympathy Is a Knife and I Might Say Something Stupid admit to jealousy and insecurity; So I is a tribute to the late producer SOPHIE; Girl, So Confusing is about a fraught friendship with another singer; I Think About It All the Time weighs whether to have children. The beats are loud and blunt, the lyrics unusually frank.","Released by Atlantic in June 2024 and made with A. G. Cook, Finn Keane, George Daniel and others, it drew on the London and New York club scenes. Its lime-green cover in plain type spawned a summer-long meme; when Charli wrote that Kamala Harris was brat, the Harris campaign adopted the colour and style. It won Grammys for Best Dance/Electronic Album and Best Dance Pop Recording.","The remix album that followed included a new version of Girl, So Confusing with Lorde, in which the two singers address the tension openly — a rare case of a pop feud resolved on record. Listen for how many tracks cut off abruptly, as if someone pulled the plug on the party.","It was one of the most acclaimed and culturally ubiquitous albums of 2024, praised for combining club energy with emotional honesty. Some argued the meme overshadowed the music; others that the meme was part of the art. It is regarded as the moment hyperpop’s ideas reached the mainstream. Critics also noted its unusually candid songs about fame, rivalry and envy among women in pop.","Play 360 loud, then So I quietly. Then hear the Lorde remix of Girl, So Confusing. Compare with Robyn’s Body Talk for another album of sadness on the dance floor. Listen for how the confessional songs sit right next to the party tracks, and play it loud."],"sade-diamond-life":["Nine songs played slowly and quietly by a four-piece band that never raises its voice. Sade Adu sings low and almost without vibrato, as if confiding rather than performing; Stuart Matthewman's saxophone answers her in short, smoky phrases; Paul Denman's bass carries most of the melody underneath. Smooth Operator, Your Love Is King and Hang On to Your Love are the songs people know, and the sound they share, all space and restraint, is what the record is remembered by.","Made in London in 1983 and 1984 with the producer Robin Millar, by a band that had split off from a funk group called Pride. Adu, born in Nigeria and raised in Essex, had studied fashion design and came to singing late. British pop in 1984 was loud, synthesised and theatrical; a record built on jazz chords, real instruments and understatement sounded like a deliberate step sideways, and it sold across Europe and then America.","The closing track is a cover of Timmy Thomas's Why Can't We Live Together, a 1972 soul hit built on a cheap organ and drum machine; the band keep its hypnotic stillness and its plea for tolerance. Listen also to how rarely the drums play a full kit pattern. Much of the album's intimacy comes from what the drummer leaves out, so the bass line becomes the real rhythm section.","It won the BRIT Award for best British album, and the band took the Grammy for best new artist. Some critics at the time filed it as tasteful wine-bar music for the new professional class. Later writers, and a generation of R&B singers from Maxwell to Frank Ocean, heard something harder to copy: a whole emotional world made out of holding back.","Play it at low volume first, as it was meant, and follow the bass rather than the voice. Then compare Smooth Operator with Marvin Gaye's Let's Get It On to hear how differently the two treat seduction: his all heat, hers cool and watchful. Any standard CD or vinyl pressing is fine."],"sade-love-deluxe":["The band's fourth album is darker and more spacious than their debut. No Ordinary Love opens it with a slow, heavy bass figure and a guitar line that seems to hang in the air; Kiss of Life and Cherish the Day follow in the same unhurried pulse. The arrangements have thinned out to a few elements with a great deal of silence between them, and Adu's voice sits almost dry and close to the microphone.","Released in 1992, when American R&B was dominated by the hard, busy drum machines of new jack swing. The band had spent long periods apart and worked slowly, a habit that would stretch the gaps between their albums to eight and ten years. No Ordinary Love found a second life the following year when it was used in the film Indecent Proposal.","The album ends with Pearls, a stark song about a woman in Somalia scraping for grains of rice, set over little more than strings and voice; it sits in the middle of an album of love songs as a deliberate shift of gaze. On Feel No Pain, listen for the loose, almost hip-hop drum loop, a sign the band were hearing the music around them after all.","It sold strongly and was well reviewed, though some critics complained the band had simply refined the same mood. In hindsight it reads as the moment the Sade sound became a template, the source of the slow, bass-led, atmospheric ballad that neo-soul and later alternative R&B singers treated almost as scripture.","Start with No Ordinary Love on good speakers or headphones and listen to how much low end there is under that quiet voice. Then play Cherish the Day, and after it Frank Ocean's Blonde, to hear a direct descendant. Late at night is the right hour."],"janet-jackson-control":["Nine tracks of hard, bright, machine-driven funk. Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis build each song from punchy programmed drums, stabbing synthesiser chords and bass lines that snap rather than roll, and Janet Jackson rides on top with a light voice made tough by attitude and spoken asides. What Have You Done for Me Lately, Nasty, When I Think of You and The Pleasure Principle are the hits; the title track opens the record as a statement of independence.","Jackson was nineteen, had just ended a brief marriage and had parted from her father as manager. A&M sent her to Minneapolis to work with Jam and Lewis, former members of Prince's circle who had been fired from the Time. Away from Los Angeles and her famous family, she co-wrote much of the album. It topped the Billboard album chart and turned her from 'Michael's sister' into a star on her own terms.","Listen to the spoken introductions and interjections: the record keeps breaking the fourth wall, with Jackson addressing the listener directly and the producers leaving in studio chatter. The last track, Let's Wait Awhile, is a soft ballad arguing for patience, which sits pointedly against the swagger of Nasty. The album's sequencing is doing the same work as its title.","Critics praised it at once, and it has held up as one of the key records of the decade. Its production approach, drum machines played like an aggressive live band, fed straight into new jack swing and much of the R&B and pop of the next fifteen years. Debate now is mostly about how much credit belongs to Jackson and how much to Jam and Lewis; the answer is plainly both.","Put on Nasty and listen to how the drums seem to hit harder than any real kit could, then to the gaps the arrangement leaves for her voice. Follow it with Rhythm Nation 1814 three years later to hear the same team turn outward from the personal to the social."],"janet-jackson-rhythm-nation-1814":["A concept album in two halves. The first is industrial dance music about the state of the world: Rhythm Nation, State of the World and The Knowledge are clanging, metallic tracks about poverty, drugs, illiteracy and racism, stitched together by short interludes. The second half turns to love and pleasure with Miss You Much, Escapade and the slow Come Back to Me. The sound throughout is dense, percussive and militarily precise.","Her label wanted a sequel to Control; Jackson and Jam and Lewis, working again in Minneapolis, made a record about social problems they were seeing on the news. It came with a long black-and-white film whose drilled, uniformed choreography became as famous as the music. The album produced seven top-five American singles across 1989, 1990 and 1991, a record at the time.","The title track is built partly on a sample from Sly and the Family Stone's Thank You (Falettinme Be Mice Elf Agin), tying its call for unity to a 1969 funk anthem of the same spirit. The number 1814 is the year the words of the American national anthem were written. Black Cat, a hard-rock track in the middle of the album, is one Jackson wrote alone.","Some reviewers found the social message vague next to its sheer commercial power; others were surprised a pop star of her rank would try it at all. Its reputation has grown steadily. It is now heard as one of the most ambitious big-budget pop albums of its decade, and its sound and staging run through the work of Beyoncé and many who followed.","Start with the title track and pay attention to the clanks, sirens and sampled noises in the mix; this is dance music built from scrap metal. Then watch the film, because the choreography is part of the work. Compare with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On for an earlier, gentler soul record about the same state of the nation."]});